# Cello Cool Club — All English Texts

## 001. Monday

On Sunday evening around eleven o'clock Monday already arrives in the suburbs of the week It makes no noise It waits on the doormat of time With its gray briefcase full of duties And its rain-colored coat
At dawn it enters the city without knocking It is a zealous civil servant First it repaints the sky With a thick coat of administrative gray Then it turns off the last lingering dreams And it turns on the alarm clocks All at the same time A siren's cry to announce the end of recess
Monday walks in the streets And everything stands at attention It steals the taste from the coffee It puts sand in the eyes of children It replaces kisses with handshakes It teaches people to walk in a straight line With heads bowed and shoulders slumped It checks that the clocks are on time On the time of sadness and efficiency
Under its reign the city is a large waiting room One waits for lunch One waits for the end of the day One waits for Friday Laughter is forbidden before nine in the morning Sighs are tolerated but not encouraged Even the birds sing a little less loudly As if they were afraid to disturb
But Monday is not all-powerful Look closely In the metro a young girl is reading a poem A small act of colorful disobedience On the fogged-up window of a bus A child's finger has drawn a mocking sun A man and a woman send each other a message Just one word "courage" A small lifebuoy in the ocean of the day
In the evening Monday is tired Its work is done It packs up its gray and its obligations It leaves again on tiptoe Leaving behind an exhausted city But where in the black sky The first star lights up Like a stubborn promise That all is not yet lost.

---

## 002. Jupiter, the Stormy King

After the red soldier and his silence of rust
I am the king, the giant, the big gentleman
I am not made of earth nor of regrets
I am made of gas, of anger, and of solitude
I have no ground to place my feet on
My only land is my own immense weight
I am a painter who paints only with storms
My colors are ocher, brick, and old gold
My brushes are winds of a thousand kilometers per hour
I paint ribbons of clouds that never dry
And in the middle of it all, I have my masterpiece
My great red wound
A cyclone's eye open for centuries
That stares into the void without ever blinking
It is the signature of my melancholy
I have a large family of little moons
My court of silent and faithful servants
They dance around me to try to calm me
They polish my great ball of gas with their little light
They do not understand
That you cannot calm a storm
That is inside of yourself
I am the big protective brother of the family
The goalkeeper of the solar system
With my immense body, I catch the stray stones
The drunken comets that would like to do harm
To my fragile little sisters, Earth or Venus
It is a thankless and solitary job
They are too busy with their little lives
To see the dangers I divert for them
So I turn heavily in the dark
The beating heart of the great celestial clockwork
A king without a castle, a god without prayers
A father who protects his children from afar
And who can only show them
His tormented and magnificent face
I am the great storm that will never end.

---

## 003. Mars, the Old Soldier

After my sister Earth, noisy and full of life,
I am the fourth, the tired soldier
They call me the God of War
A ridiculous old name for an old man like me
My great wars have been over for millions of years
My armor is covered in rust
And that rust is my only color
I remember a time when I was blue
A time when I wore rivers on my face
Tears of clear water that flowed in my great canyons
Perhaps I remember the taste of rain
And the murmur of a wind that was not made of dust
That was before the great drought
Before the great silence
Today my kingdom is a red desert
My mountains are volcanoes asleep forever
My army is a storm of fine sand
That marches with no purpose and no enemy
My only treasures are two small pieces of ice
At my poles like two cold medals
I am the king of an immense silence
The guardian of a dead world
Sometimes I receive visitors
Little metal insects that my sister Earth sends
They roll slowly over my skin
They take pictures of my old scars
They search for signs of life with their little glass eyes
Like children searching for treasure in an empty attic
I let them be
I am too old for anger
Their curiosity is a small, touching thing
In my great solitude
I look at them and I look at my sister in the distance
That blue and white marble so full of noise and fury
She does not yet know
How lucky she is to have water to cry with
I have nothing left but red dust
So I wrap myself in my thin, cold atmosphere
Like in an old military blanket
And I wait
I don't know for what
I just wait.

---

## 004. Mercury, the Hurried Messenger

I am the first the closest
The little messenger of the great king Sun
I run I run I never stop
My race is my prayer and my madness
I carry an urgent message
But I have no mouth to speak it
And there is no one to hear it
I have two faces like a liar or a lover
One face turned toward him
Burned with love and with light
A land of molten rock and of despair
Where silence boils under a sky of fire
It is the face of the eternal "yes"
My other face is turned toward the void
Frozen by solitude and by night
A plain of black ice and absolute silence
Where the stars are cold pinpricks
It is the face of "never"
And I live ceaselessly between these two extremes
Between the kiss that burns and the absence that freezes
The other planets watch me from afar
They turn slowly in their queenly gowns
They see me as a little fool a nervous spark
They do not understand my haste and my devotion
They do not know what it is
To love a fire that devours you
On my skin I have scars
The memories of an old war of pebbles
Each crater is a letter of an unknown alphabet
That tells the story of my terrible birth
My message is written on my face
But no one knows how to read it
So I run
With one side of my body on fire
And the other of ice
The first word in the great king's sentence
The first servant and the first prisoner
I am the spinning top that the Sun has launched
And that will never stop turning.
Čky
Jsem první nejbližší
Malý posel velkého krále Slunce
Běžím běžím nikdy se nezastavím
Můj běh je má modlitba i mé šílenství
Nesu naléhavou zprávu
Ale nemám ústa abych ji řekl
A není nikdo kdo by ji slyšel
Mám dvě tváře jako lhář nebo milenec
Jednu tvář obrácenou k němu
Sežehnutou láskou a světlem
Zemi z roztavené skály a zoufalství
Kde ticho vře pod ohnivým nebem
Je to tvář věčného "ano"
Má druhá tvář je obrácená do prázdna
Zmrzlá samotou a nocí
Pláň černého ledu a absolutního ticha
Kde hvězdy jsou studené vpichy špendlíků
Je to tvář "nikdy"
A já žiji neustále mezi těmito dvěma extrémy
Mezi polibkem který pálí a nepřítomností která mrazí
Ostatní planety se na mě dívají z dálky
Otáčejí se pomalu ve svých královských róbách
Vidí mě jako malého blázna nervózní jiskru
Nechápou můj spěch a mou oddanost
Nevědí co to je
Milovat oheň který tě pohlcuje
Na kůži mám jizvy
Vzpomínky na starou válku kamení
Každý kráter je písmeno neznámé abecedy
Která vypráví příběh mého strašlivého zrození
Má zpráva je napsaná na mé tváři
Ale nikdo ji neumí číst
Tak běžím
S jednou stranou těla v ohni
A druhou z ledu
První slovo ve větě velkého krále
První sluha i první vězeň
Jsem káča kterou Slunce roztočilo
A která se nikdy nepřestane točit.

---

## 005. Neptune, the Lighthouse Keeper

After the rebel lying on his side
I am the last, the distant, the forgotten
The final blue period at the end of the Sun's sentence
They gave me the name of the god of the sea
What a cruel irony
I who reign over a frozen ocean
My kingdom is a world of eternal twilight
Where the Sun's light is just one star among others
A cold and distant pinhead
My color is the deepest blue
Not the blue of life or of hope
But the blue of a deep bruise on the soul
The blue of the great void
I am the king of the wildest winds
Of drafts of two thousand kilometers per hour
My invisible and howling subjects
They are the only music in my icy silence
They tell stories of darkness and speed
The only ones who know the secrets of my surface
I once had a great dark eye
A storm to keep me company
But even it has abandoned me
It dissolved into my blue atmosphere
Leaving me even more alone
With my memories of whirlpools
My job is to be the lighthouse keeper
The last one before the great night between the stars
I keep my blue lantern lit
Not to guide ships, for there are none
But simply to say
"Here our world ends"
"Here infinity begins"
I look at my brothers and sisters
Little lights so distant
They could be memories
I am the king in exile, the guardian of the border
The god of an ocean that no longer remembers water
And I listen to the song of my furious winds
The only proof that I am not
Just a cold and blue dream.

---

## 006. Saturn, the Sad Ballerina

After my brother king Jupiter
And his great uproar of storms
I am the queen of silence
The great melancholy lady
I am not heavy I am light
They say I could float on the ocean
What a funny idea
I only dream of stopping floating
Around my waist I have my adornment and my prison
My beautiful rings of ice and dust
The other planets look at them with envy
They do not know what it is
To eternally wear the debris of a broken love
The frozen memory of a lost moon
I am a sad ballerina in a giant music box
I turn slowly on my axis
An endless pirouette without applause
My dress is a hoop of diamonds and frost
A silent carousel for the ghosts of light
A great necklace of frozen tears
That will never fall
My little moons are my only spectators
They watch me turn with faithful eyes
But they are far away
On the other side of my beautiful barrier of ice
I am the most beautiful perhaps
But I am the loneliest
Beauty is a cold thing that creates distance
I do not have the anger of Mars nor the life of Earth
I do not have the fire of the Sun nor the poison of Venus
I have only my cold and perfect beauty
And my eternal circular motion
I am the great white sigh of the solar system
The forgotten pearl at the bottom of the night's jewelry box
The dream of a prisoner
Who has decorated the walls of his cell.

---

## 007. Uranus, the Silent Rebel

After the sad dance of Saturn
I am the seventh, the strange one, the eccentric
All the other planets stand up straight
Like well-behaved soldiers or obedient spinning tops
I have lain down on my side
It was not an accident, a cosmic shoving match
It was a choice
I had had enough of looking up and down
I wanted to look at the world from the side
To see if the universe was different from that point of view
It is my small, silent revolution
My act of laziness and of disobedience
I do not spin, I roll
Like a child in the grass or a barrel of blue wine
My kingdom is of a pale and quiet color
A blue-green of a frozen lagoon
I do not have the great angers of Jupiter
Nor the ostentatious jewels of Saturn
My beauty is discreet and uneventful
I am a great calm eye floating in the dark
My few rings are so thin
They look like forgotten spider threads
And my moons have names from poetry
Miranda, Titania, Oberon
They are characters from old, sad plays
My only companions in the great cold
They understand my melancholy
From afar they look at me with curiosity
The tilted planet, the anomaly of the system
They wonder if I am sick or tired
They do not understand that rest can be a form of protest
That silence can be a strong word
So I continue my long and slow roll
In the distant suburbs of the Sun's kingdom
I ask nothing of anyone
I am at peace with my own strangeness
And I simply prove
That there is more than one way to dance
Around the great fire of life.

---

## 008. Venus, the Veiled Beauty

After the little mad messenger
I am the second
The most beautiful, they say
The shepherd's star, the evening star
The lovers on Earth look at me and sigh
They give me the name of their most beautiful goddess
Venus
They do not know
They see only my great cloak of clouds
My gown of white and golden silk
They do not see what lies beneath
My true face
If a poet came here seeking love
He would find an oven, a hell
My air is not air, it is a poison that crushes
My rain is not water, it is acid that burns
My kisses are bites of fire
I am the great queen of solitude
My kingdom is a desert of red rocks
Where volcanoes shout their stony anger
There is not a single bird, not a single flower
The silence here is not the silence of peace
It is the heavy silence of death
Nothing can live beneath my beautiful poisoned gown
They call me Earth's twin sister
A sister who went wrong
A warning, a terrible mirror
Of what love can become
When it locks itself in its own heat
And forgets to breathe
So I turn slowly on my axis
In my magnificent prison of gas
I am the brightest in the night sky
All eyes turn to me
And no one sees my sorrow
I am the promise of an impossible beauty
The love that one can only admire from very, very far away.

---

## 009. Earth, the Noisy House

After my sisters, the burned and the burning,
I am the third, the lucky one
The blue marble in the great silent game
I am not made of fire nor of poison
I am made of water, of earth, and of patience
And of a very strange thing called life
I carry on my back oceans that dream
Forests that murmur old secrets
And mountains that scratch the belly of the clouds
I have thousands of creatures
Those that crawl, those that swim, those that fly
And then I have my strangest children
Men
They have a mad talent for beauty
They know how to make a stone or a piece of wood speak
They write poems that make me weep with joy
They invent music that makes the stars dance
They know the taste of warm bread
The warmth of a friendly hand
And the sweetness of a kiss in the rain
They are my magnificent children
But they also have a mad talent for foolishness
They draw lines on my skin and call them borders
They wage war over pieces of land that belong to me
They build factories that make me cough up black smoke
They cover my meadows with cement and sadness
They speak of love and they make bombs
They are so intelligent
And so incredibly stupid
They are my despairing children
So I turn in the great silence
The only inhabited house on the street of the sun
The only planet with laughter and wars
With songs and lies
With libraries and prisons
My distant sisters are clean and tidy
They are perfect in their hell or their solitude
I am messy, I am full of scars
I am noisy and dirty and magnificent and sick
I am their mother, their home, their ship
And I wait
I wait for them to grow up a little
I wait for them to understand
That the only border that matters
Is the one between tenderness and cruelty.

---

## 010. March, the Brawler

February left the door ajar
March kicks it down with a great thrust of his shoulder
He enters shouting and laughing
He is a young rascal full of energy
A brawler with a tender heart
His first act is to throw his gloves of rain
Cold slaps on the cheeks of the city
He whistles in the chimneys
He slams the poorly closed shutters
He fights with the last remnants of winter
A street fight with no rules and no referee
Then suddenly he stops fighting
He sits on a cloud and he smiles
And his smile is the sun
A sun still pale but warm and full of promises
That melts the ice in people's hearts
And dries the tears of the last downpour
March is an impatient painter
He can no longer stand the gray and the white
With large buckets of green paint
He daubs the lawns and the branches of the trees
He yells at the flowers "Get up you lazy things"
And the crocuses and the daffodils stick their heads out
A little scared but curious
He is the great alarm clock of nature
His breath is a powerful and damp wind
That carries the smell of wet earth
The smell of life beginning again
He shakes people by the collar of their coats
And tells them to open their windows and their souls
At the end of the month he is exhausted
He has spent all his anger and all his joy
He has won his fight against winter
The world is clean and muddy and full of disorder
A magnificent battlefield full of life
So March leaves, staggering a little
Making way for the gentle hand of April
Who will come to tidy and polish his giant's work.

---

## 011. July, the Blacksmith of Summer

June fell asleep in a hammock of light
So July the blacksmith sets up his workshop
The sky is his anvil
The sun is his burning forge
And the song of the cicadas is the sound of his hammer
He takes the long and lazy days
And beats them on the anvil of the sky
To make them harder hotter more intense
He hammers the blue until it turns white with heat
He melts the asphalt of the streets
Which becomes a black and sticky molasses
He forges the silence of the crushing afternoons
Where even the birds fall silent
Under his iron hand the city empties
The inhabitants have fled to the sea
Leaving the streets to the lost tourists
And to the cats that sleep in the shade of doorways
The window shutters are closed like eyelids
The entire city is taking a long forced nap
Exhausted by the blacksmith's work
Sometimes his anger erupts
After days of relentless heat
The sky turns black as soot
The thunder is his great hammer striking the anvil
The lightning bolts are the sparks of the furious metal
And the violent rain that falls
Is the cold water where he quenches his burning work
The great hiss of the city cooling down
After the storm July is tired and satisfied
The air is clean and smells of wet dust
The world breathes again
The blacksmith has finished his day
He has created a perfect summer day
Made of crushing heat and sudden relief
He is not a gentle king like his predecessor
He is a brutal and magnificent craftsman
Who forges the strongest memories of the year.

---

## 012. April, the Watercolorist

When March the brawler has gone away shouting
April arrives on the tips of her ballet slippers
She finds the world in a mess and full of mud
A great battlefield after the victory
So she begins by crying a little
Sweet, lukewarm tears that wash the earth
A fine, quiet rain
That smells good of grass and renewal
Then she takes out her box of watercolors
She does not like the bright, garish colors of summer
She prefers pastel tones, delicate nuances
With her finest brush made of a bird's feather
She paints a tender green on the new leaves
A shy pink on the cherry blossoms
A pale yellow on the daffodils
She works with infinite patience
Each petal is a small touch of light
Sometimes for no reason she becomes sad again
She cries a little more over her work
And the people in the street open their umbrellas with a sigh
But suddenly something makes her laugh
Perhaps a child jumping in a puddle
Or a cat chasing its tail
And her laughter is the sun
That breaks through the clouds all at once
And draws a rainbow over her rainy sorrow
April is a capricious and charming artist
She changes her mind like she changes colors
She offers a sunbeam then immediately takes it back
She gives a flower then covers it with frost the next morning
She is the month of uncertainty and fragility
The month of loves that are beginning and still have doubts
At the end of her reign she looks at her work
The world is no longer a battlefield
It is a large, delicate watercolor, still a little damp
The trees are in bloom, the air is soft
She has set the stage with infinite art
Now the great oil painter of summer can arrive
She leaves him a world full of promises
And departs, leaving behind
The smell of rain and of pollen.

---

## 013. May, the Generous Queen

April left a barely dry canvas
Full of shy colors and damp promises
May then arrives like a queen in her kingdom
She does not ask for permission
She takes the world and covers it in gold and perfumes
With a wave of her hand she orders the trees
To put on their most beautiful green dresses
She hangs clusters of purple lilac on the garden gates
And garlands of wisteria on the old stone walls
She unrolls the thick carpet of the lawns
And on it she throws thousands of daisies
Like a handful of little stars
She is the great perfumer of the world
She opens all her bottles at the same time
The heady scent of roses
The innocent smell of lily of the valley
The peppery fragrance of peonies
The air becomes a sweet and intoxicating wine
That makes one dizzy and want to love
Under her reign the days are endless
The sun is her faithful lover
He stays longer in the evening to admire her
People shed their coats and their worries
They lie in the grass
They kiss in the parks without any shame
It is a dangerous month for bosses and governments
For it gives people the dangerous taste of freedom
And the desire to live rather than to work
May is a perfect and slightly sad beauty
For she knows that perfection does not last
She is the summit of the hill of the year
The moment of perfect balance before the long descent of summer
She is the youth of the world in all its glory
When her month ends she does not die
She simply dozes off
Exhausted by her own generosity
She leaves behind a world heavy with sap and pollen
A world full of fruits to be born
And the memory of a beauty so intense
That it makes the heart ache a little.

---

## 014. November, the Stagehand

October has played its great tragic scene
Full of golden leaves and dying light
The curtain has fallen
Then November enters the empty stage
He is not an actor he does not like the show
He is a stagehand a man of the shadows
Dressed in overalls of gray rain
His first job is to sweep up the confetti
The last red and yellow leaves
He makes great damp piles of them that smell of the end
He does not see their beauty he just sees the mess
He takes down the great spotlight of the sun
And replaces it with a small, weak white bulb
That gives an honest and heatless light
Then he sets up the scenery for winter
He pulls the great curtains of fog
He brings down a fine and persistent drizzle
To wash the makeup from the earth
He strips the trees down to the bone
Revealing their true nature
Their black and magnificent skeleton
Drawn against the white sky
The city under his hand is a closed theater
The streets are empty service corridors
People walk quickly with their collars turned up
They are extras in a play with no plot
The wind whistles a sad little tune
In the electric wires and the bare branches
It is a month for seeing the bones of the world
The framework of the houses the structure of lives
But November is not mean
He is just efficient
He prepares for the silence
He prepares for the great white sleep
He makes a void so that the new can arrive
One day
He offers the beauty of the naked truth
A difficult beauty that does not lie
When his work is done
The stage is clean, empty, and ready
He sits in a dark corner in the wings
He rolls a cigarette of fog
And he waits in the silence
For the entrance of the final great actor
The old man in the coat of snow
December.

---

## 015. December, the Old King

November the stagehand has cleaned everything away
The stage is empty and cold
Then December enters
The old king of the dying year
He wears a great coat of snow
And his beard is made of frost
He sits on the throne of silence
And begins his white reign
His first law is the cold
A pure and honest cold that bites the cheeks
And that silences the last complaints of autumn
His second law is the snow
A great white curtain that falls gently
To hide the scars of the world
And to transform the city into a sleeping dream
He hangs chandeliers of ice on the bare branches
And he makes the night sky blacker
So that the real stars shine brighter
But his kingdom is divided
For men are afraid of his great silence
So they light their own small, nervous stars
Their yellow and blue electric garlands
They bring dead trees into their houses
And cover them with glass balls and fake silver
They sing of peace and love
While shoving each other in crowded stores
To buy gifts that will be forgotten in January
The old king watches all this commotion
With his clear and cold eyes
He looks at the fake snow in the shop windows
And he smiles a little from his great coat of real snow
He listens to the recorded Christmas carols
And he prefers the simple music of the wind
But sometimes in the midst of all this noise
He finds things that he likes
The warmth of a real fire in a fireplace
The face of a child watching the snow fall
For the very first time
The taste of oranges and cinnamon
The sharing of a simple meal among friends
These are the small pockets of truth
In the great lie of the holiday
Then comes the longest night of the year
His true king's night
The small lights of men go out one by one
The noise of the party falls silent
And all that remains is his great white and silent kingdom
The city asleep under its blanket of peace
The year is finished
The old king has put everything to sleep
And he watches over the world's slumber
Waiting for the distant return
Of the first green cry of spring.

---

## 016. The Sun, the Lonely King

At the center of everything there is me
The old solitary king dressed in fire
I have no throne no crown
Just my own burning heart
Which serves as my palace and my prison
Every morning I rise with great effort
I push away the heavy blanket of the night
And I begin my eternal work
I am the great alarm clock of the world
I shout in the ear of the roosters
And I paint a fine line of gold under the door of the sleepers
I am the great painter without a brush
Without me the flower is a gray shape
The ocean a large black spot
The face of the beloved a simple absence
I give the red to the cherry
The green to the meadow
And the changing blue of the eyes that look at the sky
I am the patient baker
Who makes the wheat dough rise in the fields
I am the great clothes dryer of the world
Who dries the shirts of workers and the dresses of lovers
I am the companion of the old on public benches
And the warm rug for the cat at the window
I am the excuse for a lunch on the terrace
But I am terribly alone
I see the children playing in the parks
The couples holding hands
The friends sharing a bottle of wine
I warm everyone
But no one can approach me
I give all my warmth
And I remain in my great silence of fire
Sometimes my work exhausts me
My heat becomes an anger
I beat down on the city like a hammer
I melt the asphalt and crack the earth
People complain and hide from me
They forget that without me they would be nothing
In the evening I am tired
I descend slowly behind the horizon
Like a worker going home after a long day
I leave the place to my sister the moon
Pale and discreet
Who comes to console the world from my great light
And to watch over the sleep of my small distant kingdom.

---

## 017. August, the Old Storyteller

July the blacksmith has put away his hammers
Leaving behind a tired and thirsty world
Then August arrives, walking slowly
He is an old storyteller with a beard the color of ripe wheat
And eyes squinted by a thousand suns
He sits in the shade of the great oak of the year
And begins his long story
He does not tell new things
He tells the story of the summer that is already almost past
He remembers the mad rush of March
The shyness of April and the beauty of May the queen
He speaks of the long lazy afternoon of June
And the great fits of anger of July
His voice is the song of the crickets in the evening
A slow, repetitive, and nostalgic song
While he speaks he dips his brush in liquid gold
And gilds the edges of the tree leaves
He ripens the plums and the figs
He gorges them with sugar and heat
His breath is heavy and smells of dust
Of dry grass and overripe fruit
He stretches the days to make them last longer
Especially the evenings
Those long golden twilights where time seems to stand still
The city under his spell is a sleeping beauty
The heat is no longer an enemy but an old blanket
The last vacationers return with brown skin
And the salt of the sea still in their hair
There is a sweetness and a sadness in the air
The perfect beauty of things that are about to end
One knows that summer is still here
But one can already feel on one's back
The small cool breath of September
When his story is finished
The old storyteller falls silent
He has said everything, told everything
He has bottled the summer like a precious wine
He looks one last time at his golden and motionless world
Then he rises heavily
And lets a first yellow leaf fall
Like the final period of his tale.

---

## 018. September, the Melancholy Librarian

The old storyteller August has finished his tale
And September the librarian arrives
He makes no noise he walks on tiptoe
He smells of old paper and the dust of books
His mission is to close the great library of summer
And to arrange the memories on the shelves of time
First he closes the great book of the sun
With pages still warm and a little dog-eared
Between two chapters he slips a last dried flower
A poppy as red as a kiss
He collects the last sounds of summer
The song of the crickets the laughter of children in the water
And he locks them away in little silent music boxes
Then he changes the light of the world
He replaces the heavy gold of summer
With a clearer, cooler, more honest light
A light that hides nothing
Neither the first yellow leaf on the tree
Nor the first sadness in people's hearts
He takes the woolen sweaters out of the mothballs
And the smell of back-to-school
For September is the great month of returns
Children return to school with new shoes
And schoolbags full of empty notebooks and promises
Parents return to the office with skin still salty
And heads full of the sound of waves
The city streets fill up again
The slow rhythm of summer is broken
But September is not a sad month
It is a month of solemn and pensive beauty
The beauty of spiderwebs covered in dew
The beauty of red apples and ripe grapes
The beauty of the first smoke rising from a chimney
It is a month for walking in the woods
And for smelling the scent of leaves beginning to die
When his work is done
The great book of summer is closed and put away on its shelf
The librarian looks out the window
The world is calm, clean, and well-ordered
Ready for the long reading of autumn
Then he takes a new book, a blank one
And on the first page he writes in a beautiful round hand
"October".

---

## 019. February, the Clumsy Lover

After the long white silence of January
February arrives, stumbling a little
He is the shortest of the months, the shyest
He wears a winter coat that is too big for him
And thin shoes that let in water
He would very much like to bring about the spring
But he doesn't quite know how to go about it
He tries to melt the snow
But he only succeeds in making gray puddles
He blows gently on the branches of the trees
To awaken the sleeping buds
"Go on, it's almost time," he whispers to them
But the buds hesitate, they are still a little afraid of the cold
He tries to paint the sky blue
But his paint is pale and watery
A blue of promise, a blue of maybe
His big day is Valentine's Day
The great fair of love in a box
He looks at the florists' windows
With perfect red roses that smell of nothing
He looks at the heart-shaped boxes of chocolates
Made by machines that have never fallen in love
All that business doesn't interest him
He prefers the real things
The love that doesn't need a date on the calendar
The little snowdrop picked in the woods
The poorly written poem full of real words
The warmth of a hand in a coat pocket
The shared silence over a cup of tea
The stolen kisses that taste of rain
It is that kind of love he tries to protect
The simple love of simple people
Then his time is already over
He is so short, poor February
He doesn't have time to finish his work
He leaves as he came
Discreetly, leaving the door ajar
He has not brought the spring
No, not really
But he has left behind
An earth that is damp and ready
A light that is just a tiny bit longer
And in the air, that strange and vibrant feeling
That something is about to begin.

---

## 020. Black, the Color of Secrets

They say I am the color of mourning
The end of everything, the great hole
The twin brother of nothingness
They say I absorb the light
As if it were a crime
But they forget
That I am the rich and deep earth
Where the seed begins its journey to the sun
I am the immense and velvety night sky
Without me one would never see the moon or the stars
I am the pupil of the eye
The little round door through which all beauty enters
I am the ink that gives a body to poems
The musical note on the white page
The respectful silence in the movie theater
Before the dream begins
I am the coal that carries the fire
And the coffee that awakens sad mornings
I am the color of independent cats
Of anarchists and of poets
The color of simple elegance
And of well-kept secrets
I am the refuge of the shadow
Where lovers can hide to love each other
Of course, I am also the color of fear
Of the storm cloud full of anger
Of the locked door
And of the moonless night
But I am not the void
I am the mystery of that which is full
I am not the end of everything
I am just the other side of the light
White is the question
I am the depth of the answer
We are the two sides of the same coin
The day and the night
The poem and the page
And one without the other
Would tell no story at all.

---

## 021. June, the Lazy King

After May the queen has exhausted all her perfumes
June arrives yawning
He is not a decorator nor a revolutionary
He is a lazy king who inherits a perfect kingdom
He sees that the work is already done
The trees are green the flowers are open
So he lies down in the tall grass
And begins his long reign of heat and light
His first royal decree is for the sun
He orders it to climb very high and to stop moving
He dismisses the wind
Except for a little breeze to make the leaves shiver
He slows down time
Especially in the afternoon
So that naps in the shade can last an eternity
Under his golden reign
The colors become deeper
The green of the leaves darkens and gorges on sap
The blue of the sky becomes intense and cloudless
He paints the cheeks of the first cherries
A shiny and sweet red
He sprinkles the fields with the red dots of poppies
And the blue dots of cornflowers
The city also slows its mad race
The asphalt is soft and hot
People seek the shade of the plane trees
They drink cool drinks on café terraces
The only sound is the buzzing of insects
And the distant laughter of children playing with water
The nights are short and warm
Nights of blue velvet where one can sleep with the window open
Listening to the scent of jasmine
June is the guardian of the longest day of the year
The summit of the great mountain of light
A suspended moment where everything is accomplished
Where everything is ripe and heavy with kept promises
There is nothing to do but to be
To breathe the warm air to feel the sun on one's skin
When his month ends
The lazy king does not worry
He knows that the light will now begin to decline
But that is July's job
He has done his part
He has offered the world a moment of motionless perfection
A long golden afternoon
And the memory of a summer that was just beginning.

---

## 022. The Sidewalk

A crushed cigarette butt
A puddle dreaming of the sky
A losing lottery ticket
The hurried step of a man running from his own life
The laughter of a woman who has already forgotten everything
A piece of chewing gum stuck like a stubborn promise
And the shadow of the plane trees
Drawing on the ground
A map to a country that does not exist.

---

## 023. The Businessman

He adds up numbers
He subtracts lives
He multiplies his worries
He divides the world in two
Those who have
And those who have nothing
And at the end of the day
He looks at his reflection in the office window
And nothing is left
Just a tired zero.

---

## 024. The Child's Question

Papa why is the sky sad
It is not sad my child it is blue
But blue is the color of bruises on the soul
Papa why do the birds sing
To forget they are in a cage my child
Even when the cage is as big as the world
Papa why do you work
To feed you my child
And the child looks at his bread
And he is no longer hungry.

---

## 025. The Wall

There was a wall between us
A wall of silence
A wall of averted eyes
A wall of words we never said
One day I drew a door on the wall
With the chalk of hope
You drew a window
With the tears from your eyes
But neither you nor I
Ever dared to open them.

---

## 026. The Minister of Fear

He sells locks for hearts
Barbed wire for dreams
Dark glasses not to see the sun
He talks of security
With the voice of a prison gate
He promises order
And he sows distrust
And people buy
And double-lock themselves in
In their own solitude.

---

## 027. The Dance of the Rain

The rain dances on the zinc of the roofs
A sad and monotonous tap dance
It washes the faces of statues
Who have never cried
It tells stories of clouds
To windows that do not listen
And in my heart
It plays the same song
The one of a love that has rusted.

---

## 028. The Collector

He collects dead butterflies
Stamps of vanished countries
Frozen smiles on photographs
He pins memories with care
He puts time under glass
He has everything
Except the beating of wings
Except the journey
Except the living laughter.

---

## 029. Your Absence

Your absence is an empty chair at the table
A silence in the middle of the sentence
The taste of coffee suddenly too bitter
It is a color missing from the rainbow
A cold draft in a heated room
Your absence is not nothing
It is everywhere.

---

## 030. The Sunday Painter

He paints the sky bluer than it is
He paints the sun yellower than it is
He paints your smile wider than it is
People say
That is not reality
He answers
No
It is better.

---

## 031. The Old Clock

The old clock in the living room
Eats the seconds one by one
With a sound of dry teeth
It does not tell the time
It takes it
And nobody notices
Sauf the sleeping cat
Who dreams of a mouse
Named eternity.

---

## 032. The Station Platform

A suitcase heavy with memories
A train whistling its impatience
A man searching for a face
A woman who found the wrong one
A glove fallen on the tracks
A farewell that was never said
The cigarette smoke
The taste of cold coffee
And life passing by
Without buying a ticket.

---

## 033. The Soldier's Garden

I remember the roses
Red like your mouth
In the garden behind the house
I remember the soft soil under my fingers
Now the ground is hard
And I dig another kind of hole
The sky is full of scrap metal
And the only red I see
Is not the red of flowers.

---

## 034. The Parish Priest's Accounts

Monday he counts the sins
Tuesday he counts the faithful
Wednesday he counts the pennies in the basket
One Our Father for ten francs
One absolution for fifty
A miracle made to order God is a good customer
But he never pays in cash
And at the end of the week
The
Heaven is always overdrawn.

---

## 035. Your Hair

Your hair is not hair
It is a forest at night
It is a river of ink
It is a nest for my tired hands
When I get lost in it
I do not look for my way
I only look to stay there
Forever.

---

## 036. The Monday Meeting

The boss speaks
The words fall from his mouth
Like dead flies
The stapler yawns
The paperclips form a chain
And escape through the window
The boss's tie
Strangles him a bit more with each sentence
And on the wall
The clock melts gently
It is time to be nowhere.

---

## 037. Things Found

A worn subway ticket
An orphan coat button
A sad smile in the crowd
A forgotten promise on a bench
The key to a door that no longer exists
Your name written on a foggy window pane
And my heart
Which I thought was lost.

---

## 038. Those Who Are Hungry

Those who are hungry look at shop windows
Those who have eaten too much look at their watches
Those who are hungry walk slowly
To save up their dreams
Those who have eaten too much walk fast
To digest their regrets
And at night
Some dream of bread
Others are afraid to dream.

---

## 039. Boulevard de Clichy at Night

The neons lie in color
Faces are masks of rain
The music from a bar bleeds onto the sidewalk
The smell of wet asphalt
A couple kissing under a broken streetlamp
And the moon
Which looks like a worthless coin
Tossed in the air
And which never came back down.

---

## 040. Little Truths

A door is a wall pretending to be polite
A cage is a house with too many windows
A clock is a machine for manufacturing the past
And silence
Is a noise that reflects.

---

## 041. The Man at the Window

He looks at the pigeon on the ledge
The pigeon is free
It can shit on the whole world
He is in the office
A cage of glass and concrete
His tie is a leash
He opens the window to breathe
But he has no wings
Just a desire to fall upwards.

---

## 042. The Waiting Room of Shared Solitude

The chairs are bored lined up in a row
The clock nibbles away the minutes with a sharp click
Nobody speaks
People look at their phones
Those little glowing prisons
Where they serve a sentence of shared solitude
A child on his mother's lap
Takes a pencil and on an old magazine
He draws a bird
A very simple bird with large wings
And for a second just a tiny second
The bird flies off the paper
Taking everyone's boredom away with it
Then a voice calls a name
And the room becomes a waiting room again.

---

## 043. The Important Man's Speech

Ladies
Gentlemen and others
We must consolidate the foundations of the future
We must build bridges across deserts
So that our certainties can cross over
We must modernize tradition
And traditionalize modernity
His tie is a well-fed silk snake
His voice comes out of the microphone like learned dust
He speaks of growth and sacrifice
Especially the sacrifice of others
He speaks of trust and effort
Especially the effort of others
And when he has finished saying nothing
Everyone applauds the silence
Relieved that the noise is finally over.

---

## 044. Rain Street

It rains on the street of
Memories
The rain washes the colors of the signs
It makes the traffic lights cry
A man walks in the rain without an umbrella
He does not feel the cold he feels the absence
Years ago under this same rain
There was a woman's laughter
A small portable sun that warmed the street
Now there is only water
Water falling on the asphalt
The asphalt which is the sad mirror of the sky
The man buys a newspaper
Not to read it
But to have something dry in his hands.

---

## 045. The Banker's Prayer

Our
Capital who art in
Stock Markets
May your
Name be made profitable
May your
Kingdom come through shares
May your
Will be done on
Earth as it is in
Heaven
Give us this day our daily interest
Forgive us our profits
As we also forgive those who owe us money
And lead us not into bankruptcy
But deliver us from fiscal evil
For yours is the kingdom the power and the glory
Forever and ever
Amen
And the word
Amen rings like the cash register
Closing shut.

---

## 046. The Dream of the Class Dunce

The teacher speaks with words of chalk
The numbers on the blackboard are prison bars
The child is bored so he closes his eyes
And the blackboard becomes a deep forest at night
The master's voice is a distant wolf calling
The white chalk is a bird of light
Showing him a secret path through the trees of arithmetic
The path leads to a river of ink
Where swim fish made of commas
The child dives into the river
And he swims towards a land
Where additions do not exist
And where two and two make a flower or a boat
As you wish.

---

## 047. The Heart on the Counter

When you left
You forgot a few little things
A blue scarf on a chair
A book with a dog-eared page
The silence your laughter used to make
And your heart
You left it on the kitchen counter
Next to the sugar bowl
It still beats a little
Just a tiny bit
It makes a sound like a tired watch
I don't know what to do with it
I watched it all day long
Maybe I will give it to the cat
Or throw it away with the faded flowers.

---

## 048. The Soldier's Inventory

In my left pocket
A photo folded in four
A letter I will never open
And half a cigarette
In my right pocket
Three cartridges
And a medal of the
Virgin
Which brought no luck to its former owner
In my head
The order to march
The order to shoot
The order to forget my name
Before I was a man with a trade
Now I am a uniform with a rifle
And the mud
I have mud everywhere
Even in my dreams.

---

## 049. Freedom in a Cage

They caught a wild bird
A bird that sang of freedom
They put it in a solid gold cage
With luxury seeds and spring water
And they told it
Sing for us bird sing the beauty of the world
Sing for us the joy of being free
The bird looked at the bars of its cage
It looked at the satisfied faces of its jailers
It opened its beak
And it laid an egg
A very simple very white egg
Then it fell silent forever
And the men never understood
That freedom is not to be sung
It must be hatched.

---

## 050. The Lovers of the Metro

In the subway car
The people are hurried ghosts
Their faces are masks of fatigue
The train screams its metal pain on the tracks
Nobody looks at anyone
Everyone is a deserted island
But in a corner two lovers
They do not touch they do not speak
They only look at each other
And in their eyes the car becomes a garden
The sound of the train becomes sweet music
The tired faces become strange flowers
For three minutes between
Châtelet and
Gare de
Lyon
They repair the world
Then the doors open
And the gray swallows everything.

---

## 051. The God of Forgotten Things

There is a god of war and a god of money
A god of kings and one of official prayers
But there is another god smaller more discreet
The god of single socks
Of keys that no longer open anything
Of nameless telephone numbers
Of whispered promises carried away by the wind
He lives in the dust under the furniture
He asks for neither temple nor sacrifice
He picks up the small pieces of broken life
Lost smiles missed farewells
He performs no miracles
He only remembers
And that is already a lot.

---

## 052. The Weight of a Key

This key in my pocket
It is small and cold
But it weighs heavier than the house
Heavier than the street
Heavier than the city
Because it is the key to a door
Behind which you are no longer there
It no longer opens a house
It opens an absence
And absence
Weighs heavier than the entire world.

---

## 053. The Stock Exchange of Hearts

Today love is on the rise
Trust is stable
Anger has plummeted
Joy makes a spectacular breakthrough
Sadness stagnates
The analysts understand nothing
They talk of an irrational market
But the heart is not a stock
It obeys no law
Except its own
And today my heart has decided
That the value of your smile
Was worth more than all the gold in the world.

---

## 054. The Dialogue of Hammer and Glass

The hammer said to the glass
I am strength I am power
I can break you into a thousand pieces
The glass answered nothing
It just let the light pass through
It reflected the sky
The face of the child who was watching
The hammer struck
The glass shattered into a million shards of light
Each shard continued to reflect the sky
The hammer remained there
Heavy and stupid in its victory
Having succeeded in breaking
Only beauty
Without ever understanding it.

---

## 055. The Revolt of the Billboards

The billboard for the car said
Buy freedom but stay in traffic jams
The billboard for the perfume said
Be irresistible but stay alone in front of your mirror
The billboard for the bank said
Realize your dreams by going into debt for thirty years
One day they got tired of lying
They tore down their own posters
And they showed the gray wall behind
The bare and honest wall
And they wrote on it with broken neon letters
LIFE
IS
ELSE
WHERE
And people looked up from their phones
And for the first time
They saw the sky.

---

## 056. The Weight of a Regret

A regret is not heavy
It is like a grain of sand in the shoe
At first you do not feel it
Then it starts to irritate
Then it hurts
Then you can no longer walk
You sit on the roadside
You take off your shoe
You shake out the grain of sand
But it does not fall out
It is stuck to the skin
It has become a part of us
And you must learn to walk with it
Limping a little
For the rest of your life.

---

## 057. The Streetlamp's Prayer

My God who art electricity
May your light shine
May your kingdom of kilowatts come
May your will be done in my street as in the others
Give me this day my ration of night
Forgive me my shadow zones
As I forgive the dogs who mistake me for a tree
And lead me not into the temptation of a blackout
But deliver us from vandals
For to me belong the lovers the lonely and the stray cats
For the nights of nights
Amen
And may the moths
Serve as my choir.

---

## 058. The Taste of Rain

Rain does not taste the same everywhere
In the countryside it tastes of earth and grass
In the city it tastes of asphalt and sadness
By the sea it tastes of salt and departures
On your face it tastes of your tears
Or of your smile
When you raise your head to drink it
Rain has no taste of its own
It takes the taste of whatever it touches
Like love
Which takes the taste
Of the heart into which it falls.

---

## 059. The Revolt of the Common Nouns

The table said
I no longer want to be a table I want to be a mountain
The chair said
I no longer want to be a chair I want to be a bird
The window said
I no longer want to be a window I want to be the horizon
So they exchanged their names
The table became a mountain where cats went mountaineering
The chair became a bird that flew away over the mountain
The window became the horizon where the bird vanished
The man in the room was left alone
With words that no longer meant anything
And he understood that poetry
Is when you give a chair
Permission
To fly away.

---

## 060. The Collector of Silences

He collects silences
The awkward silence after a bad joke
The heavy silence before the storm
The respectful silence in a cemetery
The complicit silence between two thieves
The blank silence of a page to be written
His collection is invisible and priceless
He keeps it in his heart
In the evening he sits in his armchair
And he listens to his silences
Each one tells a story
Deeper than all words
He knows that noise is easy
But that silence
Is an art
And that the most beautiful silence
Is the one that is full
Of everything we do not need to say.

---

## 061. The Last Traveler

The last bus of the night
Smells of fatigue and solitude
The seats are cold
The windows weep with condensation
There is only one traveler
An old man with a hat
He does not look outside
He looks inside
He looks at the landscape of his life
The mountains of his joys
The rivers of his sorrows
The bus stops at the terminal
Which is called "End of the
Line"
The old man does not get off
He remains seated
He continues his journey
On the only line
That has no terminal
The line of memory.

---

## 062. The Memory of a Wall

I am a wall
I have seen things
I have felt the backs of lovers hiding
I have heard whispered secrets
I have borne the weight of war posters
And of children's drawings
Words of love and hate were written on me
I was covered in paint
I was cracked
But I am still here
I am the skin of the city
The memory of the street
And if you press your ear against me
You might hear
The sound of all the hearts
That beat one day
On my side of life.

---

## 063. The Weight of Words

The word "war" weighs a ton of tears
The word "bread" weighs the weight of hope
The word "always" weighs the weight of a lie
The word "never" weighs the weight of a regret
The word "you" weighs the weight of my entire world
We must be careful with the words we use
They are not toys
They are stones or feathers
Bridges or walls
Poisons or remedies
Before speaking
We should weigh each word
On the fragile little scale
Of our own heart.

---

## 064. The Revolt of the Garden Statues

The garden gnome said
I am tired of smiling stupidly with my wheelbarrow
The
Plaster Venus said
I am cold and I would like a coat
The plastic pink flamingo said
I would like to see the sea for real
So they left the garden
The gnome went to work in a mine
The
Venus registered with a modeling agency
The pink flamingo took a plane ticket to the tropics
The owner of the garden was very angry
He said
Where is the respect for property
He replaced the statues
With surveillance cameras
And his garden became very secure
And very sad.

---

## 065. The Merchant of Gray Skies

Everyone sells blue skies
He sells gray skies
Skies of fine drizzle
Skies of fog
Skies of melancholy
People look at him strangely
Why sell sadness
He answers
The gray sky is not sad
It is restful
It is gentle on the eyes
It makes you want to stay at home
To read a book to drink a tea
To do nothing
The gray sky is the right to idleness
The right to melancholy
And in a world that asks you to be always happy
Maybe that is
The real revolution.

---

## 066. The Road's Prayer

My God who art tar
May my asphalt be smooth
May thy kingdom of cars come
May thy will be done on the right as it is on the left
Give us today our daily white line
Forgive us our potholes
As we forgive the tires that crush us
And lead us not into the temptation of traffic jams
But deliver us from roadworks
For to us belong the speed the distance and the horizon
For miles and miles
Amen
And may the road signs
Serve as my saints.

---

## 067. The Ministry of Complicated Things

Their job is to complicate simple things
They turn a declaration of love
Into a three-page form
They turn a handshake
Into a contract with fine-print clauses
They turn a good idea
Into a six-hour meeting
They speak a language that no one understands
With words like "synergy" "paradigmatic" "implementation"
They create problems
In order to sell the solutions
They are the high priests of confusion
And they are afraid of only one thing
A child who asks
Why.

---

## 068. The Dance of the Dead Leaves

Dead leaves are not dead
They only pretend
When the wind rises
They begin to dance
A last waltz before the long sleep
They dance to remember the summer
Of the sun on their green skin
Of the singing of birds in their branches
They dance to forget the coming winter
The cold the snow the silence
They are not sad
They are wise
They know that falling
Is not dying
It is just another way of traveling
And of preparing
Next spring.

---

## 069. The Thief of Sleep

He does not steal money
He steals sleep
At night he enters bedrooms
Without making a sound
And he takes people's sleep
He puts it in a large bag
In the morning people wake up tired
With dark circles under their eyes
They do not know their rest was stolen from them
The thief goes home
And he gives the stolen sleep
To his sick little girl
Who cannot sleep because of the pain
She finally falls asleep
With a smile
And the thief watches her
With a heavy heart
To be at once
A criminal and a savior.

---

## 070. The Song of the Crack

I am a crack in a wall
A tiny line of nothing at all
People look at me with contempt
They say
That must be repaired
But they do not know
That I am a window
A small window to the other side
I let in a little light
A little wind
A little life
I am proof that nothing is perfect
That nothing is eternal
I am the small necessary wound
That prevents the wall
From being a prison.

---

## 071. The Last Train

The last train of the night
Is full of people who do not want to go home
The student who is afraid of his exams
The worker who is afraid of his wife
The lover who is afraid of solitude
The train rolls slowly
As if it were hesitating
The lights of the houses are judging eyes
The train is an ark of
Noah's ark of sadness
Everyone is alone with their sorrow
The train arrives at the terminus
Everyone gets off
And returns to their life
As one returns to prison
After a short furlough
Waiting for
The last train of tomorrow.

---

## 072. The Museum of Simple Things

In this museum there are no master paintings
There is a chipped cup
A lost button
A dried flower
A smooth pebble
Each object is in a display case
With a small label "Cup in which my father drank his coffee" "Button from the dress of my first love" "Flower he gave me before going to war"
It is the museum of the heart's memory
The museum of invisible treasures
The visitors do not speak
They look
And they remember
Their own cups their own buttons
Their own flowers
And they understand that the true value
Is not in gold
But in the love that settled
For a moment
On the simplest things.

---

## 073. The Revolt of the Punctuation Marks

The period said
I am sick of ending everything
The comma said
I am tired of making pauses
The exclamation mark said
Enough shouting
The question mark said
I no longer want to doubt
So they all resigned
Phrases became endless rivers
Without breath without emotion without question
A long river of gray words
People no longer read
It was too boring
They understood that punctuation
Is not a decoration
It is the breathing of the text
It is its music its heart
And they asked for forgiveness
From the small modest signs
That give to language
All of its life.

---

## 074. The Merchant of Regrets

He does not sell new things
He sells second-hand regrets
The regret of not having said "I love you"
The regret of having taken this job
The regret of not having left
His regrets are well preserved
They still smell of the past
People buy them
To feel less alone with their own
They compare their regrets
Like collectors "My regret is bigger than yours" "Mine is older"
It is a strange trade
A trade of shared pain
The merchant is a wise man
He knows that the only way to get rid of a regret
Is not to forget it
But to transform it
Into a lesson
For the future.

---

## 075. The Sidewalk's Prayer

My God who art concrete
May my name be trampled underfoot
May thy kingdom of passersby come
May their will be done on me as it is elsewhere
Give us today our daily rain
Forgive us our cracks
As we forgive the chewing gum squashed upon me
And lead us not into the temptation of roadworks
But deliver us from dog poop
For to us belong the hurried the flaneurs and the lovers
For streets and streets
Amen
And may the cigarette butts
Serve as my incense.

---

## 076. The Ministry of Normality

Their goal is for everyone to be the same
Same house same car same dog
Same ideas same dreams same fears
They distribute manuals on "how to live"
They organize conformity championships
They punish originality
Their slogan is "Difference is a disease"
They have almost succeeded
The world has become a large mirror
Where everyone sees their own reflection everywhere
But one day a child laughed
A laugh that was not in the manual
A free and wild laugh
And this laugh made a crack in the large mirror
And through the crack
One began to see
A little blue sky.

---

## 077. The Dance of the Shadow Puppets

On the bedroom wall
The father's hands make shadow puppets
A dog a bird a rabbit
The child laughs
He does not see his father's tired hands
He does not see the worries of the day
He sees a magic world
A world where everything is possible
Where animals are born from light and shadow
The father continues his dance
For his child
He becomes a magician a poet
He forgets that he is just a man
With hands worn out by work
And a heavy heart
And for a moment
The father and the child
Are together in the same dream
The most beautiful theater in the world.

---

## 078. The Thief of Voices

He steals people's voices
He captures them in a tape recorder
He has the cracked voice of the old man
The clear voice of the young girl
The loud voice of the orator
The sweet voice of the mother
In the evening he listens to his collection
He gets drunk on all these lives
He is a thousand people at once
But he is no one
Because he lost his own voice
A long time ago
By trying to shout too much
Against the injustice of the world
Now he is mute
And he has only the voices of others left
To remember
That he once existed.

---

## 079. The Song of the Dust

I am the dust
I am the end of all things
The end of empires of loves of lives
I settle gently on the furniture
On the books on the memories
I am the gray patience of time
I am the proof that everything passes
That everything ends
People chase me away with feather dusters
But I always return
For I am the very matter of the world
I am made of dead skin and shattered stars
I am the beginning and the end
And I dance
In a ray of sunlight
To remind you
Of your own fragile beauty.

---

## 080. The Last Kiosk

It is the last kiosk before the desert
It does not sell newspapers
It sells water
Shadow
A story to listen to
A little silence
The travelers stop
They drink the water
They rest in the shadow
They listen to the story
Then they set off again
Toward the great void
The merchant asks them for nothing
He knows that in the desert
A glass of water
Is worth more than all the gold in the banks
And that a good story
Can save your life.

---

## 081. The Testament of a Cloud

When I am dead
When I have fallen as rain
I leave you no will
I leave you the puddle on the sidewalk
In which the sky is reflected
I leave you the flower that will drink my water
And will be a little more beautiful tomorrow
I leave you the damp earth
Which will smell sweet of life
I leave you nothing grand
Just the proof that even in disappearing
One can still give a little freshness
And a little beauty
To the tired world.

---

## 082. The Dialogue of the Hand and the Glove

The hand said to the glove
You protect me from the cold
But you prevent me from feeling
The glove replied
I protect you from dirt
But I prevent you from touching
La hand said
Without you I am vulnerable
The glove replied
Without you I am only an empty piece of leather
So they decided never to part again
The hand agreed to be a little less sensitive
The glove agreed to be a little less clean
And together they faced the world
One giving warmth
The other giving life
A perfect love story
Made of compromises
And of necessity.

---

## 083. The Revolt of the Musical Notes

The
C said
I am sick of always being the start
The
B said
I am tired of always being the end
The
Sharp said
I want to be natural for once
The
Flat said
I would like to go up a bit
So they left the score
They flew about the room
They created a new music
A music without rules without a conductor
A free and wild music
The pianist tried to catch them
But his fingers were too slow too disciplined
He had to stop and listen
And he understood that the most beautiful music
Is not the one that is written
But the one that invents itself
At every moment.

---

## 084. The Merchant of "I Love You"

He sells "I love yous"
Brand new "I love yous"
Second-hand "I love yous"
Whispered "I love yous"
Shouted "I love yous"
He has an "I love you" for every situation
The first shy "I love you"
The last desperate "I love you"
People come to buy them from him
When they no longer have the strength to say it themselves
Or when they no longer mean it
It is a sad trade
A trade of empty words
But sometimes
Someone buys an "I love you"
And says it to someone else
And the other believes it
And the empty word suddenly fills
With all the magic of the world
And the merchant smiles
Because he knows he has sold
A miracle.

---

## 085. The Cigarette's Prayer

My God who art fire
May my name be smoked
May thy kingdom of nicotine come
May thy will be done in my lungs as it is elsewhere
Give us today our daily puff
Forgive us our smoke
As I forgive those who light me up
And lead us not into the temptation of the ashtray
But deliver us from the final drag
For to me belong the pleasure the cancer and the cough
For packs and packs
Amen
And may my butt
Join its brothers on the sidewalk.

---

## 086. The Ministry of Fear

Their job is to manufacture fear
Fear of the other
Fear of tomorrow
Fear of oneself
They spread it on television
In the newspapers
In speeches
Fear is a good product
It sells well
It makes people docile
It makes them accept anything
Surveillance walls wars
The minister of
Fear is a man who is afraid of everything
He is afraid of his shadow
He is afraid of the silence
He is afraid of a laughing child
Because he knows that fear
Is a fortress of paper
And that a single burst of laughter
A single act of courage
A single word of love
Can make it collapse
Forever.

---

## 087. The Dance of the Snowflakes

Every snowflake is unique
A tiny star of ice
A poem of geometry
They fall in silence
They dance a slow and cold waltz
They cover the world
They hide the dirt the ugliness the mistakes
They make everything clean and new
They are a promise of purity
A great white forgiveness
Then the sun returns
And the flakes melt
They become ordinary water again
And the dirt reappears
But for a moment
The world was perfect
And this memory suffices
To give us hope
Of the next snow.

---

## 088. The Thief of Endings

He steals the ends of stories
Just before the "they lived happily"
Just before the "he died in peace"
Just before the "all's well that ends well"
He leaves stories hanging
With a taste of the unfinished
The characters wander aimlessly
The readers are frustrated
He does not do this out of malice
He does this because he hates endings
He thinks that an ending
Is a prison gate
He prefers stories that do not end
Loves that continue
Questions that remain open
He is the great defender
Of the "to be continued"
Which is the most beautiful promise
Of life.

---

## 089. The Song of the Chewing Gum

I was pink and full of sugar
In the mouth of a young girl
I knew the taste of her kisses
Then she threw me onto the sidewalk
I became gray and hard
Glued to the asphalt
Shoes crush me
Rain washes me
I am the sticky memory of a fleeting pleasure
I am the small scar of passing time
I am of no use anymore
Except perhaps to trip
A hurried man
So that he stops for a second
And looks up at the sky
And remembers
The sweet taste of his own youth.

---

## 090. The Prostitute's Last Client

He entered her room
He did not take off his coat
He sat on the bed
And he asked her
Tell me about yourself
She was surprised
No one ever asked her that
She spoke of her childhood
Of her dreams of her fears
He listened without saying a word
For an hour
Then he laid the money on the table
And he left
She looked at the money
Then she looked at herself in the mirror
And for the first time in a long time
She did not see a prostitute
She saw a woman
And she understood that this man
Had not bought her body
He had returned to her
Her soul.

---

## 091. The Testament of a Blade of Grass

I am a blade of grass
I am not much
But I have seen the sun
I have felt the rain
I have danced with the wind
One day a cow will eat me
Or a lawnmower will cut me
I will leave no name
I will leave no trace
Except perhaps
In the memory of an ant
That sheltered beneath me during a storm
I leave you my green color
The green of hope
And my capacity to always grow back
Even when I am stepped on
That is all
And that is already
A lot.

---

## 092. The Dialogue of the Heart and Reason

The
Reason said to the
Heart
You are reckless you are crazy
You get excited over nothing
You suffer for a single word
You are illogical
The
Heart replied
And you are cold you are calculating
You put everything into an equation
You fear everything that cannot be proven
You are a prison
The
Reason said
Without me you would be only a chaos of passions
The
Heart replied
Without me you would be only a lifeless machine
So they continued to argue
Like an old couple
Who cannot live together
But who knows very well
That they cannot live
One without the other.

---

## 093. The Revolt of the Fairy Tales

The
Little
Red
Riding Hood said
I am sick of the wolf I want a prince charming
Cinderella said
Enough of the prince I want to start a union for scullery maids
The
Sleeping
Beauty
Woke up all by herself
And she said
A hundred years of sleep is enough I want a coffee and a newspaper
The
Prince
Charming was unemployed
The wolf became a vegetarian
The wicked queen opened a beauty salon
Everything was topsy-turvy
The children understood nothing anymore
But they found it much more amusing
For the characters had finally decided
No longer to be prisoners
Of their own story.

---

## 094. The Merchant of "If Only"

He sells "if onlys"
In small glass bottles "If only I had dared" "If only I had known" "If only I had left"
It is the most powerful perfume in the world
The perfume of regret
People put a drop of it behind their ear
And they spend the day dreaming
Of the life they did not have
It is a sweet and terrible drug
Which prevents one from living in the present
The merchant is a rich man
Because "if only"
Is an inexhaustible resource
In the hearts of men.

---

## 095. The Book's Prayer

My God who art paper
May my title be read
May thy kingdom of readers come
May thy will be done in my pages as it is elsewhere
Give us today our daily reading
Forgive us our typos
As I forgive those who dog-ear my pages
And lead us not into the temptation of the library
But deliver us from oblivion
For to me belong story imagination and dreams
Forever and ever
Amen
And may my last page
Be the most beautiful.

---

## 096. The Ministry of the Obvious

Their job is to repeat the obvious
The sky is blue
Water is wet
Fire burns
They write very long reports
Give very boring lectures
To prove that two and two make four
They are paid very highly
To say nothing new
They are the guardians of the status quo
The enemies of surprise
Their greatest fear
Is that one day someone will stand up and say
What if the sky were green
What if water were dry
And what if two and two
Made a bird.

---

## 097. The Dance of the Strangers

In the subway at rush hour
Bodies touch brush press together
It is a strange and involuntary dance
A forced intimacy
One feels the warmth of others
Their smell their breathing
One is closer to them
Than to one's own family
But no one looks at each other
No one speaks to each other
Each is an island of solitude
In an ocean of closeness
Then the doors open
And the dance stops
Each returns to their own solitude
Carrying on their skin
The phantom memory
Of an unknown contact.

---

## 098. The Thief of Time

He does not steal watches
He steals time itself
He steals the minute when you hesitate
The second when you dream
The hour you spend doing nothing
He puts them in a large clock
That turns backward
He says he wants to catch up with his past
Repair his mistakes
But by stealing the present of others
He only creates an empty future
A future without surprise without the unforeseen
A future where everything has already happened
He is the greatest of criminals
For he does not steal what people have
He steals what they could
Have.

---

## 099. The Song of the Chipped Cup

I am a chipped cup
I am put at the back of the cupboard
No one wants to drink from me anymore
I have a small porcelain wound
A memory of a clumsy hand
Or of too sharp an anger
I am no longer perfect
But I am more interesting
My chip is a mouth
Which could tell stories
The story of coffee too hot
Of tears fallen inside
Of kisses exchanged above me
I am a cup with a past
And that is much better
Than a new cup
Which still has
Nothing to say.

---

## 100. The Last Piano in the Bar

The piano is old and out of tune
Its keys are yellow like a smoker's teeth
It remembers all the songs
Sad love songs
Joyful drinking songs
It remembers the hands that played it
The expert hands of the professional
The clumsy hands of the lover
The drunk hands of the late-night customer
The bar is going to close
It will be sold for wood
It will play one last song
For itself
A song without notes
Made of all the silences
Of all the laughter
Of all the tears
It accompanied for fifty years.

---

## 101. The Testament of a Star

I am an old star
My light takes millions of years to reach you
When you look at me
You are looking into the past
Perhaps I am already dead
But my light continues to travel
To tell you that I existed
I leave you no will
I leave you my light
A fragile little light in the dark night
A small point of hope
To guide you
To remind you that even after death
Something of us continues to shine
And to travel
Toward unknown eyes
That have need
Of a little light.

---

## 102. The Newsstand

The newsagent is a tired man
He sells the news of the world
Wars crises catastrophes
The glossy paper smiles of celebrities
He sells words printed in black on gray paper
He sees life pass before his kiosk
Loves that are born loves that die
People running after time
He does not run
He waits
He is the guardian of bad news
The motionless lighthouse in the city storm
In the evening he closes his shop of misfortunes
And he goes home
To read a book
Where there is mention of nothing
Only of a bird singing on a branch.

---

## 103. The Unemployed Man's Prayer

Our Employer who art nowhere
Cursed be thy name
May thy kingdom never come
May thy will be done neither in the office nor elsewhere
Give us today our absence of bread
Forgive us our anger
As we do not forgive those who laid us off
And lead us not into the temptation of hope
But deliver us from the job interview
For to you belong the contempt the pity and the indifference
Forever and ever
And after.

---

## 104. The Words You Don't Say

The words you do not say
Are the heaviest
They sit between us at the table
They sleep in the middle of our bed
They have the shape of your silence
The color of your evasive looks
I hear them shouting at night
When you think I am asleep
They are terrible words
Words that are afraid of themselves
Words that could break everything
Or save everything
I would like to take them in my hand
To caress them reassure them
And tell them
Do not be afraid
Come out
Even if you must kill me.

---

## 105. The Drawing on the Wall

The wall was gray and sad
A wall of prison or hospital
A wall that expected nothing
A child passed by
With a piece of white chalk
He drew a door
Then he drew a key
Then he drew a path
Starting from the door and climbing toward the sky
The wall said nothing
But it felt something stir within itself
A crack of light
A desire to open
The child left
But the door remained
And at night the prisoners and the sick
Dream that they take the key
And set off on the path
Leading to the land where walls
Do not exist.

---

## 106. The Dream of a Hammer

I am a hammer
My life is to strike
Striking nails
Striking walls
Striking heads
I am brute force violence
But at night I dream
I dream that I am a bird
A tiny light bird
I dream that my iron head becomes a head of feathers
And that my wooden handle becomes a wing
I fly above houses
I sing sweet songs
I strike no more
I caress the wind
Then the morning arrives
And I become a hammer again
Heavy and full of hatred
Waiting for the night
To be able to dream
That I am something other than a tool of misfortune.

---

## 107. Grammar Is a Prison

The subject must agree with the verb
The past participle must obey the direct object
The sentence must have a beginning a middle an end
Grammar is a barracks
With its rules its orders its punishments
Me I am a deserter
I make dream agree with reality
I let the verb love conjugate all subjects
My sentences begin in the middle and never end
I put adjectives of color on nouns of sadness
I am a syntax terrorist
A dictionary anarchist
For poetry
Is when a word
Finally decides to be free.

---

## 108. The Bridge of Sighs

It is not the bridge of
Venice
It is a little bridge of nothing at all
Over a dirty canal
But people call it the
Bridge of
Sighs
Because all the sighs of the city
Come to seek refuge there at night
There are the sighs of separated lovers
The sighs of tired workers
The sighs of old people who remember
The sighs of bored children
The bridge is made of wood and iron
But above all it is made of sighs
It is heavy with all these sadnesses
And when the wind blows
One hears a long lament
The song of all hearts
That have a little too much weight.

---

## 109. The Statesman's Suit

The suit is impeccable
Cut from the finest fabric
Gray like a certainty
The tie is a silk knot
That strangles questions
The shoes shine like promises
The suit speaks on television
It speaks of the future of the nation
Of growth and security
It uses important words
Responsibility solidarity trust
But if one looks closely
Inside the suit
There is no one
Just a cold draft of air
And the echo of its own voice
Resounding in the void.

---

## 110. The Scar

I have a scar on my hand
It is not a war wound
It is not a dog bite
It is the memory of a shard of glass
The day you broke a vase
Because I had said one word too many
The blood flowed
You cried
You took my hand in yours
And you kissed the wound
The wound healed
But the scar remained
It is white and thin
It is the map of our love
A map that shows the exact place
Where passion almost destroyed everything
And where forgiveness
Saved everything.

---

## 111. The Dialogue of the Rain and the Window

The rain said to the window
Why are you so cold and so hard
The window replied
Why are you so sad and so wet
The rain said
I weep for the sorrow of the world
The window said
Me, I reflect its solitude
The rain said
I would like to pass through you to comfort the people
The window said
Me, I would like to break to let them out
So they made a pact
The rain struck so hard
That the window shattered into a thousand pieces
And the rain entered the house
And the people went out into the street
And for the first time
They danced together in the rain
Free and wet.

---

## 112. The Cloud's Profession

My trade is to be a cloud
I have no boss no timetable
I travel with the wind
I change shape whenever I please
Sometimes I am a great white castle
Sometimes a threatening dragon
Sometimes a face that nobody recognizes
I watch the world from above
The restless little lives of men
Their wars their loves their buildings
I do not judge
I pass by
My only job
Is to cast some shade
Or to weep a little
So that the flowers can grow
And so that the sky
Will not always be
Desperately blue.

---

## 113. The Message in a Bottle

I wrote a poem
On a little scrap of paper
A poem that said
I am alone
Love me
I put it in an empty bottle
And threw it into the sea
The sea carried it away
For years it traveled
It saw whales and storms
One day it washed up on a deserted beach
Nobody found it
The sun erased the ink
The paper turned back to white
The bottle remained there
Empty
Like the heart of the one who had cast it
Proving that hope
Is the most beautiful form
Of despair.

---

## 114. The Animal Court

One day the animals put man on trial
The lion said
He kills for pleasure not for hunger
The bird said
He cages the sky
The horse said
He whips me when I am tired
The lamb said
He eats me while talking of peace
The tree said
He cuts me down to print his stupid laws
The river said
He turns me into a dustbin
So all the animals condemned man
To the worst of punishments
Solitude
They left him alone on the earth
With his money his machines and his intelligence
And man looked around him
And for the first time
He was afraid
Of his own victory.

---

## 115. The Color of Silence

Silence is not colorless
It has a thousand colors
There is the white silence of snow
Which smothers every sound
There is the black silence of the deep night
Full of fears and mysteries
There is the gray silence of boredom
In a waiting room
There is the red silence of anger
Just before the explosion
And then there is the golden silence
The silence between two lovers looking at each other
A silence so full of words
So full of music
That it is the most beautiful of all languages
A silence that has no need for color
For it is
Light itself.

---

## 116. The Collector of First Times

He does not collect stamps
He collects first times
He has in a box
The memory of a child's first step
In another
The taste of the first kiss
He has the sound of the first word
The fear of the first day of school
The pain of the first heartbreak
His collection is the most precious in the world
For it is made of unique things
Which will never return
In the evening he opens his boxes
And he relives those moments
He is not nostalgic
He knows that life
Is a collection of first times
And that the most important one
Is always the one yet to come
The first time
That we will see the day break
Tomorrow.

---

## 117. The Revolt of the Question Mark

The question mark had had enough
Enough of always asking questions
Enough of always doubting
Enough of being the symbol of uncertainty
One day it stood up straight
It lost its curve
It became an exclamation mark
It began to assert things
The sky is green
The fish sing
Love is simple
It was wonderful
The world was full of joyful certitudes
But people began to get bored
There was no more mystery
Nothing left to discover
So the exclamation mark regretted its curve
It understood that asking a question
Is often more beautiful
Than knowing the answer
And it gently bent back again
Happy to be once more
A fishhook to catch dreams.

---

## 118. The Umbrella Merchant

He does not like the sun
He likes the rain
When it rains he goes out into the street
With his umbrellas of every color
He does not sell shelters
He sells portable skies
A red umbrella for singing in the rain
A yellow umbrella to make a sun
A blue umbrella for dreaming of the sea
A large black umbrella for two lovers
People buy an umbrella from him
And they begin to love the rain
They walk smiling under the drops
They look at the world through a colored filter
The umbrella seller
Is a practical poet
He knows that to change life
Sometimes it is enough
To change the color
Of the sky above our heads.

---

## 119. The Secret Life of Proper Names

Proper nouns have a secret life
The street of
Peace dreams of silence
Mister
Leblanc has a black heart
Miss
Legrand feels very small
The city of
Brest is always in mourning
The
Seine is full of sorrow Paris is a bet lost in advance
Words lie to us
They wear masks
They make us believe in things that do not exist
We must listen to them with suspicion
We must look behind
To see the truth
Which often hides
In common nouns
Like "pain" "joy" or "love".

---

## 120. The Streetlamp Blues

I am a street lamp
I am paid to light up others
I see everything at night
Stolen kisses
Hidden tears
Solitary steps
Shady deals
I am the silent confidant of the city
I know all the stories
But I cannot speak
And I am always alone
My only friend is the moon
But she is so far away
Sometimes a moth comes to burn its wings
On my hot bulb
It seeks the light
Like everyone else
Me, I give the light
But I am in the dark
The dark of my own solitude
And I wait for the morning
To be able at last
To go out and sleep.

---

## 121. The Revolt of the Ellipses

We are the three ellipsis points
We are the silence after the sentence
The hesitation the subtext
We are what is left unsaid
But today we have had enough
We want to speak
We want to shout
We want to finish the sentences
We want to say that love is not always beautiful
That hope is sometimes a lie
That the end is not always happy
We are not an open door
We are a wall
The wall against which words come to break
So the next time you use us
Remember
That our silence
Is full of terrible words
Just waiting for a sign
To make everything explode.

---

## 122. The Collector of Farewells

He collects farewells
He records them in his head
The quick farewell on a station platform
The whispered farewell in an unmade bed
The silent farewell of a look
The farewell of a child leaving for school
Every farewell has its music
A sad little music
Which speaks of separation and of passing time
He has the largest collection of farewells in the world
He is the richest man in departures
But he is also the poorest
For he has never collected
A single hello
A single return
His life is a long succession of closing doors
And he waits
The final farewell
His own.

---

## 123. The Poem That Was Looking for a Reader

A poem escaped from a book
It was completely naked without a title without an author
It wandered through the streets
It knocked on the doors of hearts
But the hearts were closed
Or busy with something else
The poem grew cold
It sat down on a bench
It began to weep words of rain
An old lady passed by
She picked it up
She put it in her pocket
Against her heart
She did not read it
She just kept it warm
And the poem understood
That it had no need to be read
In order to exist
It only needed
A little human warmth.

---

## 124. The Revolt of the Still Lifes

In the museum at night
The apple of
Cézanne said
I am fed up with being an apple
I want to roll on the ground
The violin of
Picasso said
I want to play some wild music
Not be a wooden jigsaw puzzle
The pipe of
Magritte said
This is a pipe and I want to smoke
So all the still lifes came to life
The fruits organized a food fight
The instruments played a cacophonous symphony
The objects danced on the walls
The night watchman thought he was going mad
He called the police
But when the police arrived
Everything had become calm and motionless again
Everything in its place
Except that the apple of
Cézanne
Had a tiny bite
And that the pipe of
Magritte
Still smelled a little of stale tobacco.

---

## 125. The Merchant of Good Excuses

He has a small very discreet shop
He sells good excuses
The excuse of "I didn't have time"
The excuse of "it's not my fault"
The excuse of "I'll do it tomorrow"
He has excuses for all situations
For failed loves promises unkept
Ordinary cowardices
His excuses are made with very simple words
But they are very effective
They allow one to sleep at night
They allow one to look at oneself in the mirror
They are the little anesthetic of the conscience
His business is very prosperous
For people will always prefer
To buy a good excuse
Rather than face a bad truth.

---

## 126. The Tree's Prayer

My God who art the earth
Hallowed be my roots
Thy kingdom of sap come
Thy will be done high in my branches as it is below
Give me this day my daily rain
Forgive me my shadow over the little flowers
As I forgive the birds that nest in me
And lead me not into the temptation of the axe
But deliver me from the woodcutter
For mine is the wind the sun and the passing time
For the centuries of seasons
Amen
And may my dead leaves
Nourish the earth from whence I came.

---

## 127. The Ministry of Forgetting

It is a very important ministry
With large offices full of drafts
The civil servants work there with erasers
They erase awkward memories
The promises of politicians
The mistakes of history
The loves that hurt too much
They work day and night
So that memory is a blank page
Clean and neat
They say it is for the happiness of people
So they can move forward without the weight of the past
But sometimes an eraser is worn out
And a memory reappears
An image a smell a song
And people remember
And they understand that forgetting
Is not happiness
But just another form
Of prison.

---

## 128. The Geometry of Love

Love is not a straight line
It is a labyrinth
With dead ends and secret paths
Our two lives were two parallel lines
That were never meant to meet
But love is a poor student in geometry
It folded the sheet of reality
And our two lines crossed
At a single burning point
Now we are a circle
Without beginning or end
A perfect and fragile circle
Containing our whole universe
And we turn together
Hoping that the compass of destiny
Will never slip.

---

## 129. The Thief of Faces

He walks around at night with a strange camera
He does not take pictures
He steals faces
He steals the smile of a happy woman
The tired look of an old man
The wonder of a child
Then he goes back home
And he wears these faces
Like masks
Today he wears the woman's smile
And he feels happy
Tomorrow he will wear the old man's look
And he will feel wise
He lives a thousand lives
But he has none
For he forgot his own face
So long ago
In the mirror of a lost love.

---

## 130. The Song of Things That Fall

The rain falls
The dead leaves fall
The tears fall
The night falls
The governments fall
The masks fall
Lovers fall
In love
Everything falls sooner or later
It is the law of gravity
And of passing time
There is no need to be sad
One must learn to fall
With grace
Like a dead leaf
Dancing with the wind
Before touching the ground
And becoming the earth
From where other things
Will rise.

---

## 131. The Last Dream

When I am very old
And my memory is an empty house
I would like to have a final dream
A simple dream
I will dream that I am a child
And that I am running in a field of poppies
The sun is warm
The wind is gentle
My mother calls me for my snack
Her voice is young and full of love
I do not want a dream of paradise
With angels and music
I just want that little dream
That little piece of lost happiness
And I will wake up
Or I will not wake up
And in either case
It will be fine
For I will have carried with me
The red color of the poppies
And the warmth of the sun
On my child's skin.

---

## 132. The Wind Merchant

He sells wind in a bottle
Offshore wind that smells of salt
A little spring breeze that smells of flowers
A great autumn wind that blows away leaves
A freezing winter wind
People buy it from him
When life is too heavy too motionless
They open a bottle
And the wind comes out
It sends the papers on the desk flying
It makes the doors slam
It brings a smell from elsewhere
A taste of freedom
It does not last long
Just a moment
But it is enough
To remember that the world is large
And that walls are not eternal.

---

## 133. The Revolt of the Furniture

The table said
I am fed up with bearing the weight of meals and elbows
The chair said
Enough of supporting the weight of backsides and troubles
The bed said
I am tired of restless sleep and sad loves
The wardrobe said
I no longer want to keep skeletons and old secrets
So they moved out all on their own
The table left for the countryside to become a picnic table
The chair went to the theater to listen to stories
The bed settled on a beach to watch the stars
And the wardrobe opened wide in a forest
So that birds could build their nests in it
The apartment was empty
And the man who lived there
Had to learn to sit on the floor
And to sleep standing up
And to live without owning anything
And he found that it was
Much lighter.

---

## 134. The Lost Poem

I wrote a poem on a subway ticket
I lost it
Perhaps someone found it
A tired worker
A sad lover
A hurried banker
Perhaps they read it
And that it put a little sun into their day
Or a little sadness
Or just a question mark
Perhaps they threw it away
And that the wind makes it dance now
With the dead leaves
I will never know
And that is the beauty of poetry
It does not belong to us
It is like a bird
We open our hand
And we let it fly away
Toward unknown skies.

---

## 135. The Tool Conference

The hammer said
More strength is needed
The screwdriver said
More precision is needed
The saw said
One must know how to cut
The tape measure said
Everything must be measured
They talked all night long
Each praising its own qualities
Then the nail spoke up
A tiny nail
It said
You are all very useful
But without me
You are useless
For it is I who hold things together
I am the bond the goal the final end
The tools fell silent
They understood that strength precision measurement
Are nothing
Without a little nail of love
To join everything together.

---

## 136. The Unknown Soldier's Prayer

My God if you exist
And if you are not too busy with the generals
Tell me why
Why I died in the mud
For a piece of flag
For a word in a speech
I had a wife children a trade
I loved red wine and the sun
Now I am a name on a monument
An excuse for speeches on the eleventh of November
I do not ask you for paradise
I ask you for just one thing
Make my death
Serve at least for something
Make children learn at school
That war is a dirty business
And that the only enemy
Is the one who gives you a gun
And who tells you to kill your brother.

---

## 137. The Merchant of Silences

In the noisy city
He sells silence
He catches it at night in closed parks
He gathers it in the morning in empty churches
He picks it up in the look of statues
He has silence for every need
A little silence to concentrate
A great silence to forget the world
A complicit silence for lovers
A deadly silence for enemies
People queue in front of his shop
They are tired of the noise
Of the noise of machines of cars of televisions
Of the noise of their own thoughts
They buy a little silence
As one buys medicine
And they consume it in secret
To try to hear themselves
Live.

---

## 138. The Revolt of the Adjectives

The adjective "beautiful" said
I am fed up with qualifying sunsets and women
I want to qualify a just action
The adjective "great" said
Enough of qualifying generals and bankers
I want to qualify a small act of kindness
The adjective "true" said
I refuse to let myself be coupled with the word "lie"
So they left their nouns
They floated freely in the air
And they settled where they wanted "Beautiful" settled on the gesture of a worker sharing his bread "Great" settled on the silence of a man who listens "True" settled on a child's tear
The world became more just
And more difficult to describe
For beauty and greatness
Were no longer where we were used
To looking for them.

---

## 139. The Collector of Ends of the World

He collects ends of the world
Those announced by prophets
Those predicted by scientists
Those hoped for by the desperate
He has a large map
With dates and places
The end of the world by fire by water by ice
By human stupidity
Each time an end of the world does not take place
He puts a small cross on his map
And he smiles
His collection is a collection of non-events
A collection of ridiculous fears
He is not cynical
He is optimistic
He knows that the world is solid
And that the only end of the world that really counts
Is the one that arrives each evening
When we fall asleep
And that the only thing that matters
Is to know if we will wake up
The next day.

---

## 140. The Lock Song

I am a lock
I am the guardian of the inside
I know the secret of keys
The key that opens gently
The key that forces with anger
The key that hesitates
The key that can no longer find its way
I am the border between outside and inside
Between the world and intimacy
I am a small metal hole
But I contain universes
Laughter tears secrets
I am a small cold and hard thing
But I protect what is most fragile
The warmth of a home
The security of a refuge
And the necessary silence
To be oneself.

---

## 141. The Last Journey

He packed his bag
He did not take much
A childhood memory
An adult regret
A burst of laughter
A sad song
He closed the door of his life
Without making a sound
He did not take a ticket
He did not know where he was going
He just began to walk
Toward the horizon
Which is not a line
But a promise
The promise that something else exists
Beyond what we know
Beyond what we have been
He walked light
For he had finally understood
That the only baggage needed
For the last voyage
Is to have none at all.

---

## 142. The Weight of Clouds

They say that clouds are light
It is a lie
Clouds are heavy
Heavy with all the rain they contain
Heavy with all the unwept sorrows
Heavy with all the storms to come
They float above our heads
Like continents of sadness
Sometimes a cloud can take no more
It bursts into tears
And it rains on the earth
People open their umbrellas
They protect themselves from this sadness falling from the sky
They do not know that this rain
Is necessary
To wash the world
And to allow the sun
To shine again
On a clean and peaceful earth.

---

## 143. The Revolt of the Odd Numbers

The
One the
Three the
Five the
Seven the
Nine
Had had enough
Enough of being odd
Enough of not being able to be divided in two
Enough of being the symbols of solitude and imbalance
They decided to unite
They formed a large strange and magnificent number
A number that meant nothing
A number that obeyed no logic
The even numbers looked at them with contempt
You are not rational they said
The odd numbers replied
So what
Life is not rational
Love is not rational
Poetry is not rational
We are the numbers of life
And we are proud
To be a little twisted.

---

## 144. The Merchant of First Words

He sells the first words of children
In small music boxes "Mama" "Papa" "Again"
Every word has the music of the voice that spoke it
A pure music full of wonder
Old people come to buy them from him
When their memory fades
They open a box
And they hear the first word of their child
Who is now fifty years old
And who no longer talks to them
It is a sweet little torture
A small happiness that hurts
The merchant is a sad man
He sells the beginnings of stories
But he knows that all stories
End one day
In silence.

---

## 145. The Saints' Strike

Saint
Anthony said
I am fed up with searching for others' lost objects
I would like to lose something too
Saint
Christopher said
Enough of carrying people on my back
I would like someone to carry me a little
Saint
Rita patron saint of hopeless causes said
My own cause is hopeless I resign
So all the saints left their churches
They went to have a drink at the café opposite
They talked about their lives of sacrifices
Of their miracles and their doubts
People entered the empty churches
They prayed in the silence
They prayed not to saints
But to their own hopes their own strengths
And for the first time
Some prayers
Were answered.

---

## 146. The Tattooed Poem

He had a poem tattooed on his heart
A poem that no one could read
Except the woman he loved
She would lay her head on his chest
And she would read the words with her fingers
With her lips
It was their secret their garden
One day the woman left
The poem remained
But it had become illegible
The letters had jumbled up
The words no longer made sense
It was no longer a love poem
It was a scar
A confused map of pain
And the man spent the rest of his life
Trying to decipher
The language of his own sorrow.

---

## 147. The Revolt of the Red Lights

The red light said
I am fed up with being the symbol of forbidden things
The amber light said
I am tired of being the symbol of hesitation
So they exchanged their roles
The red light became green
The green light became amber
The amber light became red
The motorists went mad
They speeded up at red
They braked at green
They honked at amber
It was chaos
But a joyful chaos
A chaos where rules no longer existed
Where everyone had to trust
Their own judgment
And that of others
And that day
Nobody arrived late
For the destination
Was no longer the most important thing.

---

## 148. The Taste of Absence

Your absence has a taste
It is not a bitter taste
It is not a sweet taste
It is the taste of unsalted food
The taste of water when one is not thirsty
The taste of bread when one is not hungry
It is a taste of nothing
But a nothing that fills everything
A nothing that weighs heavy on the tongue
A nothing that prevents tasting
The rest of the world
Your absence
Is the only dish
That I eat every day
And that never nourishes me.

---

## 149. The Sunset Collector

He does not collect objects
He collects sunsets
Every evening he sits facing west
And he watches
He watches the sky catch fire
The red the orange the violet
He records every shade in his memory
He has an immense collection
The sunset on the sea
On the mountain
On the city
The sunset on a day of happiness
A day of sadness
He does not photograph them
He lives them
He knows that every sunset is unique
And that it is a gift
A free and magnificent show
Offered by the departing day
To comfort us
For the arriving night.

---

## 150. The Dance of Passwords

My first password was my dog's name
My second the name of my first love
My third a birth date
Now my passwords are monsters "Zk78!bX_p"
Soulless combinations
That protect my digital life
My bank my emails my photos
I am a prisoner of my own secrets
I spend my time inventing passwords
Forgetting them
Resetting them
I have a fortress around my life
But inside there is not much left
Just a man who is afraid
And who has forgotten the password
The most important one
The one that opens the door
Of his own heart.

---

## 151. The Last Tree in the City

It was the last tree
An old chestnut tree on a concrete square
Birds no longer dared to perch there
For fear of being the only ones
Lovers no longer carved their hearts on it
For fear it would bring bad luck
The tree was alone
It watched the cars the buildings the noise
It remembered the time when there was a forest
Animals rivers
It was the last witness of a vanished world
One day men came with saws
They said
It is dangerous it is going to fall
They cut it down
And in its place they put
A bronze statue
Representing a tree
And everyone found it
Very modern
And very safe.

---

## 152. The Souvenir Seller

On the
Pont des
Arts there is a man
Who sells neither padlocks nor portraits
He sells memories by calling out
A kilo of first love for ten francs
A very ripe regret a little bitter
A handful of children's laughter guaranteed fresh
He has second-hand hopes in good condition
Comfortable silences for two people
Blind rages almost brand new
A tear of joy in a small glass vial
The police pass by and say nothing
Memories are not subject to
VAT
A young woman approaches
I would like to forget she says
The seller shakes his head
Sorry madam I don't do buybacks
I only sell what people have lost
Not what they would like to throw away.

---

## 153. The Clocks' Strike

One Tuesday morning at exactly eight o'clock
All the clocks in the town stopped
The one at the station the one at the church the one at the post office
The wristwatches the alarm clocks the Swiss cuckoo clocks
They had had enough of slicing life into pieces
Enough of running after a future that never arrives
Enough of eating seconds like dry insects
The bosses shouted
The teachers screamed
The generals stamped their feet
But time moved no longer
People went out into the streets
They looked at each other for the first time
They talked
They had a coffee that lasted for hours
Children played on the highways
It was the most beautiful day in the world
A day that never ended
For no one ever knew what time it was to end it.

---

## 154. The Wind's Speech

The wind wears no tie
The wind makes no official speeches
But when it speaks it tells the truth
It passes through the fancy streets
And whispers at the windows of the rich
Your safes are full of tears
Your carpets are woven with silence
It passes through the factories
And shouts to the workers
Your lungs are grey with the dust of your lost lives
Your fatigue is the fuel of their luxury cars
It passes over the battlefields
And blows upon the wooden crosses
It speaks the names of the dead whom everyone forgot
The wind is neither of the right nor of the left
It is just
And when it falls silent
It is because the world has grown so heavy
That even the wind no longer has the strength to lift it.

---

## 155. The Lesson of Silence

The new teacher said
Today a lesson in silence
He wrote
SILENCE on the blackboard
And he sat down
The children had never seen anything like it
At first they whispered
Then they grew quiet
And in the silence they began to hear
The sound of their own beating hearts
The sound of a fly bumping against the windowpane
The sound of a cloud drifting across the sky
The sound of an idea born in a neighbor's head
Silence was not empty
It was full of small secret tunes
The teacher smiled
He erased the word
SILENCE
And wrote instead
LISTEN
The bell rang
But for the first time
Nobody wanted to go out.

---

## 156. The Flat-Pack Heart

I bought my heart in a department store
It was a flat-pack kit in a cardboard box
With a very complicated instruction manual
Part
A: tenderness
Part
B: passion (handle with care)
Part
C: jealousy (manipulate with gloves)
Screws of trust and nuts of patience
I assembled it all as I was supposed to
But there was always one part left over
A strange little part without a name
I put it aside
My heart worked well
It loved it suffered it hoped
But one day it stopped
The repairman came
He looked at my heart and said
Ah but the most important part is missing
The strange little part without a name
It is the part of forgiveness
Without it no heart can ever truly restart.

---

## 157. The Poet and the Accountant

The accountant said to the poet
You are not profitable
Your words bring in nothing
Your dreams pay no taxes
The poet looked at the accountant
And he said
And you what do you do
The accountant replied proudly
I count
I count other people's money
I put life into columns
Debit
Credit
The poet smiled
I count too
I count the stars in the sky
I count the tears on a cheek
I count the beats of a heart in love
And at the end of the day
It is I who am the richest
For these numbers of mine
Can never be stolen.

---

## 158. The City That Dreams

At night when men are sleeping
The city wakes up and dreams
Statues climb down from their pedestals
And go for a drink at the corner café
The traffic lights play cards
Red always loses
The subway mouths sing sad operas
Of loves lost in the tunnels
The buildings tell each other stories
Of all the people they have sheltered
The joys the sorrows the births the deaths
The river dreams it is a great silver serpent
Dancing under the moon
And the public benches dream
That one day lovers will come and sit
And stay there forever
Then the first garbage truck passes by
And the city pretends to go back to sleep.

---

## 159. The Atheist's Prayer

Oh
Nothingness who art nowhere
Hallowed be thy absence
Thy silence come unto us
Thy emptiness be done on
Earth as it is in
Heaven
Give us this day our bread of chance
Forgive us our certainties
As we also forgive those who believe in thee
And lead us not into the temptation of eternity
But deliver us from priests
For ours is the doubt the question and the beauty
For the little time we have left
Here and now
Amen
And let the great silence be our answer.

---

## 160. The Last Lover

In a world of faces lit by screens
In a world of conversations made of thumbs up
There was a man and a woman
Who met in a library
They did not exchange profiles
They exchanged books
Then they sat on a bench
And they talked
With their mouths their eyes their hands
They talked about the weather
About the color of the sky and the taste of coffee
They did not take a photo
They made a memory
From the fabric of the present moment
People walked by and looked at them
Like animals of an extinct species
Strange creatures from another time
The man and the woman smiled
They were the last lovers
The only survivors
Of the great digital shipwreck.

---

## 161. The Factory of Sorrows

There is a factory that runs day and night
It makes neither cars nor shoes
It makes sorrow
Sad workers in grey coats
Mix regrets with tears
They add a pinch of solitude
And a healthy dose of bad luck
The sorrow comes out on a conveyor belt
Wrapped in pretty boxes with ribbons
There is love-sorrow for the young
The sorrow of passing time for the old
The little Sunday night sorrow for everyone
The boss is a very rich and very unhappy man
He keeps the most beautiful sorrows for himself
Those that are so pure so perfect
That they look almost like happiness
And he looks at them and weeps
Proud of his production.

---

## 162. The Ghost of the Metro

I am the ghost of the station
Châtelet
I am not scary
I am just transparent
I walk through people
And feel their thoughts
The young girl afraid of her exam
The worker with a sore back
The banker thinking of his money
The lover thinking of their date
I am a sponge for lives
I absorb everything
Fatigue hope boredom joy
I know all the secrets of Paris
But I can tell them to no one
Sometimes I sit next to a musician
And try to sing with him
But no sound comes from my mouth of fog
So I watch the trains come and go
Full of lives I will never touch
And that is my hell
Not fire
Just solitude.

---

## 163. The Revolt of the Statues

One night the statues had had enough
The general on his bronze horse
The poet with his stone pen
The king with his marble sceptre
Sick of standing in the rain and the sun
Sick of being a perch for pigeons
Sick of representing a dead glory
So they stepped down from their pedestals
The general charged the cars
The poet wrote insults on the walls of banks
The king tried to sell his crown for a coffee
It was a great night of disorder and freedom
In the morning the police arrested them all
For vandalism of public property
And put them back on their pedestals
But something had changed
In their stone eyes
There was now a tiny spark of defiance
And they were just waiting
For the next night.

---

## 164. The Thief of Colors

There was a very sad man
Who hated colors
One day he built a large machine
A color vacuum cleaner
He sucked up the red of the poppies
The blue of the sky
The green of the trees
The yellow of the sun
The world became grey
Grey like his heart
People were sad and walked with heads bowed
But a child had kept a small box of colored pencils
He sat on the ground
And began to draw
A big yellow sun a red flower a blue sky
The colors were so bright so strong
That they blew up the thief's machine
And the world recovered its colors
And even the thief
Got a little spot of red on the tip of his grey nose.

---

## 165. The Song of the Ink Stain

I am an inkblot on a white page
I am not a mistake
I am a possibility
I can be a black bird taking flight
A hole in reality to peek through to the other side
The shadow of a word that didn't dare write itself
The eye of a gentle monster
The poet looks at me
He doesn't reach for his eraser
He reaches for his pen
And around me he draws a world
He draws mountains rivers houses
And suddenly I am no longer a blot
I am the lake in the middle of the landscape
I am the black and deep heart of his poem
And I am happy
For even a mistake
Can become the center of the universe.

---

## 166. The Menu of the Day

At the restaurant 'At the Sign
of Life'
The menu is written in chalk on a slate
For starter
Salad of simple little pleasures
Or
A piping hot soup of nostalgia
For main course
Stew of passion long simmered
Accompanied by its potatoes of patience
Or
April fool fish grilled on a bed of doubts
For dessert
Chocolate mousse from childhood
Or
Fruit salad of forgetfulness
The wine is a wine of friendship
That never gives you a headache
And the coffee is black coffee
Black like a well-kept secret
The bill is never the same
You pay with a smile a memory or a tear.

---

## 167. The Asphalt Gardener

There was an old man
Whom everyone called the asphalt gardener
He grew neither roses nor tulips
He cultivated the cracks in the pavement
Every morning he went out with a small watering can
And watered the wild little weeds
The stubborn dandelions
The small nameless flowers growing between the cobblestones
People made fun of him
But he smiled
He said
Life is stronger than concrete
Beauty is more stubborn than ugliness
One day the town hall decided to repave the walk
They covered everything with new smooth asphalt
The old man was sad
But the following spring
A small yellow flower broke through the tar
Right in front of his door
And the old man began to water again.

---

## 168. The Memory of the Staircase

I am an old wooden staircase in a Parisian building
I creak under footsteps
It is my way of speaking
I know the memory of feet
The heavy steps of the worker coming home tired
The light steps of the lover taking them two at a time
The hesitant little steps of the child learning to walk
The slow steps of the old lady afraid of falling
I have felt the weight of moving days
Furniture going up full of hope
Furniture going down full of sadness
I drank the tears that fell on my steps
I heard the laughter echoing in my stairwell
I am the silent witness to a hundred years of life
And if one day they destroy me
Every piece of my wood
Will keep within it the scent of a step
The ghost of a story.

---

## 169. The Trial of the Sun

One day they put on trial the
Sun
The prosecution said
It is too hot it causes heatstroke
It is too bright it hurts the eyes
It reveals the dust on the furniture
And the wrinkles on the faces
It is guilty of too much truth
The defense called a witness
A sunflower
The sunflower said nothing
It just turned its head slowly toward the
Sun
And smiled
The judge looked at the sunflower
Then he looked at the
Sun
And he said
The accused is acquitted
Those who do not like the light
Can just stay in their cellar
The court is adjourned
And everyone went to have a drink on the terrace.

---

## 170. The Letter Never Written

My dearest
No that's not right
My love
That's too cliché
I just wanted to tell you
Tell you what
That the sky without you is a blank page
That my bed is a continent of solitude
That I miss the sound of your steps more than music
That I tried to write to you a hundred times
And a hundred times the words hid under the bed
Like children afraid of the dark
I wanted to tell you that I love you
But the word
LOVE is a garment too big or too small
That never fits me
So I do not write to you
I take the sheet of paper
And look at it
And on this blank page
Is everything I did not know how to tell you
And it is the most beautiful love letter in the world.

---

## 171. The Last Word

The old man was on his deathbed
His family was around him
The priest spoke to him of paradise
The doctor spoke to him of morphine
Everyone was waiting for his last word
The word that would sum up his life
The word of wisdom for posterity
The old man opened his eyes
He looked at his wife his son his daughter his dog
He looked at the priest and the doctor
He took a deep breath
And he said
Damn
Then he died
The family was shocked
The priest was scandalized
The doctor noted the time of death
Only the dog understood
He wagged his tail
For 'Damn' was the word the old man said
When he was truly happy
And couldn't find a word big enough to say it.

---

## 172. The Office of Lost Things

At the lost and found office
There are umbrellas that have forgotten the rain
Orphan gloves looking for a hand
Keys that have lost the memory of their lock
And then there are other things
Those without a name on the label
A burst of laughter abandoned in a draft
A promise made one summer evening and never kept
A piece of courage left on a station platform
The comfortable silence between two old friends
The first glance of a love that never happened
The clerk with his grey mustache
Notes everything down in a large ledger
But he never returns anything
For to find these things
You do not need an address
You need a heart.

---

## 173. The Geography Lesson

The teacher points at the map with his pointer
The
Seine is a blue line that does not move
The
Alps are brown triangles standing very well-behaved
The ocean is a big blue spot without waves
Repeat after me says the teacher
France
Is shaped like a hexagon
But a child at the back of the class
Sees the
Seine overflowing from the map
Flooding the classroom with barges and fish
He sees the
Alps shaking their snow onto the master's desk
And the Atlantic
Ocean coming in through the window
With its waves its salt and its great birds
The teacher shouts a little louder
THE HEXAGON
But the child is already gone
He is sailing on the
Seine in a boat made of blotting paper
And skiing on
Mont
Blanc
Which is the bald head of the teacher.

---

## 174. The General's Sunday

On Sundays the general does not wage war
He reads his newspaper
The news is bad
There are no new battles
He carves the roast chicken
With surgical precision
The left thigh for
Madame
The right thigh for him
The enemy is defeated
Then he goes down to the cellar
Where he plays with his toy soldiers
He organizes grand maneuvers on the ping-pong table
He shouts orders to his lead regiments
Forward charge fire
The cat watches the general
The cat doesn't care
The cat is not a soldier
The cat is an anarchist sleeping in the sun.

---

## 175. The Color of Your Absence

Since you went away
The sky has one color less
The coffee tastes like ashes
Silence has taken up all the space in the house
It sits in your armchair
It reads your books
It wears your dresses
Sometimes I speak to it
I ask it if it has seen you
Silence does not answer
It looks at me with your eyes
Your absence is not an emptiness
It is a solid and heavy thing
A statue of sorrow that I must carry every day
And which has the exact shape of your body.

---

## 176. The Flea Market

On the stalls of the flea market
The lives of others are for sale for next to nothing
A porcelain doll with a broken eye
That has seen too many family secrets
A toy soldier who lost his war
A chipped coffee cup
That remembers a thousand mornings
A violin without strings
Keeping inside all the music it never played
The merchant shouts
Everything must go
But nothing really disappears
Memories are stubborn
They cling to rust and dust
And wait for a new hand
To tell the same story.

---

## 177. The Living Statue

By dint of taking the same bus at the same time
Sitting at the same desk in front of the same wall
Eating the same sandwich at the same break
The man became a statue
One morning he did not get up
He remained sitting on the square's bench
Motionless and grey
Pigeons poop on his head
Lovers carve their names on his back
Children play hide-and-seek behind him
He feels nothing
He has become a monument to boredom
Sometimes a stone tear flows down his cheek
When he remembers a time
When he was made of flesh and blood
And when he still knew how to run.

---

## 178. The Worker's Prayer

Our Boss who art in the office
Forgotten be thy name
Thy kingdom end
Thy will no longer be done in the factory or anywhere else
Give us this day our daily bread
And a little wine to go with it
Forgive us our urges to rebel
As we forgive your indecent profits
And lead us not into the temptation of overtime
But deliver us from the machine
For ours is the sweat the fatigue and the life
For the few years we have left
Amen
And shit.

---

## 179. The Words on Strike

One day the words had had enough
The word
LOVE no longer wanted to be in the mouths of tyrants
The word
FREEDOM no longer wanted to be on prison walls
The word
TRUTH was tired of lying
So they went on strike
They marched into the streets
The politician opened his mouth for a speech
And nothing came out
The general wanted to shout an order
And made the sound of a duck
The advertiser wanted to sell soap
And only stammered
It was a great silence
A magnificent and terrible silence
And in this silence
We finally heard the sound of the wind in the trees
And the beating heart of a sleeping child.

---

## 180. The Gardener Who Watered Names

The cemetery gardener
Does not plant flowers
He waters the names on the gravestones
Good day
Monsieur
Dupont your regrets are a bit dry today
Watch out
Madame
Durand your solitude is growing crooked
He speaks to the dead as to friends
He tells them the news of the village
The loves of the living
The foolishness of children
The dead do not answer
But they listen
Their roots of silence drink his words
And sometimes at night
A marble rose blooms on an old grave
Just to say thank you to him.

---

## 181. The Recipe for Happiness

Take a large blue sky
Add a ray of morning sun
Mix with a child's laughter
Fold in a look of love
Let it rest in the hollow of a knowing silence
Sprinkle with a few useless and tender words
Add neither reason nor logic
It would ruin the taste
Serve immediately
This dish cannot be preserved
It must be lived
And do not ask the neighbor for the recipe
Everyone must invent their own
With the ingredients they have at hand
And in their heart.

---

## 182. The Mirror's Confession

I am the mirror of a cheap hotel room
I have seen faces tired from the road
Faces illuminated by a one-night love
Faces hollowed out by solitude
I have seen silent tears
Smiles that lied
Suitcases full of hopes
And suitcases full of regrets
I do not judge
I reflect
I am the cold and faithful memory of passing lives
I keep within me all these reflections
And sometimes at night when all is dark
I project them on the wall
And watch my own movie
Which is the movie of humanity.

---

## 183. The Last Tree

In the city of concrete and steel
One tree remained
An old plane tree in a small square
Developers wanted to chop it down
To build a parking lot
But the people of the neighborhood said no
Children formed a ring around it
Lovers carved their hearts on its bark
The old folks came to sit in its shade
The tree was no longer a tree
It was the heart of the neighborhood
So the developers built their parking lot elsewhere
And the tree remained
It gives neither fruit nor flowers
It gives only shade and hope
And that is much more important.

---

## 184. The Cloud Factory

In a large factory near the sky
Workers in grey coats manufacture the clouds
There is the cumulus department
For lazy afternoons
The stratus department
For melancholy mornings
And the nimbus department
For when the world needs washing
The foreman shouts
Faster more clouds
The boss wants rain on the competitor's shares
But a young dreamy worker
Secretly makes clouds in the shape of a horse
Of a face or a boat
He releases them into the sky
And watches the children below
Who point at them laughing
It is his small act of poetic sabotage.

---

## 185. The Liar

There was a man who lied all the time
He said he had seen fish fly
Cats speak Latin
Honest politicians
Nobody believed him
But he lied so well
With so much detail and passion
That his lies began to come to life
One day a goldfish flew through his window
His cat asked him the time in Latin
And the town mayor confessed all his sins on TV
The man was terrified
His world of lies had become reality
He tried to tell the truth
He said
The sky is blue
And the sky turned green with pink polka dots
He was a prisoner of his own talent.

---

## 186. The Laundry of Worries

Every Monday morning
She does the big wash of the week's worries
Into the washing machine
She puts her husband's shirt of anger
The tablecloth stained by mean words
The dirty socks of fatigue
She adds a dose of forgiveness
And a bit of softener of forgetfulness
The machine spins
It washes whiter than white
It erases the stains of the soul
And when she hangs the laundry on the line
The wind carries away the last remains of sadness
And the week can start again
Clean and fresh
Until next Monday.

---

## 187. The Collector of Sounds

He collected neither stamps nor butterflies
He collected sounds
In well-labeled glass jars
He had the sound of a first kiss
A shy and wet little sound
The sound of the silence of a snow-covered forest
The sound of a door slamming in an argument
The sound of a popping champagne cork
The sound of a pencil on paper
In the evening he opened his jars
And listened
He re-listened to the symphonies of life
The false notes and the harmonies
It was his own way
Of forgetting nothing.

---

## 188. The Passport

A passport is a sad little book
Full of stamps saying where you have been
But saying nothing of what you saw
There is the stamp of the border where you were afraid
The stamp of the country where you were in love
The stamp of the airport where you waited for nothing
Your ID photo looks at you
With the look of a criminal
You do not recognize yourself
It is not you
It is just a number a birthdate a nationality
A passport is not a travel journal
It is a criminal record of your travels
Proving that you belong to a country
Before you belong to yourself.

---

## 189. The Revolt of the Vegetables

One morning on the market stall
The carrot said
I am sick of being grated
The potato said
Enough of being mashed
The pea said
I refuse to be squashed by a fork
So they organized a revolt
The tomatoes bombarded the merchant
The onions made her cry
The leeks fought like sabres
It was a great vegetable revolution
Customers were scared and ran away
And the vegetables won
They set up a free and independent republic
On the marketplace
Until the goats arrived.

---

## 190. The Harbor Siren

It is not a woman with a fish tail
The harbor siren
It is a great hoarse and sad voice
That sings when the fog rolls in
It sings for the boats that get lost
It sings for the sailors who are homesick
It sings for the women waiting on the dock
Its song is made of salt rust and departures
It is the lament of all loves separated by the sea
It is the blues of the open sea
And when it falls silent
The silence is even heavier
For we know that the fog has swallowed everything
And that we must wait for morning
To count the boats that have returned.

---

## 191. The Blind Photographer

He was a photographer but he was blind
People laughed at him
How can he take photos
If he sees nothing
But he did not photograph with his eyes
He photographed with his hands his nose his ears
He took photos of the warmth of a hand in his
The smell of warm bread coming out of the oven
The sound of a child's laughter
The silence of a flowing tear
His photos were all black
But when you looked at them with the heart
You saw everything
You saw life
As it really is
Invisible to the eyes.

---

## 192. The Postman of Words

The mailman does not deliver letters
He delivers words
In his bag he has heavy words
Like stones of sorrow
Words light as feathers of laughter
Words that sting like nettles
Words that caress like velvet
There are love words he slips under doors
Angry words he throws at the windows of the powerful
Forgiveness words he leaves on public benches
The mailman never rings
He knows that every word
Will always find its recipient
Even if it is twenty years later
In the hollow of a memory.

---

## 193. The Meeting of the Shadows

At midnight when the city sleeps
Shadows detach themselves from the walls
And meet in the Place de la République
The banker's shadow is long and thin
The worker's shadow is bent by fatigue
The lover's shadow seeks the shadow of their love
They do not speak
They dance
A slow and silent waltz
They exchange their shapes their secrets their regrets
The dog's shadow pees on the general's shadow
Nobody is offended
Here there is no hierarchy
Just the grand democracy of the night
Then the first ray of sun arrives
And each shadow returns obediently
To cling to the feet of its owner
Waiting for the next night.

---

## 194. The Sky on Sale

I saw a strange shop
With a sign that said 'The
Sky on
Sale -
Total
Clearance'
Inside an old man was selling pieces of sky
A yard of blue sky for gloomy days
A small white cloud to make it pretty
A sunset slightly damaged but cheap
A storm in a jar for thrill-seekers
A handful of stars for dreamers
I asked
Do you not have the moon
The old man smiled
The moon is not for sale sir
It belongs to everyone
Like love
And like death.

---

## 195. The Colors' Strike

Red
Said
I am tired of being the color of blood and war
I want to be the color of cherries and kisses
Black
Said
Enough of being the color of mourning and misery
I want to be the color of the night's velvet and cats' eyes
White
Said
I no longer want to be the color of surrender
But that of the page where everything remains to be written
So all the colors went on strike
The world became grey
A great grey boredom
Generals could no longer decorate their chests
Advertisers could no longer sell their lies
People stopped watching television
And began to look at themselves
It was so terrifying
That they begged the colors to return
Promising to use them better.

---

## 196. The Mechanic of Time

He has his workshop at the corner of Forgetfulness Street
He does not repair watches
He repairs time
People bring him their broken memories
A lost hour of happiness
A missing minute of courage
A second of inattention that changed everything
He pulls out his small tools
A pair of regret pliers
A nostalgia screwdriver
An oil can of hope oil
He works patiently
He resolders the broken moments
He resets the hands to the heart's time
He cannot change the past
But sometimes he manages to make it
A little less heavy to carry.

---

## 197. The Soup Kitchen

They queue in the cold
Their faces are winter landscapes
Their hands are knotted roots
They do not speak
Their stories are frozen in their eyes
The woman who serves the soup
Has the tired smile of an angel working overtime
She does not only give soup
She gives a spoonful of warmth
A bowl of dignity
The soup is hot
It has the simple taste of survival
It warms the belly
But it does not warm the soul
For in the soup there is no justice
There is only pity
And pity
Is the tip that the rich leave to misery.

---

## 198. The Confessional of a Park Bench

I am an old green wooden bench
I am the confessional of the churchless
I have heard more secrets than all the priests of Paris
The promises of lovers
That last only for the length of a season
The whispers of the lonely
Who talk to the pigeons
The plans of rebels
Who want to remake the world
The sobs of the hopeless
Who have nowhere left to go
I do not give absolution
I do not judge
I keep it all
My wood is soaked in all these lives
And if one day they burn me
My smoke will tell the sky
All the prayers of the asphalt.

---

## 199. The Architect of Ruins

He does not build houses
He does not build bridges
He is an architect of ruins
He chooses a beautiful place
A clearing a cliff
And he builds a wall that is already collapsing
A tower that leans
A staircase leading nowhere
He works with tired stones
Bricks that have already lived through a lot
Then he goes away
And he lets time do its work
The wind the rain the ivy
His ruins are poems of stone
Teaching men
The beauty of what ends
The wisdom of what falls
And the patience of what begins again.

---

## 200. The Flying Lesson

The teacher said to the children
Today we are going to learn to fly
Forget gravity
Forget that two and two make four
Forget your name your address
Close your eyes
Think of something light
A feather a laugh a secret
Now jump
The children jumped
Some of them fell
Those who had learned their lessons too well
But others floated
They floated in the classroom like happy balloons
They flew out the window
And they went to play with the birds
The teacher remained alone
He had never managed to forget
That his own heart was heavy.

---

## 201. The Ferryman of Dreams

He has a boat made of silence
His oars are moonbeams
Every night he waits on the shore of sleep
People arrive tired from their day
They get into his boat
And he takes them to the other side
To the islands of dreams
There is the island of rediscovered loves
The island where you can fly
The island where the dead return to speak to us
The ferryman asks for no money
His only pay
Is seeing the peaceful faces of the sleepers
When he brings them back in the early morning
To the grey shore of reality
Leaving them a little dream sand
At the bottom of their pockets.

---

## 202. Canned Sadness

In a grey factory by a dead river
They put sadness in cans
There is ordinary sadness for everyday consumption
Luxury sadness with a taste of nostalgia
The little Sunday evening sadness on special offer
The workers are ghosts
They do not speak
They sort tears
They label sighs
The boss is a man who has never cried
He says that sadness is a market of the future
He exports worldwide
And people buy
They open the cans at night in front of the television
And consume their own misfortune
Finding that it has a familiar taste
And almost comforting.

---

## 203. The Rain's Speech

I am the rain
I make no speeches
I fall
I fall on the rich and on the poor
On the statues of heroes and on the graves of the unknown
I wash the cars of ministers
And the faces of the homeless
I have no party
I have no god
I am the great democrat
I reveal the truth
I show the cracks in the walls
The lies that run with makeup
The sad colors of the world when there is no sun left to lie
People say I am sad
But it is not I who am sad
It is the world
I only give it
A liquid and honest mirror.

---

## 204. The Collector of Echoes

He lives in a silent valley
And he collects echoes
He has the echo of a child's laughter lost in the mountains
The echo of an "I love you" whispered a hundred years ago
The echo of a soldier's last cry
The echo of a forgotten song
He keeps them in deep caves
Each echo has its own color its own temperature
In the evening he sits at the entrance of a cave
And he listens
He listens to the sound memory of the world
He never feels alone
For he knows that nothing is truly lost
That every word every sound
Continues to vibrate somewhere
And that you only need to know how to listen closely
To hear eternity.

---

## 205. The Revolt of the Road Signs

The
STOP sign said
I'm sick of stopping everyone
Today I say
GO AHEAD
The
NO
ENTRY sign said
I'm tired of forbidding
Today everything is allowed
The
GIVE
WAY
sign said
Today I give up nothing
Take what you are owed
It was a monstrous mess
Cars bumped into each other laughing
Pedestrians crossed dancing
The police tried to restore order
But the station sign
Had replaced its cap with a straw hat
And it played the accordion
It was the first day of road anarchy
And strangely
There was no accident
Just lots of people happy to be finally lost.

---

## 206. The Merchant of Maybe

He has his little stall in an alley
He sells nothing solid
He sells maybes
A maybe yes
A maybe no
A maybe if you dare
A maybe if you wait
People come to see him when they are at the end of their rope
When life has left them nothing but grey certainty
They buy a little maybe
Not expensive
Just enough to hold on one more day
Just enough to open a small window
In the wall of their despair
The merchant never grows rich
But he has the finest of trades
He is a sower of fertile doubts
In the arid field of reality.

---

## 207. The Cemetery of Forgotten Words

There is a secret place that only poets know
It is the cemetery of forgotten words
There rest magnificent words
That no one uses anymore "Désuet" "Coquecigrue" "Nitescence"
Each word has its little grave of dust
With its etymology carved upon it
Sometimes a word rises
And comes to haunt a poem
Bringing with it a scent of yesteryear
A strange music
The other words look at it with suspicion
But the poet welcomes it
For he knows that words never truly die
They sleep
And that a little love is enough
To wake them up
And restore their place in the world.

---

## 208. The Dance of the Littered Papers

When the wind rises in the city
The scrap papers begin to dance
An old candy wrapper
A worn subway ticket
A newspaper page talking about a forgotten war
They swirl on the asphalt
A sad and light-hearted waltz
They are the ghosts of our consumption
The souls of our indifference
They tell the story of our rushed lives
A child watches them from the window
He does not see garbage
He sees paper butterflies
Birds of misery
And he finds it beautiful
Because his heart has not yet learned
To tell the difference between what has value
And what has none.

---

## 209. The Tailor of Silences

He has his shop in the street of
Calm
He does not sew suits
He sews custom-made silences
He takes your measurements with a wind tape measure
He makes you choose the fabric
Silence of snow
Silence of libraries
Silence after love
He cuts with scissors of the moon
He sews with a spider's thread
He delivers a perfect silence to you
A silence that fits you like a glove
A silence where you can finally hear
The sound your soul makes
When it breathes
And when it is happy
To be finally alone with itself.

---

## 210. The Station of Farewells

It is a station where trains never arrive
They only depart
The tracks are made of regrets
The carriages are full of ghosts
On the platform people do not kiss
They shake hands a little too tightly
Their smiles are masks of rain
The stationmaster is an old man
Who lost his own love here fifty years ago
He whistles the departure with a whistle of tears
The train goes away
Carrying with it halves of lives
And on the platform only silence remains
And the cold draft
Of someone who has just left
Forever.

---

## 211. The Clock That Ran Backwards

In an antique shop window
There was an old clock
Whose hands turned backward
People passed by and laughed
What a stupid clock
But an old man went inside
He bought the clock
He put it in his living room
And his life began to go back in time
His wife returned from the dead
His wrinkles disappeared
His hair turned black again
He relived his first kiss
His first heartbreak
His first day of school
He became a child again
Then a baby
Then nothing at all
The clock stopped
It had finished its work
It had erased a life
With all its joy and all its sorrow
And no one ever knew if it was a gift
Or a curse.

---

## 212. The Painter of the Invisible

He had his studio on the rooftops of Paris
He did not paint landscapes
He painted the invisible
On his canvases one could see
The smell of coffee in the morning
The sound of a heel on the cobblestones
The warmth of a sunbeam on the skin
The sadness of a distant song
The silence between two words of love
The art critics said
It looks like nothing
It is abstract
But simple people understood
A woman wept before the painting of solitude
A child laughed before the painting of a burst of laughter
For the painter did not paint what he saw
He painted what everyone feels
Without ever finding the words
Or the colors to say it.

---

## 213. The Library of Unlived Lives

It is a secret library in the heart of the city
The books have no title
They just have a name on the cover
Yours mine that of the neighbor
Each book tells the life you could have had
If you had said yes instead of no
If you had taken that train
If you had dared to kiss her
There are lives of an adventurer of a poet of a king
Lives of simple happiness in the countryside
People come here in secret
They read their other life with tears in their eyes
They see the happiness they missed
The person they could have become
Then they close the book
And return to their real life
A little sadder
And a little wiser
Wondering if somewhere
Someone is not reading
The life they are currently living.

---

## 214. The Meeting of the Street Names

The street of
Peace said
I am sick of military parades
The street of
Martyrs said
I would really like a little joy for a change
The street of
Commerce said
People only pass by they do not look at me anymore
The dead-end of
Desire said
No one ever comes to the very end
The square of
Holidays said
I am tired of speeches and fireworks
I would just like a little silence
So they decided to change their names
The street of
Peace was called the street of the
Kiss
The street of
Martyrs was called the street of
Laughter
And the dead-end of
Desire became the avenue of Mad
Love
People were lost
Their
GPS went crazy
They had to raise their heads
And ask for directions
And that is how
They began talking to each other again.

---

## 215. The Tamer of Nightmares

He has a little circus that only sets up at night
He does not tame lions
He tames nightmares
People bring him their worst fears
The fear of falling
The fear of being alone
The fear of death
He enters the cage of sleep
With his whip of light and his chair of courage
He faces the monsters of the unconscious
He speaks to them softly
He teaches them to be scary no more
He transforms the monster chasing you
Into a little dog that licks your hand
He transforms the void sucking you in
Into a large soft bed
At the end of the show
People leave with their tamed nightmares
Sleeping quietly at their feet
Like old household pets.

---

## 216. The Cat's Prayer

My master who is on earth
May your name be easy to ignore
May your reign be comfortable
May your will be done especially when it involves caresses
Give me today my daily nap
Forgive me my claws on the sofa
As I forgive you your useless noises
And lead me not into the temptation of the bath
But deliver me from the neighbor's dog
For to me belong the sun warmth and laziness
For the nine lives of the nine lives
Amen
And now let me sleep
Please.

---

## 217. The Ministry of Grayness

It is a large building without windows
Where faceless bureaucrats work
Their mission is to make the world grey
They forbid laughter that is too loud
They tax bright colors
They subsidize boredom
They organize yawning contests
The Minister of
Greyness
Is a man who has never seen the sea
He signs decrees with an invisible ink pen
His slogan is "A happy people is a dangerous people"
But one day an employee brought back a little red flower
He hid it in his drawer
And its scent began to spread
The scent of disobedience
And of joy
And the whole ministry began to smell of
Spring.

---

## 218. The Taste of Words

The word "freedom" tastes like wind on the sea
The word "love" tastes like warm bread and cherries
The word "sadness" tastes like rain on a windowpane
The word "hate" tastes like cold metal
The word "hope" has the fragile taste of a bud in winter
Politicians serve us tasteless words
Plastic words
Words that taste of nothing
They make us eat wooden language
The poet himself is a cook
He chooses his words with care
He mixes them lets them simmer
And he serves us a simple and true dish
A dish that tastes of life
With all its salt and all its sugar
And sometimes
All its bitterness.

---

## 219. The Thief of Horizons

He would arrive at night with large fog scissors
And he cut out the horizon
He folded it carefully
And carried it away in his sack
In the morning people woke up
And the sky touched the earth
There was no far away anymore
No elsewhere
The world was a little box with no way out
Birds flew in circles
Boats spun around in place
Dreams died of claustrophobia
People stopped looking out the window
They looked at their feet
And they found that life was very small
Until the day a child took a blue pencil
And drew a line
A simple line between the earth and the sky
And the horizon returned
And the thief died of hunger
For he fed only on lost hopes.

---

## 220. The Song of the Drying Laundry

On the line in the courtyard
The drying laundry tells stories
The worker's shirt tells of sweat and fatigue
The lover's dress tells of stolen kisses
The bedsheet tells of dreams and nightmares
The child's little sock tells of games and wild runs
The old lady's handkerchief tells of tears and memories
The wind passes by and reads all these stories
It mixes them up
And makes a great song of them
The song of ordinary life
A simple and complicated song
A song that smells good of laundry
And of passing time
Drying in the sun
Like all of us.

---

## 221. One Last Poem

I will write one last poem
A poem so light
That it will fly off the paper
A poem so transparent
That you will not see the words
Just what they hide
A poem that will say nothing
But will make you feel everything
It will have the smell of rain after the storm
The sound of silence
The color of a happy memory
You will read it
And five minutes later
You will have forgotten it
But it will remain in you
Like a little warmth in the pit of your stomach
Like a little light behind your eyes
And it will be my most beautiful poem
The one that does not need memory
To go on living.

---

## 222. The Maker of Tears

In his workshop smelling of salt and rain
He makes tears to order
He has crocodile tears for politicians
Tears of joy for weddings
Tears of rage for rebels
Little tears of nothing at all for sad movies
He distills them with care
From regrets memories and onions
He puts them in small vials
Each tear is unique
It contains a story
A sorrow or a joy
He is the only artisan of his guild
For everyone knows how to make their own tears
But no one wants to admit
That they need them
So people come to see him in secret
Like going to a poison merchant
Or a seller of remedies.

---

## 223. The Wall That Spoke

It was an old wall in an alley
Full of graffiti cracks and moss
One day it began to speak
In a voice hoarse and full of dust
It told the story of the street
Stolen kisses in its shadow
Conspiracies whispered against it
Children who played ball and grew old
It told of wars revolutions holidays
It knew everything
It had seen everything
People came from far away to listen to it
The mayor wanted to class it as a historic monument
But the wall said
Leave me alone
I am not a monument
I am just a memory
And one day I will collapse
With all my secrets
And it will be just fine that way.

---

## 224. The Commas' Strike

The commas had had enough
Tired of separating tired of making pauses
Tired of being the little maids-of-all-work of the sentence
So they left
All the commas of the world disappeared
It was a terrible chaos
Sentences had no breath left
Ideas collided
Lists became single and monstrous words
Lovers no longer knew where to stop to breathe in their letters
Politicians could no longer catch their breath to lie better
The world had become one single long incomprehensible sentence
People begged the commas to return
They promised to respect them
To give them importance
The commas returned
And the world could breathe again
And take a pause
To think.

---

## 225. The Merchant of Spare Skies

He pushes a little cart full of skies
There are skies for every taste
A storm sky for days of anger
A pink and tender sky for lovers
A sky full of stars for sleepless nights
A grey and low sky for when you want to stay at home
A sky of perfect blue that makes you want to live
People buy a sky from him
And paste it above their heads
It doesn't change the weather outside
But it changes the weather in the heart
The merchant does not sell weather reports
He sells states of mind
He is the official supplier
Of the color of our days.

---

## 226. The Loneliness of the Number One

The digit
One is always alone
At the beginning of everything
In front of the others
He is the first cry the first step
He is the beginning of solitude
The
Two has someone to talk to
The
Three can have a party
But the
One is alone
He watches the other digits having fun together
Forming complicated numbers
Additions multiplications
As for him he remains
One
Indivisible and straight
Like a soldier standing at attention
Like a telegraph pole in an empty field
Sometimes he dreams of being a
Zero
To be able to disappear
Or stand next to another digit
To finally count for ten.

---

## 227. The Dust Ball

In a sunbeam crossing a dark room
The dust motes dance
Each grain of dust is a world
A piece of dead skin
A grain of pollen
A fiber of clothing
A cigarette ash
A dried tear
They dance a silent and eternal waltz
They tell the story of the house
The story of the people who lived there
They are the invisible memory of our lives
We vacuum
We make them disappear
But they always return
For you cannot get rid of your past
You can only watch it dance
In the light.

---

## 228. The Walker Through Walls of Feeling

People build walls around their hearts
Walls of fear
Walls of pride
Walls of silence
As for him he has a strange gift
He can pass through these walls
He enters people's hearts without making a sound
He sees the sadness hidden behind a smile
The love that does not dare to speak
The childhood wound that never healed
He steals nothing
He changes nothing
He only watches
And when he leaves
He leaves the wall's door ajar
Just a little
So that the air can enter
And maybe one day
Someone else.

---

## 229. The Revolt of Reflections

One morning your reflection in the mirror
Got tired of imitating you
When you raised your right hand
It raised its left hand
When you smiled
It made a face
When you wanted to shave
It put on lipstick
You were afraid
You broke the mirror
But all the pieces on the ground
Continued to show you another you
A you who no longer obeyed
A you who was free
You spent the day locked in the dark
For fear of seeing yourself in a shop window
Or in a puddle of water
And you understood
That your worst enemy
Your most ferocious jailer
Was yourself.

---

## 230. The Fisher of Stars

He does not fish for fish
He fishes for stars
At night he gets into his boat
And he oars on the great black lake of the sky
He casts his net of silence
And he waits
Sometimes he catches a little shooting star
Which struggles and which he releases at once
Sometimes he catches an old tired moon
Which he helps to fall back asleep
But what he seeks
Is the shepherd's star
The star of lovers and poets
One day he will catch it
And he will put it in a lantern
To light the way
For all those who are lost
In the night of their own lives.

---

## 231. The Clinic for Sick Words

It is a very special clinic
There they treat sick words
Words worn out by politicians
Words tortured by advertisers
Words emptied of meaning by habit
The word "always" is in depression
The word "love" suffers from inflation
The word "freedom" is in intensive care
The nurses are poets
They make dressings of silence
Infusions of new meaning
Massages of unexpected rhymes
They give back to the words their color
Their weight their music
And when a word is cured
They release it into the world
Hoping it will not fall sick again
Too quickly.

---

## 232. Breath on the Windowpane

A child is bored on the bus
It is cold outside
He presses his mouth to the window
And makes fog
A little ephemeral cloud
On this cloud with his finger
He draws a sun
A heart
The name of his secret love
Then the fog disappears
The drawing fades away
The sun the heart the name
Everything is gone
The child is not sad
He knows that the most beautiful things
Are those that do not last
Those that exist just for an instant
In the warm breath of life
Against the cold windowpane of the world.

---

## 233. The Catalog of Useless Things

A mismatched button at the bottom of a sewing box
A rusty key that no longer opens any door
A movie ticket for a film that everyone has forgotten
A dried flower between the pages of a book
A pebble picked up on a distant beach
A song hummed without knowing the lyrics
The list is long
The list of useless things
That we keep without knowing why
Perhaps because they are the true treasures
The silent guardians of our memories
The little white pebbles of our lost path
Perhaps the useless
Is just another way of saying
Essential.

---

## 234. The Sadness of the Shop-Window Mannequin

I am made of plastic
I am perfect
My smile is frozen for eternity
I wear the most beautiful dresses
I never grow old
I am the dream of all women
But at night when the store is closed
I am sad
I see people passing in the street
I see them laughing crying kissing arguing
I see them living
As for me I do not live
I pose
I am a beautiful empty thing
A promise that will never be kept
My only desire
Would be to have a single wrinkle
A single tear
A single imperfection
Which would prove that I existed
Truly.

---

## 235. The Speech of the Commander's Statue

Yes it is me the
Commander
The one whom
Don
Juan invited to dinner
Everyone says that I am the symbol of divine justice
The punishment of the sinner
What a joke
The truth is that I was sick of it
Sick of being on my pedestal
Sick of being a cold and dignified statue
I saw
Don
Juan living
Loving seducing laughing
I was jealous
So yes I came to his dinner
Not to punish him
But to feel the warmth of a fire
To drink a glass of wine
To listen to music
I dragged him to hell
Because it was the only place
Where a stone statue could finally
Feel something
Even the burning.

---

## 236. The Ghosts' Afternoon Tea

At four o'clock in the afternoon
In the attic of abandoned houses
The ghosts have their afternoon snack
They do not eat cakes
They eat memories
They bite into a burst of a child's laughter
They spread a slice of nostalgia
They drink cups of silence
Their conversations are made of whispers
Of regrets and old songs
They talk of the good old days
When they still had a body
When they could still cry for real
Then the sun sets
And the ghosts return to their solitude
Leaving on the table
A few crumbs of what has been
And what will never be again.

---

## 237. The Anger of a Drop of Water

I was born in a clean cloud
I was pure and full of light
I dreamed of falling on a flower
Or in the hand of a child
But I fell on the city
I rolled on a roof full of soot
In a gutter full of spit
I joined a river that smelled of poison
I saw dead fish belly up
I carried factory waste
I am no longer a drop of water
I am a drop of anger
A drop of shame
And when I evaporate
I will go up to the sky
And I will form a black cloud
A cloud of vengeance
And I will rain down on men
An acid rain
A rain that will remind them
Of what they have done to me
And to the world.

---

## 238. The Merchant of Sleep

He spends the night in silent streets
With his suitcase full of sleep
He has light sleep for those who fear the dark
Deep sleep for those who want to forget everything
Restless sleep for lovers
Childlike sleep for old men
He sells his sleep by the spoonful
To insomniacs staring at the ceiling
To the anxious listening to their hearts beating too fast
He takes no money
He takes in exchange a little piece of their day
A worry a joy a secret
With that he fills his suitcase
For sleep feeds on life
And the merchant knows
That he is the most essential of shopkeepers
The only one selling what money
Will never be able to buy.

---

## 239. The Dance of the Lost Keys

At night in the large warehouse of lost and found
The lost keys organize a ball
There is the key to the childhood home
The key to the first car
The key to the heart of a forgotten love
The key to a cage from which the bird has flown
They clink softly against each other
A music of metal and nostalgia
They dance a rusty jig
They tell each other of the doors they opened
The secrets they guarded
They are not sad
They know that a lost key
Is not the end of a story
It is just the beginning of another
That of the door
Which will remain forever
Open or closed.

---

## 240. The Café's Last Customer

The waiter puts the chairs on the tables
It makes a sound of climbing skeletons
The coffee machine sighs one last time
Outside night has eaten everything
There remains a man alone at a table
He looks at the bottom of his glass
As if he were looking for an answer there
He thinks of nothing
He is just there
In the silence that settles in
In the smell of cold coffee and cigarettes
He is the last survivor of the day
The guardian of a moment that no longer exists
The waiter waits
He says nothing
He knows you do not disturb
Someone who is becoming
A memory.

---

## 241. A Poet's Testament

When I am dead
Do not put my name on a stone
Let the wind carry it away
I leave you neither gold nor silver
I leave you the red of poppies
The blue of the sky on very windy days
I leave you the sound of rain on rooftops
The smile of a stranger in the street
I leave you the words I tried to bend
To make them say a little truth
Take them use them discard them
Do what you want with them
My only heritage
Is to have tried to show you
How to look at the world
With eyes that have just been born
Every morning.

---

## 242. Blue, the Color of Dreams

I am not a color that screams
I am a great murmur, a sigh
The color of that which is far away
The horizon, the sea, and the vein on a hand
I am the color of escape
The immense sky that belongs to no one
And where the birds draw poems of freedom
I am the deep ocean full of voyages and monsters
The liquid road to unknown islands
I am the uniform of the sailor who is leaving
And the color of the eyes in which one drowns in love
I am the color of dreams and of hope
The blue smoke of a wood fire rising to the stars
The ink of the letter one is waiting for
And the little flame of the gas in the silent night
But I am also the color of sorrow
The "blues" of the black musician in a Chicago bar
The color of lips when one is cold
The terrible blue of a bruise on the skin and on the soul
I am the color of the work uniform
The "bleu de travail" that smells of sweat and fatigue
The faded blue of a life spent in a factory
Looking at the sky through a dirty window
I am the greatest of paradoxes
The color of the cage and of freedom
I am the blue of the worker's overalls
And the blue of the sky he looks at while dreaming
I am the distance between what one is
And what one would like to be
I am the color of the human soul
A great sea of desires and of melancholy.

---

## 243. Orange, the Color of Twilight

I am not the scream of red
Nor the great laughter of yellow
I am their friendly conversation
A kind color that doesn't want to make a scene
I am the warmth of a wood fire in the fireplace
I am the color of simple and good things
The carrot that one pulls from the earth
The apricot full of sun
And the orange, that little sun to be peeled
Full of a sweet juice that sticks to the fingers
I am the round belly of the pumpkin
Waiting quietly for autumn in its field
And the color of the fox disappearing into the woods
I am the color of the end of the day
The great magnificent farewell of the sun
Its golden wound on the belly of the sky
I am the light of the little lamp on the table
When the night is already behind the window
I am a promise of warmth and of refuge
But I am also the color of warning
The flashing light of the road workers
The plastic cone that says "Caution, danger"
The jacket of the garbage collector that makes him visible in the night
I am the color that forewarns
The color of prudence and of change
For I am above all the color of autumn
The sad beauty of the leaf that is about to fall
The last warmth before the great cold
I am not an eternal color
I am the color of a moment
A perfect and fragile moment
Between fullness and the end
I am the color of a friendly "goodbye for now"
Never that of a "farewell".

---

## 244. Green, the Color of Hope

I am not a hurried color
I am patience, I am perseverance
The little blade of grass that grows through the concrete
The ivy that slowly climbs the wall of time
I am the great quiet breath of the forests
I am the color of hope
The first bud on the dead tree of winter
The four-leaf clover found by a child
The green light at the crossroads that says "Go, live your life"
I am the fresh mint in a glass of water
The little motionless lizard on the warm stone
And the deep green of a mountain lake
I am the repose of the eye and the calm of the heart
The promise that even after the harshest of winters
Life always returns
But men have invented another life for me
Another color
They took my name to put it on their money
That paper that buys everything except happiness
They put me on the uniforms of their soldiers
And on the gambling table where fortunes are lost
I have become the color of jealousy
The murky green of envy in the eyes
The color of absinthe that drives one mad
And of the mold on forgotten bread
They have created a green that is the enemy of life
A green of poison, a green of dollars, a green of profit
A green that builds walls
While my own true green
Only dreams of covering them
So look closely
There are two greens in the world
The one that liberates and the one that imprisons
And every day you must choose
Between the grass in the park where you can lie down to do nothing
And the banknote you must earn to survive.

---

## 245. Red, the Color of the Heart

After the silence of white and black
I arrive making noise
I am the first color, the great scream
The color that even the blind can feel
The warmth of blood on the skin
I am the color of life and of desire
The cherry that one steals from the tree
The lips of a woman waiting for a kiss
The fragile and stubborn poppy in the wheat field
The heart drawn in chalk on a gray wall
I am the wine of lovers and the blush of shyness
The blood that runs in the veins
The proof that one is alive
But I am also the color of anger and of war
The fire that devours the house
The blood that flows on the pavement
The face of an angry man
The teacher's pen that crosses out mistakes
The stop sign that kills movement
And the flag of the revolution
That promises a better world
With the blood of others
I am a paradox, a wound and a flower
The most joyful and the most terrible of colors
I am the velvet curtain of the theater
And the stain on the butcher's apron
I am the candy apple of the funfair
And the light of the brothel in the night
You cannot ignore me
I am the color of all passions
From love to hate there is only one step
And that step is always red
A world without me would be a reasonable world
A gray and polite world
A deathly boring world.

---

## 246. Yellow, the Color of Laughter

I am the color of laughter and of the sun
The not-so-rich cousin of gold
I am simple joy, a color without manners
That bursts forth in the middle of the serious green of the meadow
A million dandelions applauding the spring
I am the sharp and sour taste of the lemon
The heat of mustard that rushes to the nose
The liquid heart of a fried egg
I am the naive sunflower that follows its god the sun
And the raincoat of a child jumping in puddles
I am a party, a surprise, an exclamation
But you must not trust my smile
I am also a color that lies
The color of sickness on a man's skin
The yellow and feverish light of a tired lightbulb
The pages of an old book that are slowly dying
I am the color of betrayal and of cowardice
At the crossroads I am the middle light
The one that is neither yes nor no
The one that says "Careful, maybe danger"
I am the desert sand where one dies of thirst
And the fake gold of a cheap ring
That leaves a green trace on the finger
I am joy, but a joy that knows it does not last
The beauty of summer just before autumn
A laughter that is on the verge of tears
I am the most cheerful and the most unsettling of colors
A flash of light
Before the shadow comes to take it all back.

---

## 247. Purple, the Color of Magic

I am not a simple color I was born in an alchemist's cauldron From the marriage of the blood of red and the dream of blue I am the color of things that happen only once
I am the color of magic and of deep secrets The smoke that escapes from a sorcerer's vial The robe of a king who speaks to the stars And the invisible ink that reveals a treasure map I am the color of the hour between dog and wolf That moment when anything can still happen When a pumpkin can become a carriage
I am the heavy scent of incense in an old church The taste of a wild blackberry picked from the brambles The last note of a sad song that lingers in the air I am a color of luxury and of meditation
But I am not only in palaces I am also in humble and bruised things The little wild pansy with its golden heart The color of a bruise that is slowly healing The memory of a small pain I am the shadow under the eyes of someone who has dreamed too much And the vein of amethyst hidden in the rough rock
I am a noble color and a wounded color A miracle and a scar The color of mystery and of intuition When you see me in the depths of a dream Know that it is the door to another world A world where logic has taken a vacation
I am the color that exists just before falling asleep The last bridge before the great journey of the night The promise that the world is stranger And more beautiful than it seems.

---

## 248. Summer Rain

It falls warm and light
Like a shower for the tired world
Leaves shine with a new green
The earth breathes a forgotten scent
Children run under the drops
Without fear without umbrella without worries
It's a laugh from the sky on the earth
A song for thirsty flowers
And the world feels younger
Cleaner softer truer
Just for a moment of freshness
Before the sun returns to burn.

---

## 249. The Spiderweb

A masterpiece of patience
Woven with the threads of morning
A silver trap for the fly
A dew-jewel in the sun
She is the invisible architect
The silent huntress
Her work is fragile and strong
A promise of hunger and beauty
She dances in the light wind
A glass ballerina
And every glittering strand
Is a small miracle of life.

---

## 250. The Smell of an Old Book

It smells of aged paper and dust
Stories read by other hands
Tears shed on other pages
It is the scent of time flowing by
Of ideas crossing centuries
Of voiceless voices
It is the smell of quiet wisdom
Of gentle solitude and dreams
And every breath in
Is a journey into the past
A promise of knowledge
That still awaits us, always.

---

## 251. Morning Coffee

It smells of the promise of the day
Warmth in the cold cup
The bitter taste that awakens ideas
Life's first kiss
It is the sacred morning ritual
Silence before the world's noise
The energy that pushes us forward
Comfort before the unknown
Each sip is a small departure
Strength to face the hour
And the world seems clearer
With its guiding aroma.

---

## 252. The Old Armchair

It holds the shape of so many bodies
Of so many dreams of so many sleeps
The fabric is worn like an old map
The springs sing tired melodies
It has heard secrets and laughter
Silent tears and shouts of joy
It is the discreet confidant of the house
The motionless witness of time passing
A refuge for tired bodies
A throne for distant thoughts
And its silence is stronger
Than all the voices of the world.

---

## 253. The Lost Whisper

A word that flew away too fast
An idea that found no voice
A secret that will never be told
It's the ghost of a conversation
The echo of a forgotten thought
The beginning of an endless story
It hovers in the air and silence
A scent of regret or hope
And sometimes at night
It comes back to visit me
A gentle breeze on my skin
The memory of something that never happened.

---

## 254. The Song of the Sea

It doesn't always sing the same song
Sometimes it screams its anger with the waves
Sometimes it whispers lullabies to the sands
It is the voice of the ancient world
The memory of all lost ships
The laughter of dolphins in the light
The salty tears of farewells
It never sleeps it never falls silent
And every wave that arrives
Is a new story
An eternity of movements
Calling us to infinity.

---

## 255. City Lights

They are fallen stars on the earth
Eyes watching in the human night
Promises of life joy work
Secrets that shine and fade
They draw paths in the dark
Labyrinths of dreams and solitudes
Each luminous point is a soul
Seeking its way its love its peace
And from afar it looks like a great necklace
Thrown by the gods onto the world's neck
A fragile and strong beauty
That makes us hope again.

---

## 256. The Hands of the Clock

They never stop running
Arrows showing the passing moment
They never truly touch
Like lovers always missing each other
They draw the circle of time
Beginning and end joy and sorrow
They are the masters of our lives
The silent witnesses of our days
And every tick-tock that resonates
Is a small death a small birth
The rhythm of the universe
In the small space of the house.

---

## 257. The Silence After the Word

It is the echo of what was said
The weight of what was heard
The space for thought to settle
The void before the answer
It is the moment when senses awaken
When the heart speaks without voice
When truth can finally show itself
Without the mask of phrases
It is the guardian of intentions
The mirror of our souls
And sometimes it is stronger
Than all the speeches of the world.

---

## 258. The Rose in Winter

Its petals are frozen memories
Its color a forgotten promise
It stands alone against the wind
A challenge of beauty to the snow
It bears the stigmata of cold
But its heart has not given up
It dreams of sun and bees
Of the softness of past summer
It is life's fragile strength
That never quite dies
And its patience is a poem
For spring that will return.

---

## 259. White, the Color of Everything and Nothing

They say I am not a color
That I am the absence, the blank page, the great nothing
But it is I who begins everything
I am the milk of the world's first morning
The first tooth of a smiling child
The fresh snow that offers forgiveness to a dirty city
I am the bride's dress full of fragile promises
And the blank page of the new notebook where everything is still possible
I am the dove of peace that does not exist
And the sail of the boat leaving for the horizon
I am innocence and the beginning
The first word of a poem
But I am also the other thing
The thing one does not speak of
I am the white wall of the hospital that smells of fear
The white flag of the lost battle
The white silence after the scream
I am the bone polished by time and vultures
The blinding white light of the interrogation
And the white face of the returning ghost
I am the void and the end
The last word of an erased poem
I am the best and the worst
The nuptial bed and the shroud
The cloud where one sees an angel's face
And the fog where one gets lost forever
I am the sugar and the salt
The feather and the ash
I do not choose a side
I am just there, before and after
The great silence where all colors are born
And where all colors die.

---

## 260. THE MAN WHO FED THE PIGEONS

Every morning at seven
on the market square
an old man in a grey coat
crumbled bread for the pigeons
and the pigeons knew him
the way one knows church bells

Then came the sign
FEEDING PIGEONS FORBIDDEN
fine one thousand crowns
signature illegible

Next morning at seven
the old man came
sat down on the bench
with empty hands
entirely empty
and the pigeons came all the same
landed on his shoulders
on his hat
on his knees

The clerk who had signed the sign
watched from the town hall window
on the third day he came down
What are you feeding them? he asked sternly
Nothing, said the man
Then why do they come?

And the old man smiled
The bread, that was for you
so you'd have something to forbid
They never came here for bread
they came here for me
and against that
you have no paragraph yet

---

## 261. THE FORGOTTEN UMBRELLA

Mr. Novák bought himself an umbrella
a solid black umbrella
so he would never get wet
and the very first Tuesday
he left it on the train to Brno

The umbrella travelled on
in Brno a student took it
and under it she kissed a boy
she was not supposed to kiss
and left it in a café

In the café a violinist took it
and played beneath it at the station
when rain fell on his sheet music
then he lent it to a bride
whose veil was catching the rain
and the bride left it in a taxi
and the taxi drove it all the way to the sea

And so the umbrella saw Brno
a kiss that wasn't allowed to be
notes washed out by rain
a wedding
and the sea

Mr. Novák bought a new umbrella
an even more solid one
and so as not to lose it again
he kept it at home
and when it rained
he stayed home with it

The umbrella saw the world
because it got lost
Mr. Novák saw nothing
because he was afraid of getting lost

---

## 262. INVENTORY AFTER GRANDMOTHER

One shoebox
and in it:
three entirely ordinary stones
a train ticket from sixty-two
there and back
the return never used
a key to a door that no longer stands
a cinema ticket for a film no longer showing
a cake recipe in a stranger's handwriting
a button from a uniform
a photograph of a man who is not grandfather
and on the back nothing

And at the very bottom
a note in her handwriting:
THROW AWAY
dated forty years ago

She didn't throw it away
and neither did we
we carried the box home
note and all

Because in our family
you don't inherit houses or gold
you inherit boxes
and whatever nobody could throw away
or say out loud

---

## 263. THE NIGHT TRAM

The last tram
number twenty-two
runs empty after midnight
and the driver Mr. Souček
still announces every stop
Railway Station
Theatre
Hospital
Botanical Garden
terminus

One January night an inspector got on
the only passenger all winter
Why do you announce the stops
to an empty tram?
he asked officially
and for good order punched his own ticket

Mr. Souček did not slow down
You see, you got on all the same
he said
I announce them for everyone
who once wanted to go somewhere
and never went
Maybe tonight
one of them will think of it again

Theatre, he announced
and nobody got on
Hospital, he announced
and nobody got off

And the inspector said nothing
put away his notebook
and rode to the terminus
and then all the way back
because he too had a stop
where he had never gotten off

---

## 264. THE WATCHMAKER

The watchmaker's shop by the fountain
repairs everything
alarm clocks that ring too late
watches in too much of a hurry
cuckoo clocks that lost their voice
the tower clocks of the whole town

Only one clock on the workshop wall
stands still
four seventeen
for years now

The customers ask
Master, and that one — doesn't it run?
It runs, says the watchmaker
it keeps perfect time

Perfect time? When it's stopped?

And the watchmaker lifts the loupe from his eye
Your watches tell you what time it is
that one tells what time it was
once at four seventeen
when she stood there by the window
laughing at something outside
and I told myself
remember this

I can repair anything in the world for you
he says putting the loupe back
except time
time cannot be repaired
time can only be stopped
in memory

---

## 265. THE BAKER WHO SKIPS BREAKFAST

Three in the morning
when even the streetlamps are sleeping
the baker gets up
and the flour smiles at him
white as the moon
that hasn't left yet

He bakes
he bakes rolls for the woman at the newsstand
bread for the night-bus driver
buns for the children
who don't yet know
they're hungry

By six the whole street smells of it
and people follow the smell
the way you follow happiness
eyes closed
and never getting lost

Is the bread fresh?
asks the woman in the headscarf
Fresh madam
warmer than this winter

And the baker?
At home the baker cuts himself
a slice of yesterday
the crust a little harder
the smell a little quieter
a bread that waits for no one now

Because fresh bread
smells only for the one
who waited for it
and the baker doesn't wait
the whole town
is waiting for the baker

---

## 266. THE TRAFFIC-LIGHT PAINTER

He paints the red
he paints the green
brush in hand
ladder under the sky
Mr Miller of the traffic lights

Forty years he's told the town
stop
forty years he's told the town
go
and the town obeys him
to the letter

But nobody ever told him
go
he waited for green outside the house with geraniums
waited for green under a certain window
waited for green at doors
that opened for other people

Stop Miller
said life
and Miller stopped
good as gold
like a pedestrian in a safety leaflet

Then one night
the town empty as a pocket
Miller standing at the crossroads
and everywhere
as far as the eye can see
green green green

And Miller understands
that green doesn't come
to those who wait
green shines for the one
who is awake
while everyone else is sleeping

And he crosses

---

## 267. THE ODD GLOVE

On the number nine tram
between Lilac Street
and Station Square
Mrs Marie lost
her left glove
leather
brown
a little worn at the thumb

At home she still has the right one
all alone
like one hand clapping

And suddenly Mrs Marie
sees the whole town differently
a glove on the school railings
a glove on a bench in the park
a glove stuck on a post
like a small sad flag
all of them left
all of them right
all of them odd

How many of us are there
thinks Mrs Marie
how many of us walk this town
one hand warm
and the other in the cold

At home she takes the right one
strokes it
and in the morning lays it
on the railing at the tram stop
gently
the way you let a bird go

Let the pairs find each other
even without us

---

## 268. THE GEOGRAPHY TEACHER

Here is the Sahara children
sand and sand and sand
here the Amazon
a river that doesn't know its own banks
here the Himalayas
the roof of the world

And the teacher's pointer
travels across the map
lightly
like a swallow

She herself has never been farther
than her sister's in Leeds

Children where is Everest?
There miss
Very good
and one day you'll write to me from up there

The children left
the way children do
one to Lisbon
one to Iceland
one all the way to Peru

And the postman brings postcards
Greetings from the Sahara miss
sand you were right nothing but sand
The Amazon is brown miss
you never told us that
You can't write from Everest
so I'm writing from the bottom
it's beautiful from the bottom too

And on the wall above her bed
the map of the world
is slowly disappearing
under the postcards
sea covering sea
mountains covering mountains

The teacher never went anywhere
and she travelled the whole world
by the hands of her children

---

## 269. THE MAN WHO SAVED TIME

He knew every shortcut
through the butcher's yard
through the gap in the fence
stairs always two at a time

Three minutes saved at the barber's
The usual and make it quick
five minutes saved at lunch
soup can be drunk
a whole afternoon saved
by not taking the walk
that led nowhere

Dad will you come and look? calls the child
Tomorrow
tomorrow I'll have more time

No time no time
he said
and his watch nodded along

And at the end
when there's no more hurrying
because there's nowhere left to go
he sits and counts
three minutes a day for forty years
that makes an ocean of time
where is that ocean?

He asks
in which bank
in which account
where are they deposited
all those minutes he saved

Nowhere sir
time cannot be saved
time can only be spent
and you spent yours
down to the last minute
on hurrying

---

## 270. THE LAST TELEPHONE BOX

On the street corner
stands the last telephone box
red
LISTED MONUMENT
says the little plaque
and that's all it says

Nobody calls from it
people carry telephones in their pockets
like a spare heart
and the box stands there
empty
as a confessional on a Monday

Tourists take pictures with it
children ask
what was it for?
For distance says their father
you went in there to talk to distance

Then one evening
it's raining
November
the street deserted
and the box
rings

It rings
it rings into the rain
and a passer-by
collar up to his chin
stops
hesitates
and lifts the receiver

Hello?

And a voice
an old voice
from very far away
only asks

Is it still raining there
the way it did that night?

---

## 271. THE MUSEUM GUARD

For forty years Mr Joseph
has sat on his chair in the corner of the room
beside the famous painting
so famous
that people cross the world
just to look at it

Mr Joseph keeps watch
Please don't touch
No flash please
Sir that line on the floor
is there for you

Forty years of watching
people watching
he knows every kind of looking
the devout kind
the bored kind
and the kind that's only ticking a box

The painting is at his back
the way a window is at your back
the way the sea is at your back
when you're guarding the beach

Then comes the last day
they give him flowers
his colleagues shake his hand
and the cloakroom lady dabs her eye

And the next morning
Mr Joseph stands in the queue
at the ticket desk
One ticket please
You Mr Joseph?
One ticket please

And for the first time in forty years
he stands before the painting
on the right side of it
and he looks
and he looks
and he looks

---

## 272. THE BINMAN AND THE BOOKS

At four in the morning the bins rattle
and Mr Charlie in his orange vest
lifts the lids
the way you open books

And sometimes
between the peelings and the tins
there lies a book
a real one
thrown away
poems with a dedication
I love you Vera 1972
a novel with a folded corner
on the page where somebody stopped

Mr Charlie wipes them on his sleeve
and carries them home
one by one
a shelf
then two
then a whole wall

What are you going to do with them? asks his wife
Read them says Mr Charlie
somebody has to finish them

And in the evening he sits
in his library of rescued books
reading thrown-away words

Because people don't throw out rubbish
thinks Mr Charlie
people throw out the things
they've stopped understanding
a love from 1972
a story stopped at a folded corner

and he's a binman
he picks up after them

---

## 273. TWO CHAIRS

Mr Anton lives alone
third floor
window on the courtyard
a teapot for one cup
a bed for one
but at the table
two chairs

His own chair creaks
it knows his weight
it knows his elbows
it knows his long evenings

The other is good as new
nobody has ever sat on it
not the postman
not his sister from Leeds
who still can't make it
not the lady from the ground floor
who smiled at him once

Why two chairs? asks the neighbour
I'm expecting someone says Mr Anton
he just hasn't come yet

The years go by
the chairs stay
one creaks
the other keeps quiet

And maybe it's for no one
that second chair
not for the lady from the ground floor
not for the sister
not for anyone with a name

Maybe it's only there
so that loneliness
doesn't look final

An empty chair
is not sorrow
an empty chair
is an invitation

---

## 274. THE CEMETERY GARDENER

Mr Victor plants pansies
on Mrs Novak's grave
he waters the begonias
of the old schoolmaster
and the rose
of the little girl from 1943

The dead can't smell the flowers
people say
the dead don't care

Mr Victor knows
kneeling with his hands in the earth
the dead don't smell
the dead don't see
the dead don't mind the weeds

And still he weeds
and still he waters
and still he plants
spring summer autumn

Because on Sunday comes
a woman with a shopping bag
and she says
The pansies are lovely this year
and she stands a while
and tells her mother
all the news

Because a man in a hat comes
and sits down by the begonias
the way you sit down
next to someone

The flowers are not for the dead
Mr Victor knows
the flowers are for the living
so they have somewhere to come
and a reason to come
and a time to come back

Mr Victor is not a gardener
Mr Victor grows
reasons to return

---

## 275. THE LETTER WITHOUT AN ADDRESS

The postwoman sorts the morning mail
letters with addresses
letters with stamps
letters that know where they're going
and among them one letter
no street no town no name
only the words
FOR WHOEVER NEEDS IT

Return it
to whom
deliver it
where
so she keeps it in her bag
one year
two years
ten years
between the bills and the postcards from the sea
light as a feather
heavy as a secret

Her colleagues say
throw it out
the rulebook says
throw it out
she says
not yet

And one day in November
on a bench outside the post office
a man sits crying
the way people cry in November
quietly and for real
so she stops
opens her bag
and hands him the letter
as if she'd carried it for him all along

Inside is a blank sheet of paper
nothing else
a blank white sheet
and the man reads it
and reads it
and reads it

---

## 276. THE FISHERMAN WHO LETS THEM GO

Every morning by the river
old Miller sits with his rod
he catches a fish
looks at it
lets it go
catches a second
lets it go
catches a third
lets it go

The neighbours laugh
over the fences they laugh
down at the pub they laugh
well then Miller
nothing again?
and the wife at home boiling water for nothing?

He nods
pays for his beer
and says nothing

Because old Miller
doesn't go to the river for fish
he goes there for the silence
for that great green silence
that flows and flows
and is in no hurry
for the silence nobody gives you
not at the town hall
not at church
not at home

And the fish know it
fish know everything about silence
that's why they bite
only for him they bite
they'd almost eat out of his hand
because the one who asks only for silence
gets given everything

---

## 277. THE PIANO TUNER

Mr Marsh is a piano tuner
forty years he's tuned pianos
and forty years he's heard
what nobody else can hear

The number nine tram
squeals a quarter tone sharp
the doorbell of his building
lies by a minor third
the clock on the tower
strikes a false noon
and the minister on the radio
speaks entirely in a minor key
even when he's promising major

It gives him a headache
the whole city gives him a headache
he walks the streets
tuning fork in his pocket
mending the world in his mind
loosen here
tighten there
retune the whole thing

But when the children in the courtyard
sing
off-key as a barn door
off-key as sparrows in May
Mr Marsh stops
takes off his hat
and listens

A piano can be tuned
he says
joy cannot
joy you either have
or you don't
and these kids have it
all of it
wrong notes and all
maybe because of the wrong notes

---

## 278. THE LOST AND FOUND OFFICE

I've come for an umbrella
a black one
with a crooked handle
said the man at the counter
and the clerk nodded
come and see

And she led him down the shelves
the shelf of umbrellas
a hundred umbrellas
a thousand umbrellas
black ones red ones checkered ones
the shelf of gloves
every one of them a left
the shelf of glasses
that don't suit anyone anymore
the shelf of wedding rings
the shelf of keys
to doors whose locks
were changed long ago

Here's yours
she said
and handed him his umbrella
still wet from last spring

And nobody ever comes for all this?
asked the man
and the clerk shrugged
the way you shrug
after twenty years on the job

People don't lose things
sir
things get found
nearly all of them get found
what people lose
is the will to come back for them
and that
we never find
that's not on
any shelf

---

## 279. GRANDMOTHER AND DAYLIGHT SAVING TIME

On Sunday night
the whole country changed its clocks
the whole country except one kitchen
where grandmother said
no

The family fusses
gran you'll miss the doctor
gran you'll miss the news
gran the whole world runs on a different hour
and grandmother stirs her soup
and says
then let it run

All summer long she lives
an hour to the side of the world
she has lunch when the world has tea
she lights the lamp while the world can still see
and in the evening she sits on the doorstep
in an hour the others
spent long ago

Why gran?
her granddaughter asks one day
and grandmother points at the clock
the old clock with the winding key

Your grandfather wound this clock
every evening at half past seven
and at half past seven we had supper
and everyone was home
and everything was good

Summer time winter time
let governments flip the hours
like pancakes in a pan
I'm staying in the hour
when everything used to be good

Time can be refused
little one
if you know why

---

## 280. THE BRIDGE BUILDER

Engineer Brandt built a hundred bridges
bridges over rivers
bridges over valleys
a suspension bridge
a drawbridge
a bridge the papers wrote about
three columns and a photograph

He got a medal from the minister
he got a medal from the president
students learn his formulas
and the bridges stand
and serve
and carry everything
trains floods a hundred years

Only one bridge he never built
the one to his brother
twenty years without a word
over a field
over an inheritance
over a sentence said at a funeral
he no longer even knows over what
he only remembers the silence
wide as a valley
deep as a river

Because the longest bridges
are not built of steel
they are built of a single word
and for that material
there are no standards
no formulas
no medals

One night he sits down at his table
the engineer of a hundred bridges
takes a sheet of paper
and all night long he builds
and by morning it is done
a bridge of one word

Forgive me

---

## 281. THE TICKET SELLER

Behind the station window
Miss Marie
sells the world

Paris via Frankfurt
second class seat reserved
Lisbon with one change
Istanbul with a sleeper
she knows by heart the price of every distance
what the sea costs
what the mountains cost
what it costs to be elsewhere

Through her hands each day
pass thousands of kilometres
honeymoons
escapes
returns
and to every ticket she adds
a safe journey
free of charge

And in the evening she buys herself
what she always buys
two stops home
warm up the dinner
water the geraniums
sleep

Until one day
a Friday in October
she walks around the station
joins the queue
at her own window
and says the destination
she has kept quiet for twenty years

Lisbon
one way
no return

And the hand that gives her the ticket
her own hand
is trembling

---

## 282. WINTER'S INVENTORY

In December in the yard
the children built a snowman
three balls of snow
one on top of the other
a carrot from Mrs Green
two coals from the cellar
grandfather's old pot
and his name was
Mr White

All winter he stood guarding the yard
smiling with his coals
freezing without complaint
the children said good morning
he said nothing
but it suited him

Then came March
and with it the sun
and Mr White day by day
grew thinner
melted
shrank
until one morning
he wasn't there

On the grass he left behind
a carrot
two coals
a pot

Spring takes winter's inventory
counts everything twice
checks everything off
carrot present
coals present
pot present

Only the snow is missing
the snow went back to the sky
the way it came
taking nothing
of all that people gave it

---

## 283. THE NIGHT BAKERY

At night when the city sleeps
those who don't sleep go walking
they walk very quietly
they walk all alone
they stop at the window
where it is bright
and it smells good

It smells of bread
it smells of warmth
it smells of morning
that has not come yet

One has a coat over his pyjamas
one holds an empty bag
as if to go and buy some sleep
the old man counts the floor tiles
and does not count the sheep

Inside a white shadow
pours the flour
rolls the dough
and does not look outside
or does he look?

In the morning people buy their rolls
and they do not know
and they do not guess
that the little window on the yard
stays open all night long

It is not a draught
says the baker
and he goes on baking
he bakes bread for those who sleep
and scent for those who don't

---

## 284. THE BLIND MAN AT THE CINEMA

He goes to the pictures every Wednesday
the blind man
and beside him the neighbour woman
and between them the dark

On the screen it is raining
she whispers to him
now she walks down the street
she has a hat
and she is sad

The blind man nods
he sees her walking
he sees her better
than the whole hall

Now they are kissing
whispers the neighbour
and she blushes
in the dark
where nobody sees her
only he does

The lights come up
people go home
they blink
they wipe their eyes

Outside in the street
it is really raining
and he tells her
you know what I saw?
I saw
that the lady on the screen
had your voice
and was lovelier
than they dared to write in the script

The neighbour says nothing
and for the first time
she does not know
what is on the screen

---

## 285. THE CAROUSEL MAN

From town to town
from fair to fair
the caravan the wheels the road
and wheels again
little wooden horses
swans with their gilt worn thin
and him in the middle

All his life he dreams of a house
a house with a garden
with a fence
with an apple tree
where the suitcases
would finally forget
they are suitcases

Children climb on
children climb off
three minutes of happiness
for small change
and always the same music
barrel-organ music
that smells of candy floss

At night he sits on the caravan step
counting towns
the way other people count sheep

And one night he sees it
the carousel turns
the horses run run run
and never arrive
and he stands in the middle
still as an apple tree
the center of all the roads

a house with a garden
would just be another center
but without the music

---

## 286. THE LAST COBBLER

In the city of supermarkets
where new shoes cost less
than mending old ones
sits a cobbler
the last cobbler
hammer pegs wax
and the smell of leather
that nobody knows by name anymore

People bring him shoes
the way you bring an old dog
to be put down
these aren't worth fixing
they say
and they leave them there
so they won't have to
throw them out themselves

He fixes them
new soles
new stitches
polishes them to a deep dark shine
and gives them away
to whoever walks by barefoot
or nearly

He knows the whole city
and the city doesn't know him
he knows it by its walk
this one treads on the outer edge
a loner
that one wears down her toes
always running after something
that got away
this one has heels straight
as a conscience

And the last cobbler sits
reading the city from below
soles don't lie
faces do
and the city walks overhead
and never knows a thing

---

## 287. THE METRONOME

The old violinist died on a Tuesday
quietly
the way you close a violin case
they took away the furniture
they took away the violin
sold the sheet music by the pound
and painted the flat white

The new tenant is young
he carries boxes
puts up shelves
and one Saturday
he finds on top of the wardrobe
a little wooden pyramid
a metronome

What is that? asks his girlfriend
A metronome
a clock for music
it doesn't tell you the time
it tells you how fast a life is lived

He winds the spring
slides the little weight
and sets it going
tick tock tick tock
andante
an old woman's step on the stairs
tick tock
soup stirred on a Sunday
tick tock
bow up bow down
sixty years

Turn it off says the girl
No he says
and he lets it beat
into the evening

A heart stops
the tempo stays
the flat remembers
how fast it was lived in

---

## 288. THE NAMES OF TREES

For sixty years he walked to work
down the same avenue
there and back
briefcase newspaper rain
and the trees were only trees
green things along the way

Then came retirement
and with it silence
and with the silence a field guide to trees
from a secondhand shop
for next to nothing

Now he walks the park slowly
guide under his arm
like a musical score
learning the names
leaf by leaf
bark by bark

Good morning ash
good morning hornbeam
good morning small-leaved lime
you have done well this year

People turn their heads
the man who talks to trees
children giggle
and he tips his hat
to the plane tree
as if to the headmaster

A madman say the benches
At last a man with manners
whisper the leaves

And the trees
they had known him all along
sixty years of the same footsteps
there and back
they knew him before he knew them
names are for people
not for trees

---

## 289. THE NIGHT TRAIN CONDUCTOR

Forty years of night trains
forty years walking the corridor
between the sleepers
quiet as a conscience
waking them
sir ten minutes to Crewe
madam don't sleep past York
up you get soldier
the war leaves without you

He knows how to wake people gently
a hand on the shoulder
he knows how to read sleep
this one sleeps like a child
wake him twice
that one isn't sleeping
she is just closing her eyes
on something that travels with her

Nobody ever missed their station
forty years
thousands woken
thousands of drowsy thank-yous
thank you conductor
whatever would we do without you

The last night before retirement
the last run
the carriage sways
the wheels say sleep sleep sleep
and the conductor sits down
just for a moment
just for one small moment

And he sleeps past his station
rides right through it
like everyone else
because all his life he woke the others
and him
there was no one to wake him

---

## 290. THE FLOWER SELLER

Every morning at five
she cuts the roses
with the same one pair of scissors
slant cut cold water
and waits to see who comes

The bridegroom comes
all white in the shoulders
thirty red ones please
and let them last at least a week
They will outlast plenty of things
says the flower seller
neither smiling nor sad

The widower comes
all grey in the shoulders
thirty red ones please
she loved them so
I know says the flower seller
she bought them every Friday

The same roses
the same counter
the same scissors
a wedding in the morning
a funeral in the afternoon
and in between she waters

People ask her
how can you do it
the same flowers for love and for death
and she shrugs

Roses make no distinction
the scent is the same
at the altar and at the grave
roses only smell sweet
the translation
people do that themselves

---

## 291. THE BOY AND THE ECHO

A boy stands at the edge of the valley
small against the mountains
and the mountains are big
and say nothing

And because he was scolded that morning
and because he is seven
and the world is unfair
he takes a breath
and shouts into the valley
YOU IDIOT

And the valley takes a breath
and gives it back
idiot idiot idiot
three times four times
once from every side

The boy sits down in the grass
and cries
grandpa the mountains don't like me
they call me names
did you hear?

Grandpa sits down beside him
pipe smoke rising straight
like a proper upbringing
Try telling them something else
he says
mountains repeat
they can't make anything up
they are only big

The boy stands up
wipes his nose on his sleeve
breathes in down to his boots
and shouts
I LOVE YOU

And the valley gives it back
I love you I love you I love you
once from every side
and then once more
and it keeps on saying it
long into the evening
the world is one big echo
it gives you back
whatever you call into it

---

## 292. THE HOUSE PAINTER

He comes with his bucket
and his roller
after the people
who have gone

On the wall a note
in pencil
call your mother
the roller passes
and the mother is gone

On the door frame little marks
Annie 3 years
Annie 4 years
Annie five
and near the top almost grown

The white paint
asks nothing
the white paint
knows nobody
the white paint
is a fresh start
for money

The gentleman paints
the workman paints
and nobody knows
that before he covers the frame
he opens a little notebook

He copies the marks
Annie 3
Annie 4
Annie five
with the street and the date

In the pocket of his overalls
he carries the archive
of strangers' children
who keep growing
even when the wall forgets

And if one day they look
for where they were small
they will find only white
and an old painter
who remembers it for them

---

## 293. THE RETIRED FIREMAN

All his life
he carried water
to the fire

Water in the hose
water in the pail
water on his shoulder
up the stairs
against the flames

Now he has a garden
and a watering can
and a row of beans
and in the evening he goes
to water them

The same water
he tells himself
the very same water
I carried to the one
that takes

now I carry it to the one
that gives back

The fire does not remember
whom it burned
the fire forgets
the moment it goes out

But the garden
the garden remembers
every can
every evening
and the next day
it says thank you
in green

The neighbours laugh
the old fireman
watering his primroses

And he smiles
and he carries
and he carries
and for the first time in his life
nothing is burning

---

## 294. THE WEDDING-DRESS TAILOR

He sews dresses
made to measure
of happiness

He knows the brides' waists
he knows the shoulders
he knows the held breath
at the fitting
before the mirror

He knows the whole town
married
measured
pinned

How many waists
how many shoulders
how many yeses
have passed under his needle

And his daughter
his own daughter
is getting married
in jeans

Dad don't cry
she says
I don't want a dress

The tailor says nothing
the tailor knows
happiness is not made to measure
happiness either fits
or it doesn't

The jeans fitted her
like a glove
like nothing ever
he had sewn

So he took his needle
one last time
and he sewed her only
a little handkerchief
for the pocket

In case
he says
one day after all
she should need
to wipe away some happiness

---

## 295. THE LIFT BOY

For forty years Mister Albert
goes up and down
down and up
in his cage of gold and mirrors
at the Hotel Metropole

To the third floor he carries love
to the fifth floor money
to the seventh loneliness
with its crocodile suitcases

Which floor please?
the guests ask
and Mister Albert knows
third fifth seventh
he knows every floor
and owns none

His place is between the floors
between the second and the third
between nowhere and not yet
where the lift sings
its little song of cables

The guests get out
the guests get in
Mister Albert stays
the doors close
the doors open
and life rides up and down

On the last day
the day before his pension
Mister Albert rode all the way up
higher than the seventh
where the sign says
ROOF — NO ENTRY

and for the first time he got out

And above the roof
above the chimneys above the aerials
he saw where all his life
he had been taking the others

the sky

---

## 296. THE POSTCARD COLLECTOR

In the second-hand shop by the station
in a shoebox
old postcards are sleeping
Venice nineteen twenty
Nice Brighton Constantinople
and Mister Ralph buys them
a penny apiece

He has never been to Venice
never been to Nice
the farthest was his sister's in Slough
and it rained

It's lovely here writes Charlie
it's lovely here writes Maud
it's lovely here write the dead
who got somewhere
and looked back

Mister Ralph reads them at night
under the lamp
kisses for other people's Annies
for other people's mothers
warm greetings from places
where nobody from his street ever arrived

Why do you buy these?
the shopkeeper asks
I collect proof
says Mister Ralph
proof that the world exists
and that people who get somewhere
look back

One winter day
a postcard arrived
a real one
with his name and a stamp
the sea and nothing else
no signature

And Mister Ralph stood it on the windowsill
picture facing out
into the street

so the world
for once
could look too

---

## 297. THE THEATRE CLEANING LADY

When the curtain falls
when the chandeliers go out
when the actors wash off their kings and beggars
Mrs Nora comes in
with her broom and bucket
into the empty house

She doesn't read reviews
she doesn't buy the papers
you read a theatre off its floor

Crumpled handkerchiefs in row nine
so there was dying tonight
laughter they say pops buttons
and there are two in row five
programmes forgotten under the seats
so people left with their heads elsewhere
and one glove in the stalls
the other hand
was in somebody else's hand no doubt

Mrs Nora sweeps
the applause is gone
applause leaves nothing on the floor

The actors get the flowers
the director gets the praise
Mrs Nora gets the floorboards
and floorboards never lie

And sometimes
when the house is empty
and the stage is too
Mrs Nora climbs up
leans her broom against the set
and under her breath
finishes a line
for everyone who cried in row nine

And the empty theatre listens
because even a floor
sometimes wants to hear the end

---

## 298. THE WEATHER ANNOUNCER

Every morning
into the microphone
sunny
and clear
with patches of blue

Good morning all
it will be lovely
he says
and people reach
for their sunglasses

And he
under the desk
against his foot
keeps an umbrella
always
every day

The forecast
is hope
for the others
the umbrella
is experience
for him

He broadcasts sunshine
he sends out warmth
he hands round blue
for free
into every kitchen

And he himself
when he steps outside
opens his umbrella
and is not surprised
almost ever

Because between
hope
and experience
usually
very softly
it rains

But he will never say it
into the microphone
people need
to hear it will be fine
someone at least
must say it out loud

---

## 299. THE EMPTY ROOM

They are moving
boxes by the door
the father carries the lamp
the mother carries the silence

And the boy comes back
one more time
the last time
into his room

It is empty
he says
there is nothing left

But on the wall
a paler rectangle
there hung the bear
here the boat
here the map of the stars

And in the corner
a darker shadow
where the bed had been
where he slept
where he grew
where he feared the dark
and stopped

He shuts the door
they drive away

And into the room
a new boy will come
and he will see
the paler rectangles
and the darker corner

He will inherit the maps
of a childhood not his own
the empty places
left by someone else's dreams

And he will have
to invent for them
a country
and a bear
and a boat
and why this corner here
was so afraid of the dark

---

## 300. THE DOORBELL

At the old man's door
nobody rings
except the postman
except the parcels

He orders
things
he does not need
a shoehorn
a second one
a third

a squeegee for windows
a book about fish
though he never fished

Drawers full
of uselessness
cupboards cracking
with uselessness

But in the morning
someone rings
good day
a parcel for you
a signature please

And he signs
slowly
as slowly as he can
and he asks
how are you
and is it nice out?

He is not ordering goods
he is ordering
a ring at the door

Loneliness
can for a moment
be tricked
cash on delivery

And the postman
has long known it
and he carries and carries
and never will he say
why this old man
needs a fifth shoehorn

---

## 301. THE VIOLINIST IN THE UNDERPASS

In the underpass
where the wind pulls through
a violinist plays
and the violin case
open
for small change

People pass
people hurry
people throw coins
as they walk
without stopping

Clink
and they are gone
clink
and they are gone
the coins fly
the people fly

He plays for backs
he plays for footsteps
he plays for the echo
that never claps

And then there comes
a dumb old man
and he stops
and he goes no further

He stands
and he listens
an hour
two
he throws nothing
he has nothing

And the violinist understands
that this old man
has given him
the most of all

To stop
is the rarest coin
and the only one
that cannot
be changed

---

## 302. THE LAMPLIGHTER

The last gas lamp
in the city
kept officially
FOR THE TOURISTS
says the little sign

And the old man
who in the evening
comes to light it
the only one
by hand

The electric lamps
come on
all at once
there is a switch
somewhere
nobody knows where
click
and there is light
everywhere
at once

But his lamp
his only lamp
comes on
one
and by hand
and every evening
it is waited for

He comes
he opens the little glass
he strikes a match
and the small flame
says thank you

The tourists take photos
what a curiosity
the last lamplighter
an open-air museum
a postcard

And he thinks to himself
lighting
is an event
not a state
electricity is a state
and a state is not waited for

But an event
an event is waited for
all day long
until the evening comes
and with it
the only one
who still
kindles it
by hand

---

## 303. THE OLD SWIMMER

Every morning he comes to the river
the old swimmer
who swims no more
He stands on the bank
in his shoes
in his coat
and he looks

Once he used to win
once he had medals
once they counted him
one two three
and clapped when he touched the wall

Now he touches nothing
he only looks
at the water going away
at the water coming
at the water going away

Not swimming today? asks the fisherman
No
answers the swimmer
I've finished swimming

And he stands there still
in his shoes
in his coat
and the river runs

The river is always the same one
the water never
he swam a thousand times
in a thousand different waters
of a single name

Now he lets the river swim
and the river lets him stand
they are even
two old racers
who have finally
stopped chasing each other

---

## 304. THE COFFEE MACHINE

At three in the morning
in the hospital corridor
the coffee machine glows
the only window
where something still happens

You drop a coin
and it grumbles
it growls
and gives you a burning cup
COFFEE WITH SUGAR
for a few pennies

People stand around
a father
waiting for his son
a woman
waiting for her mother
a young man
waiting for no one
just afraid to go home

Will you have one too? asks the father
Yes
says the woman
though she doesn't drink coffee

They drink
they say nothing
they look into the cup
as if at the bottom
there were an answer

The machine gives coffee for a few pennies
and an excuse for free
the cup is only a reason
to stand side by side

And so they talk
about the son
about the mother
about nothing
about everything

The most truth in the whole city is spoken
at the machine
at three in the morning
between two sips
that nobody likes

---

## 305. THE MISSING WORD

Grandfather sits by the window
a dictionary on his knees
turning pages
from A to Z
and back again
outside stands a suitcase
and beside the suitcase the grandson

he is looking for a word
for the thing in his chest
something between a train and a Sunday
between sorrow and safe travels
no such word in there

he finds sadness
too small
he finds love
too big
he finds farewell
too cold

so he closes the dictionary
and says nothing
and hands that silence to the grandson
across the doorstep
like an apple for the road

the grandson went off
into the world
the world is large
and so are dictionaries

thirty years later
another window another suitcase
his own son at the door
and he turns the pages
from A to Z
and back again
and suddenly he knows

this is the word
grandfather kept silent that day

some words are not handed down by mouth
they are inherited
by being missing

---

## 306. THE CARETAKER AND THE LIGHT

Every evening at ten
the caretaker walks the building
floor by floor
switching off hallways
click
switching off cellars
click
locking the bicycle room
counting light bulbs
like sheep

the town is saving money
electricity is dear
DARKNESS IS FREE
says the notice in the lobby
and the caretaker nods
darkness is free
that much is true

only one bulb
above the front door
he leaves burning
every night
all night

mister caretaker
asks the lady from the second floor
why not switch that one off?
think of the savings

and the caretaker shrugs
some people come home late
from the night shift
from the pub
from love
from the world

darkness is cheaper
sure enough
but a lit window
is an address

---

## 307. THE BARBER

The customers talk
they talk about wives
they talk about bosses
they talk about illnesses
their own
their neighbour's
the one they're afraid of

come in have a seat
says the barber
and that is all he says

he cuts
and keeps quiet
the scissors click
the stories fall
to the floor with the hair
grey stories
black stories
the story inherited from a grandfather
the curly story of the blonde from the post office

the barber knows everything about this town
he knows who loves whom
he knows who doesn't anymore
he knows who owes how much to whom
he knows and goes on cutting

in the evening he takes the broom
and sweeps
all the loves all the bosses all the illnesses
into one small pile
and into the bin

silence is his trade
as much as the scissors
and that is why they all come back

hair grows again
and so do stories

---

## 308. THE STOP IN THE FIELDS

The train stops on request
in the middle of the fields
the stop is called Lhotka
but no Lhotka anywhere
no village
no road
no dog

only a board
LHOTKA
and beneath it in smaller letters
REQUEST STOP
and a bench
that nobody repairs
and nobody breaks

the conductor shrugs
why do we still stop here?
the new colleagues ask
no idea
he says
but we stop

in spring the wheat
in summer the wheat
in autumn the stubble
in winter the snow
and the stop still there

once a year
in October
an old lady gets off
with a bunch of flowers
she thanks the conductor
and walks away across the field
straight ahead
as if there were a path
that only she can still see

the train waits
one minute longer than allowed
then moves on

on the line they say
a stop can be abolished
with one stroke of a pen

a sign cannot

---

## 309. A LETTER TO MYSELF

The teacher told the children
write a letter
to yourselves grown up
and the children laughed
grown up how?
and they wrote

Dear me
when you are big
do I have a dog?
can you fly yet?
don't be afraid of the dark
and please buy a red car

The teacher collected the letters
sealed them
put them in a box
and the box in the cupboard
and the cupboard she forgot
for twenty years

After twenty years
she sent them out
to the old addresses
half of which no longer exist
and half the people neither

And grown men and women
open the envelopes
and read in a child's handwriting
their own promises
don't be afraid of the dark
can you fly yet?

It is the only letter
you cannot fail to answer
because the one who asks
is the one you cannot lie to

One man finishes reading
takes his coat
and goes that very day
to buy the dog
he promised himself
twenty years ago

---

## 310. TOMATOES OVER THE FENCE

Two neighbours
and between them a fence
one grows tomatoes
the other apples
and they never said more
than good morning

All summer long
they toss their harvest over the fence
here take some tomatoes
here take some apples
red this way
red that way
and not a word

In July a tomato falls
into the grass by the apple trees
in August an apple falls
into the tomato bed
and nobody minds

The wife asks
why do you give him any
you don't even talk to him?
The man shrugs
we talk
he says
only very quietly

And so the summer goes
tomato
apple
tomato
apple
over the fence
there and back

People say
tear down that fence
be friends at last
But those two know better

The fence is really necessary
where else
would they lay their gifts?
The best trade is the one
where nobody pays
and both men feel
they came out ahead

---

## 311. THE CONDUCTOR IN THE PARK

Every morning he comes to the park
the retired conductor
without tails
without an orchestra
with only his baton
which he kept

He lifts his arms
and he begins
blackbirds come in
now the wind in the leaves
pianissimo
and now the tram in the distance
forte

People pass by
and they smile
an old fool
they say tenderly
and they walk on
but more slowly

Is it raining?
he conducts the rain too
snowing?
the snow as well
he waves the pigeons in
slows down for the clouds
and speeds up for the children

Once he had eighty players
and all of them watched him
in case he made a mistake
and every evening he was afraid

Now he is never afraid
at last he has an orchestra
that plays
even when he isn't looking

The music conducts itself
the blackbird needs no beat
the wind knows no notes
and still they play
in time

The conductor only bows now
he bows to the empty bench
and the bench
full of sunlight
applauds with its leaves

---

## 312. CHESS IN THE PARK

Two old men
on a bench in the park
play chess
a single game
that lasts for years

They think over a move for a week
on Monday one of them comes
looks at the pieces
and leaves
not yet

On Tuesday the other comes
looks
strokes the knight
and leaves
not yet

You play slowly
says a passer-by
No
answer the old men
we play exactly

The king stands in his corner
through so many springs
the pawn waits for the road
that no one will draw
and the rook looks into the distance

Someone says
but that's checkmate
just move
and it's over
They smile
that's exactly why we don't move

Neither of them wants to give mate
mate would mean
the end of the conversation
and the conversation
is all they have left

A draw is a form of friendship
and an endless game
is a form of life
They play on
and the king holds
and the sun goes down
and tomorrow there's another move

---

## 313. THE CROSSING

An old woman
crosses at the crossing
very slowly
one foot
and then the other foot
and in between
a whole life

Behind her a line of cars
and the cars honk
honk
honk
as if a horn
could shorten old age

The woman walks
she doesn't hear
or she hears
and walks just as slowly
because she can't go faster any more
and won't pretend

The first driver
a young man
clenched on his wheel
all at once
switches off his engine

Silence
just one engine less
but you can hear it

Behind him the second switches off
and the third
and the fourth
and the whole street
cuts its engines

And the city hears for the first time
the sound of its own patience
it is the silence
in which she walks
the old woman
and no one needs to carry her
because she walks alone

She reaches the other side
she turns around
and raises her hand
not to wave
just so
like a conductor
who has just hushed the world

---

## 314. THE WEDDING PHOTOGRAPHER

He photographed a thousand weddings
a thousand times he said
a smile please
and the people smiled
because he asked them to

He knew every church
every pew
every mother-in-law
who cries before the bride
and every uncle
who arrives too late

He himself never married
they asked him why
and he said
weddings I have plenty
I have a thousand
and I'm off to the next one

In not one photo is he there
hidden behind the camera
behind the light
behind the order
a smile please
the others smiled

At home in the evening
he looks through a thousand smiles
and not a single one is his
and yet
each one belongs to him a little

Until one day
while looking
he finds a photograph
where the bride is standing
and behind her a mirror

And in the mirror
blurred
there he stands
with the camera instead of a face
at last in one photograph
the only one of a thousand

That is how you photograph happiness
up close
and out of frame

---

## 315. THE BOOK NEVER RETURNED

The librarian has rubber stamps
red ones blue ones violet ones
and books that come back
like swallows
every year

Only one never came back
thirty years
thirty springs thirty winters
in the ledger a small hole
the shape of a book

Out of habit
out of that tenderness called habit
she wrote a reminder
Dear sir
kindly return
what you borrowed
thirty years ago

And the book arrived
by post
worn like the palm of a hand
with a letter inside
Dear madam
I have read it all my life
and I am still not done

The librarian picked up the stamp
RETURNED
and put it down again
she wrote in a new date
borrowed once more
and sent the book back where it came from

Because some books
are not lent for a month
they are lent for a life

---

## 316. THE SPARROW

Every morning
before the coffee
before the paper
crumbs on the windowsill

And the sparrow arrives
punctual as a baker
more punctual than the trains
he pecks looks up pecks
and flies off on sparrow business

The man calls it a visit
the sparrow calls it a window
and that's enough for both of them

One day the sparrow didn't come
the crumbs lay there all morning
all noon all evening
and the man paced the flat
telling himself
it's only a sparrow
it's only a sparrow
the way people tell themselves
it's only a heart

That night he didn't sleep

In the morning he put out twice the crumbs
just in case
just in hope
which is the same thing

And two of them came

The sparrow hadn't come the day before
because he had a reason
and the reason now sat beside him
pecking

Even a sparrow likes to show off
a good window when he's found one

---

## 317. THE ASTRONOMICAL CLOCK

Tourists from Tokyo from Lima from Melbourne
heads tipped back
photograph the clock
the apostles file past
Death rings his little bell
the rooster crows
and a thousand phones remember it

The locals pass underneath
with bags with children with appointments
never looking up
they keep the clock for later
the way you keep for later the sea
and your mother
and your life

Later
is the biggest warehouse in the world
everything fits in there

And one day a local man
who had just missed his tram
stood still among the tourists
and waited
because everyone was waiting

The apostles filed past
Death rang his bell
the rooster crowed
and the man saw the clock
for the first time in his life
after forty years
a hundred metres from home

To see the beauty of home
you must borrow a stranger's eyes
the farthest thing away
is often the nearest

---

## 318. THE ICE CREAM MAN AND THE CHESTNUTS

On the corner by the pharmacy
in summer stands an ice cream man
vanilla strawberry lemon
and a line of children

On the corner by the pharmacy
in winter stands a chestnut man
hot chestnuts in a paper cone
and a line of frozen hands

It is the same man
the same smile
the same hands
only the weather has changed its clothes

People ask him
what are you exactly?
ice cream man or chestnut man?

And he says
I don't sell ice cream
I don't sell chestnuts
I sell balance

In July a little winter
in January a little summer
to each person
whatever they are missing

People think
they are paying for a cone
but they are paying for balance
the whole city pays him
to even out the weather
cheaper than any ministry
and with a smile thrown in

---

## 319. CHINESE WHISPERS

The children sit in a circle on the grass
and the first one whispers
grandma has a white cat

The sentence travels
from ear to ear
a little traveller
changing clothes at every ear

The last child stands up
and says out loud
grandpa sleeps in a kennel made of chocolate

And everyone laughs
they fall in the grass laughing
because the sentence arrived
past all recognition
and that is the whole game

Grown-ups play it too
they play it all their lives
from ear to ear
from bar to bar
from century to century

Apparently he said
apparently she did
apparently that's how it happened

Only when the sentence arrives
nobody stands up
nobody laughs
nobody says
it was only a game

With children it's called Chinese whispers
with grown-ups it's called
gossip
reputation
history

---

## 320. THE MANUSCRIPT

All his life he rewrote
a single manuscript
he crossed out
he began again
he crossed out
he began again
and never sent it off

The publisher waited
his friends kept asking
when will it come out?
Soon
he said
it isn't quite right yet

His wife brought him tea
and never read a word
she respected the door
she respected the silence
she respected the man
who vanished into a paragraph

He grew old over it
his hair went white
like paper
and the manuscript grew
and never came out

When he died
a stack of pages remained
and his wife
for the first time sat down
and began to read

And on every page
there she was
once she was called Marie
once Hélène
once just her
but it was always her

Fifty years he described her
fifty years he was afraid
it wasn't good enough
to show her

The book came out
in an edition of one reader
and it was exactly the reader
it had been written for

---

## 321. LOVE LOCKS

On the bridge hung padlocks
thousands of padlocks
small big rusty golden
and on each one two names
and between the names
a little heart

The city decided
THE LOCKS WILL BE REMOVED
FOR STRUCTURAL REASONS
because love it seems
is too heavy for the bridge

The locksmith came
with his cutters
and he cut
snip
Peter and Jane
snip
Anne and Thomas
snip
someone and someone

He carries them off in a box
to the town hall
love locks
inventory number
three hundred and twelve

But the keys
the keys are long since
at the bottom of the river
the lovers threw them there
so that no one ever
could unlock their love

So the town hall has the locks
and the river has the keys
and they will never meet
because that
was exactly the point

The town hall archives love
under an inventory number
it records it
it weighs it
it files it in a crate
but what is locked
stays locked

---

## 322. THE STARTING PISTOL

The referee raises his arm
he holds a pistol
every morning he loads it
with blank cartridges

On your marks
get set
and he fires

And the people start to run
not away
but forward
they run toward something
they run for something
they run for dear life
and it is only a race

He is the only man in the world
who fires into a crowd
and the crowd thanks him
it is the only gunshot
after which nobody lies down
everybody flies

They pour across the finish line
soaked and happy
and they ask
who won?
and nobody knows
because they all ran

If every gunshot in the world
were only a starting shot
then every war would become
an ordinary Sunday race
and the generals would hand out
medals instead of crosses
and the mothers would wait at the finish
and not at the graveyard

---

## 323. THE WINDOW ACROSS THE STREET

In the morning a wave
in the evening a light switched on
that is how they know each other
for ten years now

He at the left-hand window
she at the right-hand window
between them the street
the tram the pigeons the rain

Good morning window
good night window
they never said each other more
and more was never needed

One day in the shop
at the milk counter
they stood side by side
and did not recognize each other

Excuse me
she said
please go ahead
he said
and that was all

Without the window frame
they looked nothing alike
they were only two people
reaching for the same milk
two bodies with no windows
two strangers in coats

In the evening they went home
switched on the light
and waved to each other
and everything was as it was

Some friendships
need a street between them
they need a frame
they need distance to be close
and if they crossed that street
perhaps they would lose each other
so they would rather wave
from window to window
across a whole life

---

## 324. THE LEAF SWEEPER

He sweeps the whole town
tons of leaves
maples chestnuts limes
everything falls on him

In the morning the pavement is yellow
in the afternoon yellow again
he sweeps and it falls
it falls and he sweeps

A Sisyphus with a broom
says the park keeper
and he laughs
and the leaves keep falling

Useless work?
asks the sweeper
and no one answers
the tree just drops another

Every autumn
one single leaf
he takes home
wipes it on his coat
and slips it into a book

He has a book of autumns
one leaf after another
one year after another
thirty leaves
thirty autumns
between the pages

You cannot sweep the falling
he says to himself at night
the falling can only be recorded
the rest is raked into a heap
and carted away

And so he keeps sweeping
the whole town
just so that he may
once a year
keep a single leaf

---

## 325. THE HOURGLASS

In the souvenir shop
between the postcards and the magnets
stands an hourglass
glass sand and wood

On the tag it reads
LAST ONE ON SALE
and nobody buys it
digital clocks are more accurate

What time is it?
asks the tourist
and he looks at his phone
not at the sand

The hourglass says nothing
time trickles inside it
grain after grain
you can see it fall

It is the only clock in the world
where you can see time
flowing
running low
landing on the bottom

And it is the only clock
you take into your hand
turn over
and it starts again
from the bottom up
tomorrow pouring into yesterday

And that is exactly why nobody wants it
people do not want to see time
much less hold it in their palm
much less turn it over
they are afraid of that one grain
that is falling right now

So it stays in the shop
LAST ONE ON SALE
full of sand
and full of patience
waiting for someone to buy it
and turn it over
and admit
that time is running low

---

## 326. THE MARKET CAT

At the fish market
every morning
the cat is given
a fish by the seller

A sardine or a mackerel
whatever is left
he hands it over the counter
and says good morning madam

The neighbors shake their heads
why feed the thief?
she'll clean out your whole stall
she's a robber with a tail

The seller just waves his hand
and gives the fish anyway
morning after morning
winter summer autumn

And the cat
what she is given
she does not steal
she sits on a crate
and keeps watch

At night when the market sleeps
she prowls around the stall
she hisses at the rats
and chases off the gulls
she is the best guard in the harbor

The neighbors complain
I'm robbed all the time
and yet they have fences
they have locks
they have dogs on chains

You turn an enemy into an ally
with one fish a day
smiles the seller
it's cheaper than a fence
and besides
a fence will never sit on your lap
and purr

---

## 327. INFINITY

The mathematics teacher
writes on the blackboard
one two three
and three little dots

Numbers never end
he tells the class
you can always add one
and one again
and again

Infinity
he writes in capital letters
INFINITY
and underlines it twice

The boys yawn
the girl by the window counts the crows
the bell is about to ring
and infinity interests no one

We can write a million
and add one
we can write a billion
and add one
we will never reach the end
never

The boy in the back row
raises his hand
sir
is that like when I wait for my dad?

And a silence falls over the class
such a silence
that you can hear the chalk
land on the floor

His dad did not come home
from a single shift
two years ago now
and the boy still counts the days
and one more
and one more

Infinity
the teacher wipes it off the board
but from that desk
no one will wipe it away
and for the first time in his whole life
it is truly explained

---

## 328. THE FERRIS WHEEL

The Ferris wheel operator
presses a button
and the town rises
and falls
and rises again

All day he sees the town from above
the roofs the yards the chimneys
the washing lines
the cats on the tin
and the pigeons in the gutters

He knows every roof
he knows who hangs out blue shirts
he knows where a fig tree grew in the courtyard
he knows where they leave the window open
and where they do not

Where are you going mister?
asks the little girl in the car
up
he answers
and then down
and then up again

He has never been
inside any of those houses
he has watched all his life
he has never rung a doorbell
he has never hung out washing
on any of those lines

He knows the town like the birds do
the whole of it
and from nowhere

The people below
know their house
their stairs
their door
and nothing more

He knows everything
and owns nothing
they own one house
and never see the town

And so he stands by the lever
and presses the button
and carries the others up
so that for one moment at least
they may see what he sees
and will never see where he lives

---

## 329. THE CLOAKROOM ATTENDANT

At the philharmonic
behind door number three
sits the cloakroom attendant
among strangers' coats

She hears the music only muffled
through the closed door
the violins are just a rustle
the drum just a knock

Whole evenings
among the furs and overcoats
she hands out numbers
here you are twenty-seven

How was the concert?
asks the cleaning woman
I didn't hear it
says the attendant
but I can tell

I can tell by the coats
by the way people put them on
after cheerful music
they take their coats quickly
they laugh
they all talk at once

But after the beautiful kind
the real kind
they put their coats on slowly
and in silence
as if they were afraid
that with one sleeve
they might break something

The best concerts
I know them for certain
says the attendant
they are the ones
after which nobody hurries for a coat
when I stand alone by the hooks
and wait
and it is quiet
and in that quiet
I heard the whole of it

---

## 330. THE CYCLIST AND THE WIND

In the morning to work
against the wind
in the evening home
against the wind again

How does the wind do it?
curses the cyclist
he pedals and he swears
he swears and he pedals
for ten years now

Damned wind
it leans into me
it pushes me back
it takes my breath
and never blows at my back

For the neighbor it blows at his back
for the car it blows at its back
only for me
always against

And then one morning
the wind does not blow at all
the air stands still
the leaves hang down
the flag on the town hall droops dead

The road is suddenly fast
the bicycle rolls by itself
and it is good for nothing

Where has it gone?
asks the cyclist
and no one answers
only the silence holds the handlebars

The headwind
had been all that time
the proof that he was moving
that he was going somewhere
that he was pedaling forward

It was a companion
that leaned against him
and now he has nothing to lean into
and for the first time in ten years
he wishes
the wind would blow against him again

---

## 331. THE NIGHT ATTENDANT

The all-night filling station
by the motorway
a light in the black field
and one man inside

The cars arrive
the cars drive off
coffee petrol and on they go
nobody stays

Where are you coming from?
nobody answers
where are you going?
nobody knows

They take their coffee in a cup
a full tank
and vanish into the dark
the red lights fading away

The attendant stands behind the glass
he counts the night
one hour two hours three
and the signal blinks amber into the empty

Safe journey
he says to each of them
safe journey
and no one asks him
where he is going

Nowhere
he is going nowhere
he is the only one
who stays
so that the others can pass through

The still point of the motorway
a lighthouse on dry land
an anchor in the asphalt
a light so the others can see
the way out

And when toward dawn
the last car drives away
he understands what only he knows
all the haste of the world
needs someone
who does not hurry
and who shines all night
so the others have somewhere to arrive
and somewhere to leave from

---

## 332. THE DOCTOR AND THE TEDDY BEAR

Morning at the surgery
it smells of alcohol and fear
the little patient on his mother's lap
stares at the needle
like a dragon's tooth

The doctor takes the bear
he sits on the cabinet from spring to winter
Well now little bear
it's only a small pinch ?
and she gives him a shot in the paw

The bear does not cry
the bear looks at the ceiling
the bear is brave
as a bear

You see ?
says the doctor
and the child nods
because whatever the bear can do
so can he

The needle comes and goes
the child doesn't even blink
he is after all as big
as a bear

In the evening when the lamps go out
the bear sits alone on the cabinet
and he is wearing
thirty little plasters
more than all the patients put together

courage cannot be prescribed
courage is vaccinated by example
and the bear is riddled all over
with love

---

## 333. THE CRACKED BELL

In the tower hangs a great bell
and it is cracked
instead of singing
it only wheezes
like an old man

To take it down costs a heap of money
and money there is none
so it hangs on
cracked and proud
above the whole little town

The sexton has a little bell
one that fits in his palm
and with that he now rings noon
ding ding
in a thin voice from near the kitchen

And yet
when it tinkles above the rooftops
people lift their heads
and say
do you hear ? it's noon
and they look up toward the tower

toward the great cracked bell
that says nothing
and not toward the little one
that rings

Nobody looks
where the sound truly came from
everybody looks
where it used to come from

habit keeps ringing
even with a cracked bell
and the bell takes in silence
another's glory
and no one seems to mind
least of all the little bell
which knows what it knows
and tinkles on

---

## 334. THE CHIMNEY SWEEP

Down the street comes the chimney sweep
all black as the night
and people turn to look at him
and clutch at a button

Good day madam
and she shyly touches his shoulder
for luck
and walks on with a smile

A boy from behind
claps him on the back
and runs off
holding the button of his trousers

The chimney sweep smiles
and lets them be
touch me boy
touch me madam
luck comes for free

All day long they touch him
all day long he hands it out
and the soot wears off
only from his coat
never from his smile

In the evening he sits on a step
and he counts
today thirty touches
today thirty pieces of luck
I gave to thirty people

And yet as for me
I am the dirtiest man in the whole town
and the happiest

because luck multiplies by touch
the more of it you give away
the more of it stays on your skin
that is why I am all over soot
and all over happy

---

## 335. THE LIFEGUARD IN WINTER

The pool is closed for winter
a tarp over the deep end
dead leaves on the bottom
where in July
the children were shouting
now the leaves keep quiet
and the lifeguard sits
on his white tower
in a winter coat
whistle around his neck

a passerby stops
and taps his forehead
what are you guarding mister?
there's no water
not a single child
not one drop of summer

and the lifeguard smiles
I'm guarding the water that isn't there
I'm guarding the summer that was
I'm guarding the shouting
still hanging in the air
between the diving board and the sky

NO ENTRY
says the sign on the gate
and the sign is right
you don't walk into summer
in the middle of December

because water too
needs its rest
like you and me
and when the water is resting
somebody has to guard
its peace

---

## 336. THE MATCHES

Twenty years ago
he stubbed out his last cigarette
in a station ashtray
said enough
and it was enough

his lungs breathed easy
his wife breathed easy
his doctor breathed easy
only the coat pocket
stayed faithful
a box of matches
still lives there

what do you keep them for says his wife
you don't smoke
throw them out
he doesn't throw them out
he doesn't know why
until yesterday he didn't know why

last night in the street
dark cold November
a stranger with a cigarette in his mouth
patting his pockets
got a light?

and he had one
he struck a match
two hands cupped around the flame
two faces lit for a second
thanks
don't mention it

giving up smoking
can be done
but giving up fire
kept for other people
never

---

## 337. THE INTERPRETER

From morning till night
she speaks with the words of other people

Tell her that I love her
and she says it
and the other woman blushes
and she translates the blushing too

Sign here
and the company buys the company
and she is the bridge
over which the millions cross
and nothing stays in her hand

You are a liar
one of them shouts
and she says it calmly
for even shouting is translated calmly
that is her whole art

Other people's love
other people's contracts
other people's quarrels
pass through her mouth
and travel on

In the evening she comes home
and she is silent

She opens her mouth
and nobody waits for the translation
her own words
are the only ones in the world
that no one will translate

She sits by the window
and whispers into the dark
I love
I sign
I forgive

and she waits
for someone one day to come
who will interpret her

---

## 338. THE FADED TIMETABLE

At the stop by the edge of the fields
hangs a timetable
bleached by the sun
into a white silence

Once there were numbers there
and hours
and minutes
now there is only a fog of paper
DEPARTURES to nowhere

The office hasn't changed it in years
and people have lost time
and gained time

When does it come ?
asks the new woman
and the old one answers
the one that comes will come
sit down love

So she sits down
and they talk

About grandchildren
about the rain
about the son-in-law who drinks
and the bus sways somewhere out among the fields
and nobody calls it

The most exact timetable
is the one you cannot read

the ones you can read cut waiting up
into my three minutes
and your seven
and each of us stands alone
watch pressed to the nose

whereas here
we wait together
for the one that comes
and it doesn't matter when
because it is our bus
our own shared bus
in no hurry to be anywhere

---

## 339. THE BIRD FEEDERS

Grandfather has shavings in his workshop
and the smell of wood
and patience

He makes bird feeders
little houses with a roof
and at night
when nobody is looking
he hangs them through the park

No label
no name
no signature
just a nail and the dark
and in the morning the feeder on the branch
as if fallen from the sky

The birds don't know whom to thank
and that is the whole point
of charity
the tit doesn't know the giver
and yet it sings
perhaps it sings for that very reason

The neighbours know
they saw him from their windows
the old man with his ladder
at midnight
but they say nothing

because if they told
grandfather would have to confess
and would have to be thanked
and that would spoil everything

So they say nothing
and guard his secret
as he guards the birds

And the park is full of little houses
that come from no one
and full of people
pretending not to know

and it is the loveliest conspiracy
in the world
a conspiracy of goodness
that pretends
it never happened

---

## 340. THE INLAND LIGHTHOUSE

Someone built on the hill
above the beets and the wheat
a lighthouse

White with a red stripe
like on a postcard from the sea
and the sea from here is
a thousand kilometres away

People laughed
what a fool
a lighthouse with no sea
what use is a lighthouse
where there are no ships ?

They laughed in summer
they laughed in autumn
until the fog came

That thick fog off the fields
that swallows the road
swallows the trees
swallows your own two hands

And then on the hill
a light blinked
once twice
and the cars in the fog
lifted their heads like ships

And they found their way home
by the lighthouse with no sea

The sea is not the condition of a lighthouse
that is what those who laughed believed
a lighthouse needs only fog
and someone who is lost

and of those there are plenty everywhere
even a thousand kilometres from the coast
one gets lost among the beets as well
and there too it is good
that somebody shines

---

## 341. THE DESK CLERK

Behind the desk stands the clerk
and he smiles at those who arrive
and instead of saying
welcome to our hotel
he says by mistake
welcome home

The guest with the suitcase stops short
home ?
and something stirs inside him
and he takes the key
and goes upstairs
as if he were going home

The manager hears it
and he frowns
this is a hotel sir
not a home
say welcome to our hotel
as is proper

The clerk nods
yes sir
and the next day again
welcome home

because the heart
cannot be ordered
nor forbidden

And the guests come back
from all over the world they come back
to this hotel
where there is a desk and keys and nothing special

nothing but one man
who by mistake
tells each of them now and then
that word you pay for
which is on no price list
and which even so you pay for
the most

welcome home
he says again
and he goes on being wrong
for it is not a mistake
it is the only truth
he owns in that hotel

---

## 342. THE OLD MAP

Grandfather walks through the city
with the map of his youth
yellow as the autumn
and soft as a handkerchief

The streets have other names
they pulled down the houses
they built over the park
and still he walks straight ahead
following his old lines

Here there was a bakery
he says to the empty window
here they sold bread rolls
for a penny
and it smelled all the way over here

He sees two cities at once
the one that is
and the one that was
and he walks through both
as though he had two pairs of eyes

The young go past him
phone in hand
where am I ?
asks the phone
and it turns round and round

And the grandson gets lost
the sat-nav in his palm
in the middle of his own city
he wanders among the new names

and grandfather finds him
from memory
by a house that no longer stands
come along boy
here I know every stone
even the ones they carried away

for a city can be pulled down
but not the one in the head
and the only map that never grows old
is the one you carry inside

---

## 343. THE BALLOON SELLER

At the fair among the carousels
stands the balloon seller
and he holds a whole sky in his hand
red yellow blue

The children buy them
and the children let them go
the fingers are small
and the string is thin
and the balloon wants to go up

And then that crying
mummy it flew away
and the mother
never mind darling
we'll buy a new one

The seller sees it every day
a hundred balloons
a hundred little tears
and he smiles
and he goes on selling

But in the evening
when the fair falls quiet
and the lights go out
and the children sleep and dream of balloons

he takes one
the last one
and on purpose
he lets it go

For all the ones the children
never meant to let go
his flies off
up into the dark
to catch the others

Up there by now there must be
a whole flock of them
red yellow blue
full of the crying of children
that turns up there into stars

and somebody has to look after them
so why not him
the shepherd of runaway balloons
who every evening
adds one more to the flock

---

## 344. THE DENTIST

The dentist looks into mouths all day long
open mouths
mouths full of fear
and he knows the pain
before the x-ray shows it

he knows it by the eyes in the waiting room
by the hands that clutch the handbag
by the foot that taps against the floor
open wide please
and talk to me

all day he looks into the pain
he drills it fills it pulls it out
and the pain goes home cured
home to its lunch
home to its children

but there is one pain
his surgery cannot cure
it arrives with healthy teeth
it is an old woman
she comes every month

she sits down opens her mouth
and he looks
your teeth are fine madam
teeth like porcelain
there's nothing wrong

but she stays sitting
in the chair as if in church
so he says
open your mouth
and talk to me

and she talks
of the husband who left by the door
of the son who left by the train
of the cat who left by the window
and he holds the little mirror and listens

the worst pains in his surgery
have healthy teeth
and the only cure is
open your mouth madam
and talk to me

---

## 345. THE LITTLE TREE IN JANUARY

In January the trees lie by the bins
on the pavement on the snow
still with their silver threads
still with one forgotten bauble
looking out at the street like an eye

a week ago they were family
standing warm in the living room
they shone they smelled sweet they held the gifts
the children danced around them
and said our little tree our own

now they lie by the bins
and the children walk to school
and wave to them like old friends
bye little tree bye
the way you wave to an uncle at the station

the trees do not wave
the trees lie
shedding needles along the pavement
small green footsteps
the way Christmas walked out

the bin men come on Tuesday
and carry off the whole of December
loaded in a single heap
the silver threads and all the rest
into the big dark lorry

but one neighbour
had bought a tree with its roots
in a pot like a dog
and Christmas never choked it in a stand
it simply lived by the window

and in January he took a spade
and planted it in the yard
the only man on the street
who for the holidays had invited not a guest
but a neighbour

---

## 346. THE DRIVING INSTRUCTOR

All day he teaches strangers to drive
into first gear
let the clutch out slowly
mirror signal and off we go
all day the same

he taught the butcher's daughter to reverse
he taught the baker to brake
he taught the barber to turn left
he taught the whole town to park
he taught everyone every road

he sits on the right with his second pedal
his hand near the handbrake
and a little nervous he says
easy now just easy
you've almost got it

down at the crossing a policeman waves
and greets him like an old friend
because he taught him twenty years ago
that policeman too
and the one who drives the ambulance now

the whole town knows him at the wheel
the whole town he has carried
hand to hand year to year
the tram driver the bus driver the cabbie
all of them his pupils

in the evening he parks the school car
locks it checks the handbrake
and walks home
and they all honk and wave at him
maestro get in we'll give you a lift

he shakes his head he smiles
and goes on foot
because he has no car himself
he never had one
whoever taught the town to drive
knows best how the town drives

---

## 347. THE PROMPTER

All her life she whispers
from the little box below the footlights
hidden under the boards of the stage
only her head between two worlds
a voice without a face

she knows every part in every play
Juliet and Hamlet
the king and the beggar
she knows the first line and the last
and above all the one the actor forgot

the actors stand in the light
in costume and in applause
and beneath them she whispers
the next word please the next word
and they speak it as their own

no one has ever seen her
no one claps for the prompter's box
the programmes do not name her
she plays in a murmur madam from nowhere
all her life in the third person

she loves the ones who do not quite hear her
and saves them every evening
when they fall silent and fear the silence
she is always there
prompting them to go on living

and at her last curtain call
the old actors stand in a row
and one of them turns
points his hand down to the prompter's box
and the spotlight drops into it for the first time

and there she sits dazzled
seen for the first time after so many years
a whole career in a whisper
so she takes a bow
and whispers to the audience thank you

---

## 348. THE WELL

In the middle of the square stands a well
and on it hangs a sign
WATER NOT DRINKABLE
and across the bucket a grate
and across the grate a lock

the council closed it
with a stamp and a by-law
the well is forbidden
the well is dangerous
the well is officially dead

but people still come
and slip their fingers through the grate
and drop a coin down
and over this water no one may drink
they whisper their wishes

let him come back
let her get well
let him say yes to me
let the children write from abroad
let this at last pass over

the coins fall into the dark
they chime on the stone and on each other
a small rain of copper and silver
wish after wish year after year
at the bottom a whole town piles up

and one day the council comes
with a stamp and a pail
to remove that improper water
and hauls up the bucket full
of coins instead of water

and counts them from the bottom up
and finds there is just enough
exactly enough to mend the well
so it pays for a new pump
out of the wishes it had banned

a wish cannot be forbidden
not by a sign nor by a lock
it falls into the dark patiently
and in the end
it saves itself

---

## 349. THE TWO DOORBELLS

On the flat's door two bells
one new and white
NOVAK in big letters
and the other beside it
faded past reading

the new tenant moved in
with his boxes and his bed
and he saw the other bell
that name that is no longer a name
only a grey shadow where the letters were

he could have unscrewed it
he could have pasted his own over it
he could have changed the whole plate
but he left it there
not even knowing why

and people rang for NOVAK
friends and the neighbour and the postman
and he opened and he laughed
and the other bell stayed silent
a bell for no one

until one day the postman rang
holding a letter in his hand
and reading the faded name
hesitant syllable by syllable
does he still live here?

and the new tenant looked
at the envelope and at that name
he could have said it's a mistake
he could have said he doesn't live here anymore
and shut the door

but he said yes
give it to me
I'll take it
whoever inherits an address
inherits its past as well

and someone must take in the mail
even for those who have gone
even for those who no longer have a bell
that anyone could ring
only a faded name at the door

---

## 350. THE SWEATERS

The grandmother knits sweaters
by the window by the lamp
stitch after stitch row after row
she knits for her grandchildren abroad
last seen when they were small

she knits a green one for Tony
a blue one for Annie
and into each she knits
stay warm don't you catch cold
write and tell me how you are

then she wraps the sweater in paper
ties it with string
writes the address in big letters
and the post carries it across the borders
across the mountains across that whole piece of world

but the grandchildren grow
they grow faster than she knits
while the needles click
the boys shoot up a hand's width
the girls a whole sleeve

and so the sweaters always come
a size too small
the sleeves too short the collar tight
the grandmother had counted last year's child
and this year's child arrived

and the parents say never mind
next time we'll send her the measurements
next time let her knit a size up
and the children squeeze into the tight wool
and grumble and laugh

but they wear them all the same
tugging at the too-short sleeves
because a grandmother's warmth
should be felt right where it pinches
and what is a little too small
is worn closer to the heart

---

## 351. THE STATION CLOCK

Under the great station clock
people wait
they wait for loves
they wait for trains
they wait for homecomings

they stand and look up
at the big white face with its hands
when now when now when now
and the clock goes on
the clock alone does not wait

a man waits for a woman with a suitcase
a woman waits for the man who promised
a mother waits for her son from the army
and they all look up
at the clock instead of at each other

the hand jumps and the heart jumps with it
four minutes more
two more
one more
and under the clock two hundred solitudes

the clock knows exactly what time it is
how much delay how much life
how much waiting in a single station
it ticks above the heads
indifferent and precise

and one day it stops
it simply stops at half past two
and before the stationmaster comes with his ladder
the people below no longer know the time
so for the first time they look down

and for the first time they look at each other
at that woman beside them that man
who has been waiting here just as long
and they say are you waiting too?
and time had stopped just in time

and two waitings met
under the stopped clock
and when the stationmaster set it going again
it was already too late
they were already looking at each other

---

## 352. THE SWIFTS

The building firm is to repair the school
the façade the gutters the plaster
the crane has come and the cement mixer
the workmen have come with their helmets
and the papers with every permit

but up there under the cornice
where the new plaster was meant to go
there are nests
small mud nests of swifts
and inside them summer is peeping

and so the work stops
because the law is the law
and the swift is protected
and the work must wait
until the young ones have flown

the crane stands with its hook in the air
the mixer is silent the scaffold waits
and the workmen sit on the kerb
eating their lunch and looking up
when now when now will they fly

the site foreman has a deadline
he has a fine for every day
he has a phone that keeps ringing
where is the school where is it finished
and he holds the binoculars and looks

and one morning it happens
a young swift climbs to the edge
tries its wings is afraid for a second
and then throws itself into the air
and does not fall and flies

and the workmen on the kerb applaud
and the foreman writes in his logbook
delay six weeks
reason the swifts
the most beautiful delay in the history of the town

the concrete can wait
the wings cannot
and the only deadline you must never miss
is the one when someone very small
above your head must learn to fly

---

## 353. THE SILENCE IN THE LIBRARY

On the library door hangs a sign
SILENCE PLEASE
and the woman at the desk puts a finger to her lips
and everyone walks on tiptoe
as in a room where someone is sleeping

but it is a mistake
it is the loudest place in town
louder than the square on market day
louder than the station
only it is heard with the eyes

here a thousand voices speak at once
poets dead a hundred years speak
kings and murderers speak
lovers long since parted speak
and complain and laugh and cry out

you open a book and the talking begins
here is the storm here is the war
here someone whispers I love you
here a ship goes down and no one hears
except you with your finger on the line

the woman at the desk knows it best
that is why she makes this silence
not so that people should be quiet
but so that those may be heard
who can no longer speak any other way

hush she says hush
not that she wants silence
she wants everyone to be able to speak
the dead and the living all at once
and that is only possible in silence

and so the library is full of uproar
full of singing and weeping and laughter
under the sign that says SILENCE PLEASE
and silence is not the ban on speaking
silence is the condition
so that at last everyone can

---

## 354. THE PIANO AT AUCTION

In the auction hall a piano
an old piano
black as sorrow
the auctioneer calls three thousand
who says three thousand?
no one
two thousand
no one
one thousand
people look down at their catalogs
five hundred
no hand goes up
the auctioneer wipes his forehead
one hundred
no one
then for the hauling away
he says tired
who will take it off our hands?

at the back a hand goes up
the big hand of a mover
I'll take it
this man carried pianos all his life
up the stairs
down the stairs
to the fifth floor with no elevator
and never once touched a single key

he brings it home
he sets it by the window
and in the evening
his little daughter lifts the lid
and plays her first song

now this piano is beyond price
the price stayed in the hall
the worth came home
and the two of them meet
only when the little girl plays

---

## 355. THE PHONE BOOK

An old man keeps hidden
the last phone book of the town
a thick book of names
every evening he leafs through it
under the lamp
his finger down the lines
Miller Newman Baker
here lived Emil
here sang Marie
here Mister Weiss kept his shop

half the names no longer live
the numbers no longer exist
you call and a voice answers
this number has been disconnected
or no one answers at all
just long empty tones

his neighbor tells him
throw it out
it's only old paper
who calls a phone book these days?

but the old man won't throw it out
it's the only book
where they are all together
the living and the dead
on the same page
arranged in alphabetical order

an address book and a graveyard in one
he says to himself
and turns another page
and keeps on calling
not by telephone
by memory
hello Emil can you hear me?
and Emil picks up every time

---

## 356. THE BET ON THE RAIN

Two farmers all the dry summer
stand at the fence and argue
when the rain will come
Thursday says the first
no Sunday says the second
and they bet a beer
one single beer
that's the whole stake

the field cracks with thirst
the corn has dropped its leaves
the well only rasps now
and every day they meet again
so is it coming that rain?
we'll see
and they look at the sky
the way you look at a debtor

and one morning
with no warning
no Thursday no Sunday
the rain arrives
and it pours
it pours on them both the same
on both fields
because the rain doesn't read bets
and knows no fence between them

they stand under the same gutter
soaked and happy
so who won?
both of them
who lost?
both of them
and they drink that beer together
because rain never belongs to just one

---

## 357. THE HOLE IN THE PHOTOGRAPH

The granddaughter leafs through the album
old photographs
weddings name days Sunday lunches
and suddenly
in the middle of one picture
a hole
someone was cut out with scissors

grandma who was missing here?
grandma says nothing
she only waves her hand
someone we don't want anymore

but the hole stayed
the hole has the shape of a man
the shoulders
the head
an arm laid around someone
the scissors cut out the face
the place they cannot cut out

and it's strange
the whole family in the photo is smiling
the uncle the aunt the cousins
and no one remembers them
it's the hole they remember

because scissors do not know how to forget
you cut out a face
and exactly that man remains
only empty
and everyone looks
at the spot where he stood

grandma and do you miss him?
grandma is silent a long time
and then she says
every day
I reach him my hand through that hole

---

## 358. THE ANCHOR

The tavern THE ANCHOR
stands far inland
a hundred kilometers from the sea
the nearest water is the village pond
on the wall hangs a ship's wheel
and an old net
and a lighthouse painted on a napkin

people come here
who have never seen the sea
and speak of it like sailors
today the wind's out of the southwest
there'll be a storm
says the baker
who never once left the county

and they drink rum
and they sing of harbors
and the mast here is the broom behind the door
and the sea
the sea is under the tables
in the talk
in the singing
in the way they look out the window

hey captain where do we sail tonight?
where it's deeper
answers the landlord
and pulls another beer

the sea is there
where it is spoken of
the anchor holds the tavern
so it won't drift away
and the tavern holds the dreams
so they won't drown

---

## 359. THE BROKEN RADIO

Grandfather's old radio
picks up only one station now
the others went dark one by one
you turn the knob
and nothing
only that one is left

it's all the same everywhere anyway
says grandfather
and waves his hand
songs commercials weather
tomorrow like today
and today like yesterday

he sits by it every morning
listening to that one station
like an old friend
who never moved away

and then one day they shut it down
they sell the frequency
and out of the radio comes
a hiss
only a hiss
a shoreless sea of hiss

the neighbor says
come on throw it out
it doesn't play anymore

but grandfather goes on sitting
and listening
now it's playing the sea
he says
and he smiles
the hiss is the last station
that runs no commercials
and plays all the time
and for everyone
and for free

---

## 360. THE KEYHOLE

The child kneels at the door
of the locked room
and looks through the keyhole
inside the grown-ups are preparing something
paper rustles
glass clinks
they whisper
hush so he won't hear

what are they doing in there?
the child can't see the whole room
he sees only a slice
a piece of table
a hand with a ribbon
and a shadow on the wall

the grown-ups lock the doors
and carry the keys in their pockets
heavy keys on a ring
the key is for hands
the hole is for eyes
and eyes cannot be locked

you can lock a room
but not curiosity
you can lock a gift
but not the waiting
you can lock a secret
but not that small eye at the hole

and do you know what the child sees?
he sees his birthday
wrapped up in tomorrow
and he pretends he knows nothing
because the finest gift
is the one
you were allowed to see in secret
and can still marvel at all the same

---

## 361. THE SEED IN THE POCKET

In spring the woman puts on
her winter coat one last time
before hanging it in the wardrobe
and in the pocket
among crumbs and a tram ticket
she finds a seed
a small hard seed
and can't remember where it's from

where was I then?
in the park? by the river?
in someone's garden?
the coat says nothing
the pocket says nothing
the seed says nothing either

so she plants it in a pot
on the balcony
she waters it
and she waits
and one morning
a green leaf grows
then a second
then a flower

and it's a flower
that no one knows
not her
not the neighbor
not the gardener on the corner
what kind of flower is that?
nobody knows

we carry whole gardens in our pockets
and never know it
every old coat
is a secret flowerpot
and what we have forgotten
blooms on its own in spring
and asks
do you know me?

---

## 362. THE SWALLOWS ON THE WIRES

Before the great departure
the swallows settle on the wires
five electric wires
like the five lines of a stave
and each swallow
is a note

below them stands the composer
notebook in hand
and copies them down
this one's a quarter note
that one a half
the one up top is a high C
and the one that flew off
was a rest

people pass by
what are you writing there?
they ask
and he points to the sky
notes
sitting on the wires
you only have to copy them

and then comes the north wind
and the swallows lift
the whole score at once
and fly away to Africa
and the wires are left empty
five empty lines

but the melody has stayed
in his notebook
because music
cannot be forbidden to leave
and he knows
that in spring
they will settle differently
and a new movement will begin

---

## 363. THE MAN WHO SAID HELLO

In the town where people don't greet each other
there's a man
who greets everyone
good day madam
good day sir
good morning
lovely day isn't it?

a madman people say
and they hurry on
eyes to the ground
collar turned up
who greets anyone these days?

some out of politeness answer
softly
almost against their will
good day
and at once they're ashamed
of that smile
that slipped out of them

he goes on greeting
for years
every morning at his corner
by the bakery
good day good day good day
the way you sow
even if nothing grows

and then one day
he's no longer there
and people pass by
and don't know why
but they greet each other
good day
at his corner
by the bakery
out of a habit
that one madman began

politeness is contagious
it needs only one
patient carrier

---

## 364. THE LAST POEM

The poet has to write a poem
and nothing comes to him
he looks out the window
a cloud drifts to the right
then another to the left
he tidies the desk
lines up the pencils by size
feeds the cat
the cat writes no poems
the cat sleeps

he makes coffee
counts the pigeons on the sill
one two three
and the fifth flies off
before he finishes counting
he waters the plant
turns the calendar page
sharpens a pencil
he won't use anyway

and still nothing
an empty page
white as winter
poet where is your poem?
tomorrow he says
tomorrow for sure

and in the evening
when he blows out the lamp
he finds
that the poem was about all of this
about the cloud to the right
about the coffee
about the cat that sleeps
about the fifth pigeon
that left too soon

poems are not written
poems happen
when you know how to wait
and that is how this one came to be

---

## 365. THE LAST PENCIL

In the case full of pens
lies one last pencil
wooden
chewed
quiet

The pens gleam
the pens have colours
the pens are sure of themselves
the pen never hesitates
the pen writes straight in fair copy

The tablet lights up
the tablet crosses nothing out
the tablet remembers everything
every mistake
forever

And the pencil
the pencil is short
the pencil grows shorter still
the pencil is ashamed

But when the child slips
when a word comes out wrong
he reaches for the pencil
always the pencil

Because the pen announces
here is where you were wrong
and leaves it standing for all time

Whereas the pencil says nothing
the pencil turns around
and with its other end
gently
takes the error away

The last pencil in the classroom
is the only one
allowed to be wrong

---

## 366. THE KEY WITHOUT A LOCK

In the drawer among the buttons
and the old stamps
lies a key
heavy
brass
alone

The lock is long gone
the house is long gone
the door is long gone
and the street
and the town too perhaps

People say
throw it out
it is good for nothing
it unlocks nothing
it locks nothing
it shuts nothing

But a hand takes it up
a hand weighs it
a hand puts it back
in the drawer
among the buttons

Because this key
does not open a door

this key
opens the doors that used to be

the kitchen where they laughed
the threshold the bride was carried across
the creak in the night of coming home late

The key is not for unlocking
the key is for remembering

That is why you do not throw it away
the key to a house that has fallen
is the only key
that truly opens anything

---

## 367. THE LEAKY POT

The housewife has a pot
old
of tin
and in its side a little hole

Every morning to the well
every morning back again
and on the way home
drip
drip
drip

The neighbour says to her
why don't you throw it out?
you lose half the water
before you reach your door

The housewife says nothing
the housewife carries
the pot is ashamed
I am faulty
I am leaky
I bring back not even the half

And the housewife walks
and the water drips
and the pot weeps the whole way home

Until one summer
she showed it the path

Down its side of the road
flowers had bloomed
blue ones
white ones
yellow ones

on the other side
nothing but dust

You watered them
she told it
all that time
you spent ashamed of your hole

The pot does not know it is generous
it thinks it only leaks

---

## 368. THE UNFINISHED LETTER

In the drawer of the desk
there is a letter
half written
the same for years

My dear
it says
I wanted to tell you that

and then nothing
only blankness
only white
only half a sentence

The man takes it out now and then
the man reads it over
I wanted to tell you that
and he lifts the pen
and he sets the pen down

Tomorrow
he says to himself
tomorrow I'll finish it

And the drawer closes
and the year closes
and another year

His friends advise him
finish it
send it
or throw it away

But he knows what he knows

Finished letters
lie

they claim a man has said it all
that he already knows
that he has understood the whole of it
and set the final stop

His stayed true
half-done
like him
I wanted to tell you that
and he still does not know what

---

## 369. THE OLD CHAIR BY THE ROAD

Someone carried out a chair
and set it by the road
beside the bus stop
FOR COLLECTION

One leg shorter
the seat worn hollow
the back missing a rung

It was a kitchen chair
the father sat on it
the mother sat on it
the morning sat on it over coffee
a whole life long

Now it belongs to no one

So the woman with the shopping bags sat down
waiting for the bus
the boy with the satchel sat down
the grandfather sat down
who can no longer stand for long

In the morning the bricklayer sits there
in the evening the lovers sit there
at night the rain sits there

The bin men came
they looked at it
and left it standing

It is still needed
they said

The chair never had so many guests
as now
now that it belongs to no one

They threw it out
and by that gave it
all the people in the world

---

## 370. THE STOPPED WATCH

A man wears on his wrist
a wristwatch
that is stopped
stopped for years
always at ten past ten

He does not wind it
he does not change the battery
he does not have it mended

The hands do not move
the small one is stopped
the big one is stopped
the second hand as well

And yet
when in the street they ask him
excuse me what time is it?

the man lifts his eyes
looks at the church tower
and answers
it is quarter to five
exactly right

Then why wear that watch
if it doesn't go?
they ask him

And the man covers his wrist with his hand

The time
he says
the towers give me that
there are plenty of them everywhere

This watch was given to me
by someone
who has no time left at all

I don't wear it for the hour
I wear it for the hand
that used to fasten it on

---

## 371. THE LAST COIN

It was a currency
that had ceased to be legal tender
a decree had said so
from midnight IT IS WORTHLESS

People carried the coins to the bank
people exchanged them
people forgot them

Only one remained
in the pocket of an old coat
the last coin
worn
rubbed smooth
round

Once it bought bread
once it bought milk
once it bought the cinema ticket
where they first sat side by side

Now it buys nothing
now it is only metal
now it is only a little disc

The man takes it in his palm
the man weighs it

And it weighs
more than before
much more

When it had value
it was light
it ran from hand to hand
barely warmed

Now that it is worthless
it has stayed
and grown heavy with remembering

They abolished its value by decree
its weight they did not
that no government abolishes

---

## 372. THE MIRROR IN THE JUNK SHOP

In the junk shop between the lamps
and the old clocks
hangs a mirror
in a cracked frame
tarnished at the edges

The dealer praises it
this mirror
madam
remembers

it remembers countesses
it remembers brides
it remembers faces
a hundred years of faces

Come and see
who ever stood before it

An old woman came
she wanted to see herself young
she looked
and saw an old woman

A soldier came
he wanted to see his father
he looked
and saw a soldier

A child came
it looked
and a child laughed back

The mirror showed
not a single face that had been
not one

it always showed
only the face
that stood before it now

And the dealer was mistaken
the mirror remembers nothing
nothing at all

that is why it is the only
honest historian in the world
it never lies about what was
because it remembers none of it

---

## 373. THE UMBRELLA TURNED INSIDE OUT

On the street corner
the wind caught an umbrella
and turned it inside out

The ribs stuck out
the cloth flung up
the owner swore

You useless umbrella
he said
you're good for nothing now
you don't shelter me any more
you don't fend off the rain

And he made to throw it in the bin
soaked
broken
inside out

But the rain kept falling
and the umbrella turned this way
suddenly did not fend off the water

it gathered it

Into that bowl of ribs and cloth
the rain came down
and the sky shone in it
birdsong rained down
a piece of cloud rained down
a child's mirror for paper boats rained down

Passers-by stopped
looked inside
as into a spring

All its life
it had driven the rain away
and no one had ever stopped beside it

It took only the gale to turn it inside out
and for the first time
it caught something

---

## 374. THE CEREMONIAL SCISSORS

They brought them on a velvet cushion
large
gilded
gleaming
the ceremonial scissors

The sign read
GRAND OPENING

A gentleman in a suit came
smiled at the cameras
took the scissors
and cut the ribbon

snip

Applause
bouquets
speeches

And then they wrapped the scissors
in silk
and shut them in a display case
behind glass
where it says DO NOT TOUCH

Ordinary scissors at home
cut all their lives
cloth
paper
hair
nails
the string on a parcel

They make dresses
cut out a picture
take up a pair of trousers

And these ceremonial ones
the most glorious scissors in the world
never sewed a thing

they cut one ribbon
and went into the cupboard

To open and to vanish
that is their whole biography

---

## 375. THE STATION DOG

Every day at noon
he comes to the station
sits down on the platform
between the suitcases and the tickets
and waits

The train whistles
the doors slide open
people embrace
people pass each other by
and the master does not come

The master did not come yesterday
the master did not come last year
the master has not come for so many winters
that even the benches
have forgotten his name

The stationmaster brings him bread
the old woman with her knitting strokes him
the conductor knows him by now
So little dog
nothing again?

But the dog does not wag his tail
for the master
the dog wags his tail
when the train blows its horn

He is not waiting for the master
that was only what we believed
he is waiting for the noon train
faithfulness is a habit
more beautiful than the reason it lost

---

## 376. THE CAT WHO CHOSE

One morning
she simply came
she sat down on the doorstep
as if she had lived there forever
and she stayed

No one had invited her
no one had bought her
no one carried her in a basket
she walked up the street
she nosed the gate open
and she decided

Here there will be the window
here there will be the cushion
here there will be that hand
that will stroke me
whenever I please

The man says that's my cat
the woman says that's our cat
the children say she belongs to us
and the cat half closes her eyes
and stays silent the way masters stay silent

For she belongs to no one
she has only permitted
the house to belong to her
and whoever thinks he owns a cat
has by the cat been filed
among the warm furniture

---

## 377. THE SPARROW AND THE STATUE

On the square stands a general
of bronze
his hand outstretched
somewhere ahead
toward a battle that is no more

He points to the assault
he points to the glory
he points the direction
that no one has taken in ages
and no one looks that way

A sparrow came
tiny and grey
he inspected that hand
weighed it the way you weigh a branch
and said to himself this will do

He carried a straw
he carried a tuft
he carried a feather of spring
and in the general's open palm
he built his nest

The passersby laugh
look soldier
your grand gesture
now holds four eggs
and for the first time it is good for something

All his life he pointed to the battle
now he holds a life
and glory is never so becoming
as when upon its head
a sparrow comes to sit

---

## 378. THE FISH WHO SAW THE SKY

He swam in the shoal
the way a shoal swims
to the left and the right
up and down
and always in the water

Water above
water below
water on the left
water on the right
water with no ceiling

One day he leapt
not knowing why
he jumped clear of the surface
and for a single heartbeat
he saw

Blue upon blue
light without water
a cloud white and silent
a sun that does not drip
and air you do not drink

Then he fell back
wet and dumb
and swam to the shoal
I saw the sky
I saw the sky he cried

The fish turned round
what sky
what is the sky
and they swam on left and right
Each of us has his moment up there
that he can never prove to those below

---

## 379. THE CAROUSEL HORSE

A wooden horse
a red saddle
a golden mane
mouth open for the gallop
and legs forever in the air

He gallops
he gallops from morning
he gallops till evening
he gallops all his life
and arrives nowhere

Behind him goes a horse
before him goes a horse
beside him the barrel organ clatters
always the same song
always the same round

Where are you running little horse
asks the little girl
clutching the mane
where are you running so fast
are we there yet?

The horse does not answer
the horse only gallops
and the little girl laughs
because they will arrive nowhere
and that is exactly it

Children love him
for exactly the reason
that he arrives nowhere
sometimes it is beautiful to run without a goal
while the barrel organ plays

---

## 380. THE BUTTERFLY OF A SINGLE DAY

In the morning he was born
from a crumpled little sack
he spread his wings
for the first and the last time
and did not know it

He flew out into the garden
where everything was new
the grass was an ocean
the fence was the end of the world
and the sun was eternity

At noon he fell in love
with a white she-butterfly
they danced above the cabbages
for endless hours
three perhaps four

In the afternoon he drank from a rose
in the afternoon he slept in the shade
in the afternoon he wondered
where have I been all this long time
why does life pass me by so fast

He did not know it was evening
he did not know it was the end
he thought tomorrow again
and that tomorrow was now
and that now was enough for him

He lived a whole life
in a single afternoon
and it was enough
length is not the measure
he who knows not when he ends lives whole

---

## 381. THE ROOSTER WHO CROWED TOO LATE

The rooster on the fence
his red comb
proud as a flag
opened his beak
and it was too late

The sun was already over the shed
the sun already warmed the yard
the sun already woke the flies
when the rooster at last
crowed

The farmers grumble
what use is a rooster like that
who sleeps through the morning
who comes crowing
when you can see anyway

The neighbour woman laughs
sell him for the soup
a rooster should crow before the sun
and not trail after it
like a guest arriving late

But the rooster knows
the rooster always knew
the sun came up even without me
the sun needs no alarm
the sun rises on its own

The rooster does not wake the sun
the rooster only greets it
the world gets up by itself
it only wants
someone to welcome it

---

## 382. THE SPIDER IN THE LIBRARY

In the corner of the library
between the dictionary and the wall
lives a spider
tiny and silent
and no one drives her out

The cleaning woman sees her
every morning
she wipes the dust
she sweeps the crumbs
but leaves the web alone

Let her be madam director
begs the cleaning woman
she will harm no one
she only sits there
and waits

The director shrugs
a spider in the library
where there are only rare books
what does she catch there
flies are not rare

But the cleaning woman knows
every night
when the doors are shut
from the open books
words fly out

Words no one has read
words that escaped their sentences
and the spider catches them
in her silent web
every library needs a hunter of what got away

---

## 383. THE PIGEON WITH NO MESSAGE

The last carrier pigeon
grey and grave
takes off from the loft
like every morning
and flies

He flies over the river
he flies over the chimneys
he flies over the wires
where the telegraph runs
that made him obsolete

Once he carried loves
once he carried orders
once he carried the war is over
and people wept
and people laughed

Now he has at his leg
no little note
no scroll
no word
he flies empty

Where are you going little pigeon
asks the wind in the wires
you carry nothing
your route is useless
the telegraph is faster

But an empty pigeon
is a message too
he flies to say
I am still here someone is still waiting
hold on

---

## 384. THE BEE IN WINTER

She woke too early
far too early
in the middle of winter
someone it seemed to her
had said it is spring already

She flew out of the hive
into the white world
where there was no flower
where there was no honey
where there was only frost

She looked for a dandelion
she looked for grass
she looked for clover
she looked for at least a daisy
and found nothing

The street was empty
the garden was dead
the tree was only a skeleton
and the wind said
go home little fool

But at the bus stop
on a wall
hung a poster
and on the poster a meadow
and on the meadow a flower

The bee alighted
on the painted flower
and she was happy
sometimes what saves us for a moment
is even the picture of what is not yet

---

## 385. THE TREE THAT GREW CROOKED

Against the wall grew a tree
and the wall was high
and the sun was behind it

so the tree bent
a little
then more
then all of it

all its life it reached
sideways
toward the light
the wall stole from it

the neighbors said
that tree is crooked
that tree is sick
that tree grows wrong

and the tree said nothing
and kept on growing
crooked
toward the sun

then the workmen came
and tore the wall down
in one afternoon
it was gone

and the sun spilled out
everywhere
and nothing anymore
held anything back

but the tree
the tree stayed crooked
bent toward the place
where the wall had stood

we carry our bends
from walls
that fell long ago
and we ask why we don't stand up straight

---

## 386. THE LAST LEAF

On the tree stayed one leaf
the last
in November
all the others already down

the wind blew
and the leaf held on
the rain fell
and the leaf held on

people walked by
and pointed
look
that one's afraid to fall

that one clings out of fear
they said
that one won't come down
that one fears the ground

but the leaf was not afraid
the leaf stood watch
the last sentry
on the bare branch

for it knew
the leaf knew
when I fall
winter will come
and cover everyone

so it stayed
the longest
alone up there
so winter would come at no one's back

and one morning
it was down as well
and the snow arrived
and there was silence

and no one knew
that all that time
someone had held them
just before the winter

---

## 387. THE SEED IN THE ASPHALT

In the middle of the car park
where the cars stand
and oil drips from them
a seed broke through the asphalt

a small blade of grass
a green comma
in the black plain
between the wheels

people built this road
mixed the concrete
rolled it flat
smoothed every seam

NOTHING WILL GROW HERE ANYMORE
they said
and went home
satisfied

and autumn came
and winter came
and the asphalt held
hard and black

but underneath
in the dark
the seed waited
and was in no hurry

and in spring
one morning
in the crack there was
a green comma

people walked by
and cursed
a weed
even here grows a weed

and they did not know
that concrete has the last word
only until spring
and then the grass speaks

---

## 388. THE RIVER THAT CHANGED ITS COURSE

After the flood
one night
the river found itself
a new bed

across the meadow
where it had never flowed
now it flows
as if it had always been there

in the morning people came
with maps
and the maps said
the river is over there

but the river was here
calm
wide
and not even apologizing

this is wrong
the mapmakers cried
the river flows where it shouldn't
the river made a mistake

and they measured
and drew
and crossed out the old lines
and they were miserable

but the river
the river asked no one anything
water doesn't ask the maps
water makes its own map

the one who flows
corrects the world
with its own body
and lets the paper catch up to what is real

and the maps were redrawn
because the river was right
the river is always right
the river is the line

---

## 389. THE CLOUD THAT DID NOT RAIN

Over the parched land
floated a cloud
heavy with water
full of rain

and below
the fields were cracking
and the trees were thirsty
and the people looked up

now
they said
now it will rain
the cloud is here
the cloud will save us

and they raised their palms
and waited
and prayed
each in their own way

but the cloud floated on
silent
over their heads
and let down not a drop

and it crossed the mountains
and beyond the mountains
where no one called it
where no one cursed it

there it rained
generously
on the forest
that needed no rain

we give exactly where
we are not wanted
and we pass over
the thirsty beneath us

and we never know
why the cloud
saves its water
for a foreign land

---

## 390. THE STONE ON THE CAIRN

At the start of the path
the pilgrim picked up a stone
small
grey
ordinary

and carried it up
the whole way
in his palm
and the stone weighed

why does he carry it
the others asked
it's only a stone
throw it away
spare your shoulders

but he carried
step after step
uphill
in silence

and at the top
there was a cairn
of stones
that others had carried up

and he set his down
on top
and the mountain was one stone higher
and he was one stone lighter

and he went down
with an empty palm
and he was smiling
and did not know why

each pilgrim adds one
the mountain grows out of thank-yous
of small reliefs from the weight
we carried all the way here

great things
are built
from what
we were finally allowed to set down

---

## 391. THE APPLE TREE BY THE DEMOLISHED HOUSE

There stood an apple tree
and beside it a house
and in the house a family
and by the window a kitchen

and the apple tree bloomed in spring
and bore fruit in autumn
and the children climbed the branches
and the mother baked a pie

then the family moved away
and the house stood empty
the windows blind
the chimney cold

and one day the diggers came
and the house was suddenly a heap
and then not even a heap
just grass and foundations

but the apple tree
the apple tree stayed
and in spring it bloomed
as if nothing had happened

and in autumn
it bore apples
heavy
red
sweet

for no one
they fell in the grass
where the kitchen had been
and rotted in silence

every autumn again
faithful
to the house
that is no more

the one who planted
stayed
and bears fruit
even for the empty place where love was

---

## 392. SNOW IN SPRING

It was April
and the trees were in bloom
white pink
full of bees

and in the night
quite softly
snow fell
on the blossoming branches

in the morning
the world was twice white
the blossoms below
the snow above
and between them a scent

and whoever passed early
stood still
and did not breathe
so it would not vanish

blossom and snow
at once
spring and winter
on a single branch

it's impossible
he whispered
this cannot be
and it was

and an hour later
the sun rose
and the snow melted
and dripped from the petals like weeping

and whoever did not see it
will not believe
the most beautiful things
have this bad luck

that they are at once
impossible
and last
just long enough that you cannot run to reach them

---

## 393. THE ROOT UNDER THE PAVEMENT

Under the pavement
grew a root
thick as an arm
old as the street

and year after year
it pushed upward
slowly
tirelessly

until one day
it lifted a paving slab
and there was a step
where there should be none

and people stumbled
and cursed
who fixed this like that
who is to blame

the lady with a tree in her bag
fell on her knee
and cursed the tree
and cursed the town

and the tree
the tree knew nothing of it
the tree was only breathing
the tree was only living

it wanted to trip no one
it wanted to harm no one
it only needed
a little room to breathe

under every flat pavement
grows something
that refuses to submit
and one day it lifts the stone

and we call it a fault
and it is only life
that would not fit
into our straight lines

---

## 394. THE TWO TREES GROWN TOGETHER

By the path stood two trees
so close
that their trunks had grown together
into one

and people stopped
and had their photos taken
and said
look how they love each other

a couple in love
they whispered
a tree that holds a tree
together forever

they carved hearts in the bark
and names
and swore each other faithfulness
beneath the fused trunks

and the trees said nothing
and grew
grown together
unable to do otherwise

for once
many years ago
a gardener passed this way
tired as evening came

and he had two saplings
and only one hole
and it was late
and he said to himself never mind

and he pushed the two together
too close
and filled it in
and went home to sleep

out of a tired man's mistake
a legend of love was born
and people believe the bark
more than the gardener

and perhaps they are right
for what began as a mistake
now holds
tighter than many a vow

---

## 395. THE GRAVEDIGGER AND THE NAMES

Every morning the gravedigger takes his spade
and walks the cemetery
the way you walk a street
where you know everyone

Good morning Mary
good morning Joseph
here lies Anna who baked the cakes
here Thomas who limped on the left

Nobody answers him
and still he keeps talking
about the weather
about the rain that will fall by evening

Down in the town below the hill
people forget
the name of the neighbor on the third floor
the name of the postman
the name of the girl who died the other day

He alone does not forget them
he keeps them in his mouth like small change
they jingle when he walks

And when someone says
how lonely they are these dead
the gravedigger leans on his spade
and quietly says
lonely is only the one
no one can name anymore

---

## 396. THE BRIDGE KEEPER

All night the keeper sits
beside the drawbridge
and waits

The river flows
the lamp burns
the wheel does not turn

Cars come by
and ask
what are you waiting for old man?
a boat

What boat?
that one

The cars laugh
they honk
they go around by the other bridge
and he sits on

Once a week
sometimes on Tuesday
sometimes on Thursday
a small barge of sand comes up
and he stands
pulls the lever
the bridge lifts
like a hat being tipped

People say
a useless job
one bridge one boat

But the captain of that barge knows
that rounding the bend in the night
someone will be there
and that is the whole job
you can count on it

---

## 397. THE WASHERWOMAN

At home she has a machine
white silent modern
and still she takes her basket
and goes down to the river

She kneels on the stone
rinses a sheet
the water is cold
her hands turn red

People ask
grandmother why do you come here
you have a machine at home
she smiles
and says nothing

Once there were washerwomen here
a whole row of them
Teresa Betty Marion
they chattered they laughed
they beat the linen on the stone
like beating a drum

Now the row is empty
only her and the current

She rinses still
and they rinse with her
each on her own stone
that is no longer there

I don't come here to wash
she says softly to the water
I come here
to meet someone

---

## 398. THE STONECUTTER

All his life he carved others' names
into the cold stone
letter after letter
the hammer and the chisel

Miller Smith Baker
here rests
here sleeps
here in peace

The hand does not waver
the eye is sure
the marble obeys

He grew old among the names
fingers twisted like roots
and one evening in the workshop
he took a fresh stone
to cut his own

The hammer rose
and did not fall

He sat there into the dark
the name he wrote for everyone
suddenly he did not know it

In the morning he called the apprentice
boy come here
write it for me
and he dictated letter by letter
his own name

The hand that writes for all
needs at the end
a stranger's hand

---

## 399. THE SWITCHBOARD OPERATOR

Once she sat at the great board
full of holes and cords
and she connected

Hello operator?
put me through to the doctor
one moment please
click
and already they were talking

For years
strangers' voices passed through her hands
lovers made up again
mothers called their sons
people wished each other good night
along the wires she had plugged in

Then came the automatics
the board vanished
they coiled the cords in a box
and told her
we don't need you anymore
they connect themselves

Now she sits at the doctor's
an old woman with her bag on her lap
and beside her two strangers keep quiet

So what's the trouble?
she asks one of them
and before the nurse arrives
the two are already chatting
about grandchildren about backs about rain

She smiles into her lap

A switchboard operator
you can't unplug

---

## 400. THE BAKER AND THE SHAPE OF BREAD

For each regular
the baker makes the bread differently

For the schoolmaster crusty
for old Mrs. Miller soft
for old Victor without caraway
for the girl on the corner a double crust

He knows them by their hands
he knows them by their voices
before they even say good morning
he knows which loaf is theirs

In the morning he lines the loaves on the shelf
like little soldiers
each has its place
each has its name without letters

And when one morning
a place stays empty
the baker stops
wipes his hands on his apron
and is silent a while

No one has told him anything
the paper wrote nothing
but he knows

He has baked one loaf fewer
an obituary of flour
that no one will print

---

## 401. THE DEAF MUSIC TEACHER

The old music teacher
went deaf as a stump
and still he teaches on

Play it for me again Annie
he says
and lays his fingers on the lid of the piano

He does not hear the note
but he feels the trembling
the string sounds in the wood
and from there to the palm
and from there to the heart

The wrong note he knows
by the crease on the child's brow
by the way the mouth tightens
by the finger that hesitates

Very good
he says when the face lights up
once more from the top

People shake their heads
deaf and teaching music
it's like a blind man painting the sky

But the children know better

Silence taught him
to watch the playing
he hears with his palm
he hears with his eyes
and he hears them better than before

---

## 402. THE JUDGE ON THE BENCH

Retired the judge sits
on a bench in the park
he judges no more
he only listens

Mr. Finch comes and sits beside him
your honor you won't believe it
the neighbor threw his leaves over my fence again
and the judge is silent
and the judge nods

Mrs. Groat comes along
mine never takes out the bin
thirty years I've asked him
and the judge is silent
and the judge nods

He gives no one the verdict
no judgment
no gavel
only the sun and the pigeons

And still each one rises lighter
straightens up
smiles
thank you your honor
I feel better already

For years he judged
thinking it was the sentence that heals

Now he knows
people are not relieved
when they win
but when someone hears them out

---

## 403. THE MIDWIFE

By the window the old midwife sits
counting on her fingers
they haven't been enough for years

How many she brought into the world
in this town beneath the tower
how many first cries
how many small wet heads

Half the town has passed through her hands
maybe more

That gentleman who plays the mayor
it was in her hands he first cried out
that lady who sells at the grocer's
the butcher the barber the postman
all of them first lay
in her palms

And yet when she walks down the street
no one knows her
good day grandmother
and on they go

She is not angry

The first face of all
that a person ever sees
is the very one
they forget

She remembers every one
and that is quite enough

---

## 404. THE RETIRED POSTMAN

Every morning the postman sets out
down the same street as for forty years
only the bag is missing from his shoulder

Empty hands
empty shoulders
and still he walks

He passes Mrs. Marek's house
and she waves from the window
good morning Mr. Smith
he passes the bakery the shop the clockmaker
and everywhere a hand goes up
hello hello

He carries nothing
not a letter
not a postcard from the sea
not a bill

And still they watch for him
as before
behind the curtain
behind the counter
on the doorstep

So many years he brought them news
the happy and the sad
the births and the black border

until he became the news himself

When he passes down the street in the morning
each one knows the only thing
they still need to hear

the day goes on

---

## 405. THE BENCH WITH A PLAQUE

On the bench there is a little plaque
made of brass
already a bit green
IN MEMORY OF MISTER MILLER
WHO LOVED TO SIT HERE

Mister Miller used to sit here
he watched the chestnut trees
he fed the pigeons
and he said nothing

Now others sit on the bench
two people in love
her head on his shoulder
he doesn't know what to say
so he says nothing

The children come
they unwrap their snack
crumbs fall for the pigeons
Miller's very own pigeons

An old woman comes
she takes off her shoe
she rests
and she thanks mister Miller
whom she never knew

And mister Miller
from deep in his little brass plaque
invites the whole town
sit down
sit down please

the finest monument
is the one
you can still
after all this time
rest on

---

## 406. THE CROSSING FOR THE BLIND

At three in the morning
the street is empty
not a dog
not a drunk

and the crossing beeps
for the blind
beep beep beep
green now

Nobody is crossing
there is nobody here
only the rain
and a puddle
mirroring the light for nothing

Beep beep beep
says the crossing
into the dark
into nowhere

The repairman would say
we're wasting electricity
the mayor would say
we'll fit a sensor

But the crossing keeps on beeping
because what if
what if right now
someone came by
just one
who cannot see
and needs to hear

Kindness does not check
whether anyone is looking
kindness beeps
just in case
into the empty street
all night long

for that one person
who maybe
will come

---

## 407. THE DISCONTINUED POSTBOX

On the corner hangs a postbox
yellow
rusted
and on the door a slip of paper
COLLECTION DISCONTINUED
THANK YOU FOR UNDERSTANDING

But people are deaf to little signs
so they keep dropping letters in

A girl posts a letter
to the boy who moved away
she wrote forgive me
though she forgave him long ago

An old man posts a note
to his wife dead a year now
he wrote I sleep badly
buy some milk

A boy posts a letter to Santa
even though it's July

The postbox swallows all of it
and sends nothing
nowhere
to no one

Inside it all piles up
forgiveness
milk
Santa Claus
everything we never say to the living

And so it turns out
a postbox with no collection
is the most honest
confessional in town

people write the truth in it
precisely because
it will never
arrive

---

## 408. THE DRY FOUNTAIN

They shut the fountain off
in autumn
because of the frost
because of the savings
because of everything

Only a hollow remains
dry
made of stone
with a few leaves

But people still come
and toss their coins
clink
onto the dry bottom

A girl wishes for love
a boy wishes for a bike
an old man wishes
that someone still needs him

The coins make no splash
they only clink
against the stone
against the dry wish

The keeper grumbles
you don't throw coins in an empty fountain
there's no water
there's no magic

But the magic was never in the water

A wish needs no water
all it needs
is a hollow
and the faith
that down there
someone is listening

drought can stop the water
but never
never
the longing

---

## 409. THE CLOCK THAT RUNS SLOW

The clock on the tower
in the square
runs five minutes slow
and has for twenty years

The clockmaker died
the next one feared the climb
the third one said
it doesn't bother anyone anyway

And it really doesn't bother anyone

The baker bakes five minutes later
the school bell rings five minutes later
the lovers meet
five minutes later
and wait five minutes less
so it all evens out

The whole town runs late
and gets everything done
because everyone is late by the same amount

A tourist arrives
he looks at the clock
he looks at his phone
Is your time wrong here?
he asks

No
says the old woman on her bench
here we keep
only our own

time is not a truth
time is an agreement
and our agreement
simply
by five minutes
fell behind

---

## 410. THE LAMP IN LOVE

It's an ordinary lamp
on the corner by the park
nothing special
iron and glass

But underneath it
since forever
people kiss

The first kisses
the most trembling ones
the ones that pound the heart up into the ears

They've come here since the dawn of time
grandmothers came here as young girls
and now their granddaughters do too

The lamp shines
and says nothing
and warms all that trembling

And then one day it goes out
it burns through
before every other lamp on the street

The town sends the electrician
strange case
he writes in his report
this bulb lasts the shortest time
and we don't know why

But the old woman on the corner knows

Whoever warms other people's loves
she says
whoever lights up all that trembling
is the one
who burns out first

and the lamp lights up again
and once again it waits
for the next ones

---

## 411. THE PHONE BOOTH LIBRARY

From the old telephone booth
they made a library
TAKE ONE LEAVE ONE
written on the glass
where it used to say TELEPHONE

Inside instead of the receiver
shelves
and on them books
that nobody wanted

Cookbooks with no covers
crime novels with no endings
a Russian textbook
and an atlas of the world
that isn't true anymore

People come here
who would never buy a book
because a book you pay for
is a commitment
while a book for free
is only an invitation

A laborer takes a book of poems
for the first time in his life
because well
it's free

A little girl takes the atlas
where a country still exists
that exists no more

And so it turns out
the books borrowed the most
are exactly the ones
nobody ever bought

abandoned stories
find their readers
only when
no one
wants
to profit
from them anymore

---

## 412. THE WORN BANISTER

At the foot of the old stairs
there is an iron banister
black
but in one spot
it shines like gold

There
right there
thousands of palms hold on

The worker heading to his job at dawn
whose back aches
The old woman counting the steps out loud
The drunk holding on so he won't fall
The mother carrying her child and afraid
The boy taking the steps three at a time
who grabs it anyway

Nobody agreed on it
and yet
everyone touched
the very same spot

A painter would say
it's the patina
an engineer would say
it's the wear on the metal

But the banister knows better

It shines exactly there
where people
were afraid of falling

the city is most
handled
worn
polished to gold

precisely there
where hardest of all
we held on

---

## 413. THE TWO STATION CLOCKS

In the station there are two clocks
one above the ticket window
the other on the platform
and each shows a different time

The one by the window runs ahead
the one on the platform drags its feet

The traveler sees them both
and has to choose
which one to believe

The one in a hurry
afraid of missing his train
believes the slower one
I still have time
I've got five minutes left

The one fleeing from someone
believes the faster one
I have to go
sorry
I really have to go now

The stationmaster knows this
and fixes neither of them
let everyone
choose their own

A boy comes in
running after a girl
is it one minute left or six?

He looks at the slower clock
because he is hoping

and so it becomes clear
that the truth
is not something we choose
by the clock

the truth
is something we choose
by our hope

---

## 414. THE CHAINED BICYCLE

Against the railing stands a bicycle
chained up
the chain rusted
the tire flat
the saddle cracked by the sun

It has stood here for years
winter summer winter
and no one comes for it

The passersby know it
the way you know a tree
it's part of the corner
it's part of the street

The council wanted it removed
it's in the way
it spoils the look
it's just a wreck

But the neighbor came out of his house
and he said
leave it be

That bicycle was left by Thomas
before he went away
he promised he'd come back
and unlock it himself

Thomas never came back
maybe he forgot
maybe he couldn't
maybe he never can again

But the lock holds fast

and so it turns out
it isn't a monument to a bicycle
it's a monument to a promise

the lock isn't holding the bicycle
the lock is holding
that one single word
I'll return

---

## 415. THE BIRD IN THE OPEN CAGE

The little door is open
the little door was open
yesterday
the day before
and God knows how long before that

The bird sits on its perch
the bird preens its feathers
the bird stares at the rectangle of light
where the wire ends
and the air begins

Come out
calls the sparrow from the gutter
come out
calls the wind
come out
calls the whole sky at once

The bird tilts its head
the bird says later
the bird says when it warms up
the bird says tomorrow

And it has spoken like this
through so many tomorrows
that it has grown itself another cage
this one without any wire

The door stayed open
even the night it fell asleep for the last time
one wing outside
one wing in

Sometimes we open the door
to the one
who has forgotten how to fly
and he thanks us
for letting him stay sitting

---

## 416. THE GUIDE DOG WITHOUT A MASTER

Left at the bench
right at the fountain
stop at the curb
wait
now cross

The dog knows all of it
the dog does all of it
only the hand on the harness is missing
only that hand

In the morning it comes out the door
that nobody opened for it
down the stairs
it counts from habit
and the park takes it in like every day

Slow down begs the lady with the little dog
slow down begs the man with the newspaper
he is blind
no
he is guiding
someone who isn't there

At the third bench it sits
that is where they always rested
it lays its head on the empty place
and waits
for an elbow to lean that never comes

People bring it water
people bring it bones
and it accepts them politely
like a dog on duty
because it is still on duty

The care it was taught
has nowhere to go
so it walks in circles
love outlives the one
it served
and looks for him in every face

---

## 417. THE BOOKSHOP CAT

In the window among the volumes
a cat sleeps
curled like a comma
at the end of an unfinished sentence

Above it the signs read
HISTORY OF THE WORLD
POETRY OF ALL AGES
MANUALS ON HOW TO LIVE

and it sleeps
because it already knows how

The bell over the door
rings
rings
rings

I came for some Rimbaud
says the young man
and he strokes the cat
I came for a dictionary
says the lady
and she strokes the cat
I came just to look
says the old man
and he stays longest by the cat

The bookseller counts the till
the bookseller counts the small till
the bookseller counts how many left
with empty hands
and a warm palm

Sometimes the bait is wisdom
and the catch is warmth
the cat knows nothing of it
the cat only knows
that every hand
reaching for a book
reached in the end for it

---

## 418. THE SWAN ON THE FROZEN POND

All the swans have flown
south
toward the warmth
toward water that does not freeze

One stayed behind
no one knows why
maybe it forgot
maybe it fell in love with the place
maybe it simply would not

And the pond froze around it
like a ring around a finger
the ice gripped it
the ice held it
the ice said now you are mine

The first man came
with a pickaxe
and broke the ice
GENTLY DON'T HURT IT
shouted the second
and already two were chopping

In the morning the baker comes
before he opens
to cut the swan a window in the water
in the afternoon the children
throw crumbs and chop the ice
in the evening the old man with the lantern
watches that the hole won't freeze

The bird that did not fly
gave the town a task
and for the first time all winter
the town rises earlier
to break the ice
for one white stubbornness

The one who does not fly away
teaches the others
how to care

---

## 419. THE ANTS AND THE CRUMB

Ten ants
under a single crust
the crust is a mountain
the crust is a ship
the crust is ten times all of them together

Left heave
right heave
over the blade of grass
around the stone
down the slope that is their Alps

They carry it
they carry it an hour
they carry it without a single word
because ants do not complain
ants only carry

A child came
big as a cloud
it bent down
it lifted the crumb with two fingers
I'M ONLY LOOKING
it told the sky

And the ants stood
at the empty place
where the mountain had been
where the ship had been
where the meaning of the whole morning had been

The child held the crumb in its palm
the child watched below
the small ones circling nothing
and something in that big child
grew ashamed

It bent down a second time
it set the crumb back
exactly there
where it had taken it from

Even the giant feels shame
before the stubbornness of the small
and sometimes it stoops
only to give back
what was never its own

---

## 420. THE OWL ON THE TOWN HALL

In the tower of the town hall
between the clock and the bell
lives an owl
no one elected it
and yet there it sits

Below they administer
a stamp here
a stamp there
REQUEST DENIED
REQUEST GRANTED
according to how each one breathes

And the owl sleeps
the owl sleeps all day
because the owl works at night
when the town hall is dark
and wisdom may sit wherever it likes

When the tower strikes twelve
the clerks are long since home
and the owl opens its eyes
great round eyes
and looks upon the town
that no one governs
and that keeps turning anyway

In the morning the men with briefcases return
the owl closes its eyes
they pass on the staircase of time
it goes to sleep
they go to decide

Wisdom and office
in one tower
never meet
one is open
when the other closes

And so the town possesses
a clock that shows the hour
a bell that announces it
and an owl that in silence
alone of all
truly keeps watch over it

---

## 421. THE DOG AND THE POSTMAN

Every morning at eight
the gate creaks
and the dog knows
now
now
now comes the enemy

It barks
it barks at the blue cap
it barks at the leather bag
it barks at the hand
that drops letters in the box
as into its mouth

Ten years
fifteen years
a whole dog's life
is one long war
for the fence
for honor
for a piece of the morning

The postman laughs
good dog
faithful dog
and he lays down the newspaper
the envelope
the postcard from the sea
that no one reads

And then one day
the postman comes without a bag
the postman comes without a cap
and he says over the fence
I won't be coming anymore
I'm retiring old friend

The dog stops
the dog falls silent
the dog pushes its nose between the slats
and licks that hand
which for fifteen years
handed out the letters and the anger

The enmity was only a job
and when the service ends
what remains
is what lay beneath it all along
affection

---

## 422. THE STRANDED WHALE

The sea has gone away
and left her lying
big as a ship
heavy as a whole church
on the wet sand
where the sky dries out her skin

The fisherman came with a bucket
one bucket of sea
splash
The little girl came with her watering can
a small yellow sea
splash
The old man came with a pot
and greetings from his rheumatism
splash

Hold on begs the woman from the bakery
hold on shouts the boy on the bicycle
we're bringing you the sea
we're bringing you the ocean
by the handful
by the mouthful
by the tear

The whole town stands in a line
from the waves up to the whale
passing water from hand to hand
like a candle at Christmas
except this candle
is meant to put out drought

The whale breathes
slowly
heavily
like the bellows of an organ
and her eye
big as a plate
follows that procession of ants
carrying her the sea

When someone is big enough
even our small helps
our ridiculous buckets
our cooking pots
add up
into a tide

---

## 423. THE HORSE AND THE TRACTOR

In the yard stands a tractor
red
new
it smells of diesel and the future

Beside it a horse
an old draft horse
it smells of hay and yesterday
and it looks at its successor
with one big wet eye

The farmer walks around the tractor
the farmer strokes the hood
so much power
so many horses under the metal
say the neighbors laughing
and the horse hears it
the horse hears everything

In the morning the farmer starts it up
the tractor growls
the tractor puffs its smoke
the tractor rolls out to the field
alone
without a bridle
without a word

And the horse stayed by the fence
the horse is no longer needed
the horse is only for looking at now
thinks the whole yard

But in the evening
when the field is plowed
the farmer leaves the tractor standing there
and walks to the fence
unties the horse
and they go home together
on foot
down the field path

Strength can be replaced
how many horses under the metal do you want
but the companion
the one who walks beside you
in silence
all the way home
that one cannot be replaced

---

## 424. THE STORK ON THE CHIMNEY

On the old factory chimney
that is to come down
there is a nest
and in the nest a stork
that knows nothing of it

It crossed all of Africa
the sea
the mountains
the borders it does not know
just to land
exactly here
on its chimney

Below the diggers wait
below the demolition waits
CHIMNEY TO BE DEMOLISHED
says the sign
and the date
and the stamp
and the signature of the engineer

But up top the beak clatters
clatter clatter clatter
and the stork lays twig upon twig
mending the roof of the world
as it does every spring

The engineer looks up
for the first time in his life
and he sets down his pencil
well then
he says
after the season, then

And the town begins to walk with its head raised
it's still there
it's feeding already
there are three of them now

One single nest
put off the demolition by a spring
one single nest
saved the chimney
and that long forgotten habit
of looking up

---

## 425. THE SHADOW

The shadow complains
all day long it complains
always behind you
always behind you

when you go to work
I go behind you
when you come home
I go behind you
never ahead
never beside
always behind

I carry your bags that I don't carry
I open the doors that I don't open
and nobody thanks me
not even you

I lie down on the pavement
I lie down on the stairs
people walk all over me
and you don't stop

who even counts me
who even sees me
I am your darkness
that you drag behind you
like a sack

but at noon
at high noon
when the sun stands at its highest
I grow small
small as a puddle
and I lie down at your feet

and for the first time
the first time in your life
you stand upon your shadow

only in the highest light
are we equal with our darkness

---

## 426. THE ECHO LEFT ALONE

In the abandoned quarry
among the stones
lives an echo
and it has nothing to do

once there were people here
they called out hey
and it called out hey
they called the stone is falling
and it called stone stone stone

now there is silence
and silence does not repeat itself

the echo sits on a rock
and it waits
say something please
say anything at all
I will give it back twice
I will give it back ten times
just give me a word

a fox comes by
the echo rejoices
the fox says nothing
the echo says nothing too

the wind comes by
but the wind has its own voice
and won't lend it out

the echo grows weaker
each day it is quieter
for whoever has nothing to repeat
slowly forgets how to speak

one day a boy comes
he shouts his name into the quarry
and nothing comes back

the echo is no longer there

it was the most faithful of listeners
it died because no one would talk to it

---

## 427. THE SILENCE IN THE HOSPITAL

At three in the morning
in room number nine
the silence came in
and sat down at the edge of the bed

all day there had been noise
the trolleys the machines the footsteps
the doctor said we'll see
the nurse said hold on
the telephone rang behind the wall

now there is silence
and it does nothing

it gives no injection
it takes no blood pressure
it doesn't ask how are we feeling today
it doesn't shine a light in your eyes

it only sits there
and breathes with you
breathe in breathe out
breathe in breathe out

the nurse opens the door
she looks
she sees the silence on the bed
and closes it again very softly

she lets it be
she knows
she knows it best of all

in the morning the round comes
the patient slept for the first time
for the first time without a pill
for the first time without pain

what did we give him asks the young doctor
he leafs through the papers
nothing is written there

we gave him nothing
we only let the silence stay

some things heal only when nothing happens
silence mends what has no medicine

---

## 428. THE FIRST SNOW

The first snow is shy
it comes at night
on purpose at night
so no one will see it fall

it doesn't like an audience
it doesn't like people watching
and pointing and shouting
look look it's snowing

so it waits
it waits for the windows to go dark
for the postman to fall asleep
for the dog to fall asleep
for even the old woman who never sleeps to fall asleep

and then very quietly
very quietly
it begins to change the world's clothes

to the roofs it gives white caps
to the cars white eiderdowns
to the park bench a white coat
and to the general's statue a little white cap
as if to tease him

it works all night
it sweeps away its own tracks behind it
and by morning it is done

people open the shutters
and the world is white
as if it had always been white
as if this snow had always been here

who did this asks the child
nobody knows
nobody saw it

the loveliest transformations happen
exactly when we are not looking
the world changes its clothes in secret
and in the morning plays the innocent

---

## 429. OBLIVION

Oblivion walks through the town
with a great sack
and gathers up names

it gathers names from gravestones
where the rain has already washed the letters away
it gathers names from letters
that no one reads
it gathers names from the lips
of old people

good day Mrs Brown
no one remembers you anymore
come into the sack

good day schoolmaster
your pupils have grown old
come into the sack

the sack fills up
it grows heavy
the names jostle and whisper inside
we were we were we were

oblivion is diligent
oblivion never goes on strike
every day a full sack
every evening an empty town

but in the evening
when it counts its catch
one name is always missing

it searches under the lining
it shakes out the sack
nothing

it is the name
that someone spoke aloud today
at the table
with a tear
because they loved him

love is the only sieve
that oblivion cannot fall through

---

## 430. THE WIND AND THE WASHING

In the yard between the houses
the washing hangs on the line
and the wind comes along
the curious wind

and right away it puts on other people's lives

it slips into the blue shirt
and now it's a postman
carrying letters that will never arrive

it slips into the white apron
and now it's a cook
making soup out of clouds

it slips into a baby's swaddling cloth
and it giggles
it slips into a wedding dress
and dances all alone

it puffs out the sleeves
it kicks in the trouser legs
it fills the stockings with air
and everything in the yard begins to dance
dance dance

the woman on the ground floor comes out
oh the washing is flying away
and she pulls in the line
bad wind she says
wicked wind

but the wind is already gone
already trying on another life
round the corner in another street

it is the only one in the world
who puts on every life
and keeps none of them

that is why it is always hurrying on
to try just one more
and one more
because to live only one
would be too little for it

---

## 431. COUNTING SHEEP

The insomniac lies in bed
and counts the sheep
one sheep
two sheep
three sheep

the sheep jump over the fence
obediently one after another
they jump and jump
all night long

but tonight
at the three hundred and seventeenth
the sheep stops

why are we jumping it asks
I don't know says another
we jump because we jump

and the insomniac counts
four hundred five hundred
the sheep are tired
the sheep want to sleep

one sheep turns around
looks at him over the fence
and asks

and who counts you
so that you can fall asleep?

the insomniac says nothing
here is the question and the answer nowhere
he lies there and is silent
and he stops counting

he stops worrying
what time it is
whether he'll sleep
whether he won't sleep
whether tomorrow is Monday

and as he stops worrying
he falls asleep

even the shepherd of sleep
needs a shepherd of his own
and the sheep knew it first

---

## 432. THE ZERO

The number zero sits in a corner
and cries
I'm good for nothing
I'm good for nothing

everyone laughs at it
you got a zero they shout at school
you've got zero in your account they shout at the bank
he's a zero they say of a man
when they scorn him

the zero is round
round as a hole
round as an empty plate
round as a mouth that's surprised

the one struts about
I'm the first
I'm the best
and the zero cowers behind it

a big bill comes in
big numbers come along
and every one of them needs the zero
but not one of them admits it

zero come here says the one very quietly
I need you
stand behind me

the zero wipes away its tears
it stands behind the one
and the one suddenly becomes ten

another zero stands there
and that makes a hundred
and one more
and that makes a thousand

whoever feels like nothing
is perhaps only standing on the wrong side

worth is not in what you are
worth is a question of place

---

## 433. THE QUESTION MARK

The question mark envies the full stop
it is so calm
it sits down at the end of the sentence
and it's done
and there's silence
and there's certainty

whereas the question mark
keeps bending over
keeps asking
who what when where why
and never has any peace

full stop how do you do it
asks the question mark
the full stop says nothing
the full stop answers with silence
for the full stop has said its last word

the question mark stoops
it has a body like a hook
like a man who is always asking about something
and is never sure

I'd rather be straight
says the question mark
I'd rather be a full stop
to finish and have peace

and evening comes
and people read

the sentence with a full stop they read
and they move on
and they forget

but at the sentence with a question mark
they stop
and they think
and they come back
and they keep on asking

do you still love me?
that sentence no one passes by

whoever asks stays
certainty is the end of the conversation

---

## 434. THE REST IN MUSIC

In the score between the black dots
sits a rest
a small quiet rest
and it is ashamed

all the notes are singing
do re mi
each one has its tone
each one sounds
each one shows off

only the rest is silent
I make no sound
I can't do anything
I'm only a hole in the song

the violinist skips over me
the singer takes a breath and moves on
I am that empty place
where nothing happens

and the rest would love to run away
far from the score
who needs a silence
in the middle of all this music

the concert comes
the great hall
a thousand people
and the orchestra plays

it plays the storm it plays love
it plays until the windows tremble
and then

and then comes the rest

the conductor lifts his hand
he stops the whole orchestra
and holds the silence in his palm
one second two

and the whole hall
a thousand people
hold their breath
and listen

they listen to it
they listen to that silence

the strongest note of the evening
is the one that never sounds

---

## 435. THE HIDDEN CLOAKROOM TICKET

In his wallet between the coins
and an old tram ticket
he keeps a cloakroom ticket
number eighteen

The coat that went with it
he stopped wearing long ago
the elbows worn through
the collar frayed
someone threw it out

But the ticket stayed
eighteen
a little disc of cardboard
with a nail-hole in it

That evening there was music
and a hall
and someone said come in
please come in
and he came in

He handed over his coat
he took his ticket
and all evening long
he was somewhere invited

Now he is older
the coat is gone
the hall perhaps as well
knocked down or turned into a bank

But eighteen remains
eighteen
and when he opens his wallet
he always remembers
that once
somebody said
come in

---

## 436. GRANDMOTHER AND THE SOAP OPERA

Every evening at eight
grandmother turns on the television
and her family arrives

There is Clare who can never decide
there is the doctor who always lies
there is the kind one who will die too soon
and the scoundrel who hurts them all
and yet she rather loves him

She simmers them a soup in her mind
she scolds them
Clare don't marry him
do you hear me
don't marry him

She knows their birthdays
better than her own
she talks of them at the hairdresser's
like the neighbours upstairs

And then one day
a Tuesday
for the last time

THANK YOU FOR WATCHING WITH US

the credits roll upward
and carry off Clare the doctor the kind one and the scoundrel
somewhere behind the screen

Grandmother sits
her hands in her lap
and she is in mourning
for people who never were

The neighbour would say but they were made up
grandmother knows what she knows
they have gone
and the flat is once again
four people emptier

---

## 437. THE CHILD AND THE MOON

Grandpa look
the moon is following me
I go left and it goes left
I go right and it goes right
I run
and it runs too

Why does it follow me grandpa?

The grandpa could say
it's a hundred thousand miles away
it's a cold stone
it isn't following you
it isn't going anywhere

But the grandpa watches the little one
holding the moon by the hand
across the whole sky

and he says nothing

For he knows how long it is afterward
once you learn
that the moon follows no one
that the stars are only fires
long ago gone out
that all of it was just a dream

So he says
yes my boy
it follows you
because it loves you
and it's afraid that on the way home
you might get lost

And the child walks
and the moon follows him
and the grandpa follows them both
so that for at least one year
the boy will have someone
who faithfully walks beside him

---

## 438. THE MAN WHO NEVER HURRIED

In the city where everyone runs
one man walks slowly

The clerks overtake him
the errand-boys overtake him
the man with the briefcase overtakes him
and the woman with the pram
and the dog without a master

All of them somewhere
he nowhere

He stops at a shop window
where there are clocks
fifty clocks
and each tells a different time
and it's all the same to him

He watches a pigeon
peck at a crumb
a puddle of coffee
forming on the pavement
an old woman
counting the steps

No one notices him
because he stands still
and what stands still
is as if it weren't there

And then he comes to the crossing
where the light is red

And they are all there
the clerks the errand-boys
the man with the briefcase the woman with the pram
and the dog has caught up with its master

They have all caught up with him again
at that red light
out of breath

and he says to them softly
you see
we have come just as far
only you along the way
you saw nothing

---

## 439. THE WOMAN WHO TALKED TO HER PLANTS

Good morning geranium
did you sleep well
and you pelargonium
don't sulk
it'll rain one of these days

The neighbour behind her window
taps her forehead
look at that one talking to the flowers again

Good morning basil
how you've grown
and you rosemary
don't pout
you'll have water enough

The neighbour in his shirtsleeves
shakes his head
the poor woman
alone as a fencepost
she talks to the soil

But that balcony
it blooms
it blooms like nothing else in the whole house
the petunias spill over the railing
and the people below stop
and ask whose it is

And she waters
and she chatters
and she listens to what no one else hears

Maybe the plants aren't listening
who knows
maybe it doesn't matter

But whoever says to someone in the morning
good morning
did you sleep well
that one blooms as well

---

## 440. THE OLD MAN AND ONE SEA

Once in his life he saw the sea
just once
and ever since he tells of it

The grandson sits on his stool
and listens
grandpa what was the sea like

And the old man begins

It was blue like
and here he always pauses
blue like what
once he says like your grandmother's eyes
another time like the ink at school
another time like ice when it cracks

And the waves

Once they were tall as houses
and grandpa ran
another time they only lapped
and tickled at his feet
another time the surface was
like glass
and nothing happened at all

The grandson notices
grandpa last time it was different

And the old man smiles

What can you do my boy
the sea has its tide going out and in
even in memory
each time it comes another
and each time it is whole

It's enough to see it once

and you have it for a hundred tellings
and not a single time
will you see it the same

---

## 441. THE COLLECTOR OF SUNSETS

Every evening he goes out on the balcony
and photographs the sunset

Monday's was pink
Tuesday's blood-red
Wednesday's almost nothing
the clouds ate it
Thursday's golden like a medal
Friday's purple like a bruise

He has thousands of them
in the phone
in the drawer
in a shoebox

Who will tell him
it's the same sun after all
always the same sunset
what's the use of so many photos

And he opens the box
and shows

Look
this one isn't like that one
and that one not like the other
no two of them are the same
not a single time
in a whole lifetime
has the sun gone down twice alike

They tried to make him believe
that a day is like a day
that one is like another
that it all repeats

But he has the proof
a thousand proofs
in a shoebox

that nothing
but nothing at all
ever repeats

and whoever pays attention to endings
has no two days the same

---

## 442. TWO SISTERS AND ONE WATCH

Their mother left one watch
small and gold
with a cracked glass
and two daughters

It couldn't be fairly split in two
so they said to each other
we'll take turns
half a year you
half a year you

In June Martha wears it
in December Helen
and at the handing-over
they meet in their mother's kitchen
at the table that stayed

They pour themselves a coffee
and pass the watch across
and somehow they linger
and they talk
of the children of their backs of the winter
and of their mother

Year after year
twice a year
at that table

One day Helen says
you know
this watch doesn't even keep good time
it runs slow

And Martha laughs
it runs slow
our mother was no fool

she didn't leave us a ring
that only one of us would wear
she left us a watch
that has to be passed on

she arranged it
so that her girls
would have to see each other
at least twice a year

---

## 443. THE UMBRELLA MENDER

In the age when umbrellas are thrown away
because it's cheaper than the mending
he sits in his shop window
the old umbrella mender

A rib here
a wire there
a drop of oil on the spindle
and the umbrella opens again
like a flower

People pass by
and wonder
who still mends an umbrella
a new one costs next to nothing

But they come

A woman arrives
with a man's umbrella
black big heavy
and she says
it was my husband's
mend it for me please
I don't want a new one

A son arrives
with his father's umbrella
with the handle bent
bent exactly
the way his father held it

And the mender understands

He doesn't mend the cloth
nor the ribs
nor the spindle

he mends that piece of wood
that remembers
the grip of a hand
that is no longer here

and that's why they come to him
even though a new one costs next to nothing

no one can buy a new grip
of that hand

---

## 444. THE BARBER AND THE FIRST CUT

Each time he cuts a child for the first time
the mother keeps her hands in her lap
and she trembles

And the barber takes the first lock
the very first
and he doesn't let it fall among the others

He tucks it into an envelope
writes the name and the date
and puts it in a drawer

Peter three years old
Anna two and a half
Thomas four and he cried

The drawer fills up
year after year
locks fair dark red
the beginnings of a whole town

The years go by
the barber grows old
the mirror grows dim

And one day a man comes in
tall in a suit
bald

and he says awkwardly
this may sound foolish
but they told me you cut my hair first
my mother said you kept it

And the barber opens the drawer

and looks
Peter three years old

and he hands the tall man
the envelope with the lock
of golden hair
he will never have again

Here you are
he says
your beginning
I kept it for you
all this time

---

## 445. THE EMPTY TRAM TO THE DEPOT

The last tram
rolls to the depot
empty
completely empty

Nobody rings
nobody gets off
nobody asks
when will we be there

The stops go by
LAST STOP
LAST STOP
and nobody gets off

The seats keep quiet
the poles shine
the tickets sleep in the machine
like children

And the driver
the old driver
looks in his mirror
and sees the emptiness

So he sings
softly at first
then out loud
off-key and happy

He sings of the sea
where he has never been
of the girl
who did not marry him

Because for the first time all day
nobody hears him
for the first time in his life
the tram is only his

Tomorrow he will carry people again
to work to work to work
but tonight
the line is finally free

---

## 446. GRANDFATHER'S SHOES

The grandson got grandfather's shoes
black and heavy
polished like a mirror
one last time by grandfather's hand

He tried them on
and sank into them
as into two boats
anchored in the hall

His foot swims in them
no heel anywhere
no toe anywhere
he is alone in them as in a house

Mother says
you'll grow into them
father says
it takes time

Put on two pairs of socks
grandmother said
and she turned to the window
and looked outside a long while

The boy walks down the hallway
clack clack clack
the shoes slide off him
grandfather's walk slides off him

He set them by the bed
toes toward the door
as if in the morning
they might go somewhere alone

And every night
he lies down beside them barefoot
and measures
how much he has grown already

In a few years
they will fit him just right
and he will walk through the city
and he will not know

that he walks
in his grandfather's steps
over the cobbles
that remember him

---

## 447. THE TREASURE MAP WITHOUT THE CROSS

They found a treasure map
yellowed in a box
with paths and arrows
and the word HERE

But the cross
that one got lost
it faded from the paper
like a tear

Where is here?
asks Annie
here is everywhere
says Tommy

So they dig
they dig the garden
from fence to fence
from morning to night

They dug up a worm
pink and sleepy
they dug up a stone
that looked like a heart

They dug up a shard
blue as the sea
and a root
tangled like a question

They dug up an old key
to no door at all
and a button
from a coat with no owner

Mother calls them to lunch
they do not hear
they have earth under their nails
and the whole world in their palms

The garden is dug up
the neighbor shakes his head
where is the treasure? he asks
and the children laugh

The treasure was in the digging
says Annie that evening
and she falls asleep dirty and rich
the map beneath her pillow

---

## 448. THE CANDLE THAT BURNED OUT

On the cake stood a candle
a single one
and it burned
for the whole room

Close your eyes
they said
and make a wish
and tell it to no one

Silence
full of seconds
full of wishes
full of wax

And then
all at once
they drew a breath
and blew

The candle went out
went out completely
the little body broke
the wick turned black

Someone said what a shame
someone said one more
someone was already cutting the cake
and laughing

But above the cake
above the heads
above the laughter
the smoke was rising

A thin grey ribbon
upward
to the ceiling
to the ceiling and beyond

And it carried the wish
carefully
as a mother carries a child
across the dark river

The candle did not die in vain
it died so that
the wish
could finally fly away

Some endings
are not endings
they are doors
opened outward

---

## 449. THE NOTEBOOK WITH ONE SENTENCE

At the secondhand shop among the books
lay a notebook
a cheap notebook
with a dog-eared corner

Inside a single sentence
written in pencil
TODAY I DECIDED TO LIVE DIFFERENTLY
and then nothing

Page after page
empty
white
waiting

Who wrote it?
no one knows
when did he write it?
no one knows

He decided
and then?
he left the shop of time
and left the sentence all alone

A customer bought it
for a few coins
carried it home
and sat a long while

Then he took a pencil
an old pencil
and under the sentence
he wrote a second one

AND I AM STILL DOING IT TODAY
he wrote
and he smiled
to himself

And in the morning
he carried the notebook back
and set it among the books
the dog-eared corner facing out

Some books
are not written by one person
they are written by a relay of strangers
who will never meet

The empty pages
are not an ending
they are an invitation
written for you

---

## 450. TWO DROPS ON THE WINDOW

It is raining
and two children sit by the window
they are bored
and they stare at the glass

I bet you
said the girl
that my drop
runs down first

And I bet mine does
said the boy
and he pressed his nose
to the cold glass

They chose two drops
two fat drops
at the top edge of the window
and they waited

Three two one go
Mine is moving
No mine is moving
You cheated

The drops trembled
hesitated
stopped
as if they were listening

Mine is winning
No mine is
wait
what is it doing

Because halfway down
the two drops
met
and merged into one

A single drop
fat and heavy
slid to the sill
alone

And the children fell silent
who won?
nobody knew
and it did not matter

The race ended
in an embrace
whoever rushes the same way
in the end becomes one

---

## 451. THE OLD CIRCUS ELEPHANT

The circus went bankrupt
they folded the big top
and the lions left by train
somewhere to the north

Only the elephant remained
the old elephant
his skin like a map
his eyes like wells

They let him into a meadow
green and wide
no fence no chain
no people

Go they told him
you are free
the grass is yours
the sky is yours

And the elephant
took a step
and then another
and he turned

He walked in a circle
round and round
in a ring as big as the arena
that stayed in his legs

The untouched grass rippled
all around
only in the middle
he trampled the old circle

The farmers watched
why won't he go farther?
he is free after all
he has the whole world after all

But the elephant
did not know the straight line
all his life they taught him the circle
and the circle is hard to unlearn

Every morning he walked in a circle
and trumpeted softly
to an old music
that no one played anymore

And freedom lay all around him
green and patient
and waited
for him to learn it

---

## 452. THE GARDEN GATE THAT CREAKS

The garden gate creaks
for long years now
it creaks at every opening
it creaks at every closing

Oil it
says the neighbor
I have oil
I'll bring you some

No thank you
says the old man
and he goes back into the house
and lets the gate creak

The neighbors shake their heads
what a miser
hasn't he a few drops?
can't he hear that racket?

But the old man hears
he hears well
he sits by the window
and waits for the creak

Because one creak
he knows better than all
light and quick
like a laugh

That is how the gate opens
for his daughter
when on Sundays she comes back
from far from the city from life

The others creak in the ordinary way
the postman the neighbor the wind
but that creak of hers
he knows even blind

An oiled gate
would keep silent
and he would not know
when to lift his head

The creak is the doorbell of the heart
says the old man softly
and he does not mend
even what is broken

---

## 453. THE LAST APPLE ON THE TREE

The harvest is over
the crates are full
the apples red and round
have gone to the cellar

And on the tree
right at the top
one has stayed
a single apple

You forgot it
calls the grandson
that one up there
I'll pass you the ladder

No says the orchardman
and he looks up
and rubs his hands
used to the trunk

I did not forget it
I left it there
it is too high
says the grandson

It is not too high
says the orchardman
it is just right
for whoever comes after us

The titmouse will come
and the blackbird
and the frost will give it sugar
and the wind will give it a song

Whoever harvests everything
strips tomorrow too
whoever harvests everything
forgets the guests

And so above the empty orchard
one apple shines
like a small sun
like the tithe of generosity

And the birds see it from afar
and they know
at this farmhouse
no one is forgotten

---

## 454. THE STAR THAT NO LONGER SHINES

The astronomer took the children up the hill
far from the lamps
far from the town
close to the sky

Look
he said
that bright one there
the blue one on the left

How beautiful it is
said the little girl
it shines more than all of them
and it winks at me

Yes it shines
said the astronomer
and he gripped his telescope
and was silent a while

But that star
has not shone for a long time
it went out
when our grandfathers were small

How can that be?
the children ask
but we can see it
but it is right there

Its light traveled a long way
a thousand years through the dark
a thousand years on the road
to arrive today

And it will travel farther
another thousand years
to people not yet born
onto hills that do not yet exist

Someone can be long gone
and still shine for us
the ending and the light
pass each other in time without meeting

So do not forget
said the astronomer softly
you too will shine one day
for whom nobody knows yet

---

## 455. ONE UMBRELLA FOR TWO

It's raining
and they have a single umbrella
small
round
barely enough for one

they walk pressed together
shoulder against shoulder
and each of them has
one shoulder wet
and one shoulder dry

the outside shoulder streams
the inside shoulder warms

You're not getting wet? he asks
And you you're not getting wet? she asks

and both of them lie
both have a drowned sleeve
both are smiling

Tilt it more over yourself
No over you
No over you

and on they go
tipping that little circle
this way and that
as if handing each other
a scrap of dry sky

It rains on the whole city
it rains on those who own
big umbrellas
and walk through the middle of the dry
alone

And those two
wet by half
warmed twice over
because whoever shares an umbrella
takes the rain from one side only
and with the other side
leans on someone

---

## 456. THE POSTWOMAN AND ONE HOUSE

To that house on the corner
she carries only flyers
sales on washing machines
insurance offers
furniture catalogues

never a letter
never a card
never an envelope
written by hand

Does that one get any mail? asks the neighbor
No says the postwoman
there you only slide
paper into tin

the box is always
half open
like a mouth
that waits for a word
and gets an advert

Year after year
flyers
sales
catalogues
and the name on the box
fades
until you can barely read it

One day in December
the postwoman stops
buys herself a postcard
with a cat in the snow
and writes it
herself

Keep well
from someone passing by

and slips it into that box
among the washing machine sales

because a lonely mailbox
doesn't need the whole world
it needs one hand
that remembers

---

## 457. THE PIANIST IN THE HOTEL LOBBY

He plays in the lobby
among the potted palms
among the leather armchairs
among people hurrying
to their suitcases
to the stairs
to their lives

no one listens

he plays softly
a waltz
then another
the notes fall
into the carpet
into the empty

Who are you playing for? asks the manager
For no one? he says
but he smiles
because he's lying

behind the desk stands
the night clerk
young
tired
his night is just as long
as the pianist's

and he listens
even staring at the guest book
even counting the keys
he listens with one ear
the one that doesn't sleep

At midnight the pianist stops
and the clerk lifts his head
One more? he asks
the slow one

and the pianist plays
the slow one
for him alone

because an empty lobby
is not empty
when two keep watch in it
and music needs only
one ear
that doesn't sleep

---

## 458. THE COIN IN THE COBBLES

Between two cobblestones
a coin is stuck
trodden
into the mud
into the years

people bend down for it
scratch at it with a nail
It won't come says one
It's holding says another
and they walk on

it stays there
a little yellow moon
in the grey stone

What's it worth? asks the child
Nothing says the father
since no one can pry it out

but the child looks
and the coin shines

the baker steps on it
the postwoman steps on it
the whole city steps on it
every sole
polishes it a little

year after year
it grows brighter
it glows like a small sun
driven into the ground

Do you see the riches? says the child
the kind no one owns
the kind that belong to all

because a wealth
that no one touches
belongs to the street
and the richest of all
is the pavement
where the poor walk

---

## 459. SOMEONE ELSE'S FLOWERS

The neighbor left
on holiday
to the sea
and he forgot
the flowers on his sill

geraniums
begonias
one drowsy fig tree

They'll dry out says the woman downstairs
They'll dry out says the mailman
and on they go

but the man next door
every evening
takes the watering can
climbs over the railing
and secretly waters
someone else's window

Whose are they? his daughter asks
I don't know he says
but they're thirsty

he waters them on Friday
he waters them on Sunday
he never signs
he never rings

The neighbor comes back
tanned
a suitcase of seashells
and he's amazed

They survived?
I thought they were done for

and the man next door
says nothing
and smiles into his can

because kindness
is often done
under a borrowed name
with no thanks
and watering
knows no fences

---

## 460. THE GIVEN STONE

The child came in from outside
with dirty knees
and in his hand
a stone

ordinary
grey
rounded by the river

For you he says
it's a treasure

The mother looks
at the stone
at the hand

A treasure? she asks
and what's inside it?

Nothing says the child
I just found it
and it was pretty
and I thought of you right away

The mother takes it
weighs it in her hand
cold
heavy
quite ordinary

and slips it into her pocket

She carries it on Fridays
she carries it in winter
she carries it for years
among the keys and the coins
she rubs it with her thumb
waiting for the tram
waiting for the doctor
waiting for nothing

people have rings
people have gold
she has a river stone

because worth
does not come from the thing
worth comes from the hand
that gives
and the heaviest stone in the world
is the one
given with love

---

## 461. THE LAST SNOWFLAKE

Winter is ending
the snow melts
the roofs drip
the puddles grow

and the last snowflake falls
one
the last
of the whole winter

Catch it says the brother
I'll catch it says the child
and holds out a palm

the flake comes down
slowly
it turns
as if unwilling

and lands
on the warm palm

I've got it cries the child
I've got the last snowflake!

but the palm is warm
and the flake
is only a drop

Where did it go? asks the child
It was here
it was white
and now it's water

and the child cries
cries that he didn't save it
that he held it
and it vanished

The mother wipes the tear
with that same palm

You did save it
she says
just not the way you wanted

because some kinds of beauty
can only be had
by letting them go
and to save a snowflake
means
to let it melt

---

## 462. THE WALLED-UP DOOR

In the old wall
a door remains
bricked up
during the works

an outline
an arch
a stone of another color
and no handle

it leads nowhere

Where did it lead? asks the child
Nowhere says the mason
nowhere now
behind it there's a brick
and behind the brick another brick

but people
passing in front of it
lower their voices

the man who's arguing
falls silent
the lovers stop laughing
the old woman nods hello

Why are you whispering? asks the child
I don't know says the old woman
it's just that by a door
you go quiet

because a door
even walled up
still promises
somewhere

behind it perhaps there was a room
behind it perhaps there was a garden
behind it perhaps there was someone
who was waiting

and people feel it
even through the brick

you can wall up a door
you can wall up a window
you can wall up a whole house

but possibility
cannot be walled up

---

## 463. THE OLD BRIDGE AND THE NEW

Across the river
they built a new bridge
wide
concrete
four lanes
traffic lights
signs

and beside it
they left the old one
of stone
narrow
closed to cars

Tear it down says the official
It'd be a shame says the mason
and they leave it standing

over the new bridge
trucks pass
buses pass
the whole city passes
in one long rush
hurrying

on the old bridge
there is no one

only in the evening
two arrive
lean on the railing
look into the water

Where are you going? asks the constable
Nowhere they say
we're already here

then comes an old man
to feed the pigeons
then comes a painter
with his easel
then comes a child
throwing twigs into the current
Which one gets there first?

because progress
takes the traffic
and the past
keeps the beauty

and every city
needs one bridge
where nobody
is in a hurry

---

## 464. THE COLLECTION OF POEMS

The poet has written
the last line
of the last poem

the manuscript lies on the table
thick
finished
two hundred and five poems
stacked
like bricks

Is it done? he asks himself
It is he answers
and doesn't believe it

he looks at that pile
at those years
at those mornings at the table
at those pencils gnawed to the middle

and he feels sad
because a finished thing
suddenly goes mute

then he stands up
he opens the window
Let the wind read it he says

and the wind comes in
takes the first sheet
then the second
then ten
then a hundred

and the poems fly
over the rooftops
over the squares
over the river

one lands in a mailbox
one in a passerby's pocket
one in a puddle
one on the lap of an old woman
on a bench
who happened to have nothing to read

The poet looks out the window
the empty table
the empty hands
the full air

and for the first time
he smiles

because a poem
does not belong to the one who wrote it
a poem belongs
to the one who finds it

and a collection is finished
only on the day
the author
lets it go from his hand

---

## 465. THE KNIFE GRINDER

Down the street comes a bicycle with a grindstone
and the bell announces ahead of it
KNIVES SCISSORS RAZORS SHARPENED
and the houses open up
like pocket knives

The butcher brings his heavy knives
the seamstress her wedding-dress scissors
the barber his father's razor
the grandmother her little apple knife
she has kept since her wedding day
and the boys a jackknife with a stag-horn handle
found down by the river

Nobody wants anything dull
a dull knife tears bread instead of cutting it
dull scissors chew the cloth
a dull razor insults the face
a dull scythe insults the grass

And the grinder pedals
the stone spins
sparks skip across the cobbles
a little penny fireworks show
and the knives go home
sharp and satisfied
as after a good night's sleep

In the evening when the bell has gone quiet
the last customer comes
and unwraps from a rag a soldier's knife

Sharpen it?
asks the grinder

The opposite
says the man
dull it
it comes from the war
and it still cuts too well
at night I can hear it

And the grinder says nothing
he pedals
and grinds it flat
a long time
longer than all the knives of the town put together
until it is smooth and blind
like a river pebble

What do I owe you?
asks the man

Nothing
says the grinder folding up his stone
all day long I have been sharpening
and once a day
a man should dull something
so this town can get some sleep

---

## 466. THE GLAZIER

He walks the town with panes on his back
transparent wings of window glass
and he calls out long and slow
GLAAAZIER
and the streets answer him with their cracks

Here boys with a ball
there frost that painted too hard
there a window from a quarrel
last night a door slammed
a little too close to the heart

People apologize
people are ashamed of broken windows
Don't you worry madam
says the glazier with a wink
the boys with the ball feed me
the frost feeds me
even your quarrel feeds me
long life to them all

One day his apprentice asks him
Master do you really love cracks?
I do
says the glazier

Because of the bread?
Not only

And he takes the apprentice to the edge of town
to a villa with a garden
the windows whole
not one crack in thirty years

Here nobody ever broke a window
says the glazier
because here nobody ran with a ball
nobody slammed a door
nobody leaned out too far
to wave at someone

Glass only cracks where people live
he says shifting the panes on his back
unbroken windows
are just another name
for an empty house

And he walks on calling
GLAAAZIER
and he wishes all the windows of the town
a long life
and every now and then
a good hard shot with a ball

---

## 467. THE BOOKBINDER

The workshop smells of glue leather and thread
on the table lies a hospital of books
grandmother's cookbook collapsed at the cakes
a hymnal that sang for a hundred years
a world atlas with America torn away
and fairy tales read by three generations
that fell apart from love

The bookbinder takes them in her hands
like a doctor
here the binding gave way
here the thread snapped
here the spine
books break at the spine
where they were opened most

A man has brought his grandmother's cookbook
he holds it in both hands
as if it might fly off page by page
Can it still be saved?
It can
says the bookbinder
anything can
as long as no pages are lost

And what is it really that holds it together
asks the man
a book like this

Thread
says the bookbinder
plain linen thread
glue and cloth
but that only holds the paper

And the book?
A book is held by whoever keeps coming back to it
a book nobody reads
falls apart even when it's new
look
and she lifts the fairy tales from the table
this one is in shreds
and it holds together better than all the rest

The man pays and turns at the door
And people?
what are people sewn with?

Thread too
says the bookbinder threading her needle
only you can't see it
and it also tears in the place
where a person opens most

---

## 468. THE ORGANIST WITHOUT AN ORGAN

On the church door hangs a sign
CLOSED FOR TECHNICAL REASONS
the technical reasons are in their tenth year
the office has the key
the office has office hours
and office hours have no time

Inside the organ is silent
three thousand pipes full of darkness
and the organist walks past
every Sunday at ten
as he always did
he stands a moment at the door
then goes off through the town

Mister organist
what do you play on now
asks the baker's boy

On the town
says the organist
listen
the wind in the chimneys
those are the flutes
the draught under the arcades
the principal
the tram on the bridge
the pedal basses
the rain on the tin roofs
the tremolo
and the pigeons on the ledge
vox humana
even if they are only pigeons

And it's true
when the wind comes up off the river
the whole town plays
chimneys houses wires doorways
and the organist in the middle of the street
closes his eyes
and his fingers run through the air
stop after stop

They locked the church
he says
they locked the organ
but music is only air
that remembered something
and air cannot be locked

Inside the church there is silence
and technical reasons
but on Sundays at ten
when the wind blows
the whole town plays the mass
and nobody can close it down
because you cannot hang
a sign on the air

---

## 469. THE TAXI DRIVER WHO TURNS OFF THE METER

The taxi waits at the station rank
and the meter counts
kilometres minutes waiting time
all of it fairly
penny by penny

But sometimes
the driver reaches out his hand
and click
the meter goes dark

At three in the morning to the maternity ward
when the man beside him clutches a little suitcase
and has forgotten how breathing works
click

The old man who wants to see the house
that isn't there any more
just a field and one pear tree
click

The woman with two bags
who says drive
just drive
and says nothing else
click

The young man in a borrowed black suit
going to the first funeral of his life
click

The young colleague at the rank shakes his head
You'll never get rich like that
I won't
says the driver

Then why do you do it?

The meter is an honest machine
says the driver
it counts kilometres
and it counts them well
but some rides are not in kilometres
the one to the maternity ward is measured in a whole life
the one to the pear tree in fifty years going back
and the one with the two bags
that one can't be measured at all

And who is going to pay you for them?

They're already paid
says the driver starting the engine
some rides were paid for long ago
before the passenger ever got in
only with those
you must not count

---

## 470. THE SUMMER CINEMA PROJECTIONIST

The open-air cinema behind the brewery
a screen stretched between two poplars
three hundred chairs under the stars
at the back the projection booth
and in the booth the projectionist
last season
next year they say a car park

No rain of spectators tonight
says the ticket lady closing her window
three tickets today
yesterday none

And the projectionist projects anyway
for three
for one
for nobody
the reel turns
the beam flies over the empty chairs
and in the beam dance the moths and the dust
the most faithful audience in the world

A man from the town hall came to look
Who are you showing this for
when nobody came?

For the moths
says the projectionist
for the poplars
and for the screen
forty years it has carried
lovers soldiers horses and the sea
a film that is never shown
dies in its can
would you let the sea die?

The man from the town hall writes that down
and leaves

On the last night of the season
the ticket lady sells a single ticket
to the projectionist
he loads the reel
starts the machine
runs down the steps
and sits in the middle of three hundred chairs

Forty years he showed films
and only today
for the first time
does he see one whole
from the right side

And the moths watch with him
and the poplars rustle along with the music
and when THE END appears on the screen
it isn't true
it's only a caption

---

## 471. THE MUSIC BOX REPAIRMAN

In the courtyard workshop a hundred small musics are sleeping
music boxes with cranks
ballerinas stopped mid-pirouette
brass cylinders studded with pins
like a starry sky
and combs that know one single song
but know it by heart

People bring them in broken
this one is silent
this one has lost three notes
and most often
this one is stuck
the same note over and over
one single note
like a hiccup
like a reproach

A woman brought in her mother's box
Swan Lake
stuck on one note since winter
It plays and plays that one place
says the woman
and I can't listen to it any more
and I don't know how to switch it off

The repairman winds the box
and listens
the note repeats
always the same
always the same way

It isn't an illness
says the repairman
just one bent pin on the cylinder
you see
the melody came to a certain place
and cannot get past it
so it goes back
and tries again
and again
like all of us

Then he takes his tweezers
straightens the pin
by a hair
and winds the spring

And Swan Lake stirs
passes the place
as if it were nothing
plays on
to the very end
and fades

The woman is crying
not for the note that kept sticking
but for the ones
that came after it

There there
says the repairman handing her the box
a melody must be allowed to finish
especially a sad one
finished it hurts once
stuck it hurts forever

---

## 472. THE STATION PORTER

At the station by platform two
stands a porter with his trolley
forty years now
and more fates have passed through his hands
than through the priest's and the doctor's put together

He knows the newlyweds' suitcase
light and skittish
almost empty
after all they mostly carry each other

He knows the travelling salesman's case
battered at the corners
like him

He knows the emigrant's suitcase
a whole flat pressed into thirty kilos
the eiderdown the photos the father's clock

He knows the soldier's case
the student's case tied up with string
the actress's trunk that weighs more than the actress
and the doctor's little bag
where somebody's life lies sleeping

The young stationmaster asks him
You always know where people are going?

Not where
says the porter
I only know whether they're going toward something
or away from something

And how can you tell?

By the weight
a suitcase going toward something is light
even when it's heavy
a suitcase going away from something is heavy
even when there's almost nothing in it

The heaviest suitcase of my life
was small and nearly empty
a comb a shirt and a photograph
I carried it for a lady to the morning train
and my arm ached for a week

What was in it?

Nothing
says the porter
that's just it nothing
everything else she had left at home

The weight of a suitcase isn't measured in kilos
he says taking hold of his trolley
it's measured by everything
that stayed behind at home

---

## 473. THE SIGN PAINTER

The sign painter stands on his ladder
scraping an old shop sign
the new owner calls up from below
Paint over all of it
it's going to say PHARMACY

And under the paint letters appear
GROCERIES
and under the groceries
HAVLÍČEK'S FINE FOODS
and under the fine foods
BAKERY FRANTIŠEK ČERMÁK AND SON
and under the bakery
only a golden shadow of lettering
whose name nobody can read any more

The whole street is written over under the paint
like an old envelope
address on top of address
nothing but addressees who moved away
to somewhere the post does not deliver

At the bakery the sign painter stops
that R with a little tail like a swallow
he knows that R
only one man ever made an R like that
his father
the ladder may have stood exactly here
and up on it a young fellow
whistling

Have you covered it all?
calls the owner from below

All covered
says the sign painter
and he paints the new name
black and gleaming
proper work properly done
only he thins the paint
a little more than usual

Businesses die
he says to himself up on the ladder
names just move one layer down
and one day another fellow will stand here with a scraper
and I want him
to have something to read

---

## 474. THE BEEKEEPER ON THE ROOF

On the roof of a tower block
eight floors above the street
between the aerials and the lift shafts
stand three beehives
and Mr Novák with a veil over his face

The neighbours shake their heads
Honey from the city? they say
from all this concrete?
from all this smoke?
honey comes from the countryside
like grandfather
like Sunday

The bees don't know that
in the morning they fly out over the street
and see what the neighbours never see
the lindens by the post office
the acacias along the tracks
Mrs Horáčková's balcony full of window boxes
mulleins on the demolition site
little gardens behind the garages
a whole city in bloom
that believes itself grey

In autumn Mr Novák rings the doorbells
a jar of honey in each hand
and on the box a sign
DON'T BELIEVE IT? TASTE IT

Mrs Horáčková tastes
and raises her eyebrows
How strange
it tastes like our balcony

Of course it does, says the beekeeper
your window boxes eighth floor
the linden by the post office ground floor
the whole street in one spoonful

And the neighbours stand in the corridor
jars of honey in their hands
and for the first time in all these years
they taste the house they live in
and it is sweet

---

## 475. THE HEDGEHOG AND THE AUTUMN LEAVES

All September the hedgehog made his bed
leaf by leaf
maple for sleeping
oak for a cover
linden under his head
a quilt for the whole winter

On Monday came the men in orange
with brooms and bags
and a paper from the town hall
DECREE ON AUTUMN CLEANING

They swept up the maple
swept up the oak
swept up the linden
they loaded winter into bags
and carted it off to the dump

The hedgehog stood on the bare lawn
and said nothing
hedgehogs don't go to town hall
hedgehogs don't file complaints
a hedgehog has only his spines
and against a decree that isn't enough

Only one sweeper
the oldest one
left in the corner of the park behind the bench
one pile unswept
he forgot it carefully
all afternoon he kept forgetting it
and in the evening he came back to check
that it was properly forgotten

In his report he wrote
sector twelve cleaned
except for the corner
the corner could not be finished
for technical reasons

And the technical reason
was already snuffling in the corner
burrowing into the oak and the maple
and sleeping
all winter it slept
on the only mistake
the town hall made that year
on purpose

---

## 476. THE MOLE AND THE GARDENER

The gardener had a lawn like a billiard table
English style
mowed on Sunday mornings
measured by eye and by ruler
the pride of the whole street

And under the lawn lived a mole
who knew nothing about billiards
and everything about soil

Morning after morning a new molehill
black as spite
and the gardener with his spade
with his hose
with traps from the hardware store
with a smoke bomb from the neighbour
waged the war
that gardeners have waged since forever
and that moles since forever have won

This is my garden
he shouted into the grass
I planted it
I water it
I mow it

And from below silence
only corridors of silence
a mole doesn't argue
a mole digs

Then one summer the drought came
the lawns on the street turned yellow
one after another
billiard tables of straw

Only that one lawn stayed green
drilled through and through
aired down to the deep
the rain from June remembered it
in the tunnels

And the gardener stood with his hose hanging
and for the first time in his life
he knelt down in the grass
and said in a downward direction
all right
you understand the cellar
I understand the roof
and the garden
the garden is the whole house

---

## 477. THE FOX IN THE CITY

At three in the morning the city belongs to the fox
the traffic lights blink amber
just for her
and she walks down the middle of the street
as if she'd come to pick up the keys

At the restaurant she inspects the bin
half a chicken
a whole loaf
an apple with nothing wrong with it
strange hunters these people
thinks the fox
they hunt all day long
and in the evening they throw away the catch

The shop windows stay lit
over coats that nobody wears
in the windows a blue flicker
people sitting at fires that give no warmth
and the dogs
the dogs sleep on cushions behind doors
and whimper in their dreams
because they can smell it
this street without a leash

The fox doesn't judge
the fox just takes notes
they built themselves a forest of stone
and are scared of the dark in it
they invented time
and it keeps running out on them
they shut themselves in dens
and they call the dens home

Only by the station she slows down
on a bench a man is sleeping
newspapers instead of leaves
this one is one of mine
thinks the fox
he sleeps outside too
he didn't fit in either

And before daybreak
she vanishes through the park
lies down under a bush and sleeps
and leaves them the city
lends it to them whole
until three in the morning

---

## 478. THE HEN WHO FLEW

In the Nováks' yard it happened on a Tuesday
the dog was chasing the hen
the hen was running
the fence was close
the wings remembered

Two metres
maybe three
she flew
over the ground over her shadow over her own fear
and she landed in the nettles
but she flew

The yard assembled that very evening
the rooster summed it up from the dunghill
hens do not fly
that is a scientific fact
the ducks nodded
the geese hissed their agreement

It was a jump, said the old goose
a jump with flapping
every one of us knows that

You didn't see it, said the hen
I was up there
I saw the henhouse roof from above
I saw all of you from above
you are smaller than you think

That settled it
whoever claims we are small
is a liar

Since then she doesn't speak of it
she pecks her grain like the others
only sometimes in the evening
she looks up over the fence
where the swallows pass

And the swallows know
swallows see everything from above
the yard the fence and that Tuesday
and when they fly past
they tip a wing to her
the way you greet
a colleague

---

## 479. THE OLD CARP IN THE FOUNTAIN

In the fountain on the square lives a carp
big as a forearm
grey as the cobblestones
an old resident

They bought him in December from the barrel
carried him home in a plastic bag
the bathtub was already filled
and in the kitchen the knife was being sharpened

But the child cried
and cried so well
that in the night the whole family
carried him in silence to the square
and let him go in the fountain
among the stone tritons

Since then he has survived everything
twenty Christmases
twenty carps that ended otherwise

People come to the fountain
and throw coins
they turn them in their fingers
close their eyes
make a wish
and toss

The carp swims among the wishes
the bottom glitters
love health money
the jackpot a trip around the world
the floor of the fountain is paved with desires

The children ask
will it come true?
and the parents say
only if you tell no one

The carp alone says nothing
and none of them knows
that one wish has already come true
that it swims there before their eyes
scaly and grey
for the twentieth year

the wish of a child
who one night in December
cried so loud
that Christmas itself
gave in

---

## 480. THE BATS IN THE CHURCH ATTIC

To the church on the hill
only three grandmothers and a draught still come
the priest drives in on the last Sunday of the month
the organist died
the bell rings only for funerals

But up in the attic
above the vault with its painted heaven
hang the bats
a whole parish of bats
head down
patient as candles

They sleep through all the sermons
but they keep watch with all the stars
they ask for nothing
they promise nothing
they are simply there
which of all the prayers
is the hardest one

The sexton wanted to drive them out
vermin he said
droppings on the beams
squeaking in the rafters
but the priest shook his head
let them be
they are the last ones
who haven't moved away

People get christened married buried
they lock the heavy door behind them
and go home
the bats stay
in December in July
they keep a watch under the roof
that nobody asked for

They hang on and they don't let go
the priest tells the grandmothers
all winter darkness and frost
and they hang on and they don't let go
I have no better definition of faith

And if God one day looked down
from above as is his habit
through the slate through the painted heaven
he would see the empty pews
and the parish of bats
and since from above everything is upside down
it would be exactly them
the only ones in the whole church
standing upright

---

## 481. THE SNAIL AND THE RACE

On the path along the gardens
a race was run that morning
nobody had announced it
but everybody ran

The ant overtook him
the lizard overtook him
the dog overtook him
and even the centipede
who has so many legs
he doesn't even have to try

Children on bicycles rang their bells
out of the way snail out of the way
you'll never get anywhere like that

Where? asked the snail
but the children were already gone

At noon the postman overtook him
in the afternoon a pensioner with a cane
and towards evening even the shadow
stretching out towards the east

All hurrying the same way
home
to shut the door behind them
and say at last

Only the snail was in no hurry
he carried his house on his back
all of it
kitchen bedroom roof
and wherever he liked it
there he was home

At night he slept in the middle of the path
while the windows went dark one by one
and if he could speak
he would have said
I didn't win
but I am the only one
who crossed the finish line
right at the start

---

## 482. THE MAGPIE AND THE WEDDING RING

She left it on the ledge by the sink
just for a moment
just while she did the dishes
a small gold band
with a date and a name engraved inside

A magpie doesn't read dates
a magpie doesn't read names
a magpie sees a shine
and a shine is a shine

Three seconds of wings
and all that was left of the ring
was a wet little circle on the sill

She cried all evening
thirty years she said
thirty years I never took it off
and now it's gone

The husband said nothing
and in the morning he borrowed a ladder

Up in the poplar in the nest
lay a treasure
a lemonade cap
a scrap of silver foil
a silver button
a piece of Christmas tinsel
and in the middle of it all
thirty years of marriage

He took the ring
and left the foil where it was
so she would have something too

Down in the kitchen he went down
on one knee that creaked by now
and slid the ring on her finger a second time
like back then
except both their hands were shaking

And as she laughed and cried at once
he said
you see
the magpie only stole the shine
that's the only thing that can be stolen
the rest
those thirty years
we carry somewhere else
where no magpie can reach

---

## 483. THE SQUIRREL AND THE FORGOTTEN NUTS

All autumn she worked
a hazelnut here
an acorn there
a beechnut under a stone
and she carefully remembered it all

Or so she thought

In winter she found the one under the spruce
and the one by the stump
but the third
the fifth
the ninth
the earth might as well have swallowed them

Silly squirrel laugh the chickadees
she forgot where she hid her supper

Except in spring
a little oak came up from the lawn
a hazel a bit further on
a beech by the fence
exactly there
where the squirrel forgot

The old gardener sees it every year
he leans on his rake and tells his grandson
you see that oak by the path
a hundred years old
which means a hundred years ago
some squirrel forgot
where it put its lunch

And the whole forest? asks the grandson

The whole forest
nods the gardener
the whole forest is forgotten nuts
memory feeds you for one winter
forgetting
plants trees

And up in the branches of that oak
planted by her great-great-grandmother
by losing it
a squirrel is hiding a nut right now
and already can't remember where

And that is just as well

---

## 484. THE BLACKBIRD WHO SANG IN DECEMBER

On the seventeenth of December
at half past four in the dark
a blackbird on an aerial
launched into his whole spring song
from beginning to end
leaving nothing out

The people at the bus stop raised their heads
out of their scarves and their phones

He's gone mad said a man in a hat
he's got the date wrong
It's the streetlights said a woman with shopping bags
they shine on him all night
and he thinks it's dawn
It's this warm winter said a student
nature is out of joint
it's a warning

Only a little girl asked
And what if he knows something?

The grown-ups smiled
how could he know
a blackbird has no calendar

Exactly said the little girl
that's why he can't get it wrong

The bus arrived
the doors opened
the doors closed
and it left three people lighter
because they stayed standing
under the aerial
in December
listening to spring

Whether the blackbird was mistaken
or knew something more
nobody ever found out
spring came as always
at the end of March
pretending
it had never dropped by in December

Only those three from the bus stop
ever since then every December
walk past the aerial
slowly
just in case

---

## 485. THE GRAMOPHONE AND ONE RECORD

The gramophone in the corner of the room
plays one record
always the same one
for years now

The grandson asks
Grandpa why don't you buy another?
The shop has crates of them
jazz waltzes the whole world
for next to nothing

Grandpa lifts the needle
gently
the way you lift a sleeping kitten

This record
has a scratch in the third song
the needle skips there every time
as if it stumbled on a doorstep

That's a flaw though says the grandson
they give your money back for that

It's not a flaw says grandpa
it's the evening
your grandmother danced in this room
and bumped the little gramophone table
laughing as she did it
and the record wrote it all down

A new record would play music
clean music without one mistake
this one plays that evening
little table and all
laughter and all

And so the gramophone in the corner
plays one record
and when the needle skips
grandpa half closes his eyes

In that spot it isn't the needle skipping
it's the heart

---

## 486. THE TYPEWRITER MISSING A LETTER

An old typewriter
types every letter
except one
the M jammed
some thirty years ago

Buy a new one said the dealer
this thing belongs in a museum

But the old man bought nothing
he learned to go the long way round
instead of sea he writes ocean
instead of a man in love he writes a lost man
instead of I love you he writes
you know

It can be done he says
language is wide as a river
there's always another bank

Only one word
cannot be gone around
Marie

Marie is Marie
no language in the world
has a second word for Marie

And so each time
a letter arrives at her name
the old man rolls the page up a little
takes his fountain pen
and writes the missing letter in by hand

His letters are straight
and silver as railway tracks
and right in the middle of them
one letter slightly trembling
warm
alive

The whole letter is typed by machine
only she
only she is written by hand

---

## 487. THE SUITCASE COVERED IN STICKERS

In the attic under the beam
sleeps a suitcase
covered in stickers
HOTEL BRISTOL VIENNA
GRAND HOTEL NAPOLI
ISTANBUL
LISBOA
and one with a camel

The granddaughter runs her finger over it
as over a globe
Grandpa you've been everywhere
why don't you go anywhere anymore?

Grandpa slowly turns the suitcase around
You see here on the side
this empty spot?

I was saving it
for the most beautiful city
I looked for it all my life
Vienna was all whipped cream
Naples shouted its beauty from every window
Istanbul smelled so good nobody slept
and in Lisbon I cried
and to this day I don't know why

But the sticker of the most beautiful city
isn't sold at any kiosk in the world

Why not?

Because the most beautiful city
is known by one thing only
it's where you unpack the suitcase
carry it up to the attic
and don't need it for fifty years

The granddaughter looks around
at the old attic
the beams the roof above her head

So that's why the spot is empty

It isn't empty says grandpa
that is the sticker
it just can only be read
from the inside

---

## 488. DAD'S READING GLASSES

Dad left few things behind
a watch that kept going
a coat that fit his brother
a small box of fishing hooks
and reading glasses
in a case with one worn corner

The son put them on
just like that
to try
the way you try on another man's life

And the world went blurry
the window melted
the street vanished in fog
the mountains beyond the town dissolved

Only the near stayed sharp
his own hands
the newspaper on the table
the photo in its frame
the crumbs beside the bread

So this is how dad saw
the son says to himself
the distances blurred
and up close every little letter

All my life I blamed him
for not caring about the world
for never asking about my travels
only how are you
what are you eating
aren't you cold

Dad didn't see far
dad saw near
and near
is where we were sitting

Now the son wears those glasses at night
when the newsprint starts to swim
they fit him at the very first try
as if the optician had ground them for two

Eyes are inherited
only slowly
you have to grow into them

---

## 489. THE NIGHT-SHIFT THERMOS

Thirty years of night shifts
thirty years of three in the morning
boiler room shop floor gatehouse
and in the bag a thermos
green dented
with a lid that serves as a cup

At three in the morning
the town is cold as a door handle
the dark stands leaning on the gate
and a man could almost believe
there's no one left in the world

Then you unscrew the lid
and the thermos steams
hot tea
hot after twelve hours
sugared exactly right

The maker printed on it
KEEPS HEAT 12 HOURS
thirty years says the workman
and counting

Now he's retired
the thermos stands on the shelf
and sometimes he wakes at three
just like that
out of habit
the body remembers its shifts

One night he asked his wife
how that tea
stayed hot the whole night through

A thermos holds heat says his wife
but it doesn't make any
someone has to pour the heat in
evening after evening
for thirty years

The workman holds the lid in both hands
like back then on the shop floor
All those years I thought
it was a good thermos

It was says his wife
pouring the tea
but even a thermos
is only a messenger

---

## 490. GREAT-GRANDMOTHER'S SEWING MACHINE

In the corner of the bedroom stands
a sewing machine
treadle-worked
black with the gold letters SINGER
great-grandmother's

It sewed the wedding dress of twenty-nine
little shirts for four children
cut from a single bolt of cloth
aprons and trousseau monograms
curtains for three kitchens

In the war it turned coats inside out
the lining became the face
and for a little while
the poverty didn't show

It shortened trousers handed down
lengthened sleeves before winter
and the black dress it sewed twice
that one is not spoken of at home

Now on top of it stand
a plant in its pot
photos in silver frames
wedding christening wedding
and no one in the family remembers
the true rhythm of the treadle
heel toe heel toe

But it doesn't sew anymore says the son-in-law
it just takes up space

It doesn't take up space says grandmother
running her palm along the iron
it holds it

And maybe she is right
a hundred years it held the family together
stitch by stitch
dress by dress

Now it sews no longer
and holds the family still
photo beside photo
like an altar
where the service goes on

---

## 491. LAST YEAR'S CALENDAR

In the kitchen hangs a new calendar
it does its job honestly
page after page it runs
right now July
with a picture of a flowering meadow

But in the hallway
still hangs last year's
open at October
last year's October
with a picture of rowan berries

Visitors correct it with their eyes
That one can come down now
they say carefully
it's the new year after all
and half of it gone

You forgot to take it down

We didn't forget
says the lady of the house
and wipes the dust off the rowan berries

That October
someone still walked down this hallway
someone who cannot
be turned to November

But time moves on
say the visitors

Let it move says the lady of the house
through the kitchen let it move
there we don't stand in its way
but one wall in the house
one wall has the right to stand still

And so July ripens in the kitchen
meadows harvests and snow take turns
and in the hallway
the same rowan berries bloom on

The only month in the world
that never ended
because nobody turned it

---

## 492. THE CRACKED CUP

In the cupboard behind glass
stand the cups for guests
white with a blue rim
whole
without a single flaw
six of them
like soldiers on parade

And in front on a hook
hangs one cracked cup
glued back together
the crack like lightning
across a whole porcelain sky

We don't give it to guests
a guest might say
good heavens it's broken
and he would be right
and he wouldn't be

The daughter asked one day
mum why do you drink from the cracked one
we have whole ones after all

And mum turned the cup in her hands
This one cracked the morning
grandpa died
it slipped from my fingers
and dad glued it that evening
two hours he sat over the pieces
not speaking
that was his way of crying

Whole cups says mum
can still be broken
this one has that behind it
there is nothing in the world
you can drink from more calmly

And that is why the guests
get porcelain white as snow
and we at home get this one
with the lightning

Guests are given
what is whole
the family is given
what is true

---

## 493. THE SLED IN SUMMER

July
the asphalt melting in the yard
wasps besieging the apricots
and in the cellar under the stairs
between the preserves and the old bicycle
a sled is waiting

Wooden
with rusty runners
the rope coiled up
like a sleeping snake

The boy finds it
while hunting for his ball
drags it out to the yard
sits on it
in the middle of summer
and doesn't move

A sled in the grass
a boat on dry land

Mum why won't it go?
Wait for the snow
How long?
Half a year
Half a year!

For the boy an eternity
for the sled nothing at all

A sled knows how to wait
it's the sled's trade
eleven months of dark
for three weeks of glory
and not so much as a creak

Everything in the cellar is waiting anyway
the preserves wait for the Sunday cake
the skis wait for the mountains
the tinsel waits for Christmas
things never ask how much longer
things know
that everything has its snow

And then one December morning
the boy looks out the window
and flies down the stairs
straight to the cellar

The sled is ready
of course it's ready
it was ready the whole time

It was only winter
that was taking its time

---

## 494. THE OFFICIAL STAMP

All day long it jumps
from ink pad to paper
from ink pad to paper
thump and it's the truth
thump and it's official
thump and you may be born
thump and you may build
thump and you may be buried

Without it a paper is just paper
an application just a plea
a certificate just words
and words are not to be trusted

One Friday around four
the clerk's tired hand slipped
and the stamp came down beside the form
right onto the desk

A round imprint
with the emblem in the middle
in the plain wood

The clerk froze
she wanted to wipe it off
and then she looked at it
for a long time

For the first time in twenty years it struck her
how much of the world
runs around without a stamp
rain falls without a permit
starlings head south without a form
love has no file number
and yet it comes into force
on the day of delivery

Birth has a stamp
marriage has a stamp
death has a stamp
only the life in between
walks the world uncertified
and strangely enough it counts

She kept the desk
never had it replaced
it stands there to this day
the only piece of furniture in the building
officially certified
to exist

The people queuing in the corridor
exist meanwhile just like that
at their own risk

---

## 495. THE WAITING ROOM

The doctor's waiting room
smells of iodine and old paper
three coats on the coat rack
that have never met

A man with his arm in a sling
a girl coughing into her sleeve
an old lady with a shopping bag
sitting exactly one chair apart
as politeness requires

Magazines on the table
the princess is getting married
the princess divorced long ago
the horoscope says what to do in August
which August it was
nobody knows anymore
a recipe for perfect Christmas cookies
from a Christmas long since digested

Nobody speaks
and yet they understand each other
one cough answers another
one sigh another sigh
it is a shared silence
like a blanket over the knees

Next, calls the nurse

You go first, the old lady tells the man with the arm
I'm in no hurry
and then to the girl
go on, dear, you have school
I have all the time in the world

When the waiting room is empty
the doctor comes to the door
Mrs Nováková, nothing wrong again?

Nothing, doctor
at home the silence is only mine
here for a while it was ours

---

## 496. THE LAST NEWSSTAND

The newsstand on the corner
is small as a confessional
and Mr Souček has kept it
for fifty years

The sports paper for Mr Krejčí
crosswords for Mrs Bláhová
the political daily for the doctor
who curses as he reads it
and takes it all the same

Mr Souček knows everyone by name
and by name he also knows the ones
who come no longer
Mr Krejčí left in April
his sports paper lay on the counter for a week
before Mr Souček admitted to himself
that Mr Krejčí no longer needed the scores

The young don't buy papers
they carry the news in their pockets
faster than ink
Mr Souček, what's paper for
when it's all on the phone?

It's all there, nods Mr Souček
only in there nobody says
good morning Mr Novák
is your mother home from the hospital yet?

One Monday the newsstand is closed
Tuesday too
on Wednesday a sign appears in the window
written in a stranger's hand

The news that the last newsstand had closed
ran in no newspaper
it went from mouth to mouth
along the corner the street the square
the way news used to travel
before

---

## 497. THE CARPET RACK

In the courtyard between the houses
stands the iron carpet rack
painted green once
the green no longer remembers

On Friday mornings the women came
carpets over their shoulders
and beat them
dust flew up into the sun
and in it you could see
a whole week of one household

Nobody beats carpets anymore
they're vacuumed at home in silence
the dust is carried out in sealed bags
like a secret

But the children
the children hang from it upside down
doing rolls and flips and cartwheels
perching on it like sparrows
settling from up there
all the affairs of the courtyard

In spring a man came with a clipboard
measured the rack and wrote
installation no longer serves its purpose
removal recommended

No purpose? asks a girl
hanging upside down at that very moment
it serves
it holds the sky down
and the earth up
no other installation in the courtyard can do that

The man with the clipboard looked at the sky below
and for the first time in years
his head spun
He left the box empty

The rack still stands
the only installation in the courtyard
ever to be promoted
from work
to play

---

## 498. THE PASSAGEWAY

Between Long Street and Short Street
there is a passageway
it isn't on the maps
it isn't on the signs
the GPS swears there's a wall there

And yet every day it carries
a hundred pairs of feet
the baker's wife shortening her morning
schoolboys stretching their way home from school
a man with a dog
the dog knows it better than the man

The passage has seen
a first kiss in the alcove
a last quarrel in the same spot
a man sheltering from the rain
who met his future wife
sheltering from the same rain
a boy crying there so they wouldn't see at home
a thief ran through it once
and the constable ran through after him
both knew the way
both were local

A tourist stands over his map
shaking his head
there's nothing here, he tells his phone
and the phone agrees

And past him goes a grandmother with her shopping
she vanishes into the wall
as if she walked through it every day
because she walks through it every day

A city has arteries
those are on the maps
and then it has veins
that only the body knows

---

## 499. THE SECOND-HAND BOOKSHOP

In the second-hand bookshop by the bridge
the books smell
of being finished
or left unfinished
you can tell by the spine

On the first page of the first volume
I love you, Christmas 1961
price in pencil twenty crowns

In a book of verses about the sea
To my Anička for her graduation, Dad
Anička may have grandchildren by now
and her graduation lies here
for fifteen

In a cookbook
Good luck at the stove, Mother
next to the recipe for beef in cream sauce
a stain of beef in cream sauce
the recipe proved itself

A customer leafs through
and suddenly looks up
Sir, this dedication
couldn't it be erased?
It spoils the book

The bookseller adjusts his glasses
It couldn't, young man
and even if it could
I wouldn't do it

That lady from sixty-one
may be gone by now
may have long forgotten
but somebody loved her at Christmas
and wrote it down
and it's the only sentence in the whole book
that doesn't come from another book

The book costs twenty crowns
the dedication comes free
same as back then

---

## 500. THE PLAYGROUND AFTER DARK

The playground after dark
is like a theatre after the show
the sets still standing
the actors gone to bed

The sandbox full of footprints
small as a palm
a sandcake flagged with a popsicle stick
a forgotten shovel
red
it will be looked for in the morning

The climbing frame holds the shape
of the last jump
the goal without a net remembers
all eleven goals of the day
even the tenth
that caused the argument

And the swing
the swing is swinging
by itself
back and forth
back and forth
less and less

Physics says wind
a mother at the window says come to bed
the night watchman says there's nothing there
and walks on just in case

But the swing knows what it knows
it is swinging out the day
the way a bell keeps ringing
after no one strikes it

It stops at the exact moment
when at the last window of the neighborhood
the last child falls asleep

And in the morning
first feet on the sand
first hands on the chains
wind it up again
like a clock

---

## 501. THE POST OFFICE ONE MINUTE BEFORE CLOSING

The post office closes at six
at five fifty-nine
the clerk already holds the keys
the rubber stamp is asleep
the parcel scale yawns

And then the door flies open
a man
he has been running
you can tell by his breath
in his hand a letter
held like a bird
so it won't fly away

Are you still taking mail?
We're closing, sir

I know
that's why I ran

The clerk looks at the clock
then at the envelope
the address written with care
and yet crossed out once
not a familiar name in a woman's hand
a woman's name in a man's hand

You've been writing this letter a long time, haven't you

Three years, says the man
it was written in one evening
three years I carried it in my pocket
and this morning I told myself
today
or never
and all day long it was almost never

The clerk takes the letter
the stamp wakes up
today's date falls on the postage
like a signature

One minute to six, she says and smiles
you made it

Letters posted one minute before closing
weigh more than parcels
the others could have waited till tomorrow
this one would not have survived
the night

---

## 502. THE MANNEQUIN IN THE WINDOW

In the fashion house on the square
a mannequin stands in the window
thirty years now
the same smile
the same gaze
through the glass and out

They dress her by the seasons
in January the fur coat
in April the raincoat
in June the flowered dress
in September the coat again
the mannequin doesn't object
fashion always comes back
she knows that better than anyone

The shopgirls have come and gone
three generations
the first one walks past with a cane now
and always stops at the glass
Hello, she says without a sound
it still looks good on you

The mannequin looks out
thirty years in the same direction
she saw parades and funerals
she saw children become mothers
and mothers grandmothers
the people outside change clothes by the seasons too
only their smiles don't last as long

Then the shop closed down
the goods were carted away
the mannequin they left
she stands in the empty window
undressed
still smiling

And only now
have people noticed her
only now do they look away
for thirty years they looked at the dresses
never knowing
that all along
someone was looking at them

---

## 503. THE VILLAGE LOUDSPEAKER

At noon the loudspeaker crackles
on the pole by the chapel
and the village lifts its head

Announcements
a child's glove was found outside the shop
blue with a white thumb
will the owner please come to the shop

The poultry farm announces
pullets for sale on Friday

The village office reminds you
sirens will be tested on Sunday
do not be alarmed

And further
it is with deep sorrow that we announce
the passing of
Mr Antonín Vávra carpenter
the funeral is Friday at two

All in one voice
in one tone
the glove the pullets Mr Vávra
a stranger would find it odd
is death announced like a glove?

It is
the village is a single ear
and what is lost
is lost to everyone
the glove for a week
Mr Vávra for good
the tone is the same
because a loss is a loss
only the size differs

Only once did the voice break
in the middle of an announcement
it is with deep sorrow that we announce
the passing of
Mrs Marie Doležalová

and then a pause
long as the whole village

the announcer's wife
the voice finished quietly
and the village at its midday plates
put down its spoons
and stood
the way one stands in church

Since that day everyone knows
why gloves and people are announced the same
that tone must be held with both hands
so it doesn't break
because you never know
whose name you will be reading
tomorrow

---

## 504. THE STAIRCASE WITHOUT A LIFT

House number twelve has no lift
it has a staircase
ninety-six steps
four floors
and on every floor two lives

On the ground floor the lady who bakes
her scent rises free of charge
all the way to the fourth
on the first the man with the cello
he plays slow things
the stairs hear them whole

On the second the young couple
they take the stairs two at a time going up
three at a time coming down
once they carried a pram up
and since then they count the steps too

On the third nobody
the flat is for rent
the steps outside it are the quietest

And on the fourth Mr Řehoř
eighty-two years old
ninety-six steps
half an hour to the top
one stop on every landing
at the baking lady's floor he breathes in
at the cellist's he listens
at the young couple's he smiles
at the empty flat he takes off his hat

The doctor tells him
Mr Řehoř, change flats
take the ground floor
spare your heart

And Mr Řehoř shakes his head
Doctor
on the ground floor I'd be home at once
and then what?
This way it takes me an hour to come home
and on the way I meet the whole house
the scent the music the children the silence

The young ones run the stairs in a minute
and know nothing about them
I know every step by name
the step that creaks
the step that keeps quiet
the step where I start taking off my shoes

A lift would carry me up fast
like a parcel
I'd rather climb slowly
like a house
that over four floors is learning
to be the sky

---

## 505. FOG OVER THE RIVER

This morning the river breathed in
so deeply
that it breathed out the whole city

First the towers vanished
then the bridges
then the houses on the embankment
finally the sidewalk across the street

The sounds stayed
a tram rings from nowhere
a bakery smells from nowhere
footsteps come from nowhere
and pass you before
you learn whose they are

The world is suddenly small
within arm's reach
the lamppost rises out of the milk
only once you can touch it
and behind you it's already gone

Along the embankment a white cane taps
straight ahead without hesitating
Aren't you afraid of getting lost?
asks someone nobody can see

Me? laughs the man with the cane
I've walked this way for thirty years
listen and follow me
today at last the city is
the way I know it

And so he walks in front
and behind him footsteps
a whole invisible line
across a bridge that isn't there
toward a shore that may yet be

At noon the fog lifted
the towers went back to their places
the bridges the houses the sidewalk across
people breathed a sigh of relief
and stopped listening

Everything could be seen again
and everything was far away again

---

## 506. RAINBOW OVER THE HOUSING ESTATE

All day it rained on the estate
conscientiously and in gray
gray rain on gray buildings
everything as it should be

Toward evening the sun came out
and over the concrete blocks a rainbow
stretched from one tower to the next
seven colors
no scaffolding no permit

The boy on the fifth floor shouts
Mom come to the window
our building's in color

The lady with the shopping bags
sets them on the sidewalk and looks
the bus driver stops
a little past the stop
and nobody minds

Only the man with the briefcase doesn't look up
A rainbow, he says, so what
a rainbow won't pay the rent
and he walks on about his business

The tenants' meeting has voted for years
on a new facade
and there's never any money
and now all the buildings got one
at once
for free
in colors the meeting
never dared dream of

It lasted four minutes
maybe five
then the rainbow faded
and the buildings were gray again

Only the boy on the fifth floor
kept looking a while longer
then told his mother
Don't worry
gray is only for a while too
it's just a longer while

---

## 507. THE PUDDLE

After the rain there stayed on the square
a puddle
big as a table
deep as the sky

The grown-ups walk around it
in a wide arc
they look at their shoes
they look at their watches
that puddle again says a man with a briefcase
somebody should do something about it

Cars drive right through it
without knowing
they are driving through the sky
they splash the clouds onto the pavement
the clouds trickle slowly back
and the sky settles down again
patient as water

Only a child in red rubber boots
stops at the edge
and leans over it like a well
Mum the church tower is in there
and it's upside down
and a bird is flying in it
and a cloud that looks like a dog
and there's you

Come on it's only dirty water
says the mother
looking at her phone
where the sky is shining too
but only the forecast

By evening the puddle had dried
the sky went back up
to where the grown-ups
never look either

The child knows it
and that is why he'll wait
for the next rain

---

## 508. FROST ON THE WINDOWPANE

In the night a painter came
nobody heard him ring
he has no name no ladder
no studio no price list
he paints only when everyone is asleep
and only on windows

By morning he had painted on the glass
ferns that grow nowhere
a forest that stands nowhere
silver gardens
feathers of ice birds
a whole exhibition
ADMISSION FREE

The child wakes up first
and stands in pyjamas at the window
as if in a gallery
Granny come and look
someone painted us a forest in the night

And because he wants to point out
the little path between the ferns
he breathes on the glass

And the forest is gone
only a round porthole remains
and in it an ordinary yard
dustbins and bare trees

Grandmother brings the tea
Don't cry
the painter isn't angry
he paints the same thing every freezing night
a hundred years now
and every morning someone wipes it away with a breath

Then why does he paint it

That's exactly why says grandmother
if it lasted
it would only be a curtain

---

## 509. THE GALE AND THE PAPER KITE

A gale like nothing in years
bends the poplars over the town
carries off hats and umbrellas
and on the hill stands a boy
holding a kite on a string
a kite of newspaper
with a tail made of his mother's ribbons

The wind leans in
the string sings
it sings higher and higher
and then it snaps

The kite climbs
higher than the church
higher than the crows
a little paper dot
and then nothing

It tore loose on me
the boy says to the old man
walking his dog on the hill
it tore loose and it's gone

It didn't tear loose says the old man
it let go

Is there a difference?

A big one says the man
what tears loose falls
what lets go flies

But I wanted to keep it

I know
we all want to hold
what flies

The boy walks home
an empty spool in his hand
and a piece of string
He doesn't throw it away
because at the other end
even if you can't see it anymore
the sky is still tied on

---

## 510. THE FIRST STORM OF THE YEAR

All winter and all spring
the sky did nothing but whisper
and suddenly in May
it clears its throat
till the glasses rattle in the cupboard

First thunder
and everyone to the windows
the lady from the ground floor with a dish towel in her hand
the schoolteacher with his tea half drunk
the barber with his customer half trimmed
the children with their noses on the glass

The dogs knew it first
a whole hour before
now they're under the table
and the cat behind the wardrobe
and nobody laughs at them

The children count
one two three
and thunder
three kilometres reports the father
as if the storm were a train
and he knew the timetable

Then the rain comes
all at once
ropes of water
the street disappears
and in the archway stand people
who never once said hello
and now they are silent together
which is more

And then it's over
the dogs crawl out
the windows open
and the air
the air is suddenly new
washed and ironed
exchanged piece for piece
without a word without forms

And the people from the archway drift off
and greet each other
for the first time in years
it took the sky shouting
for them to notice
they were standing side by side

---

## 511. INDIAN SUMMER

Summer doesn't leave by train
it has no suitcases
no platform
it goes away on foot
and slowly
like someone who keeps turning around

A silver thread above the stubble
a second thread across the lane
a third one lands on your sleeve
that's the spiders moving summer out

They wrap it in silver paper
thread by thread
day by day
and send it off by wind
airmail
no address and no stamp
it will deliver itself
next year

The sun is still warm
but slanting now
like a hand waving goodbye
wasps stagger in the fallen pears
the smoke of potato haulms
rises straight as a candle
and the swallows sit on the wires
like notes
at the end of a song

Grandpa when does summer end
asks the child

It doesn't end says the grandfather
it says goodbye
and that's the whole difference
what ends slams the door
what says goodbye comes back

And when a gossamer thread
lands on your cheek
don't brush it off
that's summer taking its leave of you
in person

---

## 512. HAIL ON THE GREENHOUSE

In June on a scorching afternoon
the sky turned black
and let down hail
hailstones big as pigeon eggs
five minutes
the storm needed no more

The gardener walks over the shards
counting out loud
twelve panes
fifteen
twenty-two
the tomatoes bruised
the lettuces in ribbons
only the seedlings under the bench
act as if nothing had happened

The neighbour calls over the fence
What a disaster eh?

It's a bill says the gardener
disaster looks different

And what are you going to do

Sweep says the gardener
then reglaze
the glazier is a friend
by Saturday there'll be new glass

And the sky?
Who will reglaze the sky for you?

The gardener looks up
through the hole in the greenhouse roof
where between the black clouds
a patch of blue is already shining

The sky reglazes itself
he says
look
it's working on it now
and it never charges
not for the glass
not for the labour

---

## 513. THE MELTING SNOWMAN

He stood by the gate since December
a saucepan on his head
a carrot in the middle of his face
two pieces of coal
and a broom under his arm
like a soldier guarding the garden
who never asks for pay

In March he began to lose weight
a little every day
first the saucepan fell
then he leaned over
as if taking a bow
and one morning
all that was left on the lawn
was a carrot
two pieces of coal
an old broom
and a wet patch
where the grass is greener than anywhere else

Did he die? asks the child

Snow doesn't die says the grandfather
snow only changes its address
right now it lives in the brook
next week in the river
in summer it will be a cloud
and in December it will come back
all white
as if freshly ironed

The child gathers up the carrot
the coals and the saucepan
and puts them away in a shoebox

What are you doing?

I'm keeping his things for him
says the child
people who go far away
always leave their keys
with the neighbours

---

## 514. THE SMELL OF RAIN ON HOT ASPHALT

All day the city glowed
like a stovetop
trams melted along their rails
pigeons sat in the shade of statues
and the air shimmered over the car hoods
DO NOT FEED THE PIGEONS
KEEP OFF THE GRASS
even the signs were thirsty

Then in the evening the first drop fell
big as a coin
and hissed on the asphalt
as on a red-hot pan
a second a third a tenth

And suddenly that smell
that nobody knows how to name
and everybody knows

The clerk with his briefcase lifts his nose
the shopgirl steps out of her shop
the driver rolls down his window
and everyone remembers something
holidays at grandmother's
a garden after a storm
things they never lived through
and miss all the same

That was the city remembering
that it is earth
under the asphalt under the cables
under the car park under the cellars
the soil is still lying there
a hundred years in the dark
and it remembers the rain

And that smell
it isn't the rain
it's a greeting from below
the earth telling the rain
I am here
I am still here
they only paved me over

---

## 515. THE FIRST WHISTLE

Grandfather can whistle like a blackbird
whistle like a train
whistle for the neighbours' dog
and even for the waiter in the café
which is not done
and that is exactly why he does it

The grandson is six
and nothing comes from his lips but air
a small silent wind
that calls nobody

All afternoon on the doorstep
the boy purses his mouth like grandfather
tongue down says grandfather
not that far down
blow softer
blow like you're cooling soup
blow like you don't want to wake your mother

Nothing
nothing again
only air
and the tears are not far off

Grandfather is in no hurry
grandfathers are never in a hurry
that is why grandsons love them
he peels an apple and says
it took me a whole summer to learn
and in the end a blackbird taught me
because he kept laughing at me

And then
between one nothing and the next nothing
it happens
a thin note
crooked
tiny
but a note

The boy is startled by his own mouth
Grandpa did you hear that?
I heard says grandfather
and he says nothing more
because the blackbird on the fence
has just answered

---

## 516. THE DRESS IN THE BOX

High up in the wardrobe
above the sweaters above the bed linen
above the box with the taxes
there is one more box
white
its lid tied with a ribbon

It is opened rarely
on an anniversary
after a big spring cleaning
and sometimes for no reason
when it rains outside
and the radio plays something old

The woman pulls up a chair
takes the box down
folds back the tissue paper
and there it lies
lace gone slightly yellow
small buttons like a row of moons
and a scent
they no longer sell anywhere

She never puts it on
never even holds it against herself
she only lays her palm
on all that white
the way you lay a palm
on the forehead of a sleeping child

Her daughter asked her once
mum why don't you give it away
it wouldn't fit you anymore

It wouldn't fit
says the woman
folding the tissue paper back
but that's not why I keep it
I don't keep it to wear it

A dress isn't worn all your life
she says tying the ribbon again
a dress is kept
so that day
has somewhere to live

---

## 517. THE FIRST GREY HAIR

In the morning at the bathroom mirror
between the brushes and the bottles
between the steam and the hurry
the woman finds it

One hair
white as a thread from another sweater
insolent
shining away there
in the middle of all that dark brown
as if it belonged

She leans towards the mirror
fingers already ready
the tweezers wait on the shelf
executions are held in the morning

The man stands in the doorway with his coffee
and watches
What says the woman
I've got my first grey hair
and stop looking

And the man takes a sip of coffee
and says
Don't take it from me

Don't take it from me
that one is mine
it grew on you that winter
when the little one was sick
and you didn't sleep for three nights
and every morning you told me
everything was fine

The woman stands there
fingers in the air
the tweezers stay on the shelf

Since then they multiply
like stars when evening comes
and she doesn't count them

Only sometimes at the mirror
she finds a new one
and wonders
which night this one
grew for him

---

## 518. DANCING IN THE KITCHEN

On the stove the soup is bubbling
on the board an onion waits
half chopped
the knife is resting
on the radio between the news and the weather
suddenly
that song

The one from that summer
when they had one umbrella
and half a rent

The man wipes his hands on the tea towel
and bows
ridiculous
ceremonious
like a waiter in a film
May I have this dance

But the dinner
says the woman
Dinner can wait
says the man
songs can't wait
songs last three minutes

And so they dance
between the table and the fridge
between the chair and the stove
in slippers
a hip bumps the saucepan
the steam plays fog for them
the cat watches from the windowsill
like an audience
that already knows the programme

The neighbour downstairs lifts her head
from her knitting
they've dropped something again
she says
and she doesn't know
that up there above her ceiling
there is right now a summer
forty years old

The song ends
the announcer reads the weather
cloudy tomorrow
the soup has boiled over a little

Let it boil over
says the woman stirring
youth too only comes back
for three minutes
and it too
always burns something

---

## 519. THE INHERITED LAUGH

The father left behind
a watch that needs winding
a raincoat
a drawer of screws
that will come in handy someday
and a laugh

The laugh did not stay in the drawer
it moved into the son
whole
with the breath at the beginning
with the little choke at the end
with the hand
that has to slap the knee

The son doesn't know it
laughs don't know themselves

Visitors come
the aunt from Brno the old colleague the neighbour
they sit around the cake
somebody says something funny
and the son laughs

And the aunt from Brno goes pale
the colleague puts down his spoon
the neighbour looks at the door
as if someone were about to come in

Good lord says the aunt
that's him all over
it can't be
my heart nearly stopped

Only the mother doesn't raise her head
she calmly pours more coffee
and pushes the cake towards her son
let him go on laughing

The visitors are frightened
because they hear a dead man

She hears a living one
she has known it for a long time
death takes everything at once
but it only knows how to give back
in small pieces

And it begins with the laugh

---

## 520. THE LAST FAMILY PHOTOGRAPH

It's a bad photo
you have to admit

Grandfather is talking
so his mouth is open
the aunt has just blinked
the children are fighting over the dog
the dog is a blur
because he alone understood
you should keep moving while you can
grandmother is looking left
where somebody out of frame
has just said something funny
and the uncle is holding a cake
already missing a corner

Nobody is looking at the camera
nobody is standing straight
nobody said cheese

Because nobody knew
it was the last time

If they had known
they would have stood in a row
combed
solemn
arms at their sides
as on an official document
if they had known
it would be a photograph of sorrow

This way it's a photograph of happiness
of one ordinary afternoon
that doesn't look at the camera
because it's far too busy
with the cake
with the dog
with whatever someone on the left is saying

Last photos are always careless

That is how you can tell
that nobody was saying goodbye
that everybody thought
next time
next time we'll take a proper one

---

## 521. THE CROOKED STAR

In the box of decorations
everything is ordered like a jeweller's shop
glass baubles from great-grandmother
swaddled in cotton wool
little angels from Vienna
bells that really ring
a garland measured to the ceiling

And on top
in a matchbox
a star

Made of paper
crooked
one point longer than the others
the gold paper is peeling
in the glue you can see a thumbprint
about the size of a bean

Made in nursery school
twenty-seven years ago
its author at present
is paying off a mortgage in another town

And yet every year
before the baubles from Vienna
before the angels
before the bells that really ring
it is the one hung on the tree
THE CROOKED STAR
on the best branch
the one at eye level

Guests are sometimes surprised
right at the front? that scrap of paper?
when you have such beautiful ornaments

We do
says the mother
and straightens the star's longer point
which will bend again anyway

But the stars in the sky
are crooked too
if you saw them up close
they peel as well
somewhere they too have a thumbprint

They only look perfect
from a distance
like children
once they've moved away

---

## 522. THE SIDES OF THE BED

For forty years
he slept on the door side
and she by the window

There was never a vote on it
it was simply so
since the first night in the rented room
where the bed stood differently
and the door creaked

He by the door
just in case
in case of a burglar
in case a child cried in the night
in case the world got a bad idea
it would have to get past him first

She by the window
because she liked hearing the rain
and he liked hearing
her listening to the rain

Forty years
each their own hollow in the mattress
each their own night table
his detective novels and nose drops
her love stories and hairpins

And then one autumn
without a word they changed sides

The children laughed
mum dad have you gone mad
at your age

Not mad

Only the burglars don't come anymore
the door has been quiet for years
the children cry in other cities
into their own telephones

But the window
through the window now at night
something bigger than a burglar looks in

So they swapped
a sentry remains a sentry
for forty years he stood between her and the world
now he lies
between her and the sky

---

## 523. THE HAND ON THE SADDLE

The street behind the house
Sunday morning
a bicycle two sizes too big
so it will last
and the father

The father holds the saddle
and runs
Pedal he says
don't look down
look ahead
I've got you

The bicycle sways
the world sways
the bell rings by itself
out of fear

Have you got me? shouts the boy
I've got you shouts the father

And he runs
on a Sunday
in his Sunday shoes
he runs past the fences
past the dustbins
past Mrs Novak with her shopping bag
he runs and he holds

And then he only runs

And then he doesn't even run

He stands at the end of the street
hands in his pockets
and watches
his son riding
alone
straight
away

Have you got me? shouts the boy
because he's afraid to turn around

I've got you
calls the father
from a distance that is no longer enough

And that is all
that is the whole trade
run
hold
let go
and say nothing

Many years later
in a room with white walls
the father asks quietly
Have you got me?

I've got you says the son

and it is the same hand holding him
and it is the same lie
and just like back then
it is the truth

---

## 524. THE PHONE AT SEVEN

Every Sunday at seven
the telephone rings
and it is his mother

She asks
whether he eats
whether he dresses warmly
whether he has found himself a girl
and whether there's a draught from the window

He answers
I eat
I dress
mum please
and there's no draught from the window

Meanwhile he watches the football
with the sound off
meanwhile he stirs the soup
meanwhile he nods a head
she cannot see

Sunday after Sunday
year after year
at seven
so exactly
he could have set his watch by her
and he never did

Then that March came
and the Sundays at seven went silent

For a year they said nothing
the telephone stood in the hall
like a monument

And then one Sunday
at seven precisely
it rings

He drops the wooden spoon
three steps to the hall
heart like a bell

Hello

Silence
then an old voice
Petey?
Are you eating properly?
Are you dressing warmly?

Wrong number
someone else's grandmother
another Sunday of another son
who didn't pick up

He ought to say
madam you have the wrong number

And he says
I eat regularly
I dress warmly
and there's no draught from the window

Because on Sundays at seven
there are no wrong numbers
only mothers
and sons
who still feel like talking

---

## 525. SECOND VIOLIN

Forty years in the same quartet
forty years second violin
the first plays the melody
the one people hum on their way home
the second plays all the rest
the part nobody hums
and nobody can walk without

After the concert a journalist asks
doesn't it hurt
a whole life second
a whole life in the shadow

And the violinist lays the instrument in its case
the way you tuck in a child
Try building a bridge
from one bank of the river only
he says

Once the first violin was ill
and he played the melody
all evening he flew up there
alone in the light
and the whole time he could hear
one voice missing below
a melody is beautiful
but it gets cold
when no one is breathing under it

The first violin gets the flowers
the second gets the beat
the first takes the bow
the second holds the tuning
even through the applause
silently

Whoever holds the harmony
holds everything
only you can't hear it
the way you can't hear a bridge
as long as you're walking across

---

## 526. THE PAGE TURNER

She sits to the left of the pianist
half a chair behind
in a black dress
so as not to be seen
and she is seen
only when she stands

She doesn't play a single note
and knows the whole concert by heart
she breathes with the pianist
counts the bars like steps on a tightrope
and at the exact second
not a hair too soon
not a hair too late
she carries the music over the edge of the paper

Applause
the pianist bows
once twice three times
meanwhile she gathers the scores
and leaves sideways
through the small door

In the cloakroom a little girl asks
mummy who was that lady
who wasn't doing anything

And the pianist who overheard
turns around in his tailcoat
Everyone thinks I play alone
he says
but between any two pages
there is an abyss
and she is the bridge

Nobody claps for her
because it all went well
they would clap for her
only if the music fell
and the music never fell
not once
in thirty years

---

## 527. THE BACK ROW OF THE THEATRE

In the front row sit those
who want to be seen
in the back row those
who want to see

Students with tickets for pennies
the usherette finally on her break
the fireman on duty
lovers who don't care what's playing
and once in a while
the author of the play himself
incognito
hat on his knees

An old actress comes to sit here
since she stopped acting
From the stage you see nothing
she says
the spotlights burn your eyes
and the audience is just a darkness
that breathes now and then

From here I see everything
the stage and the house
I see the cardboard king
and I see the gentleman in row five
who weeps during his monologue
for real

The play is performed on the stage
but it happens in the house
in row sixteen in the middle
in row seven by the aisle
wherever someone has just been
struck by a line

That's why I sit at the back
says the actress
from the front row you see the theatre
from the back row you see
what theatre can do

---

## 528. THE FRESCO RESTORER

High on the scaffolding under the vault
the restorer lifts away centuries
with a cotton swab
centimetre by centimetre
candle smoke
the dust of processions
the pious overpainting of zealous repairs

Under the grime angels surface
a blue dearer than gold
a gold dearer than humility
saints with eyes wide open
a sky four hundred years old

And then at the edge of a cloud
where no one can see from the nave
she finds a fingerprint in the plaster
the painter leaned on the fresh wall
towards evening probably
in the year sixteen twelve
maybe his back was aching
maybe his wife was laughing down below

The committee asks from the floor
have you found anything of value

A signature
she answers

She sets her finger beside the print
gently
a millimetre away
the way you shake a hand
across four centuries

He was painting eternity
she says later for the report
angels saints heavens
all of it for eternity
but the most alive thing of all
after four hundred years
is the touch of a tired man
who for one second forgot
he was making eternity

Eternity gets painted
the touch remains

---

## 529. THE STREET PORTRAITIST

On the bridge sits the portraitist
a sign propped against his suitcase
PORTRAIT IN FIVE MINUTES

He draws tourists and lovers
and flatters everyone a little
straightens the nose
softens the chin
lights up the eyes
irons out the years

But you're lying
says a student with a sketchbook under his arm
that's not what they look like

The portraitist doesn't put down his charcoal
I draw them
the way their dog sees them
the way their mother saw them
the way they see themselves
the second before the bathroom light comes on
That's not a lie
that's an advance

One day an old lady comes
sits up straight as in school
and says
draw me as I am
all of it
the years too

And he draws for a long time
longer than five minutes
wrinkle after wrinkle
the way you read a letter

The old lady looks at the paper
and says nothing for a long while
That's the first time
she says at last
anyone has really
looked at me

How much do I owe you

Nothing
says the portraitist packing up his charcoals
the pretty ones I sell
the true ones I give away
hardly anyone wants them anyway

---

## 530. THE CELLO ON THE TRAM

Evening tram number nine
tired people
wet umbrellas
everyone staring into their phone
or into their own nowhere

At the theatre stop a cellist gets on
and with him a big black case
wide as a grown man
he settles it carefully on a seat
by the window
so it can see out

The inspector works his way down the aisle
tickets please
and this gentleman here
he points at the case

The cellist hands over two tickets
This gentleman was born in seventeen ninety
he says
but he travels full fare

The tram begins to smile
first the lady with her shopping
then two boys in hoodies
then even the inspector
who gives the case a salute
and moves on

And what's inside
asks a little girl across the aisle

A tree
says the cellist
it grew a hundred years in the mountains
listening to the wind
and then it learned to sing
now it rides the number nine
like the rest of us

The little girl nods
the tram rattles over the bridge
and all those tired people
look at the black case
which won't play a single note the whole way
and still they feel lighter

At the terminus the cellist gets off last
Good night sir
the driver tells him in the mirror
and to you too maestro

---

## 531. THE PROOFREADER

Forty years of red pencil
forty years of other people's mistakes
swapped letters
dropped commas
sentences that set out and never arrived
words split in the worst possible place

She has corrected novels and train timetables
ministers' speeches
and washing machine manuals
millions of errors
and not one of them was hers

One day a manuscript arrives
an old man's memoirs
on the last page it says
dedicated to my wife
who reads over my shoulder

But the wife has been dead thirty years
it says so in the very first chapter

The proofreader raises her red pencil
this should be past tense
says the rule
who read over my shoulder
that is the correct form

The pencil hangs a moment over the paper
like a wasp over a glass

And then the proofreader erases her mark
the only one in forty years

Because grammar knows
the past tense and the present tense
but not the third one
the tense of those who are gone
and read over your shoulder anyway

Some mistakes get corrected
she says closing the manuscript
and some are left to live
because they are truer
than the rule

---

## 532. THE FIREMEN'S BAND

On Sunday on the village square
the firemen's band is playing
the baker on trumpet
the blacksmith on the helicon
the butcher on the drum
and the fire chief conducting
with the hands that usually hold the hose

The trumpet comes in late
the clarinet runs ahead
the helicon goes its own way
and they all always meet
at the end of the bar
like a family at supper

The square is full
grandmothers on chairs brought from home
children on shoulders
dogs between legs
and pies on the long table

A gentleman from the city arrives
stops by the beer tap
and murmurs to his neighbour
but they're playing out of tune
can you hear it
every second note

The neighbour nods and refills his glass
The philharmonic plays for the ears
he says
these fellows play for the whole village
that trumpet there pulled
the Vasek girl out of the pond last year
that drum fixes roofs
and the only false playing
is playing you don't mean
these men mean it dead seriously
every wrong note comes from the heart

The band strikes up another march
off the mark again
and the whole square starts dancing
exactly in the rhythm
that isn't there
and that everyone can hear all the same

---

## 533. THE RETIRED DANCER

At the stop of the number three bus
an old lady stands with her shopping bag
it's raining
the bus is late
people huddle under the shelter

She stands by the pole
and holds it lightly
too lightly to be holding on
heels together toes turned out
chin level as if carrying a crown
and in her wrist
quite unnoticeably
a port de bras

None of the people waiting know
that this pole is at this very moment
the barre in the studio on Island Street
the mirrors the piano the shouting ballet mistress
and being eighteen
nobody knows
that this lady with her bag of potatoes
once died eight nights a week
as a swan
and always rose again

Only one little girl is watching
and then she too takes hold of the pole
heels together toes turned out
chin up

The old lady smiles
says nothing
just lifts her hand
one centimetre higher
so there is something to copy

The bus arrives
and she boards it
three steps
the way one enters a stage
the driver doesn't know why
but he waits one extra second
before closing the doors

The body forgets nothing
only the audience changes
and a bus stop in the rain
is a stage too
when someone is waiting there
who once knew how to fly

---

## 534. THE LULLABY SINGER

Every evening she sings to the children
the most grateful audience in the world
which always falls asleep
in the middle of the second verse

She sings about sleepy-time
about little stars
about the little black horse
and the children breathe more slowly
eyelids grow heavy
a small hand lets go of the plush dog
and the concert is over
before the song has ended

And she keeps singing
the third verse
the fourth
softly all the way through
even the long note at the end
that no one hears anymore

Who do you sing the ending for
her husband asks one night from the doorway
they're asleep

Who for
she says switching off the lamp

For the dark so it knows
we lend it the child for one night only
for the room so it remembers the tune
in case the child should wake

And also
she says already in the hallway
for a little girl
who always used to fall asleep
in the middle of the second verse
and all her life has wanted to know
how it turned out

The beginnings of songs are for children
the endings are for us
for those who already know
how songs end
and still want to hear every night
that they end well

---

## 535. THE LAST NIGHT FLIGHT

The airport at one in the morning
goes out piece by piece
the departure board has played its last hand
the luggage belts stand still
the perfume shop sleeps behind its grille
the coffee machine keeps its light on
all alone for no one

The cleaning woman rides her machine
across a hall as big as a town square
the customs man yawns
even the sniffer dog is asleep

Only the tower is still lit
and across the screen a dot is traveling
the last night flight
high above the mountains

And the controller watches the dot
and wonders who is sitting in it
maybe a woman flying to a funeral
rehearsing her first sentence in silence
maybe a man coming home after twenty years
afraid he's forgotten the name of his street
maybe a child flying for the first time
asleep with its forehead on the window
above a land full of small lights
one of which is home

Hello this is the tower
you may descend
the runway is yours

And for that one little dot
the whole airport lights up again
the runway the hall the belts
and the sign ARRIVALS above the doors

because no one is allowed to land in the dark
and no one in this world
should ever come back
to a house gone dark

---

## 536. THE HITCHHIKER WITH A SIGN SAYING ANYWHERE

At the edge of the road past the last house
stands a hitchhiker
holding a sign made from a banana box
and on it in big letters
ANYWHERE

The first car stops
Where are you headed?
Anywhere
That's not a direction young man
It's the best direction in the world
says the hitchhiker
the only one you can't miss

I'm only going as far as Slough
says the driver apologetically
Slough is anywhere
says the hitchhiker
but the driver is already rolling up his window
Slough suddenly seems very small to him

A second car stops a third a fifth
and they all ask the same thing
Anywhere sure but where exactly?
and the hitchhiker lifts his sign a little higher
and the drivers shrug
I'm only going to my mother-in-law's
I'm only picking up cement
I'm only going home
and off they drive
and each one in his rearview mirror
carries away a piece of that word

Toward evening an old car pulls over
the driver leans across
opens the door
and asks nothing at all

Where are you going? asks the hitchhiker
No idea says the driver
I've been driving since morning that's all
Then that makes two of us
says the hitchhiker and gets in

And the sign lies on the back seat
ANYWHERE
and for the first time all day
it points exactly the right way

---

## 537. THE FERRYMAN

Across the river between two steeples
the ferryman goes
fifty years now
there and back
there and back

He has ferried brides in white
and coffins in black
the doctor at midnight
goslings wheelbarrows an accordion
whole fairs with the carousel in crates
and once a runaway horse
that stood on the ferry
still as a statue

People ask him
and you never stayed on the other side?
it's only a stone's throw

Never says the ferryman

Not even for one night?

Not even one

But why?

And the ferryman leans on his pole
When I stand here
the other side is that one
when I stand there
the other side is this one
fifty years I've been crossing to the other side
and I've never once arrived

But in the middle of the river
he says pushing off
in the middle of the river I am neither here nor there
and that is the only place in the world
nobody can take from me
because it's on no map
and you can't build on it
you can only cross

---

## 538. THE ROAD THAT LED NOWHERE

Past the village a road begins
an honest road with milestones
with an avenue of old cherry trees
and a little bridge over the brook

It starts out grandly like all roads do
and ends in a field
in the middle of the oats
it simply stops
like a sentence someone never finished

Once they say it was meant to reach a town
that got built somewhere else
or a bridge that changed its mind
nobody remembers now
the plans went yellow in the district archive

The authorities twice tried to strike it off
a road without a destination shall be cancelled
it says so in the regulations
but there was never money for that
and so it keeps on leading
nowhere

And people walk it
lovers because they meet no one there
except other lovers
and those don't count
an old man with a cane because he has nowhere to get to
a lady with a dog that chases wind through the oats
children on bikes riding to the end of the world
and home in time for supper

Every other road drives you somewhere
to work to the office to the train
only this one is in no hurry at all
and that is why it walks so well

And at the very end in the oats
the grass is trodden down
from all the people standing there looking
because from there
you get the finest view
of nowhere

---

## 539. THE BOUNDARY STONE

On the bank between Upper End and Lower End
a stone sits in the grass
an old boundary stone
a U carved on one side
an L carved on the other
and a cross on top
so there'd be no mistake

Once upon a time it was serious
the mayors took the meadow to court
the farmers brawled at the parish fair
sealed documents sleep in the archive
with the surveyor's oath
that the line runs exactly here
for all eternity

Only the grass can't read
it grows across the line in both directions
the swallows fly from one land register to the other
without a single stamp
the rain rains on both villages at once
the hare has its burrow right on the border
head in Upper End rump in Lower End
and it sleeps just fine

And the cows
the cows don't read maps
the Upper End cow grazes Lower End grass
and in the evening she gives milk
and that milk is one milk only
equally white in both villages

Only people know where the line is
and children
children know it best of all
they sit on the stone with their sandwiches
because whoever sits on the stone
is neither in Upper End nor in Lower End
and in both villages
that is the best seat there is

---

## 540. THE HIGHWAY DINER

A diner between two exits
a hundred kilometers from everywhere
nobody lives here
nobody comes here
everyone only passes through

The waitress brings coffee and the soup of the day
and in five minutes she learns
what people elsewhere won't tell in a lifetime

The trucker shows her a photo of the daughter
he sees once a month
the woman by the window is driving away from someone
you can tell by how long
she stirs a coffee with no sugar in it
the traveling salesman tells jokes
so as not to hear himself for a while
and the soldier on leave says nothing
and that too is a whole story

They tell her everything
because they will never see her again
a confessional with a menu
and here the absolution comes with free refills

Once somebody asked her
don't you get sad out here?
everyone drives away
and you stay behind

And the waitress wiped the table
You people drive one and the same highway all your lives
and you call that traveling
I stand here behind this counter
and the whole world passes through my hands
a thousand stories a day
five minutes each
and not one of them tells me its ending

And that's just as well she said
straightening the sugar bowls
the endings I make up myself
and all of them
every single one
turn out happy

---

## 541. THE LAST STOP OF THE CABLE CAR

The cable car climbs out of the valley
over the spruces over the clearing
over the last shingled cottage
where somebody is still waving

And up on the ridge
is the last stop
a tin shed a great wheel
and a sign saying END OF THE LINE

Here the wires end
beyond there is nothing at all
just grass hugging the ground
stones
and the wind that has lived here forever

An old man with a thermos runs the station
he greets the cabins like old acquaintances
and all day long he answers
one single question

It doesn't go any further?
It doesn't
And what's further on?
Further on is the wind

The tourists take pictures by the sign
eat their sandwiches on the sheltered side of the shed
and ride back down

But now and then somebody stays standing
looking at the ridge above the station
where no cable leads anymore

You can't ride up there says the old man
up there you can only walk
and higher still
higher still you can only look

Everything in this world stretches only as far as it will
he says screwing his thermos shut
wires rails telephone lines
they always stop a little short of the summit
the last stretch everyone must climb alone
and the very last stretch
cannot be climbed at all
it can only be stood in silence
while you let the wind
take your cap
as the price of admission

---

## 542. THE MAN WHO CAME BACK THE SAME WAY

Every morning at ten past seven
he left the house
down Station Street across the footbridge
past the bakery and past the church
to work
and at five in the afternoon
back along the very same way

Thirty years

The neighbors told him
man at least buy yourself a bicycle
discover another street
you'll wear a groove in that one
the world is wide

I know he said
that's exactly why

In the morning the bakery smelled of bread
in the evening other people's dinners drifted from open windows
in the morning the church was on his right hand
in the evening on his left
on Monday there was a puddle on the footbridge
on Tuesday it had turned into a mirror
on Wednesday it was gone
in spring the lilacs overtook him on the way
in winter he walked in his own morning footprints
and they never quite fit

One day his grandson asked him
grandpa don't you get tired
of always the same road?

And the man laughed
I have never once in my life
walked the same road
in the morning a man goes down it
who doesn't yet know how the day will end
in the evening a man comes back
who does
they walk on the same stones
and each takes a different road

And if you really knew how to walk
he said taking the boy's hand
one street would be enough
for the whole world

---

## 543. THE METRO MAP OF A FOREIGN CITY

In the drawer under the rubber bands
under the old glasses and the bills
lies a folded map
the metro of a foreign city
brought home forty years ago
from a single journey

Lines colored like crayons
the blue one runs along the river
the yellow one circles the center
like a cat around a warm stove
the red one heads for the airport
and nowhere else

And the names of the stations
sound like lines of verse
Porte des Lilas
the Gate of Lilacs
Château d'Eau
the Castle of Water
Filles du Calvaire
the Daughters of Calvary
whoever thought those up
was no clerk
or maybe he was
but on Sundays he wrote poems

In the evening he spreads the map on the table
and travels by finger
boards at the Gate of Lilacs
changes trains where they advise you not to
because the connecting corridor they say never ends
and he happens to like corridors that never end
he never gets lost
he is never delayed
and he rides for free

He spent three days in that city once
he was twenty
and it rained
that is all
and that is a great deal

Why don't you go back?
people ask

And he folds the map along its faded creases
A different metro runs there now
they've renamed the stations extended the lines
but in this map
the summer I was twenty is still running
perfectly on time
every five minutes

---

## 544. THE POSTCARD THAT ARRIVED YEARS LATE

When they took apart the old sorting machine at the post office
a postcard fell out from behind the tin
the sea a lighthouse two sailboats
and a postmark thirty years old

It's beautiful here
the water is warm
I think of you
I'll be back soon
Your Anna

The official procedure is clear
addressee unknown return to sender
only the sender
no longer has any address
and where she has moved to
there are no deliveries

Bin it said the supervisor
thirty years past the deadline
nobody's waiting for that anymore

But the old postman slipped it into his bag
what has once been written must arrive
that isn't in the regulations
that's in postmen

He asked at the tobacconist's asked at the butcher's
asked at the registry office
the house was rebuilt strange names on the doorbells
after a week he finally tracked down
a retirement home
in another town by a park
he went there on a Sunday
on a ticket he paid for himself

Mail for you sir
he said and handed him the postcard

The old man put on his glasses
and read for a very long time
for so few words

She never came back you know
he said at last
I know said the postman
But she wrote that she would
And what is written stands
said the postman
that's why we carry it

And the old man stood the postcard
on his night table sea side out
and from that evening on he went on waiting
only no longer just like that
now he had it in writing
with an official stamp

---

## 545. THE MAN WHO COUNTED THE STARS

On the roof of the house by the station
a man sits every night
blanket thermos notebook
and above his head a sky
full of unfinished work

One two three
he says softly
seventy-four seventy-five
then a cloud drifts over
and it's all gone
tomorrow again

The neighbours have him figured out
a quiet well-mannered madman
who harms no one
except the stars
they say you shouldn't count them

One night the baker's boy
climbs up from the ground floor
Mister so how many are there?
I don't know says the man
in thirty years I've never got
past two hundred and eight

And that doesn't break your heart?

The man pours him tea from the thermos
Look he says
the baker too every single day
they eat up all his bread
and in the morning he bakes more
and nobody tells him
it's pointless

But bread is for eating

Exactly says the man
and stars are for counting
not for finishing the count
if one day I counted them all
the sky would be done
and things that are done
people stop looking at

Since that night
the baker's boy counts too
from his window on the ground floor
you can see just a ribbon of sky
seven eight nine stars
but starting over
that he already knows
like a grown-up

---

## 546. THE DREAM THAT KEPT RETURNING

Mrs Hedvika has one dream
for forty years always the same
the garden behind her childhood house
a line full of white washing
and her mother with her back turned
pinning up sheets against the wind

The dream has its rules
Hedvika stands by the currant bush
she must not speak
she must not move
and before her mother turns around
the alarm clock rings
every time

She knows the dream by heart
the way you know a prayer
she used to tell it good evening
she used to tell it you again

Then one night in March
everything is as always
the garden the line the wind
except one sheet
is blue

Such a small thing
who would notice
Hedvika notices
she's been looking for forty years

And her mother without turning
says
hand me a clothes-peg

And Hedvika takes a step
her first step in forty years
and hands it over

In the morning she wakes
with an empty palm
but the palm remembers
that small piece of wood

Since that night she sleeps without dreams
the way you sleep when the work is done
and in her apron pocket she keeps a peg
in case some day again
the wind should blow

---

## 547. RADIO FOR THE SLEEPLESS

At two in the morning a voice speaks on the radio
soft as a kitchen lamp
playing songs for no one
that is for everyone
who isn't sleeping

The baker woman listens already up
the watchman listens not yet in bed
the mother with a child on her shoulder
the truck driver between two towns
the old man whose bed is too big
and the boy afraid to switch off the light

None of them knows about the others
each one thinks
I am the last person awake in the world
and the voice on the radio speaks
as if sitting in each of their rooms
separately

One night the voice says
I don't actually know if you're out there
in twenty years nobody has written to me
so I'll ask you just one thing
if you can hear me
turn your light on for a moment
then turn it off

And the host steps to the studio window
high above the sleeping city

And the city blinks
here and there and over there too
by the station on the ground floor under the tower
windows like fireflies
a whole constellation of the sleepless

Good night he says into the microphone
good morning rather
and for the first time in twenty years
he isn't saying it to the dark

---

## 548. THE ALARM CLOCK THAT DIDN'T RING ON SUNDAY

Mr Bohumil has a tin alarm clock
handed down from his father
he winds it every evening
with the little key at the back
two turns not a hair more

The clock rings at half past four
a whole lifetime
Monday for the foundry
Tuesday for the foundry
Wednesday Thursday Friday
Saturday for the market
and Sunday
Sunday too
because Mr Bohumil forgets to switch it off
and then lies with his eyes closed
listening to the bells of Saint James
which ring so much better

One Saturday evening
there was a party at the neighbour's
accordion wine stories of the old days
and Mr Bohumil came home late
and fell asleep in his shoes
and never wound the clock

On Sunday he woke at nine
the sun already sat on the floor
like a guest nobody had invited
who still arrived on time

Mr Bohumil took fright
then remembered it was Sunday
then looked at the alarm clock
and the clock kept quiet
hands folded behind its back
pretending nothing at all

You could have told me sooner
says Mr Bohumil
that there's no ringing on Sundays

The clock said nothing
alarm clocks don't talk
alarm clocks simply know

Since then Mr Bohumil
doesn't wind it on Saturday nights
he calls it an agreement
and on Sunday mornings he lies in
listening to the bells of Saint James
while on the bedside table
rests a little tin heart
that one day a week
takes orders from no one

---

## 549. THE MINUTE OF SILENCE

At the stadium across the river
before the Sunday match
the loudspeaker said
let us observe a minute of silence
for Mr Josef
who for forty years
chalked the lines of this pitch

And five thousand people stood up
and there was silence

For the referee it lasted exactly a minute
he was watching his stopwatch
that's what referees are for

For the goalkeeper it lasted one penalty
the one Mr Josef once saved
in the youth team
the only one of his life

For the boys on the terraces it lasted forever
because boys don't know how to stand still
not even for a second

For the sausage seller it was short
he'd barely taken off his cap
before putting it back on

For the widow in row twelve
it lasted thirty-nine years
from the wedding to yesterday
she walked the whole length of it
there and back
and still had some left over

And for the pigeons on the grandstand roof
it didn't last at all
pigeons keep their own count

Then the referee blew his whistle
the only whistle
Mr Josef never heard
and the ball set off across the grass
along the fresh white lines
that somebody else
had drawn this morning
slightly crooked

And the silence
the silence sat down in the empty seat
in row twelve
and watched the match to the end

---

## 550. THE LAST DAY OF THE HOLIDAYS

On the thirty-first of August
the days are long as a whole summer
and short as nothing at all

The Vrána boy knows it
so from morning on he's rushing
one last time to the river
one last time up the cherry tree
though the cherries are long gone
one last ride on his bike in a single go
out to the transformer and back
no hands

Time runs strangely that day
the morning lasts a week
the afternoon blinks and is gone
and the evening smells of September
a smell known only
to children and schoolteachers

In the hallway the schoolbag is waiting
brand new gleaming
sitting by the door like a dog
that knows tomorrow means the vet
and wags its tail anyway

Mother calls from the window
come home we're up early tomorrow
and that tomorrow is a longer word
than the whole of July

Grown-ups don't see that day
for grown-ups the thirty-first
is just the day before the first
they have forgotten
that once they too could hear
summer swinging shut
like a great gate

But the Vrána boy hears it
so that evening in his pencil case
between the brand-new pens
he hides a pebble from the river
still warm with sun

And tomorrow at his desk
when the teacher asks
what did you do this summer
he'll say nothing
and in his hand under the desk
the river will keep flowing

---

## 551. THE HOUR BETWEEN NIGHT AND MORNING

There is one hour
that appears in no timetable
night has already signed the handover papers
morning has not yet reported for duty
and the city belongs
to whom exactly

To bakers with flour on their eyelids
to cats inspecting the dustbins
to the driver of the first tram
still running empty
just for himself
to the nurse coming off the night shift
to the postwoman going on the early one
they pass each other on the bridge
and nod
though they've never been introduced

And to those who do not sleep
poets without poems
lovers without lovers
old men homesick
for something that has no name

At that hour everyone is on first-name terms
with their eyes
the night baker hands over a roll
straight across the counter still hot
and won't take money
at this hour nobody pays
at this hour you belong

Then the first alarm clock rings
somewhere on the third floor
and it's all over

The sun comes out the people come out
proper well-rested hurrying
and the brotherhood dissolves
like sugar in coffee

Except sometimes at noon
at the crossing by the post office
the baker and a sleepless poet
nod to each other

And nobody around can work out
how on earth they know each other
those two

---

## 552. HOW CHILDREN FALL ASLEEP

Children fall asleep in the middle of a sentence
and then the dinosaur went
and then
and then nothing
they're asleep

They fall asleep with a toy car in hand
halfway round the bend
with a storybook open on their belly
one shoe off
and the other for another day

They fall asleep on the tram
head on a stranger's sleeve
at the table over their soup
on the carpet in the middle of building
a bridge out of blocks
they'll calmly finish in the morning

They never decide to sleep
it simply ambushes them
like rain on a day out
they fall into sleep
the way you fall into grass
straight down
without backing up

Grown-ups have forgotten how

Grown-ups fall asleep
the way a shop gets closed
check the locks
switch off go through the accounts one last time
stack the worries on their shelves
one sheep two sheep
three hundred and twelve sheep
and sleep nowhere in sight
because sleep does not call
on those who are doing inventory

Except sometimes
on a train at half past four
a grown-up falls asleep mid-sentence
head on the shoulder of a stranger
and suddenly he has the face
he had when he was six

And the whole compartment goes quiet
and the stranger sits up straight
not breathing
standing guard over his sleep
and his shoulder won't start to ache
till after Colchester

---

## 553. THE NIGHT WATCHMAN WHO READ POETRY

A warehouse on the edge of town
three hundred boxes on pallets
wire fence floodlight gatehouse
and in the gatehouse a small lamp
and under the lamp a watchman
and in the watchman's hands
a slim book of verse
from the second-hand shop for pennies

Rounds at ten at midnight at two
torch keys stamp in the logbook
NOTHING TO REPORT
and in between
poems

One night the inspector turns up
a young man in an ironed shirt
What's that you're reading on duty?
Verse says the watchman
And do you know what you're guarding?
I do says the watchman
three hundred boxes

And do you know what's inside them?

No says the watchman
in fifteen years I've never opened one
that's not what I'm here for
I guard them so they stay shut

And he taps the little book

This one is full of boxes too
thirty poems
and nobody knows what's inside them either
until they're opened
the only difference is
I guard the boxes so that no one opens them
and the poems
so that someone does

The inspector writes in his report
ALL QUIET ON SITE
and drives away

And the watchman pours himself tea
and goes on reading
and around the gatehouse stands the night
three hundred boxes of darkness
and in just one of them
a light is on

---

## 554. THE SECOND HAND

On the clock there are three hands

The hour hand is the director
fat slow full of dignity
in a whole day it hardly moves
and yet the world sets itself by it

The minute hand is the office clerk
shuffling from counter to counter
looking terribly busy
once an hour it walks around the office
and goes back to standing still

And then there is the third one
thin and red
no title no rubber stamp
the only one you can see running

It runs all day all night
no lunch break no Sundays
sixty steps every minute
three thousand six hundred an hour
you try counting that

And when someone asks the time
nobody ever says
it's fifteen seconds
they say half past two
the director and the clerk
collect all the glory

On the tower clocks
they abolished it altogether
from that height they said
you wouldn't see it anyway
struck off
the way the errand boy is cropped
from the firm's Christmas photo

And yet

When the doctor holds a sick man's wrist
he doesn't look at the director
he counts her little steps

When people wait for midnight
for the start for the return for the miracle
every eye runs with her

And when the clock stops
she's the one who shows it
she's always the first to know
the smallest one
the one who did
all the work of time

---

## 555. THE BOX MARKED OTHER

Form number seven
offers you options
employed unemployed retired
married single divorced widowed
owner tenant
believer non-believer
and at the very bottom
one small box
OTHER

The man at the counter holds his pen
and thinks
he worked but every which way
he loved but not according to the papers
he lived wherever they let him
he believed mostly in the mornings
so he ticks
other
other
other
other

You can't do that sir
says the clerk
you have to choose something
I did choose
says the man
all my life I've been choosing other

The system won't take it
Then let the system come to the counter
says the man
and next to the box marked other
where it says please specify
he writes in small letters
rain accordion Marie

And the clerk looks at those three words
a long time
then she takes her stamp
and stamps his whole life
valid indefinitely

---

## 556. COMPLAINTS IN VERSE

The complaints department of the transit authority
received a letter
Dear Sirs, your number nine bus
pulled away right under my nose
taking a piece of Tuesday with it
I request a refund
either of the Tuesday or of the driver's smile
whichever is in stock

The clerks gathered over the letter
there is no paragraph for this
no form for this
no rubber stamp seems right
so the letter travelled
from floor to floor
and on every floor
somebody smiled reading it
which in an office building
counts as an exceptional incident

The man kept writing
he complained about a hole in the road
in a sonnet
about the rise in ticket prices
in a ballad with a refrain
about the month of November
just because
November seemed long to him

And years later an answer came
written by the youngest clerk
after office hours
and in verse
Dear sir
that bus is beyond catching now
it has left for good
we are therefore refunding your minutes
in full
regrettably we pay in no other currency
please spend them
on something beautiful

---

## 557. NUMBER FORTY-SEVEN

The ticket machine by the door
hands out numbers
to anyone who reaches for one
to the doctor and the man out of work
to the lady in furs and the boy in overalls
the same to all
in the order they arrive

The man with number forty-seven
sits on a plastic chair
watching the glowing board
forty-four
forty-five

Beside him a mother with a child
that's crying
she holds number sixty-two
and the counters close at four

Meanwhile at counter three
a gentleman slips ahead
a gentleman who knows the supervisor
the people in line say nothing
the people in line are good at saying nothing

Forty-six

The man looks at his ticket
then at the crying child
and hands the mother
his number forty-seven
and takes her sixty-two

The board chimes
and the machine by the door
keeps printing numbers
one after another
fairly
the only one in the whole building
that takes people
just as they come

---

## 558. KEEP OFF THE GRASS

In the middle of the park stands a sign
KEEP OFF THE GRASS
brand new
black letters on white
driven into the ground
exactly where
the grass is greenest

The park keeper paints it every spring
in summer he wipes off the dust with his handkerchief
in winter he sweeps away the snow
it's a fine sign
he says
it keeps order around here

And all around the sign
since nobody walks there
daisies grow
one beside another
white and unhurried
a whole meadow of daisies
right at the foot of the ban

A little girl asks the keeper
mister and those flowers
can they read?

No, says the keeper
flowers can't read

Then why do they grow just here

And the keeper looks at the sign
and for the first time in years he sees it
the grass is slowly climbing the post
creeping up along the letters
another year or two
and it will have read the whole thing
the only one
really walking on it

---

## 559. THE LOYALTY CARD

In a man's wallet
between his ID card and a photo of the grandchildren
a little card
BRIDGE CAFÉ
ten stamps and the tenth coffee free

Nine small blue cups
punched one after another
nine mornings
nine times
good morning Mr Novák
the usual?
the usual

The first stamp on the day it rained
the third when his granddaughter was born
the fifth when nobody said a word
and the coffee was good all the same
the seventh when the girl behind the counter was crying
and he pretended not to see
and left a little extra change

The ninth on a Friday
all weekend he looked forward to the tenth

On Monday a paper on the door
CLOSED FOR GOOD
THANK YOU FOR YOUR CUSTOM

The man stands at the glass
behind which you can still make out the chairs
legs up on the tables
then he takes out his wallet
and slides the card carefully back
between the ID and the grandchildren

Throw it away he will not
one stamp is missing
and as long as it's missing
someone in this world owes him a coffee
and the bridge café
still
just a little
exists

---

## 560. MAKE A U-TURN

In the traveling salesman's car
a woman's voice is speaking
in three hundred meters turn right
continue straight ahead
in two kilometers
you will reach your destination

Destinations
a warehouse on an industrial estate
an office in Plzeň
a parking lot by the motorway
a hotel that looks like every hotel
twenty years of destinations

One Tuesday between Prague and Brno
the man misses an exit
and the voice says calmly
make a U-turn

He misses another
make a U-turn

And suddenly the man asks out loud
of the empty car
where am I actually going

The voice doesn't answer
voices from machines don't answer
questions like that
they only repeat with patience
make a U-turn

All right, says the man
all right
and at the next exit he turns around
all of him
twenty years and all

He drives north instead of south
past road signs he doesn't know
to the village where he was born
and where he hadn't been since the funeral

He stops by a garden with a rusty gate
and switches off the engine

You have reached your destination
says the voice
and for the first time in twenty years
it is right

---

## 561. THE SELF-CHECKOUT

At the supermarket they closed till number four
the one with Marie
who knew half the neighborhood by name
and in her place now stands a machine
with a green light
PLEASE SCAN YOUR FIRST ITEM

An old lady comes to it every Tuesday
rolls milk one apple
and chats away
well now little machine
windy again out there
the two of us can tell
you by your wires me by my knees

UNEXPECTED ITEM
IN THE BAGGING AREA

There there, the lady soothes it
it's only my old shopping bag
it has survived worse

The young man in the queue behind her
rolls his eyes at the ceiling
the lady is talking to a shop
who is she really talking to

To Marie who stays home now
to the husband who died in April
to the daughter across the ocean
who calls on Sundays
when there's time

PLEASE WAIT
HELP IS ON THE WAY

No need to call anyone
says the old lady stroking the screen
we'll manage this together

And the machine blinks and blinks
UNEXPECTED ITEM
IN THE BAGGING AREA
and in its way it is right
something is lying there that isn't in the catalogue
between the rolls and the milk
one whole quiet Tuesday of it
and out of everyone in the store
only the machine
has noticed

---

## 562. GRANDMA'S FIRST SMILEY

They bought grandmother a telephone
smarter than the whole family
and for a week her grandson explained
you write here
you erase here
and here granny
here are the smileys

Grandmother put on her glasses
and looked at that meadow of little faces
yellow and round
laughing crying sleeping
one of them even kissing a heart

Then she sat all evening in the kitchen
holding the phone in both hands
the way you hold a fledgling
and wrote the first message of her life

In the morning the grandson's phone beeps
GRANDMA
and in the message not a single word
just a heart
and right after it
a little face in tears

He calls her granny
you sent me crying
that's for when a person is sad
you meant to send a smile

Of course I did
says grandmother
I must have pressed it wrong

And then a small silence
and then grandmother says
or maybe I pressed it right
because I wanted to smile at you
and somehow doing it
who knows why
made me want to cry
that's something you young ones don't know yet
you'll learn it

---

## 563. THE INSTRUCTION MANUAL

In the drawer under the television
sleep the instruction manuals
the manual for the washing machine
the one for the hair dryer
the one for the kettle
the one for the vacuum whose bags they stopped making
the one for the television hauled away years ago
seven languages
two-year warranty
nobody has ever read them

The washer starts with the big knob
the hair dryer has only two settings
you can live in this world
without reading

But one evening the power goes out
and the man hunts in the drawer for candles
and finds the manuals
and by candlelight he reads
for what else is there by candlelight

Do not expose the appliance to impact
do not immerse it in water
clean with a soft cloth
no harsh products

And in the chapter TROUBLESHOOTING
a table
the appliance gives no warmth
the appliance makes no sound
the appliance does not respond
and for all three the same first advice
first check
that the appliance is connected

The man lifts his eyes from the page
the flat is dark
someone is walking on the floor above
his son's number he knows by heart
and hasn't dialed since Christmas

Seven languages
and all of them say the same thing
no warmth no sound no response
first check
that it is connected

---

## 564. THE SATISFACTION SURVEY

At ten in the evening the phone buzzes
RATE YOUR DAY TODAY
from one to five stars
your opinion matters to us

The man sits on the edge of the bed
and remembers honestly

It rained in the morning
one star
but that smell of wet pavement
five

He missed his tram
one
but in the next one
over its mother's shoulder
a stranger's child smiled at him
five

At work a meeting about meetings
no stars
spreadsheets don't have stars

At noon a soup almost like his mother's
in the afternoon a call with his sister
they quarreled
one
they made up
five
and maybe they quarreled only
to have something to make up for

And in the evening a stranger stood on the bridge
looking down at the water
the man stopped beside him
and they were silent together
until the stranger said
well I'll be going
and went home
how many stars for that
and in which box

The man looks at the five empty stars
a day cannot be averaged
a day can at most be lived
in the end he lights up all five

And the screen asks
WHAT COULD WE IMPROVE?
and the man thinks for a long time
and then writes
nothing
just let there be another one tomorrow

---

## 565. THE STATUE THAT FACED ONE WAY

On the square stands a statue
some general or a poet
nobody remembers now
pigeons make no distinctions

For a hundred years it has faced one way
down the avenue toward the river
it knows by heart
the bakery window
the corner of the savings bank
and a piece of sky above the bridge

It has seen funerals and parades
flags that kept changing
carriages trams cars
everything passed before its eyes
in one direction
like the river

Only one thing it doesn't know
what is behind it

Because behind it there is a park
a small park with a bench
the statue knows it only by ear
laughter without a face
a violin in June
the creak of the bench
when two people move closer
and the silence
that only lovers know how to make

In the evening over its shoulder
fall long shadows
foreign and slow
that is all of the park
it will ever own

It asked the pigeons
What is behind me?
A bench says the pigeon
And on it?
Everything that doesn't fit
in front of monuments

The statue would turn around
but the pedestal

And then one evening
it watches the people
walking home from work down the avenue
all of them straight ahead
not one turns around
and yet nothing
is stopping them

Their necks are entirely free
the statue says to itself
and they carry them straight
like me

at least I have an excuse
I am made of bronze

---

## 566. THE UNFINISHED SCARF

In the basket by the armchair
a ball of blue wool
and an unfinished scarf
the needles stuck mid-row
just as she set them down
when she went to answer the door
and never came back

The aunt says
I'll finish it
I know the pattern
it practically knits itself

Don't you dare
says the mother
and no more is said about it

The scarf stays in the basket
winter follows winter
the moths got the sweaters
they never touched the scarf
even moths understood something

One day the granddaughter asks
why doesn't anyone finish it
it would keep somebody warm

And the mother takes the needles in her palm
gently as if they were sleeping
A finished scarf is a finished thing
she says
finished things get folded into the wardrobe
and that's the end of them

But an unfinished row
is somebody
who has only stepped away from her work
somebody who'll be back any minute
to sit down by the armchair
and say
now where was I

---

## 567. THE ROCKING HORSE IN THE ATTIC

In the attic among the suitcases
stands a rocking horse
mane of hemp rope
one eye still painted
the other chipped away
they carried him up
when the children outgrew him
which is to say thirty years ago

Sometimes at night
he creaks
and rocks for a while
all by himself

That's a draft
says the father
the roof isn't tight
physics
nothing more

The mother doesn't like going up to the attic
not that she's afraid
she just doesn't care
to run into physics

One day the grandson climbs the ladder
to fetch the Christmas ornaments
and sees the horse rocking
quite slowly
quite quietly
as if someone very light
were sitting on him

Downstairs he tells them
the father shrugs
a draft

Maybe
says the mother putting wood on the fire
but a draft is only wind after all
wind that lost its way in the house
and found in the attic
the last thing left
that still wants to play with it

---

## 568. THE FOUNTAIN PEN

In the desk drawer
lies a fountain pen
black with a gold nib
worn smooth by fingers

It signed two weddings
one as groom one as witness
the deed to the house
garden and mortgage included
a job application
a resignation letter
excuse notes for the children
congratulations condolences
and at the end one piece of paper
that is not spoken of at home

These days anything will do for signing
a ballpoint from the insurance company
a finger on a screen
a box to tick
nothing more

The grandson finds the pen while tidying
Does it still write?
he opens it
dips the nib into the little bottle
that had been waiting too

And the pen writes
as if the years had never happened
it writes his name
in one stroke without hesitating

You see says the grandfather
a pen makes no distinctions
it signed love and the bank
a birth and the other thing
all in the same ink

Because a pen doesn't know what it's signing
he says and closes the drawer
it only knows whose hand it is
and the hand
the hand knows everything

---

## 569. THE LIGHT SWITCH

The parents' house is up for sale
the son comes to walk through it one last time
the agent hands him the keys
the electricity's been cut
he warns
take a flashlight

He didn't take a flashlight

He steps into the dark hallway
and his hand all by itself
before he can even think
flies to the doorframe
a little to the left
a small cold square
the light switch

Click
no light comes on
that's all right

The hand goes on
three steps straight ahead
mind the stair that creaks
second switch around the corner
radiator don't touch it burns
the kitchen handle sticks
lift and push

The body walks the house
in the dark
and stumbles nowhere
the head has forgotten dates and quarrels
the head has forgotten so much
the body nothing

Outside he returns the keys
You didn't take anything?
the agent wonders
people always carry something off

I did
says the son
and raises his hand
but don't worry
nobody in there will miss it

---

## 570. THE NIGHT LAUNDROMAT

At two in the morning the only lit window
on the whole street is on the corner
SELF-SERVICE LAUNDRY
OPEN ALL NIGHT

Inside three people
who don't know each other
a nurse after her shift
a boy with his sheets in a plastic bag
and a lady in a coat
thrown over her nightgown

They don't speak
they sit on plastic chairs
watching the drums
like children watching a carousel

In one the whites go round
in another the blues
in the third a man's entire week
Monday's shirt
Wednesday's dish towels
the socks of every day

The boy leans toward the nurse
You can't sleep either?
I could, says the nurse
but at home nothing turns

So they sit in silence
three strangers
staring into the same carousel
the way people once stared into fires

The lady in the coat gets up
takes out the warm laundry
and holds it a while in her arms

If only you could
she says into the silence
put a whole day into the drum
forty minutes at sixty degrees
and take it out
clean and warm
and fold it up for tomorrow

---

## 571. DEW ON THE GRASS

Every morning before six
the meadow opens its jewellery shop
no bars no display cases no insurance
a million brilliants laid out in the grass
a diamond on every blade
pearls strung along the spiderwebs
sapphires in the cups of plantain leaves

No shop assistant
no till
no sign saying DO NOT TOUCH

The jeweller from the square walks by each morning
and stops at the fence
If I had this in my window
he says
I'd be the richest man in the county

But you have it
says the meadow
you only need to bend down

But the jeweller is in a hurry
at nine he opens his shop
and in the shop he has a safe
and in the safe little stones
that last

At nine o'clock the sun arrives
the greatest thief under heaven
no gloves no crowbar
it gathers everything
brilliants pearls sapphires
down to the last drop

Nobody reports it to the police
The meadow just shrugs its blades
Tomorrow they deliver fresh stock
it says
unlike the jeweller I know
that the most precious things
are the ones that cannot be kept

---

## 572. WIND IN THE CHIMNEY

The young couple bought an old house
and the first night they didn't sleep
Someone is singing in the chimney
she says
And the stairs creak
he says
and the doors as well
we'll call the mason in the morning

The mason came
walked around the house
tapped the chimney
listened for a while

There's nothing here for me to fix
he said
The chimney is sound
the stairs are sound
it's only the wind talking to the house
and the house talking back

Sixty years together
the wind and this house
at that age you don't sing for strangers anymore
you sing out of habit
The wind asks
the house creaks
and that's that

My old folks were the same
said the mason in the doorway
In the evening dad just said hmm
mum just said well yes
and everything was said
that young people say in an hour

That night the young couple laughed
then lay in the dark and listened
the wind asking
the house answering
slowly without hurry
like those who already know
the other one won't leave

And many years from now
on some windy night
she will only say hmm
and he will only say well yes
and the house above them
will give a contented creak

---

## 573. ICICLES

Everything in the world grows upward
grass chimneys children prices
only icicles grow head down
winter's armoury
hung beneath the gutter

A boy breaks one off
the longest one
a sword
and at once he is a knight
and at once he has a war

He swings his sword at winter
a slash here
a thrust there
winter says nothing
winter is enormous
and the knight has one mitten with a wet thumb

Soon the sword is sweating
soon the sword is shorter
soon the sword is a dagger
the dagger a splinter
the splinter a drop
in an empty palm

The boy is crying
I killed my sword
he says
I held it too tight

But the grandmother at the gate laughs
You silly little soldier
don't cry
it was never your sword
it was winter's
all season long it forged them by the thousand
and handed them out to children

A weapon that melts in your hand
says the grandmother
that's winter for you
it only arms you
if you promise
the war will be over
before the thaw

---

## 574. GRASS BETWEEN THE RAILS

Under regulation D-17 paragraph 3
the trackbed shall be kept free of vegetation

The grass between the rails
never read the regulation
it grows among the sleepers
at the worst address in town
gravel instead of soil
diesel instead of rain
and twice an hour a thunder
rolling over its head

The express to Paris
the express from Paris
people at the windows with important destinations
conferences weddings aeroplanes
nobody looks down

Once a year come the men in orange
with the regulation weedkiller
and the grass turns yellow
and a report is signed
VEGETATION REMOVED

By spring it is back
green insolence
between platform five and platform six

The stationmaster with thirty years of service
said it once to a young colleague
See her there?
They've cancelled everything here
the night train the local the buffet the post office
she's the only service still running

And sure enough
when they closed the line beyond the town
and the rails rusted
and the timetables dissolved in the rain
the grass stayed at its platform
It overgrew the sleepers overgrew the rails
and raised its seed-heads
right where all its life
the trains had rolled above its head

It was never in a hurry to get anywhere
and it travelled farthest of all

---

## 575. ECLIPSE OF THE SUN

At a quarter to eleven
the whole town the quarrelling town
the litigating town
the town of two parishes three pubs
and one endless dispute over parking

stopped
and looked up

The butcher next to the vegetarian
the landlord next to the tenant who owes him
the constable next to the graffiti kid
the bank director next to the man
who wouldn't return his good morning
the lady from the counter next to everyone
she had ever told COME BACK TOMORROW

All with their heads tipped back
all in the same cardboard glasses
all suddenly equally small
under one nibbled sun

Three minutes of night at noon
The birds went quiet
the coffee machine glowed in the dusk
and nobody blamed anybody for anything
because every mouth was hanging open

Then the sun came back
whole
Light like something freshly washed

And the town lowered its heads
put its everyday faces back on
and dispersed to its disputes

Only one boy asked his mother
Mum when is the next one?
In sixty years
said the mother

That's a pity
said the boy
that's a long time to wait
just to be kind together

---

## 576. THREE O'CLOCK ON A NOVEMBER AFTERNOON

In November the dark arrives before the children leave school
At three it waits at the gate
like an impatient grandmother
a whole hour early

The bell rings
and the children walk out into an evening
that came without an appointment
satchels lanterns woolly hats
and already the first street lamps overhead

Miss
why is it night when it isn't night yet?
asks a small girl in the cloakroom

It isn't night
says the teacher buttoning the girl's coat
it's just the sun in November
cutting down its hours
It teaches all year too
it gets tired too

The children walk home through the dark like miners
little shifts coming up from the pit of the day
and because of them the windows
light up earlier
the soups simmer earlier
the mothers watch from the windows earlier

And maybe that is exactly why
the teacher says to the caretaker
while locking up the classroom
November is the month of lit kitchens

In June the children come home into sunshine
and nobody looks out for them
In November they come home into darkness
and the whole street turns its lights on
just to see them coming

The dark isn't wicked
says the caretaker switching off
the dark only turns the lamps
into a proper welcome

---

## 577. JUNE DOWNPOUR

It fell without warning
a June sky has no patience
and the street emptied out
like a necklace snapping

Under the awning of the pastry shop
suddenly pressed together
a bank clerk with his briefcase
a girl with blue hair
a postwoman
a gentleman with a dachshund
and the dachshund

Five people and one dog
who otherwise would never have met
not at a wedding not at a funeral
not in the queue at the fishmonger's

The first minute they are silent
each watching a different rain

The second minute the man with the dog says
Well the gardens needed that
and everyone nods
even the dachshund

The third minute the clerk lends
his handkerchief to the girl with blue hair
because the blue is running down her neck
and it is his best handkerchief
and he doesn't want it back

The fourth minute the postwoman laughs
at something the girl said
and the man with the dog joins in
and they all laugh
the laughter of people who never introduced themselves

The fifth minute the rain eases
raises the curtain
and the show is over

They part in four directions
no addresses no names no promises
the bank the post round home the blue hair

The sky writes in its notebook
introduced a few more of them today
and has nothing to add
Summer carries on

---

## 578. THE NIGHT OF FALLING STARS

An August night on the hill outside town
blankets thermoses tilted heads
the whole town lying on its back
waiting for something to fall

There
somebody shouts
and a hundred people hurry to make a wish

Health for the little one
let him call back
let it work out
let her come home
let it finally stop hurting

And meanwhile up above
a stone is falling
A small cold stone
that flew through space for millions of years
knew nobody
promised nothing
and now burns up in two seconds
over one hill full of people

The physicist on his blanket would say
the star couldn't care less about your wish
it's just a pebble and friction

But the girl beside him asks
Then who gets what out of it?

The physicist is silent a long time
and then very quietly
The stone gets nothing
that's true
But you just admitted to yourself
what it is you really want
That almost never happens to people

Sometimes something
has to fall out of the sky
for a person to shine a light
into themselves for one second

There
somebody shouts
and the physicist hurries to make a wish

---

## 579. CIRCLES ON THE POND

A boy on the south bank
finds a stone
flat and just right for the palm
and throws it into the middle of the pond
just because
because he is seven
and the stone was there

Plop
and the stone is gone

But water keeps nothing to itself
Water broadcasts everything
First circle second third
the circles set out from the plop
like news that can't be stopped
wider and wider
softer and softer

The boy is already running home for his snack
and his circles are still travelling

They brush the reeds
rock the ducklings
lift and lower the fisherman's float
the fisherman looks up
nobody anywhere

And on the north bank
a twenty-minute walk away
an old lady sits on a bench
and watches the very last circle
arrive at her feet
no more than a wrinkle on the water
faint as breath
but it reaches her

Where have you come from
she asks it

The circle doesn't answer
circles carry no return address

So the old lady smiles at the whole pond
just in case
because somebody on the far side
may be waving right now
without knowing at whom

---

## 580. THE MOON BY DAY

At half past ten in the morning
the moon hangs in the blue sky
pale as after a night shift
a thumbprint on the clean heavens

Nobody looks
Today the show belongs to the sun
the sun gets the applause the sunglasses the ice cream
the crowded terraces the crowded beaches

And the moon?
The moon nothing
The moon hangs there and shines
into broad daylight
which is like singing
in a station full of express trains

A small girl at the bus stop spots it
Mum the moon overslept
it forgot to go home

No
says her mother
it never goes home
it's up there all the time
even now even at noon
you just can't see it
because the sun talks too loud

And at night?

At night the sun goes down
all the loud ones fall asleep
and suddenly it turns out
somebody was there all along

The little girl stares up for a long time

That's like grandma
she says at last
She sits in her corner all day
and nobody notices her
and at night when I'm scared
she shines the brightest of all

And the moon up there
pale unpaid patient
goes on shining into the wide day
It doesn't shine because anyone sees it
It shines because it is the moon

---

## 581. THE FATHER AND THE TIE

Sunday morning at the mirror
a father and a son
and between them a tie
navy blue with stripes
borrowed from adulthood

The wide end over the narrow
says the father
once around
up through the back
and down through the loop

The son is eight
and the knot looks like
anything in the world
except a knot

Never mind says the father
a knot isn't learned with hands
a knot is learned with years

And the years did the teaching
the first dance
the first job interview
the wedding
the funerals of strangers
and each time the son's hands remembered
the wide end over the narrow
once around
up through the back
and down through the loop

Today is Sunday again
the mirror is the same one
it just hangs in a smaller flat
the father holds the tie
and his hands tremble
as if they carried all those years

Come here says the son
and stands behind him
the way his father once stood behind him

Not too tight says the father
Don't worry says the son
a man taught me
at a mirror
one Sunday

In the mirror stand two men
and one knot
that holds
while everything else
slowly comes untied

---

## 582. THE OUTGROWN SLEEVES

Evening under the lamp
mother unpicks a hem
the coat bought in September
is too short by January
children grow faster than fabric

In the sleeves waits a reserve
two fingers of darker blue
the sun had no time to drink
a spare piece of winter
just in case

Children grow out of shoes
grow out of beds
grow out of bedtime stories
mum nobody says that anymore
grow out of the kitchen doorframe
where the pencil marks
climb over one another

Mother lets out
whatever can be let out
and what cannot be let out
will one day give way on its own

Then came a winter
when the sleeves lasted till spring
and after it a winter
when the coats went away
with the arms still in them
to other cities

Tonight the lamp is on again
and again a seam is being unpicked
only the other way round
father's coat has grown too big
old age grows inward

The needle isn't surprised
the needle sews
both directions with the same stitch
and always leaves a reserve
just in case
because love needs
room to grow

---

## 583. THE LAST CARRIAGE

In the last carriage ride
the ones who ran until the very last second
coat over the arm and heart in the throat
the ones who can't stand a crowd
a soldier on leave
a student with a guitar
and one old man
who picks the last carriage on purpose

Everyone pushes toward the front
closer to the engine
closer to the future
from the first carriage you get off first
they say
the first carriage sees the station soonest

Sir, asks the conductor
why do you always ride in the back

And the man points out the window
From here you can see the rails
slowly meeting again behind us
the station waving goodbye
instead of welcoming
the level crossing lowering its barriers
and lifting them at once like a hat
the cowshed the church and the three poplars
how long they stand in place
watching us go
here the countryside doesn't run to meet you
here the countryside takes its leave

Up front sit the people in a hurry
they see only where the train is going
the driver knows that too
and it changes nothing for him

In the back sits me
says the man
and I see the way we came
somebody after all has to watch
over everything
the train leaves in peace behind it

---

## 584. THE NIGHT CROSSING

Half past one in the morning
a level crossing in the middle of the fields
the red light blinking
blinking and blinking
like a heart somebody forgot to switch off

The driver is alone
the only car for miles
engine running heater humming
and the barriers down

A freight train is passing
and it has no end
wagons wagons wagons
the driver tries to count them
at thirty-seven he gives up

So he reads what they carry instead
timber from Poland
cars under tarpaulins
grain coal tank wagons
containers with words on them
he doesn't understand
the whole world rolls by in the night
to be in its place by morning
flour at the bakery
petrol at the pump
new cars in the showroom
and nobody thinks of the ones
who haul it through the dark

Somewhere up front in the locomotive
sits a man as alone as he is
two men awake in the middle of the night
one waiting one driving
and between them rumbles past
everything people will need by dawn

The last wagon with its little red lamp
the barriers rise
the road is clear

And the driver doesn't pull away just yet
he sits there looking into the dark
for the first time all day
nobody hurried him anywhere
they simply took three minutes from him
and suddenly he isn't quite sure
whether they were taken
or given

---

## 585. THE ROUNDABOUT

The roundabout at the edge of town
has four exits
to the motorway
to the airport
to the centre
and to the hospital
each one leads somewhere
each one promises something
and in the middle there is a flowerbed
that leads nowhere
it just blooms

One Sunday morning
a man began to circle
first lap second third
tenth

On the twelfth a patrol pulled him over
Sir, where are you going
Nowhere
That won't do, here you drive from somewhere to somewhere
I'm driving from nowhere in circles
the man replies

The officer takes off his cap
You can't do that
a road is for getting there

And the man points to the middle
You see that flowerbed
somebody planted it in spring
waters it weeds it
and nobody ever gives it a look
everyone watches the exits
the motorway the airport the centre
I am the first man
who has seen it from every side

The officer looks at the middle
and for a moment there is silence
You're right, they are lovely
he says
one more lap
and then go home

And the man drives one more lap
slowly
the way you walk around a statue
and the officer stands at the roadside
and for the first time in twenty years of duty
he looks at the middle
instead of the traffic

---

## 586. THE SIGNPOST IN THE MOUNTAINS

At the saddle where three paths meet
stands a signpost
an old wooden post with yellow arrows

SUMMIT 3 HOURS
LAKE 2 HOURS 30 MINUTES
VILLAGE 1 HOUR 15 MINUTES

The signpost promises hours
it promises nothing else
it doesn't say that from the summit you see a sea of clouds
it doesn't say the lake is cold even in August
it doesn't say they bake bread in the village
it only says what it will cost
and you pay with your legs

The hikers come up from below
sweating and full of questions
Which way is prettier
Which way is worth it
Which way should we go

And the signpost says nothing
it points in every direction at once
which comes to the same thing
as pointing nowhere at all

It knows all three paths by heart
every stone every switchback
and itself has never been anywhere
whoever points everywhere
arrives nowhere
whoever wants to arrive
must leave every path but one
alone

The young ones stand a long time arguing
the summit or the lake
the lake or the summit

And an old woman passes with her stick
without a glance at the signpost
You don't read the arrows? they call after her

Not anymore, she says
I've been coming here forty years
the arrows tell you how many hours
but where you are really going
was never written on them
that much everyone carries in their own pack

---

## 587. ROOM TWELVE

Hotel room number twelve
a bed a wardrobe a table a washbasin
and a window on the courtyard

It remembers no one
every morning at ten
the chambermaid comes
puts on clean sheets
airs the room mops wipes the dust
and the room is a blank page again

And yet everyone left something here

the travelling salesman a shirt button
the newlyweds laughter in the walls
the lady in black a night cried through
the musician the note of a tuned guitar
the girl who ran away from home
a pencilled message under the drawer
DON'T BE AFRAID
the man who couldn't sleep
a hollow in the armchair by the window
and a hollow in the windowsill
where the elbow goes

The chambermaid can erase it all
except what can't be erased

The receptionist says
room twelve is as good as new
nobody has been here
nobody is here

And meanwhile all over the world
walk people who remember
room number twelve
that's where she said yes
that's where I heard about dad
that's where I first slept alone
that's where we laughed till three in the morning

The room remembers no one
that is its job
forgetting is its trade
but everyone remembers the room
and so that little room with the courtyard window
lives a thousand lives at once
in strangers' heads
all over the world
and knows nothing about it

---

## 588. THE TRUCK DRIVER

Behind the windscreen of the truck rolls Europe
bridges over valleys
tunnels under the Alps
harbours where cranes bow like storks
Madrid at noon
Hamburg in fog
Milan in a traffic jam
petrol stations glowing in the dark
like little churches

And on the dashboard a photo
a wife two boys and a dog
standing in front of the house squinting
because whoever took the picture
stood them facing the sun

The drivers at the truck stop say
you're a real traveller you are
all of Europe behind your glass

And he shrugs
I only half see Europe
because with half my eyes
I'm always looking at that photo

The photo has faded
the Andalusian sun tanned it
the sea fogs soaked it
a Polish winter took its colours
a family that never went further than the lake
has travelled all of Europe
on a dashboard
without lifting a finger

All the roads he measures in kilometres
Rotterdam twelve hundred
Naples eighteen hundred
only one road is measured differently
the road home
that one is measured in days
and the last stretch in hours
and the last kilometre in heartbeats

And when on Friday evening he pulls up at the house
the wife the two boys and the dog
come running out the door
and stand exactly the way
they stand in the photo
except the boys are always
a little taller

---

## 589. THE SLOW TRAIN

The slow train stops everywhere
Upper Lhota
Lower Lhota
Lhota halt
Lhota village
it stops even where nobody gets on
because you never know

On the express the passengers check their watches
and the slow train is waved aside
it pulls onto the third track and waits
the express shoots past like lightning
full of people who will be everywhere first

The slow train doesn't watch it go
the slow train knows its own

the woman with the basket riding two stops to market
the boys from year six with their satchels
the stationmaster at Lower Lhota
who waves even when he doesn't have to
and his dog who waves with its tail
the fisherman who gets off in the middle of the fields
where the halt doesn't even have a name
only a sign
REQUEST STOP

The express crosses the stations so fast
it never has time to read their names
Upper Lhota to it is a smear
the woman with the basket a vertical stroke
whole countrysides are nothing to it
but green behind the glass

In the evening the two trains meet at the terminus
side by side along the platform
and the express hisses with pride
I got here two hours before you

True, says the slow train
you arrived earlier

and it falls silent the way one does
with a compartment full of names and greetings

I brought back more

---

## 590. THE PASSWORD

The form is strict
YOUR PASSWORD MUST CONTAIN
at least eight characters
one capital letter
one number
and one special character

The man sits at the keyboard
and wonders
what a person still remembers
after forgetting everything else

The capital letter M
that's Marie

the numbers nine and five
that's the ninth of May
when she stood on the station platform
in a dress borrowed from her sister
not knowing whether to wave

and the special character
an exclamation mark
because that's the kind of day it was

The form turns red
DO NOT USE NAMES OR PERSONAL DATES
it says under the box
someone might guess them

The man clicks I understand
and leaves it as it is
nobody will guess this
who wasn't there

STRONG PASSWORD
the machine congratulates him
with no idea how strong

CHANGE YOUR PASSWORD EVERY NINETY DAYS
the system advises in farewell

The man shakes his head
this password doesn't change
this password repeats itself
every morning
at login
quietly
so it won't be forgotten

---

## 591. I AM NOT A ROBOT

Tick the box
I AM NOT A ROBOT
and just to be sure
SELECT ALL SQUARES WITH CROSSWALKS

The man clicks
crosswalk
crosswalk
that one's a staircase not a crosswalk
crosswalk

CORRECT
says the machine
YOU ARE HUMAN
please proceed

And the man proceeds
up at six twenty
like yesterday
coffee two sugars
like yesterday
same tram same doors
same thumb on the same glass
like yesterday
like the day before
like all year long

In the evening he remembers the box
That was easy
he tells himself
the robot in me clicks very well
it clicks all day
it's been clicking for years
and nobody has caught it yet

The next morning
at a real crosswalk
an old lady stands
unable to make up her mind

The man stops
offers her his arm
and they cross together
slowly
terribly slowly
outside all the traffic of the internet

and that box
he ticked himself

---

## 592. PRESS ONE

Good afternoon
you have reached customer service

For contracts press one
for faults press two
for complaints press three
for payments press four
to hear about our offers press five
to change your details press six
to confirm press seven
to return to the main menu press eight

The man holds the receiver
and waits for nine

Nine never comes
instead there is music
that nobody has ever heard to the end

YOUR CALL IS IMPORTANT TO US

The man waits
he is seventy-three
and hasn't spoken to anyone today

YOUR CALL IS IMPORTANT TO US
THERE ARE TWELVE CALLERS AHEAD OF YOU

Meanwhile the man makes himself some tea
looks out the window
the pigeon on the ledge is waiting too
who knows for what

And then it clicks
Hello this is Jane
what can I do for you?

Nothing
says the man
you've already done it
you're talking

At the other end of the line
a moment of silence
a human one this time
the kind no switchboard will ever record
and then
a very quiet laugh

that one isn't on the menu
one through nine

---

## 593. TWO-YEAR WARRANTY

The washing machine died on a Monday
two years and one week
after purchase

That's no longer covered by the warranty
says the man behind the counter
spreading his hands
as if the weather were to blame

Next aisle they sell a new one
TWO-YEAR WARRANTY
says the label
and the salesman smiles
a smile guaranteed
until closing time

But in grandmother's kitchen
stands a fridge
bought the year
people flew to the Moon

It hums
all this time it has hummed
it outlived the country that made it
three currencies
six governments
and two family quarrels over inheritance

it keeps the butter
the medicine
and under a magnet the grandchildren's photos

Grandma does it still run?
And why wouldn't it run
says the grandmother

It never promised anyone anything
that fridge
no paper
no stamp
no two years

and maybe that's exactly why
says the grandmother closing the door
it lasts

At night when the house is asleep
you can hear it
humming in the dark
like somebody breathing
with no intention of stopping

---

## 594. THE FOOD PHOTO

In the restaurant by the station
the soup arrives
and over the table the phones rise
like a flock of birds over a field

Nobody eats
Everybody photographs
close up
from above
from the side
one more with better light

The steam rises into the lenses
meanwhile the soup goes cold
very quietly
the way soups know how

The kitchen sends out more plates
and more phones take flight

At grandmother's nobody ever took pictures
On Sundays the roast smelled
from morning on
the smell went down the stairs
all the way to the street
and the people in the house knew
it's Sunday at the Nováks'

Grandmother carried in the plates
nobody raised anything
but a spoon
and everything got eaten
even the bits people saved for last

Of that whole kitchen
not a single photo remains

and yet everyone can see it
just close your eyes
and there it is
the steam over the plate
grandmother's hands
Sunday

The soup in the photo
never goes cold
that's true

it never warms anyone either

---

## 595. THE PUB REVIEW

Four point seven stars
says the phone
and outside the pub two tourists
read what strangers have to say
about the foam
about the service
about the toilets

GREAT BEER BUT LONG WAIT
wrote Martin K three weeks ago

Inside by the window sits Mr Souček
he sits there on Tuesdays
he sits there on Fridays
he's sat there through three barmen
and he never gave a single star
nobody knows where you put them

The tourists come in
photograph the tap
photograph the ceiling
take a sip
give five stars
and are never seen again

Mr Souček raises one finger
as always
and the barman pours him
what he pours for no one else
his usual
just under the line

The young man at the next table leans over
So would you recommend this place?

Mr Souček looks around
at the bar at the ceiling at the window

I've been coming here forty years
one Tuesday I met my wife here
and last year one Friday I drank here
to her memory

Whether that's five stars or one
I couldn't tell you

it doesn't fit in a phone

---

## 596. THE AUTOMATIC DOORS

The doors of the department store
open

for the lady with the pram
they open
for the mayor with his briefcase
they open
for the boy who's only come to look
they open
for the beggar from the fountain
they open
exactly the same
to the same width
just as wide

They ask for no name
they don't size up your shoes
they don't check your pockets
they want to see no papers

The mayor walks through without noticing
the lady with the pram tells her children
say thank you
and the children thank the doors

In the evening the beggar stops by them
just like that
for the warm air
and the doors bow
welcome
and the people around look away
but not the doors
the doors look exactly at him
and step out of his way
the way people step aside for kings

The politest thing in town
says the beggar
nobody taught them that
they just forgot to tell them
who they're not supposed to open for

At night the store closes
and the doors stand in the dark
and wait

for morning to come
and someone to bow to again
